window.PYREF={"sections":[{"id":"tk-window","title":"Windows","blurb":"Opening, sizing, placing and closing windows","group":"tkinter","n":25},{"id":"tk-label-button","title":"Labels and buttons","blurb":"Showing text, changing it, and running code on click","group":"tkinter","n":23},{"id":"tk-entry-text","title":"Text input","blurb":"Entry boxes and multi-line Text boxes","group":"tkinter","n":20},{"id":"tk-choices","title":"Choices and lists","blurb":"Checkbuttons, radio buttons, dropdowns, listboxes, sliders, spinboxes","group":"tkinter","n":16},{"id":"tk-layout","title":"Layout","blurb":"pack, grid, place, frames, spacing, resizing and scrolling","group":"tkinter","n":20},{"id":"tk-events","title":"Events and keys","blurb":"bind, keyboard, mouse, shortcuts, focus","group":"tkinter","n":17},{"id":"tk-vars","title":"Variables","blurb":"StringVar, IntVar, BooleanVar, trace, sharing values","group":"tkinter","n":7},{"id":"tk-time","title":"Timers, loops and threads","blurb":"after(), animation, clocks, freezing, background work","group":"tkinter","n":10},{"id":"tk-menus","title":"Menus and dialogs","blurb":"Menu bars, right-click menus, message boxes, file pickers","group":"tkinter","n":15},{"id":"tk-canvas","title":"Canvas and drawing","blurb":"Shapes, text, moving things, collisions, drawing apps","group":"tkinter","n":15},{"id":"tk-images","title":"Images","blurb":"PNG, JPG, resizing, backgrounds, why images vanish","group":"tkinter","n":11},{"id":"tk-style","title":"Look and style","blurb":"Colours, fonts, ttk themes, dark mode, making it look modern","group":"tkinter","n":12},{"id":"tk-adv","title":"Tables, tabs and progress","blurb":"Treeview tables, Notebook tabs, Progressbar","group":"tkinter","n":10},{"id":"tk-structure","title":"Organising an app","blurb":"Classes, multiple pages, sharing data, saving settings, bigger programs","group":"tkinter","n":9},{"id":"tk-errors","title":"tkinter errors","blurb":"What tkinter error messages mean and how to fix them","group":"tkinter","n":11},{"id":"tk-recipes","title":"Complete small apps","blurb":"Whole working programs to copy, run and change","group":"tkinter","n":11},{"id":"tk-extras","title":"Sharing and extras","blurb":"Making an .exe, sound, clipboard, links, keyboard and system tricks","group":"tkinter","n":11},{"id":"py-basics","title":"Basics","blurb":"print, input, variables, numbers, if, loops, functions","group":"python","n":32},{"id":"py-strings","title":"Strings","blurb":"Text: slicing, splitting, joining, searching, changing","group":"python","n":16},{"id":"py-lists","title":"Lists and tuples","blurb":"Adding, removing, sorting, searching, copying, comprehensions","group":"python","n":20},{"id":"py-dicts","title":"Dictionaries and sets","blurb":"Key/value data, counting, grouping, sets","group":"python","n":12},{"id":"py-files","title":"Files and folders","blurb":"Reading, writing, CSV, JSON, paths, folders","group":"python","n":13},{"id":"py-errors","title":"Error messages","blurb":"What each error means and how to fix it","group":"python","n":31},{"id":"py-classes","title":"Classes and objects","blurb":"class, self, __init__, inheritance, dataclasses","group":"python","n":10},{"id":"py-modules","title":"Modules and installing","blurb":"import, pip, venv, useful built-in modules","group":"python","n":19},{"id":"py-howto","title":"Common tasks","blurb":"Small, frequently searched programs and techniques","group":"python","n":18}],"entries":[{"id":"tk.first-window","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I make a window in tkinter?","qh":"How do I make a window in tkinter?","a":"Create one <code>Tk()</code> object, add widgets to it, then call <code>mainloop()</code> as the last line.","aka":"create window, open gui, empty window, tk.Tk, hello world gui, start tkinter, blank window","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()              <i class=\"c\"># the main window</i>\nroot.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;My app&quot;</i>)        <i class=\"c\"># text in the title bar</i>\n\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hello!&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\n\nroot.mainloop()             <i class=\"c\"># keeps the window open and responding</i></pre></figure><p><code>root</code> is just a name; <code>window</code> or <code>app</code> works the same. Make only <b>one</b> <code>Tk()</code> per program. For extra windows use <a href=\"#/a/tk.toplevel\">Toplevel</a>.</p>","see":["tk.mainloop","tk.import-style","tk.geometry"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() # the main window root.title( \"My app\" ) # text in the title bar tk.Label(root, text= \"Hello!\" ).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop() # keeps the window open and responding root is just a name; window or app works the same. Make only one Tk() per program. For extra windows use Toplevel ."},{"id":"tk.mainloop","s":"tk-window","q":"Why does my window open and close instantly (or never show)?","qh":"Why does my window open and close instantly (or never show)?","a":"You're missing <code>root.mainloop()</code> at the end, or something after it never gets reached. <code>mainloop()</code> is what keeps the window alive.","aka":"window disappears, window not showing, mainloop missing, gui closes immediately, nothing happens when I run","err":"","h":"<p><code>mainloop()</code> waits for clicks and key presses and redraws the window. Code <b>after</b> it only runs once the window is closed.</p><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Still here&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nroot.mainloop()\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;This prints after you close the window&quot;</i>)</pre></figure><p>Other causes:</p><ul><li>Double-clicking a <code>.pyw</code>/<code>.py</code> file that crashes. Run it from a terminal or IDLE so you can see the error.</li><li>An infinite <code>while True:</code> loop before <code>mainloop()</code>. Use <a href=\"#/a/tk.after\">after()</a> for repeating work instead.</li></ul>","see":["tk.first-window","tk.after-not-sleep"],"t":"mainloop() waits for clicks and key presses and redraws the window. Code after it only runs once the window is closed. import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"Still here\" ).pack(padx= 30 , pady= 30 ) root.mainloop() print ( \"This prints after you close the window\" ) Other causes: Double-clicking a .pyw / .py file that crashes. Run it from a terminal or IDLE so you can see the error. An infinite while True: loop before mainloop() . Use after() for repeating work instead."},{"id":"tk.import-style","s":"tk-window","q":"Should I use import tkinter as tk or from tkinter import *?","qh":"Should I use <code>import tkinter as tk</code> or <code>from tkinter import *</code>?","a":"Prefer <code>import tkinter as tk</code> (and <code>from tkinter import ttk</code>). It keeps names clear and avoids clashes. <code>from tkinter import *</code> works but hides where names come from.","aka":"import tkinter, from tkinter import star, tkinter vs Tkinter, ttk import, import ttk","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk, messagebox\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nttk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hi&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: messagebox.showinfo(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hi&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Hello&quot;</i>)).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><ul><li>Python 3: <code>tkinter</code> (lowercase). Python 2 used <code>Tkinter</code>. If a tutorial says <code>import Tkinter</code>, it is very old.</li><li><code>messagebox</code>, <code>filedialog</code>, <code>ttk</code>, <code>simpledialog</code>, <code>colorchooser</code> are <b>submodules</b>: <code>import tkinter</code> alone does not load them, so <code>tk.messagebox</code> can fail. Import them explicitly.</li></ul>","see":["tk.install","tk.ttk-vs-tk"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk, messagebox root = tk.Tk() ttk.Button(root, text= \"Hi\" , command= lambda : messagebox.showinfo( \"Hi\" , \"Hello\" )).pack(padx= 30 , pady= 30 ) root.mainloop() Python 3: tkinter (lowercase). Python 2 used Tkinter . If a tutorial says import Tkinter , it is very old. messagebox , filedialog , ttk , simpledialog , colorchooser are submodules : import tkinter alone does not load them, so tk.messagebox can fail. Import them explicitly."},{"id":"tk.install","s":"tk-window","q":"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'tkinter'. How do I install it?","qh":"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'tkinter'. How do I install it?","a":"tkinter comes with Python; you don't <code>pip install</code> it. On Windows/macOS re-run the python.org installer and tick \"tcl/tk and IDLE\". On Linux install the system package.","aka":"install tkinter, pip install tkinter, tkinter missing, no module named _tkinter, tkinter not found linux, tkinter mac","err":"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'tkinter'|No module named '_tkinter'|ImportError: No module named Tkinter","h":"<figure class=\"code sh\" data-tag=\"terminal\"><pre># Debian / Ubuntu / Raspberry Pi OS\nsudo apt install python3-tk\n# Fedora\nsudo dnf install python3-tkinter\n# Arch\nsudo pacman -S tk\n# macOS with Homebrew Python\nbrew install python-tk</pre></figure><p>Test it:</p><figure class=\"code sh\" data-tag=\"terminal\"><pre>python3 -m tkinter</pre></figure><p>A small window with a \"Click me!\" button means tkinter works. <code>pip install tkinter</code> always fails because it isn't on PyPI.</p>","see":["tk.import-style","tk.file-named-tkinter"],"t":"# Debian / Ubuntu / Raspberry Pi OS sudo apt install python3-tk # Fedora sudo dnf install python3-tkinter # Arch sudo pacman -S tk # macOS with Homebrew Python brew install python-tk Test it: python3 -m tkinter A small window with a \"Click me!\" button means tkinter works. pip install tkinter always fails because it isn't on PyPI."},{"id":"tk.file-named-tkinter","s":"tk-window","q":"AttributeError: module 'tkinter' has no attribute 'Tk'","qh":"AttributeError: module 'tkinter' has no attribute 'Tk'","a":"You named your own file <code>tkinter.py</code> (or there's a <code>tkinter</code> folder next to it), so Python imports <b>your</b> file instead of the real one. Rename it, and delete any <code>__pycache__</code> folder beside it.","aka":"partially initialized module tkinter, tkinter has no attribute, circular import tkinter, my file is called tkinter.py","err":"AttributeError: module 'tkinter' has no attribute|partially initialized module 'tkinter'","h":"<p>The same happens with <code>random.py</code>, <code>math.py</code>, <code>turtle.py</code>, <code>time.py</code>, <code>socket.py</code>... Never name a script after a module you import.</p><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(tkinter.__file__)   <i class=\"c\"># shows which file was really imported</i></pre></figure>","see":["py.err.circular-import"],"t":"The same happens with random.py , math.py , turtle.py , time.py , socket.py ... Never name a script after a module you import. import tkinter print (tkinter.__file__) # shows which file was really imported"},{"id":"tk.title","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I change the window title?","qh":"How do I change the window title?","a":"<code>root.title(&quot;New title&quot;)</code>. Call it again any time to change it.","aka":"title bar text, rename window, window name, set title","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Score: 0&quot;</i>)\nscore = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">add</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> score\n    score += <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n    root.title(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Score: </i>{score}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;+1&quot;</i>, command=add).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.title( \"Score: 0\" ) score = 0 def add (): global score score += 1 root.title( f\" Score: {score} \" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"+1\" , command=add).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.geometry","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I set the window size?","qh":"How do I set the window size?","a":"<code>root.geometry(&quot;400x300&quot;)</code> sets width x height in pixels. Add <code>+x+y</code> to also set its position: <code>&quot;400x300+100+50&quot;</code>.","aka":"window size, width height, geometry, resize window, set window dimensions, 800x600","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;400x300&quot;</i>)        <i class=\"c\"># width x height</i>\n<i class=\"c\"># root.geometry(&quot;400x300+200+100&quot;)  # ... and 200px from left, 100px from top</i>\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;400 x 300&quot;</i>).pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Without <code>geometry()</code> the window shrinks to fit its widgets. Note the <code>x</code> is a lowercase letter, not <code>*</code>.</p>","see":["tk.center","tk.resizable","tk.minsize","tk.fullscreen"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"400x300\" ) # width x height # root.geometry(\"400x300+200+100\") # ... and 200px from left, 100px from top tk.Label(root, text= \"400 x 300\" ).pack(expand= True ) root.mainloop() Without geometry() the window shrinks to fit its widgets. Note the x is a lowercase letter, not * ."},{"id":"tk.center","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I center the window on the screen?","qh":"How do I center the window on the screen?","a":"Read the screen size with <code>winfo_screenwidth()</code>/<code>winfo_screenheight()</code>, work out the top-left corner, and pass it to <code>geometry()</code>.","aka":"centre window, middle of screen, center gui, position window center","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">center</i>(win, w, h):\n    sw = win.winfo_screenwidth()\n    sh = win.winfo_screenheight()\n    x = (sw - w) // <i class=\"n\">2</i>\n    y = (sh - h) // <i class=\"n\">2</i>\n    win.geometry(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{w}<i class=\"s\">x</i>{h}<i class=\"s\">+</i>{x}<i class=\"s\">+</i>{y}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ncenter(root, <i class=\"n\">420</i>, <i class=\"n\">260</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Centered&quot;</i>).pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>If you don't know the size in advance, call <code>win.update_idletasks()</code> first, then use <code>win.winfo_width()</code> / <code>winfo_height()</code>.</p>","see":["tk.geometry","tk.screen-size"],"t":"import tkinter as tk def center (win, w, h): sw = win.winfo_screenwidth() sh = win.winfo_screenheight() x = (sw - w) // 2 y = (sh - h) // 2 win.geometry( f\" {w} x {h} + {x} + {y} \" ) root = tk.Tk() center(root, 420 , 260 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"Centered\" ).pack(expand= True ) root.mainloop() If you don't know the size in advance, call win.update_idletasks() first, then use win.winfo_width() / winfo_height() ."},{"id":"tk.screen-size","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I get the screen width and height?","qh":"How do I get the screen width and height?","a":"<code>root.winfo_screenwidth()</code> and <code>root.winfo_screenheight()</code> return pixels.","aka":"monitor size, screen resolution, display size, winfo_screenwidth","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nw, h = root.winfo_screenwidth(), root.winfo_screenheight()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Your screen is </i>{w}<i class=\"s\"> x </i>{h}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>With display scaling on Windows (e.g. 150%), the numbers may be the scaled size. See <a href=\"#/a/tk.blurry-windows\">Why does my tkinter window look blurry on Windows?</a>.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() w, h = root.winfo_screenwidth(), root.winfo_screenheight() tk.Label(root, text= f\" Your screen is {w} x {h} \" ).pack(padx= 30 , pady= 30 ) root.mainloop() With display scaling on Windows (e.g. 150%), the numbers may be the scaled size. See Why does my tkinter window look blurry on Windows? ."},{"id":"tk.resizable","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I stop the user resizing the window?","qh":"How do I stop the user resizing the window?","a":"<code>root.resizable(False, False)</code> (width, height). This also greys out maximize on Windows.","aka":"fixed size window, disable resize, disable maximize, lock window size, resizable false","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;300x200&quot;</i>)\nroot.resizable(<i class=\"k\">False</i>, <i class=\"k\">False</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Can&#x27;t resize me&quot;</i>).pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.minsize","tk.geometry"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"300x200\" ) root.resizable( False , False ) tk.Label(root, text= \"Can't resize me\" ).pack(expand= True ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.minsize","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I set a minimum or maximum window size?","qh":"How do I set a minimum or maximum window size?","a":"<code>root.minsize(w, h)</code> and <code>root.maxsize(w, h)</code>.","aka":"minsize, maxsize, smallest window size, limit resize","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.minsize(<i class=\"n\">300</i>, <i class=\"n\">200</i>)\nroot.maxsize(<i class=\"n\">800</i>, <i class=\"n\">600</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Resize me, within limits&quot;</i>).pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.minsize( 300 , 200 ) root.maxsize( 800 , 600 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"Resize me, within limits\" ).pack(expand= True ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.fullscreen","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I make the window fullscreen or maximized?","qh":"How do I make the window fullscreen or maximized?","a":"<code>root.attributes(&quot;-fullscreen&quot;, True)</code> for true fullscreen. For maximized use <code>root.state(&quot;zoomed&quot;)</code> on Windows/macOS or <code>root.attributes(&quot;-zoomed&quot;, True)</code> on Linux.","aka":"full screen, maximize window, zoomed, kiosk mode, F11 fullscreen, escape exit fullscreen","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;F11 toggles fullscreen, Esc leaves&quot;</i>).pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">toggle</i>(event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    root.attributes(<i class=\"s\">&quot;-fullscreen&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">not</i> root.attributes(<i class=\"s\">&quot;-fullscreen&quot;</i>))\n\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;F11&gt;&quot;</i>, toggle)\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Escape&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: root.attributes(<i class=\"s\">&quot;-fullscreen&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">False</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Always give the user a key to leave fullscreen; there is no title bar to close it.</p>","see":["tk.bind-keys"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"F11 toggles fullscreen, Esc leaves\" ).pack(expand= True ) def toggle (event= None ): root.attributes( \"-fullscreen\" , not root.attributes( \"-fullscreen\" )) root.bind( \"<F11>\" , toggle) root.bind( \"<Escape>\" , lambda e: root.attributes( \"-fullscreen\" , False )) root.mainloop() Always give the user a key to leave fullscreen; there is no title bar to close it."},{"id":"tk.icon","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I change the window icon?","qh":"How do I change the window icon?","a":"Use <code>root.iconphoto(True, tk.PhotoImage(file=&quot;icon.png&quot;))</code>, which works on all systems. <code>root.iconbitmap(&quot;app.ico&quot;)</code> works with <code>.ico</code> files on Windows only.","aka":"window icon, iconbitmap, iconphoto, taskbar icon, feather icon, ico file","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nicon = tk.PhotoImage(file=<i class=\"s\">&quot;icon.png&quot;</i>)   <i class=\"c\"># keep this variable around</i>\nroot.iconphoto(<i class=\"k\">True</i>, icon)              <i class=\"c\"># True = also for later windows</i>\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;New icon&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>On Windows the taskbar may still show the Python icon when run as a script; it shows correctly once packaged (e.g. with PyInstaller <code>--icon</code>).</p>","see":["tk.photoimage"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() icon = tk.PhotoImage(file= \"icon.png\" ) # keep this variable around root.iconphoto( True , icon) # True = also for later windows tk.Label(root, text= \"New icon\" ).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop() On Windows the taskbar may still show the Python icon when run as a script; it shows correctly once packaged (e.g. with PyInstaller --icon )."},{"id":"tk.bg-color","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I change the background colour of the window?","qh":"How do I change the background colour of the window?","a":"<code>root.configure(bg=&quot;lightblue&quot;)</code> (or a hex code like <code>&quot;#223344&quot;</code>). Widgets keep their own background, so set <code>bg</code> on them too.","aka":"window background, bg color, background color, root color, configure bg","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;300x200&quot;</i>)\nroot.configure(bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#1e2a38&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Dark&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#1e2a38&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>).pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>ttk widgets ignore <code>bg=</code>; style them with <a href=\"#/a/tk.ttk-style\">ttk.Style</a>.</p>","see":["tk.colors","tk.ttk-style"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"300x200\" ) root.configure(bg= \"#1e2a38\" ) tk.Label(root, text= \"Dark\" , bg= \"#1e2a38\" , fg= \"white\" ).pack(expand= True ) root.mainloop() ttk widgets ignore bg= ; style them with ttk.Style ."},{"id":"tk.topmost","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I keep the window always on top?","qh":"How do I keep the window always on top?","a":"<code>root.attributes(&quot;-topmost&quot;, True)</code>.","aka":"always on top, stay on top, topmost, float above other windows, pin window","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.attributes(<i class=\"s\">&quot;-topmost&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">True</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;I stay on top&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>To bring a window to the front once, call <code>root.lift()</code> then <code>root.focus_force()</code>.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.attributes( \"-topmost\" , True ) tk.Label(root, text= \"I stay on top\" ).pack(padx= 30 , pady= 30 ) root.mainloop() To bring a window to the front once, call root.lift() then root.focus_force() ."},{"id":"tk.alpha","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I make a window transparent?","qh":"How do I make a window transparent?","a":"<code>root.attributes(&quot;-alpha&quot;, 0.8)</code> sets the opacity of the whole window (0 = invisible, 1 = solid).","aka":"transparency, opacity, see through window, alpha, transparent background","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.attributes(<i class=\"s\">&quot;-alpha&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">0.85</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;85% opaque&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Making only the <b>background</b> see-through (not the widgets) is Windows-only: <code>root.attributes(&quot;-transparentcolor&quot;, &quot;white&quot;)</code> makes every pure white pixel transparent.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.attributes( \"-alpha\" , 0.85 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"85% opaque\" ).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop() Making only the background see-through (not the widgets) is Windows-only: root.attributes(\"-transparentcolor\", \"white\") makes every pure white pixel transparent."},{"id":"tk.no-titlebar","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I remove the title bar (borderless window)?","qh":"How do I remove the title bar (borderless window)?","a":"<code>root.overrideredirect(True)</code>. You then have to provide your own close button and dragging.","aka":"overrideredirect, no border, frameless window, hide title bar, custom title bar, splash screen","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.overrideredirect(<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;260x120+300+200&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;No title bar&quot;</i>).pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Close&quot;</i>, command=root.destroy).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">8</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Good for splash screens. Such a window won't appear in the taskbar on Windows.</p>","see":["tk.drag-window"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.overrideredirect( True ) root.geometry( \"260x120+300+200\" ) tk.Label(root, text= \"No title bar\" ).pack(expand= True ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Close\" , command=root.destroy).pack(pady= 8 ) root.mainloop() Good for splash screens. Such a window won't appear in the taskbar on Windows."},{"id":"tk.drag-window","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I drag a window that has no title bar?","qh":"How do I drag a window that has no title bar?","a":"Remember where the mouse went down, then move the window by the difference on <code>&lt;B1-Motion&gt;</code>.","aka":"move borderless window, drag frameless window, move window with mouse","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.overrideredirect(<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;240x100+300+300&quot;</i>)\nbar = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;drag me&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#333&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\nbar.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Close&quot;</i>, command=root.destroy).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">start</i>(e):\n    root._dx, root._dy = e.x, e.y\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">move</i>(e):\n    root.geometry(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">+</i>{e.x_root - root._dx}<i class=\"s\">+</i>{e.y_root - root._dy}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\nbar.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, start)\nbar.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;B1-Motion&gt;&quot;</i>, move)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.no-titlebar","tk.mouse"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.overrideredirect( True ) root.geometry( \"240x100+300+300\" ) bar = tk.Label(root, text= \"drag me\" , bg= \"#333\" , fg= \"white\" ) bar.pack(fill= \"x\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Close\" , command=root.destroy).pack(pady= 10 ) def start (e): root._dx, root._dy = e.x, e.y def move (e): root.geometry( f\" + {e.x_root - root._dx} + {e.y_root - root._dy} \" ) bar.bind( \"<Button-1>\" , start) bar.bind( \"<B1-Motion>\" , move) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.close-protocol","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I run code when the user clicks the X (close) button?","qh":"How do I run code when the user clicks the X (close) button?","a":"<code>root.protocol(&quot;WM_DELETE_WINDOW&quot;, your_function)</code>. Your function must call <code>root.destroy()</code> if it wants the window to actually close.","aka":"on close, WM_DELETE_WINDOW, confirm exit, are you sure you want to quit, save before closing, close event, intercept close","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> messagebox\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Try closing me&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">on_close</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> messagebox.askokcancel(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Quit&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Really quit?&quot;</i>):\n        root.destroy()\n\nroot.protocol(<i class=\"s\">&quot;WM_DELETE_WINDOW&quot;</i>, on_close)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.quit-vs-destroy","tk.messagebox"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import messagebox root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"Try closing me\" ).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) def on_close (): if messagebox.askokcancel( \"Quit\" , \"Really quit?\" ): root.destroy() root.protocol( \"WM_DELETE_WINDOW\" , on_close) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.quit-vs-destroy","s":"tk-window","q":"What's the difference between quit() and destroy()? How do I close the window from code?","qh":"What's the difference between <code>quit()</code> and <code>destroy()</code>? How do I close the window from code?","a":"Use <code>root.destroy()</code>. It closes the window and ends <code>mainloop()</code>. <code>root.quit()</code> only stops <code>mainloop()</code> and can leave the window on screen (common in IDLE).","aka":"close window button, exit button, quit button, root.quit, root.destroy, close program, end tkinter","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Exit&quot;</i>, command=root.destroy).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>To close a second window only, call <code>that_window.destroy()</code>.</p>","see":["tk.close-protocol"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Button(root, text= \"Exit\" , command=root.destroy).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop() To close a second window only, call that_window.destroy() ."},{"id":"tk.toplevel","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I open a second window?","qh":"How do I open a second window?","a":"Use <code>tk.Toplevel(root)</code>. Do not create a second <code>tk.Tk()</code>; it causes strange bugs like \"image doesn't exist\".","aka":"new window, popup window, Toplevel, open another window, child window, multiple windows, second Tk","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Main&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">open_window</i>():\n    win = tk.Toplevel(root)\n    win.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Second&quot;</i>)\n    win.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;220x120&quot;</i>)\n    tk.Label(win, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hello from window 2&quot;</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n    tk.Button(win, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Close&quot;</i>, command=win.destroy).pack()\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Open&quot;</i>, command=open_window).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">50</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.modal","tk.err.pyimage","tk.pass-data-windows"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.title( \"Main\" ) def open_window (): win = tk.Toplevel(root) win.title( \"Second\" ) win.geometry( \"220x120\" ) tk.Label(win, text= \"Hello from window 2\" ).pack(pady= 20 ) tk.Button(win, text= \"Close\" , command=win.destroy).pack() tk.Button(root, text= \"Open\" , command=open_window).pack(padx= 50 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.modal","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I make a popup window that blocks the main window until it's closed (modal)?","qh":"How do I make a popup window that blocks the main window until it's closed (modal)?","a":"In the Toplevel call <code>grab_set()</code> (all clicks go to it), <code>transient(root)</code> (stays on top of root), then <code>root.wait_window(win)</code> if you need to wait for the answer.","aka":"modal dialog, grab_set, wait_window, block main window, dialog box, custom dialog","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nresult = tk.StringVar(value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;(nothing yet)&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">ask_name</i>():\n    win = tk.Toplevel(root)\n    win.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Name&quot;</i>)\n    win.transient(root)\n    win.grab_set()\n    e = tk.Entry(win)\n    e.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n    e.focus()\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">ok</i>():\n        result.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(e.get())\n        win.destroy()\n    tk.Button(win, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;OK&quot;</i>, command=ok).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\n    root.wait_window(win)       <i class=\"c\"># code below waits until win closes</i>\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Got:&quot;</i>, result.get())\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ask name&quot;</i>, command=ask_name).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\ntk.Label(root, textvariable=result).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">20</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.toplevel","tk.simpledialog"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() result = tk.StringVar(value= \"(nothing yet)\" ) def ask_name (): win = tk.Toplevel(root) win.title( \"Name\" ) win.transient(root) win.grab_set() e = tk.Entry(win) e.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) e.focus() def ok (): result. set (e.get()) win.destroy() tk.Button(win, text= \"OK\" , command=ok).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.wait_window(win) # code below waits until win closes print ( \"Got:\" , result.get()) tk.Button(root, text= \"Ask name\" , command=ask_name).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 20 ) tk.Label(root, textvariable=result).pack(pady=( 0 , 20 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.withdraw","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I hide a window and show it again?","qh":"How do I hide a window and show it again?","a":"<code>win.withdraw()</code> hides it, <code>win.deiconify()</code> shows it again. <code>win.iconify()</code> minimizes it.","aka":"hide window, show window, withdraw, deiconify, iconify, minimize window, hide main window, show dialog without main window","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nother = tk.Toplevel(root)\ntk.Label(other, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Peekaboo&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">toggle</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> other.winfo_viewable():\n        other.withdraw()\n    <i class=\"k\">else</i>:\n        other.deiconify()\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hide / show&quot;</i>, command=toggle).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>To show only a messagebox with no empty main window, call <code>root.withdraw()</code> first.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() other = tk.Toplevel(root) tk.Label(other, text= \"Peekaboo\" ).pack(padx= 30 , pady= 30 ) def toggle (): if other.winfo_viewable(): other.withdraw() else : other.deiconify() tk.Button(root, text= \"Hide / show\" , command=toggle).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop() To show only a messagebox with no empty main window, call root.withdraw() first."},{"id":"tk.blurry-windows","s":"tk-window","q":"Why does my tkinter window look blurry on Windows?","qh":"Why does my tkinter window look blurry on Windows?","a":"Windows stretches programs that aren't DPI-aware. Tell Windows your program is DPI-aware <b>before</b> creating <code>Tk()</code>.","aka":"blurry text, fuzzy fonts, high dpi, dpi awareness, pixelated tkinter, scaling 150%","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> sys\n\n<i class=\"k\">if</i> sys.platform == <i class=\"s\">&quot;win32&quot;</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">import</i> ctypes\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        ctypes.windll.shcore.SetProcessDpiAwareness(<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> Exception:\n        <i class=\"k\">pass</i>\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Sharp text&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Everything will look smaller but crisp. Use <code>root.tk.call(&quot;tk&quot;, &quot;scaling&quot;, 2.0)</code> to scale up if needed.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk import sys if sys.platform == \"win32\" : import ctypes try : ctypes.windll.shcore.SetProcessDpiAwareness( 1 ) except Exception: pass root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"Sharp text\" ).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop() Everything will look smaller but crisp. Use root.tk.call(\"tk\", \"scaling\", 2.0) to scale up if needed."},{"id":"tk.window-pos","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I get the window's current size and position?","qh":"How do I get the window's current size and position?","a":"<code>winfo_width()</code>, <code>winfo_height()</code>, <code>winfo_x()</code>, <code>winfo_y()</code>. Call <code>update_idletasks()</code> first if the window was just created, or you'll get 1.","aka":"window x y, winfo_x, winfo_width, where is the window, window coordinates","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;320x180+100+100&quot;</i>)\ninfo = tk.Label(root)\ninfo.pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">show</i>(event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    info.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{root.winfo_width()}<i class=\"s\">x</i>{root.winfo_height()}<i class=\"s\"> at </i>{root.winfo_x()}<i class=\"s\">,</i>{root.winfo_y()}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Configure&gt;&quot;</i>, show)\nroot.update_idletasks()\nshow()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>root.geometry()</code> with no argument returns the same info as one string like <code>&quot;320x180+100+100&quot;</code>.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"320x180+100+100\" ) info = tk.Label(root) info.pack(expand= True ) def show (event= None ): info.config(text= f\" {root.winfo_width()} x {root.winfo_height()} at {root.winfo_x()} , {root.winfo_y()} \" ) root.bind( \"<Configure>\" , show) root.update_idletasks() show() root.mainloop() root.geometry() with no argument returns the same info as one string like \"320x180+100+100\" ."},{"id":"tk.label","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I show text in a window?","qh":"How do I show text in a window?","a":"Use a <code>Label</code>: <code>tk.Label(root, text=&quot;Hi&quot;).pack()</code>.","aka":"label, display text, show message, print to window, put text on screen, tk.Label","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hello, world&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Big and red&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">20</i>), fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Useful options: <code>text</code>, <code>font</code>, <code>fg</code> (text colour), <code>bg</code>, <code>padx</code>/<code>pady</code> (inside space), <code>width</code> (in characters), <code>anchor</code>, <code>justify</code>, <code>wraplength</code>, <code>image</code>.</p>","see":["tk.label-change","tk.label-font","tk.label-wrap"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"Hello, world\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"Big and red\" , font=( \"Arial\" , 20 ), fg= \"red\" ).pack(pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() Useful options: text , font , fg (text colour), bg , padx / pady (inside space), width (in characters), anchor , justify , wraplength , image ."},{"id":"tk.label-change","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I change a label's text after it's created?","qh":"How do I change a label's text after it's created?","a":"Keep the label in a variable and call <code>label.config(text=&quot;new&quot;)</code>. Or link it to a <code>StringVar</code> with <code>textvariable=</code>.","aka":"update label, change label text, set label text, label config, refresh label, label not updating, change text of label","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlabel = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Not clicked&quot;</i>)\nlabel.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">clicked</i>():\n    label.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Clicked!&quot;</i>)      <i class=\"c\"># same as label[&quot;text&quot;] = &quot;Clicked!&quot;</i>\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Click&quot;</i>, command=clicked).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>If you get <code>&#x27;NoneType&#x27; object has no attribute &#x27;config&#x27;</code>, you wrote <code>label = tk.Label(...).pack()</code>. See <a href=\"#/a/tk.err.none-grid\">AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'get' (or 'config', 'insert'...)</a>.</p>","see":["tk.err.none-grid","tk.stringvar"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() label = tk.Label(root, text= \"Not clicked\" ) label.pack(padx= 40 , pady= 10 ) def clicked (): label.config(text= \"Clicked!\" ) # same as label[\"text\"] = \"Clicked!\" tk.Button(root, text= \"Click\" , command=clicked).pack(pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() If you get 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'config' , you wrote label = tk.Label(...).pack() . See AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'get' (or 'config', 'insert'...) ."},{"id":"tk.label-font","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I change the font and size of text?","qh":"How do I change the font and size of text?","a":"Pass <code>font=(&quot;Family&quot;, size, &quot;bold&quot;)</code>, e.g. <code>font=(&quot;Helvetica&quot;, 16, &quot;bold&quot;)</code>. Styles: <code>&quot;bold&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;italic&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;underline&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;overstrike&quot;</code>, or several: <code>&quot;bold italic&quot;</code>.","aka":"font size, bigger text, bold text, italic, change font, font family, text size label button","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Normal&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">12</i>)).pack()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Bold 18&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">18</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;bold&quot;</i>)).pack()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Italic underline&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Times&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">14</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;italic underline&quot;</i>)).pack()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Mono&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Courier&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">14</i>)).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>If the font isn't installed, Tk silently picks a fallback. A font name with spaces must be inside the tuple: <code>(&quot;Comic Sans MS&quot;, 12)</code>.</p>","see":["tk.font-object","tk.font-list"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"Normal\" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 12 )).pack() tk.Label(root, text= \"Bold 18\" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 18 , \"bold\" )).pack() tk.Label(root, text= \"Italic underline\" , font=( \"Times\" , 14 , \"italic underline\" )).pack() tk.Label(root, text= \"Mono\" , font=( \"Courier\" , 14 )).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop() If the font isn't installed, Tk silently picks a fallback. A font name with spaces must be inside the tuple: (\"Comic Sans MS\", 12) ."},{"id":"tk.label-wrap","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I make long text wrap onto several lines?","qh":"How do I make long text wrap onto several lines?","a":"Use <code>wraplength=</code> (in pixels) on a Label, or put <code>\\n</code> in the text. <code>justify=&quot;left&quot;</code> lines the lines up on the left.","aka":"wrap text, wraplength, multiline label, text goes off screen, newline in label, line break label","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlong = <i class=\"s\">&quot;This is a long sentence that would run off the side of a small window if it did not wrap.&quot;</i>\ntk.Label(root, text=long, wraplength=<i class=\"n\">220</i>, justify=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Line one\\nLine two&quot;</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>To wrap to whatever width the window is, update <code>wraplength</code> on resize:</p><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Resize the window and this text rewraps to fit the new width. &quot;</i> * <i class=\"n\">3</i>, justify=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>)\nlab.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nlab.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Configure&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: lab.config(wraplength=e.width - <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() long = \"This is a long sentence that would run off the side of a small window if it did not wrap.\" tk.Label(root, text=long, wraplength= 220 , justify= \"left\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"Line one\\nLine two\" ).pack(pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() To wrap to whatever width the window is, update wraplength on resize: import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"Resize the window and this text rewraps to fit the new width. \" * 3 , justify= \"left\" ) lab.pack(fill= \"x\" , padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) lab.bind( \"<Configure>\" , lambda e: lab.config(wraplength=e.width - 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.label-align","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I align text left or right inside a label?","qh":"How do I align text left or right inside a label?","a":"<code>anchor=&quot;w&quot;</code> moves the text to the left of the label's area (<code>&quot;e&quot;</code> right, <code>&quot;center&quot;</code>). <code>justify</code> only lines up multiple lines relative to each other.","aka":"anchor, justify, left align, right align, text alignment, align label text","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;300x150&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#eee&quot;</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;right&quot;</i>, anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;e&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#eee&quot;</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;center&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#eee&quot;</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>anchor</code> only matters when the label is wider than its text, which is why <code>fill=&quot;x&quot;</code> is used here.</p>","see":["tk.pack-fill","tk.grid-sticky"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"300x150\" ) tk.Label(root, text= \"left\" , anchor= \"w\" , bg= \"#eee\" ).pack(fill= \"x\" , padx= 10 , pady= 4 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"right\" , anchor= \"e\" , bg= \"#eee\" ).pack(fill= \"x\" , padx= 10 , pady= 4 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"center\" , bg= \"#eee\" ).pack(fill= \"x\" , padx= 10 , pady= 4 ) root.mainloop() anchor only matters when the label is wider than its text, which is why fill=\"x\" is used here."},{"id":"tk.button","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I make a button that runs a function?","qh":"How do I make a button that runs a function?","a":"Pass the function <b>name without brackets</b> as <code>command=</code>: <code>tk.Button(root, text=&quot;Go&quot;, command=go)</code>.","aka":"tkinter button, make a button, create a button, add a button, button click, button command, onclick, on click event, button callback, button does nothing, call function button","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">say_hi</i>():\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hi!&quot;</i>)\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Say hi&quot;</i>, command=say_hi).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>command=say_hi()</code> is wrong: it calls the function once at startup and gives the button its return value (<code>None</code>).</p>","see":["tk.err.command-runs-early","tk.button-args","tk.button-state"],"t":"import tkinter as tk def say_hi (): print ( \"Hi!\" ) root = tk.Tk() tk.Button(root, text= \"Say hi\" , command=say_hi).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop() command=say_hi() is wrong: it calls the function once at startup and gives the button its return value ( None )."},{"id":"tk.err.command-runs-early","s":"tk-label-button","q":"Why does my button's function run straight away instead of when I click?","qh":"Why does my button's function run straight away instead of when I click?","a":"You wrote <code>command=func()</code> with brackets. That calls it right away. Write <code>command=func</code>, or <code>command=lambda: func(arg)</code> if you need arguments.","aka":"command runs immediately, function runs on start, button runs once, button command called at startup, button not working on click","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre>tk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Save&quot;</i>, command=save())   <i class=\"c\"># runs save() now, once</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">save</i>(name):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Saving&quot;</i>, name)\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Save&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: save(<i class=\"s\">&quot;notes.txt&quot;</i>)).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.button-args","tk.lambda-loop"],"t":"tk.Button(root, text= \"Save\" , command=save()) # runs save() now, once import tkinter as tk def save (name): print ( \"Saving\" , name) root = tk.Tk() tk.Button(root, text= \"Save\" , command= lambda : save( \"notes.txt\" )).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.button-args","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I pass arguments to a button's command?","qh":"How do I pass arguments to a button's command?","a":"Wrap the call in a <code>lambda</code>: <code>command=lambda: add(5)</code>. <code>functools.partial(add, 5)</code> also works.","aka":"button function with parameters, command with arguments, lambda button, partial, pass value to function button","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> functools <i class=\"k\">import</i> partial\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntotal = tk.IntVar(value=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">add</i>(n):\n    total.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(total.get() + n)\n\ntk.Label(root, textvariable=total, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">20</i>)).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;+1&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: add(<i class=\"n\">1</i>)).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;+10&quot;</i>, command=partial(add, <i class=\"n\">10</i>)).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.lambda-loop"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from functools import partial root = tk.Tk() total = tk.IntVar(value= 0 ) def add (n): total. set (total.get() + n) tk.Label(root, textvariable=total, font=( \"Arial\" , 20 )).pack(pady= 10 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"+1\" , command= lambda : add( 1 )).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"+10\" , command=partial(add, 10 )).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.lambda-loop","s":"tk-label-button","q":"I made buttons in a loop and they all do the same thing (the last value). Why?","qh":"I made buttons in a loop and they all do the same thing (the last value). Why?","a":"A lambda remembers the <b>variable</b>, not its value at that moment. Freeze the value with a default argument: <code>command=lambda n=n: pick(n)</code>.","aka":"lambda in loop, buttons all same value, loop variable lambda, late binding, every button prints last number, buttons in for loop","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre><i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">5</i>):\n    tk.Button(root, text=n, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: pick(n))   <i class=\"c\"># every button gives 4</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nout = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Pick one&quot;</i>)\nout.pack()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">pick</i>(n):\n    out.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">You picked </i>{n}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">5</i>):\n    tk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"b\">str</i>(n), width=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i> n=n: pick(n)).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.button-args","tk.button-grid"],"t":"for n in range ( 5 ): tk.Button(root, text=n, command= lambda : pick(n)) # every button gives 4 import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() out = tk.Label(root, text= \"Pick one\" ) out.pack() def pick (n): out.config(text= f\" You picked {n} \" ) for n in range ( 5 ): tk.Button(root, text= str (n), width= 4 , command= lambda n=n: pick(n)).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 2 , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.button-grid","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I make a grid of buttons (like a keypad or tic-tac-toe board)?","qh":"How do I make a grid of buttons (like a keypad or tic-tac-toe board)?","a":"Create them in nested loops with <code>grid(row=r, column=c)</code> and store them in a list so you can change them later.","aka":"button grid, 3x3 buttons, keypad, many buttons loop, board of buttons, list of buttons","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nbuttons = []\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">press</i>(r, c):\n    b = buttons[r][c]\n    b.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;X&quot;</i>, state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;disabled&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> r <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">3</i>):\n    row = []\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> c <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">3</i>):\n        b = tk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot; &quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">3</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i> r=r, c=c: press(r, c))\n        b.grid(row=r, column=c, padx=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">2</i>)\n        row.append(b)\n    buttons.append(row)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.lambda-loop","tk.grid","tk.recipe-tictactoe"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() buttons = [] def press (r, c): b = buttons[r][c] b.config(text= \"X\" , state= \"disabled\" ) for r in range ( 3 ): row = [] for c in range ( 3 ): b = tk.Button(root, text= \" \" , width= 6 , height= 3 , command= lambda r=r, c=c: press(r, c)) b.grid(row=r, column=c, padx= 2 , pady= 2 ) row.append(b) buttons.append(row) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.button-state","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I disable a button (grey it out) and enable it again?","qh":"How do I disable a button (grey it out) and enable it again?","a":"<code>button.config(state=&quot;disabled&quot;)</code> and <code>button.config(state=&quot;normal&quot;)</code>. Works for most widgets (Entry, Text, Checkbutton...).","aka":"disable button, enable button, grey out, state disabled, unclickable button, button state normal","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ngo = tk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Go&quot;</i>, state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;disabled&quot;</i>)\ngo.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nagree = tk.BooleanVar()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">changed</i>():\n    go.config(state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;normal&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> agree.get() <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;disabled&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Checkbutton(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;I agree&quot;</i>, variable=agree, command=changed).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>For ttk widgets use <code>btn.state([&quot;disabled&quot;])</code> and <code>btn.state([&quot;!disabled&quot;])</code>, or <code>config(state=...)</code>, which also works.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() go = tk.Button(root, text= \"Go\" , state= \"disabled\" ) go.pack(padx= 40 , pady= 10 ) agree = tk.BooleanVar() def changed (): go.config(state= \"normal\" if agree.get() else \"disabled\" ) tk.Checkbutton(root, text= \"I agree\" , variable=agree, command=changed).pack(pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() For ttk widgets use btn.state([\"disabled\"]) and btn.state([\"!disabled\"]) , or config(state=...) , which also works."},{"id":"tk.button-size","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I change a button's size?","qh":"How do I change a button's size?","a":"<code>width</code> and <code>height</code> are in <b>characters/lines</b> for text buttons. For pixel sizes, use <code>padx</code>/<code>pady</code>, or put the button in a Frame of fixed size, or use <code>place(width=, height=)</code>.","aka":"button width height, bigger button, button size pixels, resize button, large button","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;width=20&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">20</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;height=3&quot;</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">3</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;padding&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">12</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;fills the width&quot;</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>If a button shows an image, <code>width</code>/<code>height</code> are in pixels.</p>","see":["tk.pack-fill"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Button(root, text= \"width=20\" , width= 20 ).pack(pady= 4 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"height=3\" , height= 3 ).pack(pady= 4 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"padding\" , padx= 30 , pady= 12 ).pack(pady= 4 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"fills the width\" ).pack(fill= \"x\" , padx= 10 , pady= 4 ) root.mainloop() If a button shows an image, width / height are in pixels."},{"id":"tk.button-color","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I change a button's colour? (And why doesn't it work on Mac?)","qh":"How do I change a button's colour? (And why doesn't it work on Mac?)","a":"<code>tk.Button(root, bg=&quot;green&quot;, fg=&quot;white&quot;, activebackground=&quot;darkgreen&quot;)</code>. On macOS the native button ignores <code>bg</code>; use a <code>Label</code> that acts like a button, or ttk with the <code>&quot;clam&quot;</code> theme.","aka":"button background color, button bg, button color mac, button fg, activebackground, colored button macos","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Green&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#2e7d32&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, activebackground=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#1b5e20&quot;</i>, activeforeground=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"c\"># a label that behaves like a button: works everywhere, including macOS</i>\nfake = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Flat button&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">16</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">8</i>, cursor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;hand2&quot;</i>)\nfake.pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nfake.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;clicked&quot;</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.hover","tk.ttk-style"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Button(root, text= \"Green\" , bg= \"#2e7d32\" , fg= \"white\" , activebackground= \"#1b5e20\" , activeforeground= \"white\" ).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 10 ) # a label that behaves like a button: works everywhere, including macOS fake = tk.Label(root, text= \"Flat button\" , bg= \"#c23b22\" , fg= \"white\" , padx= 16 , pady= 8 , cursor= \"hand2\" ) fake.pack(pady= 10 ) fake.bind( \"<Button-1>\" , lambda e: print ( \"clicked\" )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.hover","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I change a widget's colour when the mouse hovers over it?","qh":"How do I change a widget's colour when the mouse hovers over it?","a":"Bind <code>&lt;Enter&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;Leave&gt;</code> and change the colour in each.","aka":"hover effect, mouse over, mouseover, on hover, Enter Leave events, highlight on hover","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nb = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hover me&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ddd&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nb.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nb.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Enter&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: b.config(bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ffcc66&quot;</i>))\nb.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Leave&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: b.config(bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ddd&quot;</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Plain <code>tk.Button</code> on Windows/Linux also has <code>activebackground</code>, the colour while pressed or hovered.</p>","see":["tk.bind","tk.tooltip"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() b = tk.Label(root, text= \"Hover me\" , bg= \"#ddd\" , padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) b.pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) b.bind( \"<Enter>\" , lambda e: b.config(bg= \"#ffcc66\" )) b.bind( \"<Leave>\" , lambda e: b.config(bg= \"#ddd\" )) root.mainloop() Plain tk.Button on Windows/Linux also has activebackground , the colour while pressed or hovered."},{"id":"tk.tooltip","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I show a tooltip when hovering over a widget?","qh":"How do I show a tooltip when hovering over a widget?","a":"tkinter has no built-in tooltip. Make a small borderless <code>Toplevel</code> on <code>&lt;Enter&gt;</code> and destroy it on <code>&lt;Leave&gt;</code>.","aka":"tooltip, hint text on hover, hover text, balloon help, popup text mouse over","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Tooltip</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, widget, text):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.widget, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.text, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.tip = widget, text, <i class=\"k\">None</i>\n        widget.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Enter&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.show)\n        widget.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Leave&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.hide)\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">show</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n        x = <i class=\"k\">self</i>.widget.winfo_rootx() + <i class=\"n\">10</i>\n        y = <i class=\"k\">self</i>.widget.winfo_rooty() + <i class=\"k\">self</i>.widget.winfo_height() + <i class=\"n\">4</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.tip = tk.Toplevel(<i class=\"k\">self</i>.widget)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.tip.overrideredirect(<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.tip.geometry(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">+</i>{x}<i class=\"s\">+</i>{y}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n        tk.Label(<i class=\"k\">self</i>.tip, text=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.text, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ffffe0&quot;</i>, relief=<i class=\"s\">&quot;solid&quot;</i>, borderwidth=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">2</i>).pack()\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">hide</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>.tip:\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.tip.destroy()\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.tip = <i class=\"k\">None</i>\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nb = tk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Save&quot;</i>)\nb.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">60</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nTooltip(b, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Save the file (Ctrl+S)&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.hover","tk.no-titlebar"],"t":"import tkinter as tk class Tooltip : def __init__ ( self , widget, text): self .widget, self .text, self .tip = widget, text, None widget.bind( \"<Enter>\" , self .show) widget.bind( \"<Leave>\" , self .hide) def show ( self , event= None ): x = self .widget.winfo_rootx() + 10 y = self .widget.winfo_rooty() + self .widget.winfo_height() + 4 self .tip = tk.Toplevel( self .widget) self .tip.overrideredirect( True ) self .tip.geometry( f\" + {x} + {y} \" ) tk.Label( self .tip, text= self .text, bg= \"#ffffe0\" , relief= \"solid\" , borderwidth= 1 , padx= 6 , pady= 2 ).pack() def hide ( self , event= None ): if self .tip: self .tip.destroy() self .tip = None root = tk.Tk() b = tk.Button(root, text= \"Save\" ) b.pack(padx= 60 , pady= 40 ) Tooltip(b, \"Save the file (Ctrl+S)\" ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.button-image","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I put an image on a button?","qh":"How do I put an image on a button?","a":"Load a <code>PhotoImage</code> and pass <code>image=</code>. Add <code>compound=&quot;left&quot;</code> to show text beside it. Keep a reference to the image.","aka":"image button, icon button, button with picture, button image and text, compound","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nimg = tk.PhotoImage(file=<i class=\"s\">&quot;icon.png&quot;</i>)\ntk.Button(root, image=img).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot; Open&quot;</i>, image=img, compound=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.img = img      <i class=\"c\"># keep the image alive</i>\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.photoimage","tk.err.image-gone"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() img = tk.PhotoImage(file= \"icon.png\" ) tk.Button(root, image=img).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) tk.Button(root, text= \" Open\" , image=img, compound= \"left\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) root.img = img # keep the image alive root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.button-enter","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I make the Enter key press a button?","qh":"How do I make the Enter key press a button?","a":"Bind <code>&lt;Return&gt;</code> on the window (or the Entry) to the same function. The function receives an event, so give it <code>event=None</code>.","aka":"enter key button, return key submit, default button, press enter to submit","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ne = tk.Entry(root)\ne.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ne.focus()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">submit</i>(event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Submitted:&quot;</i>, e.get())\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Submit&quot;</i>, command=submit).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Return&gt;&quot;</i>, submit)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.bind-keys","tk.entry-get"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() e = tk.Entry(root) e.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) e.focus() def submit (event= None ): print ( \"Submitted:\" , e.get()) tk.Button(root, text= \"Submit\" , command=submit).pack(pady= 10 ) root.bind( \"<Return>\" , submit) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.relief","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I change the border style of a widget?","qh":"How do I change the border style of a widget?","a":"Use <code>relief=</code> (<code>&quot;flat&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;raised&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;sunken&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;groove&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;ridge&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;solid&quot;</code>) and <code>borderwidth=</code> (<code>bd=</code>). The focus ring is <code>highlightthickness=</code>.","aka":"relief, border, borderwidth, flat button, sunken, raised, groove, ridge, remove border, highlightthickness","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> r <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;flat&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;raised&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;sunken&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;groove&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;ridge&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;solid&quot;</i>):\n    tk.Label(root, text=r, relief=r, borderwidth=<i class=\"n\">3</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">12</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">3</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;no border&quot;</i>, relief=<i class=\"s\">&quot;flat&quot;</i>, borderwidth=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, highlightthickness=<i class=\"n\">0</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">8</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() for r in ( \"flat\" , \"raised\" , \"sunken\" , \"groove\" , \"ridge\" , \"solid\" ): tk.Label(root, text=r, relief=r, borderwidth= 3 , width= 12 , pady= 4 ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 3 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"no border\" , relief= \"flat\" , borderwidth= 0 , highlightthickness= 0 ).pack(pady= 8 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.cursor","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I change the mouse cursor over a widget?","qh":"How do I change the mouse cursor over a widget?","a":"<code>cursor=&quot;hand2&quot;</code> on the widget. Others: <code>&quot;arrow&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;watch&quot;</code> (busy), <code>&quot;cross&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;xterm&quot;</code> (text), <code>&quot;fleur&quot;</code> (move), <code>&quot;sb_h_double_arrow&quot;</code>.","aka":"cursor hand, pointer cursor, hand2, busy cursor, watch cursor, change mouse pointer","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;hand&quot;</i>, cursor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;hand2&quot;</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;cross&quot;</i>, cursor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;cross&quot;</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;watch (busy)&quot;</i>, cursor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;watch&quot;</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>To show \"busy\" for the whole window: <code>root.config(cursor=&quot;watch&quot;)</code>, then <code>root.update()</code>, and set it back to <code>&quot;&quot;</code> afterwards.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"hand\" , cursor= \"hand2\" , pady= 10 ).pack(fill= \"x\" ) tk.Label(root, text= \"cross\" , cursor= \"cross\" , pady= 10 ).pack(fill= \"x\" ) tk.Label(root, text= \"watch (busy)\" , cursor= \"watch\" , pady= 10 ).pack(fill= \"x\" ) root.mainloop() To show \"busy\" for the whole window: root.config(cursor=\"watch\") , then root.update() , and set it back to \"\" afterwards."},{"id":"tk.widget-destroy","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I delete or remove a widget?","qh":"How do I delete or remove a widget?","a":"<code>widget.destroy()</code> removes it for good. To clear a frame: <code>for w in frame.winfo_children(): w.destroy()</code>.","aka":"remove widget, delete label, destroy widget, remove button, clear widget, delete all widgets in frame, clear frame","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nbox = tk.Frame(root)\nbox.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">4</i>):\n    tk.Label(box, text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">item </i>{i}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>).pack()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">clear</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> w <i class=\"k\">in</i> box.winfo_children():\n        w.destroy()\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Clear&quot;</i>, command=clear).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.forget"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() box = tk.Frame(root) box.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) for i in range ( 4 ): tk.Label(box, text= f\" item {i} \" ).pack() def clear (): for w in box.winfo_children(): w.destroy() tk.Button(root, text= \"Clear\" , command=clear).pack(pady= 10 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.forget","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I hide a widget and show it again later?","qh":"How do I hide a widget and show it again later?","a":"<code>pack_forget()</code> / <code>grid_forget()</code> hide it (it still exists). Show it again with <code>pack()</code> / <code>grid()</code>. <code>grid_remove()</code> remembers its grid options so a plain <code>grid()</code> puts it back.","aka":"hide widget, show widget, pack_forget, grid_forget, grid_remove, toggle visibility, make invisible","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nsecret = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Now you see me&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ffcc66&quot;</i>)\nsecret.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">toggle</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> secret.winfo_ismapped():\n        secret.grid_remove()\n    <i class=\"k\">else</i>:\n        secret.grid()\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Toggle&quot;</i>, command=toggle).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>With <code>pack</code>, the widget may come back in a different place. Pack it again with the same options, or use <code>before=</code>/<code>after=</code>.</p>","see":["tk.widget-destroy"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() secret = tk.Label(root, text= \"Now you see me\" , bg= \"#ffcc66\" ) secret.grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) def toggle (): if secret.winfo_ismapped(): secret.grid_remove() else : secret.grid() tk.Button(root, text= \"Toggle\" , command=toggle).grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() With pack , the widget may come back in a different place. Pack it again with the same options, or use before= / after= ."},{"id":"tk.widget-config-get","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I read a widget option, like its current text or colour?","qh":"How do I read a widget option, like its current text or colour?","a":"<code>widget.cget(&quot;text&quot;)</code> or <code>widget[&quot;text&quot;]</code>. Set with <code>widget.config(text=...)</code> or <code>widget[&quot;text&quot;] = ...</code>.","aka":"cget, get label text, widget option value, read button text, label text get","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hello&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;blue&quot;</i>)\nlab.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(lab.cget(<i class=\"s\">&quot;text&quot;</i>), lab[<i class=\"s\">&quot;fg&quot;</i>])\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(lab.keys()[:<i class=\"n\">6</i>], <i class=\"s\">&quot;...&quot;</i>)   <i class=\"c\"># every option this widget has</i>\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"Hello\" , fg= \"blue\" ) lab.pack(padx= 30 , pady= 30 ) print (lab.cget( \"text\" ), lab[ \"fg\" ]) print (lab.keys()[: 6 ], \"...\" ) # every option this widget has root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.message-widget","s":"tk-label-button","q":"What's the Message widget?","qh":"What's the Message widget?","a":"<code>tk.Message</code> is like a Label that wraps text automatically, keeping a width/height ratio (<code>aspect</code>). Most people just use a Label with <code>wraplength</code>.","aka":"tk.Message, message widget, multiline text display","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Message(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;A Message widget wraps long text on its own, aiming for a shape set by aspect.&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">200</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.label-wrap"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Message(root, text= \"A Message widget wraps long text on its own, aiming for a shape set by aspect.\" , width= 200 ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 20 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.entry-get","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I get what the user typed in an Entry box?","qh":"How do I get what the user typed in an Entry box?","a":"Call <code>entry.get()</code> <b>when it's needed</b> (e.g. in a button's function), not right after creating the Entry, when it's still empty.","aka":"entry get, read input, get text from entry, textbox value, input box, user input gui, entry value","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nname = tk.Entry(root, width=<i class=\"n\">25</i>)\nname.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nout = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\nout.pack()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">greet</i>():\n    out.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Hello, </i>{name.get()}<i class=\"s\">!</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Greet&quot;</i>, command=greet).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>get()</code> always returns a <b>string</b>. See <a href=\"#/a/tk.entry-number\">How do I get a number from an Entry (and handle bad input)?</a> for numbers.</p>","see":["tk.entry-number","tk.err.none-grid","tk.button-enter"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() name = tk.Entry(root, width= 25 ) name.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) out = tk.Label(root, text= \"\" ) out.pack() def greet (): out.config(text= f\" Hello, {name.get()} ! \" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Greet\" , command=greet).pack(pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() get() always returns a string . See How do I get a number from an Entry (and handle bad input)? for numbers."},{"id":"tk.entry-number","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I get a number from an Entry (and handle bad input)?","qh":"How do I get a number from an Entry (and handle bad input)?","a":"Convert with <code>int()</code> or <code>float()</code> inside <code>try</code>/<code>except ValueError</code> so letters or an empty box don't crash the program.","aka":"entry int, convert entry to integer, entry float, ValueError entry, invalid literal for int entry, number input tkinter, calculator input","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\na = tk.Entry(root, width=<i class=\"n\">8</i>); a.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;+&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\nb = tk.Entry(root, width=<i class=\"n\">8</i>); b.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nresult = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\nresult.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, columnspan=<i class=\"n\">3</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">add</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        total = <i class=\"b\">float</i>(a.get()) + <i class=\"b\">float</i>(b.get())\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> ValueError:\n        result.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Please type two numbers&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n    result.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">= </i>{total:<i class=\"s\">g</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;black&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Add&quot;</i>, command=add).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, columnspan=<i class=\"n\">3</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.entry-validate","py.err.valueerror-int"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() a = tk.Entry(root, width= 8 ); a.grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , padx= 5 , pady= 10 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"+\" ).grid(row= 0 , column= 1 ) b = tk.Entry(root, width= 8 ); b.grid(row= 0 , column= 2 , padx= 5 ) result = tk.Label(root, text= \"\" ) result.grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , columnspan= 3 ) def add (): try : total = float (a.get()) + float (b.get()) except ValueError: result.config(text= \"Please type two numbers\" , fg= \"red\" ) return result.config(text= f\" = {total: g } \" , fg= \"black\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Add\" , command=add).grid(row= 2 , column= 0 , columnspan= 3 , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.entry-set","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I put text into an Entry, or clear it?","qh":"How do I put text into an Entry, or clear it?","a":"Clear with <code>entry.delete(0, &quot;end&quot;)</code>, insert with <code>entry.insert(0, &quot;text&quot;)</code>. Or use a <code>StringVar</code>: <code>var.set(&quot;text&quot;)</code>.","aka":"entry insert, set entry text, clear entry, delete entry text, reset input box, empty textbox, entry default value","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ne = tk.Entry(root)\ne.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ne.insert(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;default value&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Clear&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: e.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Fill&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: (e.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>), e.insert(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;filled&quot;</i>))).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>&quot;end&quot;</code> (or <code>tk.END</code>) means \"after the last character\".</p>","see":["tk.stringvar"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() e = tk.Entry(root) e.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) e.insert( 0 , \"default value\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Clear\" , command= lambda : e.delete( 0 , \"end\" )).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Fill\" , command= lambda : (e.delete( 0 , \"end\" ), e.insert( 0 , \"filled\" ))).pack(side= \"left\" , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() \"end\" (or tk.END ) means \"after the last character\"."},{"id":"tk.entry-password","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I make a password box that shows stars?","qh":"How do I make a password box that shows stars?","a":"<code>tk.Entry(root, show=&quot;*&quot;)</code>. Set <code>show=&quot;&quot;</code> to reveal it again.","aka":"password entry, hide input, show asterisks, mask text, show password checkbox","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\npw = tk.Entry(root, show=<i class=\"s\">&quot;*&quot;</i>)\npw.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nvisible = tk.BooleanVar()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">toggle</i>():\n    pw.config(show=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> visible.get() <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;*&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Checkbutton(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Show password&quot;</i>, variable=visible, command=toggle).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.recipe-login"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() pw = tk.Entry(root, show= \"*\" ) pw.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) visible = tk.BooleanVar() def toggle (): pw.config(show= \"\" if visible.get() else \"*\" ) tk.Checkbutton(root, text= \"Show password\" , variable=visible, command=toggle).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.entry-placeholder","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I add placeholder (hint) text to an Entry?","qh":"How do I add placeholder (hint) text to an Entry?","a":"tkinter has no placeholder option. Insert grey text, and remove it when the box gets focus (<code>&lt;FocusIn&gt;</code>); put it back on <code>&lt;FocusOut&gt;</code> if empty.","aka":"placeholder, hint text, greyed text in entry, watermark text, default text that disappears, ghost text","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">HintEntry</i>(tk.Entry):\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, master, hint, **kw):\n        <i class=\"b\">super</i>().__init__(master, **kw)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.hint = hint\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.normal = <i class=\"k\">self</i>[<i class=\"s\">&quot;fg&quot;</i>]\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;FocusIn&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">self</i>._in)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;FocusOut&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">self</i>._out)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>._out()\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">_in</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, e=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>[<i class=\"s\">&quot;fg&quot;</i>] == <i class=\"s\">&quot;grey&quot;</i>:\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.config(fg=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.normal)\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">_out</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, e=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> <i class=\"b\">super</i>().get():\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.insert(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.hint)\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.config(fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;grey&quot;</i>)\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">get</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>[<i class=\"s\">&quot;fg&quot;</i>] == <i class=\"s\">&quot;grey&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"b\">super</i>().get()\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nHintEntry(root, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Your name&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nHintEntry(root, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Your email&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk class HintEntry (tk.Entry): def __init__ ( self , master, hint, **kw): super ().__init__(master, **kw) self .hint = hint self .normal = self [ \"fg\" ] self .bind( \"<FocusIn>\" , self ._in) self .bind( \"<FocusOut>\" , self ._out) self ._out() def _in ( self , e= None ): if self [ \"fg\" ] == \"grey\" : self .delete( 0 , \"end\" ) self .config(fg= self .normal) def _out ( self , e= None ): if not super ().get(): self .insert( 0 , self .hint) self .config(fg= \"grey\" ) def get ( self ): return \"\" if self [ \"fg\" ] == \"grey\" else super ().get() root = tk.Tk() HintEntry(root, \"Your name\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) HintEntry(root, \"Your email\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.entry-validate","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I only allow numbers (or limit length) in an Entry?","qh":"How do I only allow numbers (or limit length) in an Entry?","a":"Use <code>validate=&quot;key&quot;</code> with a <code>validatecommand</code> that returns <code>True</code> to allow the change. <code>%P</code> passes what the text <b>would</b> become.","aka":"validate entry, numbers only, digits only, restrict input, max length entry, validatecommand, vcmd, character limit","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">only_digits</i>(new_text):\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> new_text == <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">or</i> new_text.isdigit()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">max_five</i>(new_text):\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"b\">len</i>(new_text) &lt;= <i class=\"n\">5</i>\n\nvd = root.register(only_digits)\nvl = root.register(max_five)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Digits only&quot;</i>).pack()\ntk.Entry(root, validate=<i class=\"s\">&quot;key&quot;</i>, validatecommand=(vd, <i class=\"s\">&quot;%P&quot;</i>)).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Max 5 characters&quot;</i>).pack()\ntk.Entry(root, validate=<i class=\"s\">&quot;key&quot;</i>, validatecommand=(vl, <i class=\"s\">&quot;%P&quot;</i>)).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>For decimals use a test like: try <code>float(new_text)</code> and return <code>False</code> on <code>ValueError</code> (allow <code>&quot;&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;-&quot;</code> and <code>&quot;.&quot;</code> too, so the user can start typing).</p>","see":["tk.entry-number"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() def only_digits (new_text): return new_text == \"\" or new_text.isdigit() def max_five (new_text): return len (new_text) <= 5 vd = root.register(only_digits) vl = root.register(max_five) tk.Label(root, text= \"Digits only\" ).pack() tk.Entry(root, validate= \"key\" , validatecommand=(vd, \"%P\" )).pack(padx= 20 , pady=( 0 , 10 )) tk.Label(root, text= \"Max 5 characters\" ).pack() tk.Entry(root, validate= \"key\" , validatecommand=(vl, \"%P\" )).pack(padx= 20 , pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop() For decimals use a test like: try float(new_text) and return False on ValueError (allow \"\" , \"-\" and \".\" too, so the user can start typing)."},{"id":"tk.entry-readonly","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I make an Entry read-only (can select and copy but not type)?","qh":"How do I make an Entry read-only (can select and copy but not type)?","a":"<code>entry.config(state=&quot;readonly&quot;)</code>. <code>&quot;disabled&quot;</code> also blocks selecting. Switch to <code>&quot;normal&quot;</code> before changing the text from code.","aka":"readonly entry, non editable entry, disable typing, entry state readonly, lock entry","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ne = tk.Entry(root)\ne.insert(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;You can copy me&quot;</i>)\ne.config(state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;readonly&quot;</i>)\ne.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() e = tk.Entry(root) e.insert( 0 , \"You can copy me\" ) e.config(state= \"readonly\" ) e.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 20 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.entry-focus","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I put the cursor in an Entry automatically (focus)?","qh":"How do I put the cursor in an Entry automatically (focus)?","a":"Call <code>entry.focus()</code> (same as <code>focus_set()</code>). Tab moves to the next widget in the order they were created.","aka":"focus entry, autofocus, focus_set, cursor in textbox at start, move to next entry, tab order","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nfirst = tk.Entry(root); first.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nsecond = tk.Entry(root); second.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nfirst.focus()\nfirst.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Return&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: second.focus())   <i class=\"c\"># Enter jumps to the next box</i>\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Change the Tab order with <code>widget.lift()</code> or by creating widgets in the order you want.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() first = tk.Entry(root); first.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) second = tk.Entry(root); second.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) first.focus() first.bind( \"<Return>\" , lambda e: second.focus()) # Enter jumps to the next box root.mainloop() Change the Tab order with widget.lift() or by creating widgets in the order you want."},{"id":"tk.entry-width","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I make an Entry wider or taller?","qh":"How do I make an Entry wider or taller?","a":"<code>width=</code> is in characters. Entry is always one line; it gets taller with a bigger <code>font</code>. For several lines, use a <a href=\"#/a/tk.text\">Text</a> widget.","aka":"entry width, entry height, bigger textbox, large input, entry font size","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Entry(root, width=<i class=\"n\">40</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Entry(root, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">18</i>)).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Entry(root).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Entry(root, width= 40 ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) tk.Entry(root, font=( \"Arial\" , 18 )).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) tk.Entry(root).pack(fill= \"x\" , padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.entry-changes","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I run code every time the Entry text changes?","qh":"How do I run code every time the Entry text changes?","a":"Give the Entry a <code>StringVar</code> and use <code>var.trace_add(&quot;write&quot;, callback)</code>.","aka":"entry on change, text changed event, live search, trace entry, detect typing, onchange","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntext = tk.StringVar()\ntk.Entry(root, textvariable=text).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ncount = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;0 characters&quot;</i>)\ncount.pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">changed</i>(*args):\n    count.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{<i class=\"b\">len</i>(text.get())}<i class=\"s\"> characters</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\ntext.trace_add(<i class=\"s\">&quot;write&quot;</i>, changed)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.trace"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() text = tk.StringVar() tk.Entry(root, textvariable=text).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) count = tk.Label(root, text= \"0 characters\" ) count.pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) def changed (*args): count.config(text= f\" { len (text.get())} characters \" ) text.trace_add( \"write\" , changed) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.text","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I make a multi-line text box?","qh":"How do I make a multi-line text box?","a":"Use <code>tk.Text(root, width=40, height=10)</code>. Unlike Entry, its positions are <code>&quot;line.column&quot;</code> strings: start is <code>&quot;1.0&quot;</code>, end is <code>&quot;end&quot;</code>.","aka":"text widget, textarea, multiline input, big text box, notes box, text editor box, tk.Text","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nbox = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">8</i>, wrap=<i class=\"s\">&quot;word&quot;</i>)\nbox.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nbox.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Type anything here.\\nIt can have many lines.&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>wrap=&quot;word&quot;</code> wraps at spaces; <code>&quot;char&quot;</code> (default) can split words; <code>&quot;none&quot;</code> doesn't wrap.</p>","see":["tk.text-get","tk.text-insert","tk.text-scroll"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() box = tk.Text(root, width= 40 , height= 8 , wrap= \"word\" ) box.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) box.insert( \"1.0\" , \"Type anything here.\\nIt can have many lines.\" ) root.mainloop() wrap=\"word\" wraps at spaces; \"char\" (default) can split words; \"none\" doesn't wrap."},{"id":"tk.text-get","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I get all the text from a Text widget?","qh":"How do I get all the text from a Text widget?","a":"<code>text.get(&quot;1.0&quot;, &quot;end-1c&quot;)</code>. <code>&quot;1.0&quot;</code> = line 1, character 0. <code>&quot;end-1c&quot;</code> drops the newline that Tk always adds at the end.","aka":"text get, read text widget, get contents of textarea, text get 1.0 end, get text box value","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nbox = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nbox.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nbox.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;line one\\nline two&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">show</i>():\n    content = box.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end-1c&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">repr</i>(content))\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;lines:&quot;</i>, content.count(<i class=\"s\">&quot;\\n&quot;</i>) + <i class=\"n\">1</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Get&quot;</i>, command=show).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() box = tk.Text(root, width= 30 , height= 5 ) box.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) box.insert( \"1.0\" , \"line one\\nline two\" ) def show (): content = box.get( \"1.0\" , \"end-1c\" ) print ( repr (content)) print ( \"lines:\" , content.count( \"\\n\" ) + 1 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Get\" , command=show).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.text-insert","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I add, replace or clear text in a Text widget?","qh":"How do I add, replace or clear text in a Text widget?","a":"<code>insert(&quot;end&quot;, &quot;more\\n&quot;)</code> appends, <code>delete(&quot;1.0&quot;, &quot;end&quot;)</code> clears everything. Call <code>see(&quot;end&quot;)</code> to scroll to the newest line.","aka":"text insert, text delete, clear text widget, append text, replace text, write to text box, log window","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlog = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nlog.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nn = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">add_line</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> n\n    n += <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n    log.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Message </i>{n}<i class=\"s\">\\n</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n    log.see(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Add&quot;</i>, command=add_line).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Clear&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: log.delete(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.text-readonly"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() log = tk.Text(root, width= 40 , height= 6 ) log.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) n = 0 def add_line (): global n n += 1 log.insert( \"end\" , f\" Message {n} \\n \" ) log.see( \"end\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Add\" , command=add_line).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 10 , pady=( 0 , 10 )) tk.Button(root, text= \"Clear\" , command= lambda : log.delete( \"1.0\" , \"end\" )).pack(side= \"left\" , pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.text-readonly","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I make a Text widget read-only?","qh":"How do I make a Text widget read-only?","a":"<code>text.config(state=&quot;disabled&quot;)</code>. To change it from code, switch to <code>&quot;normal&quot;</code>, edit, then back to <code>&quot;disabled&quot;</code>.","aka":"read only text, non editable text box, disable text widget, output box, stop typing in text","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nout = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">36</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;disabled&quot;</i>)\nout.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">write</i>(msg):\n    out.config(state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;normal&quot;</i>)\n    out.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, msg + <i class=\"s\">&quot;\\n&quot;</i>)\n    out.config(state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;disabled&quot;</i>)\n    out.see(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Write&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: write(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Read-only output&quot;</i>)).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() out = tk.Text(root, width= 36 , height= 5 , state= \"disabled\" ) out.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) def write (msg): out.config(state= \"normal\" ) out.insert( \"end\" , msg + \"\\n\" ) out.config(state= \"disabled\" ) out.see( \"end\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Write\" , command= lambda : write( \"Read-only output\" )).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.text-scroll","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I add a scrollbar to a Text widget?","qh":"How do I add a scrollbar to a Text widget?","a":"Easiest: <code>from tkinter import scrolledtext</code> and use <code>scrolledtext.ScrolledText</code>. Or connect a <code>Scrollbar</code> by hand.","aka":"scroll text, scrollbar text box, scrolledtext, scrolling textarea","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> scrolledtext\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nbox = scrolledtext.ScrolledText(root, width=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">8</i>, wrap=<i class=\"s\">&quot;word&quot;</i>)\nbox.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nbox.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;\\n&quot;</i>.join(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Line </i>{i}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">51</i>)))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.scrollbar"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import scrolledtext root = tk.Tk() box = scrolledtext.ScrolledText(root, width= 40 , height= 8 , wrap= \"word\" ) box.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 , fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) box.insert( \"1.0\" , \"\\n\" .join( f\" Line {i} \" for i in range ( 1 , 51 ))) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.text-tags","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I colour or style part of the text in a Text widget?","qh":"How do I colour or style part of the text in a Text widget?","a":"Create a tag with <code>tag_configure(&quot;name&quot;, foreground=..., font=...)</code>, then use it when inserting or with <code>tag_add(name, start, end)</code>.","aka":"text tag, highlight text, colored text, bold part of text, tag_configure, syntax highlight, change color of word","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nt = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Courier&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">12</i>))\nt.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nt.tag_configure(<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>, foreground=<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>)\nt.tag_configure(<i class=\"s\">&quot;big&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Courier&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">16</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;bold&quot;</i>))\nt.tag_configure(<i class=\"s\">&quot;hl&quot;</i>, background=<i class=\"s\">&quot;yellow&quot;</i>)\n\nt.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Normal &quot;</i>)\nt.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;red &quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>)\nt.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;big\\n&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;big&quot;</i>)\nt.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;highlight this word&quot;</i>)\nt.tag_add(<i class=\"s\">&quot;hl&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;2.10&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;2.14&quot;</i>)    <i class=\"c\"># line 2, characters 10 to 14</i>\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.text-search"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() t = tk.Text(root, width= 40 , height= 6 , font=( \"Courier\" , 12 )) t.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) t.tag_configure( \"red\" , foreground= \"red\" ) t.tag_configure( \"big\" , font=( \"Courier\" , 16 , \"bold\" )) t.tag_configure( \"hl\" , background= \"yellow\" ) t.insert( \"end\" , \"Normal \" ) t.insert( \"end\" , \"red \" , \"red\" ) t.insert( \"end\" , \"big\\n\" , \"big\" ) t.insert( \"end\" , \"highlight this word\" ) t.tag_add( \"hl\" , \"2.10\" , \"2.14\" ) # line 2, characters 10 to 14 root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.text-search","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I find and highlight a word in a Text widget?","qh":"How do I find and highlight a word in a Text widget?","a":"Loop with <code>text.search(word, start, stopindex=&quot;end&quot;)</code>; it returns the index of the next match or <code>&quot;&quot;</code>.","aka":"search text, find in text, find all occurrences, highlight search results, text search","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nt = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nt.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nt.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;the cat sat on the mat with the hat&quot;</i>)\nt.tag_configure(<i class=\"s\">&quot;found&quot;</i>, background=<i class=\"s\">&quot;yellow&quot;</i>)\nq = tk.Entry(root); q.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>)); q.insert(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;the&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">find</i>():\n    t.tag_remove(<i class=\"s\">&quot;found&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n    word = q.get()\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> word:\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n    pos = <i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">while</i> <i class=\"k\">True</i>:\n        pos = t.search(word, pos, stopindex=<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, nocase=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> pos:\n            <i class=\"k\">break</i>\n        end = <i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{pos}<i class=\"s\">+</i>{<i class=\"b\">len</i>(word)}<i class=\"s\">c</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>\n        t.tag_add(<i class=\"s\">&quot;found&quot;</i>, pos, end)\n        pos = end\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Find&quot;</i>, command=find).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.text-tags"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() t = tk.Text(root, width= 40 , height= 5 ) t.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) t.insert( \"1.0\" , \"the cat sat on the mat with the hat\" ) t.tag_configure( \"found\" , background= \"yellow\" ) q = tk.Entry(root); q.pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 10 , pady=( 0 , 10 )); q.insert( 0 , \"the\" ) def find (): t.tag_remove( \"found\" , \"1.0\" , \"end\" ) word = q.get() if not word: return pos = \"1.0\" while True : pos = t.search(word, pos, stopindex= \"end\" , nocase= True ) if not pos: break end = f\" {pos} + { len (word)} c \" t.tag_add( \"found\" , pos, end) pos = end tk.Button(root, text= \"Find\" , command=find).pack(side= \"left\" , pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.text-undo","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I add undo/redo to a Text widget?","qh":"How do I add undo/redo to a Text widget?","a":"Create it with <code>undo=True</code>. Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y (Cmd+Z / Cmd+Shift+Z on Mac) then work, and you can call <code>edit_undo()</code> / <code>edit_redo()</code>.","aka":"undo text, ctrl z text widget, redo, edit_undo","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nt = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, undo=<i class=\"k\">True</i>, maxundo=-<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\nt.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">undo</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        t.edit_undo()\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> tk.TclError:\n        <i class=\"k\">pass</i>      <i class=\"c\"># nothing to undo</i>\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Undo&quot;</i>, command=undo).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() t = tk.Text(root, width= 40 , height= 6 , undo= True , maxundo=- 1 ) t.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) def undo (): try : t.edit_undo() except tk.TclError: pass # nothing to undo tk.Button(root, text= \"Undo\" , command=undo).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.text-cursor","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I get or move the cursor position in a Text or Entry?","qh":"How do I get or move the cursor position in a Text or Entry?","a":"The cursor is the <code>&quot;insert&quot;</code> index. Read it with <code>text.index(&quot;insert&quot;)</code>; move it with <code>text.mark_set(&quot;insert&quot;, &quot;1.0&quot;)</code>. For an Entry use <code>entry.index(&quot;insert&quot;)</code> and <code>entry.icursor(n)</code>.","aka":"insert cursor, cursor position, caret, current line number, move cursor end, index insert","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nt = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nt.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nt.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;hello\\nworld&quot;</i>)\npos = tk.Label(root)\npos.pack()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">update</i>(e=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    line, col = t.index(<i class=\"s\">&quot;insert&quot;</i>).split(<i class=\"s\">&quot;.&quot;</i>)\n    pos.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Line </i>{line}<i class=\"s\">, column </i>{<i class=\"b\">int</i>(col) + <i class=\"n\">1</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\nt.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;KeyRelease&gt;&quot;</i>, update)\nt.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;ButtonRelease-1&gt;&quot;</i>, update)\nt.mark_set(<i class=\"s\">&quot;insert&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\nupdate()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() t = tk.Text(root, width= 30 , height= 4 ) t.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) t.insert( \"1.0\" , \"hello\\nworld\" ) pos = tk.Label(root) pos.pack() def update (e= None ): line, col = t.index( \"insert\" ).split( \".\" ) pos.config(text= f\" Line {line} , column { int (col) + 1 } \" ) t.bind( \"<KeyRelease>\" , update) t.bind( \"<ButtonRelease-1>\" , update) t.mark_set( \"insert\" , \"end\" ) update() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.text-indexes","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"What do Text indexes like \"1.0\", \"end-1c\", \"insert lineend\" mean?","qh":"What do Text indexes like \"1.0\", \"end-1c\", \"insert lineend\" mean?","a":"<code>&quot;line.column&quot;</code>: lines start at 1, columns at 0. You can add modifiers: <code>+5c</code> (characters), <code>-1l</code> (lines), <code>linestart</code>, <code>lineend</code>, <code>wordstart</code>, <code>wordend</code>.","aka":"text index, 1.0, end-1c, linestart, lineend, wordstart, text positions","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nt = tk.Text(root)\nt.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;first line\\nsecond line\\n&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(t.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;1.end&quot;</i>))              <i class=\"c\"># first line</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(t.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;2.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;2.6&quot;</i>))                <i class=\"c\"># second</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(t.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0 + 5c&quot;</i>))           <i class=\"c\"># first</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(t.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;2.3 wordstart&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;2.3 wordend&quot;</i>))   <i class=\"c\"># second</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(t.index(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end-1c&quot;</i>))                  <i class=\"c\"># 3.0 (very last position)</i>\nroot.destroy()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() t = tk.Text(root) t.insert( \"1.0\" , \"first line\\nsecond line\\n\" ) print (t.get( \"1.0\" , \"1.end\" )) # first line print (t.get( \"2.0\" , \"2.6\" )) # second print (t.get( \"1.0\" , \"1.0 + 5c\" )) # first print (t.get( \"2.3 wordstart\" , \"2.3 wordend\" )) # second print (t.index( \"end-1c\" )) # 3.0 (very last position) root.destroy()"},{"id":"tk.checkbutton","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I make a checkbox and read whether it's ticked?","qh":"How do I make a checkbox and read whether it's ticked?","a":"Link it to a <code>BooleanVar</code> (or <code>IntVar</code>) with <code>variable=</code>, then read <code>var.get()</code>.","aka":"checkbox, checkbutton, tick box, check box value, is checked, BooleanVar checkbox","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nsugar = tk.BooleanVar(value=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\ntk.Checkbutton(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Sugar&quot;</i>, variable=sugar).pack(anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">order</i>():\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Sugar:&quot;</i>, sugar.get())     <i class=\"c\"># True or False</i>\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Order&quot;</i>, command=order).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>command=</code> runs a function every time it's clicked. With <code>IntVar</code>, it gives 1/0; set <code>onvalue</code>/<code>offvalue</code> for other values.</p>","see":["tk.checkbutton-many","tk.booleanvar"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() sugar = tk.BooleanVar(value= True ) tk.Checkbutton(root, text= \"Sugar\" , variable=sugar).pack(anchor= \"w\" , padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) def order (): print ( \"Sugar:\" , sugar.get()) # True or False tk.Button(root, text= \"Order\" , command=order).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop() command= runs a function every time it's clicked. With IntVar , it gives 1/0; set onvalue / offvalue for other values."},{"id":"tk.checkbutton-many","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I make many checkboxes from a list and see which are ticked?","qh":"How do I make many checkboxes from a list and see which are ticked?","a":"Keep a dict of <code>{name: BooleanVar}</code> and loop over it.","aka":"multiple checkboxes, list of checkbuttons, checkboxes in loop, get selected checkboxes","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntoppings = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;Cheese&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Ham&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Pineapple&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Olives&quot;</i>]\nchosen = {}\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> t <i class=\"k\">in</i> toppings:\n    chosen[t] = tk.BooleanVar()\n    tk.Checkbutton(root, text=t, variable=chosen[t]).pack(anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">show</i>():\n    picked = [t <i class=\"k\">for</i> t, v <i class=\"k\">in</i> chosen.items() <i class=\"k\">if</i> v.get()]\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Picked:&quot;</i>, picked)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Done&quot;</i>, command=show).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() toppings = [ \"Cheese\" , \"Ham\" , \"Pineapple\" , \"Olives\" ] chosen = {} for t in toppings: chosen[t] = tk.BooleanVar() tk.Checkbutton(root, text=t, variable=chosen[t]).pack(anchor= \"w\" , padx= 20 ) def show (): picked = [t for t, v in chosen.items() if v.get()] print ( \"Picked:\" , picked) tk.Button(root, text= \"Done\" , command=show).pack(pady= 10 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.radiobutton","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I make radio buttons (choose one option)?","qh":"How do I make radio buttons (choose one option)?","a":"Give every Radiobutton the <b>same</b> variable and a different <code>value=</code>. The variable holds the chosen value.","aka":"radio button, option buttons, choose one, multiple choice, radiobutton value, quiz options","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nsize = tk.StringVar(value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;M&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> text, val <i class=\"k\">in</i> [(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Small&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;S&quot;</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Medium&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;M&quot;</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Large&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;L&quot;</i>)]:\n    tk.Radiobutton(root, text=text, variable=size, value=val).pack(anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;OK&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Size:&quot;</i>, size.get())).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>If all of them start selected (or none can be picked), they don't share one variable, or the variable was garbage-collected (it was created inside a function and not stored).</p>","see":["tk.recipe-quiz"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() size = tk.StringVar(value= \"M\" ) for text, val in [( \"Small\" , \"S\" ), ( \"Medium\" , \"M\" ), ( \"Large\" , \"L\" )]: tk.Radiobutton(root, text=text, variable=size, value=val).pack(anchor= \"w\" , padx= 20 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"OK\" , command= lambda : print ( \"Size:\" , size.get())).pack(pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() If all of them start selected (or none can be picked), they don't share one variable, or the variable was garbage-collected (it was created inside a function and not stored)."},{"id":"tk.combobox","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I make a dropdown menu (combobox)?","qh":"How do I make a dropdown menu (combobox)?","a":"Use <code>ttk.Combobox(root, values=[...], state=&quot;readonly&quot;)</code> and read it with <code>.get()</code>.","aka":"dropdown, drop down list, combobox, select box, choose from list, ttk.Combobox, picker","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nfruit = ttk.Combobox(root, values=[<i class=\"s\">&quot;Apple&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Banana&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Cherry&quot;</i>], state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;readonly&quot;</i>)\nfruit.current(<i class=\"n\">0</i>)             <i class=\"c\"># select the first item</i>\nfruit.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Show&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(fruit.get())).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Without <code>state=&quot;readonly&quot;</code> the user can also type their own value.</p>","see":["tk.combobox-event","tk.optionmenu"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() fruit = ttk.Combobox(root, values=[ \"Apple\" , \"Banana\" , \"Cherry\" ], state= \"readonly\" ) fruit.current( 0 ) # select the first item fruit.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Show\" , command= lambda : print (fruit.get())).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop() Without state=\"readonly\" the user can also type their own value."},{"id":"tk.combobox-event","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I run code when the user picks something from a Combobox?","qh":"How do I run code when the user picks something from a Combobox?","a":"Bind the virtual event <code>&quot;&lt;&lt;ComboboxSelected&gt;&gt;&quot;</code>.","aka":"combobox selected event, ComboboxSelected, on select dropdown, combobox change, combobox callback","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nbox = ttk.Combobox(root, values=[<i class=\"s\">&quot;Red&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Green&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Blue&quot;</i>], state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;readonly&quot;</i>)\nbox.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nswatch = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;     &quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nswatch.pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">picked</i>(event):\n    swatch.config(bg=box.get().lower())\n\nbox.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;&lt;ComboboxSelected&gt;&gt;&quot;</i>, picked)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.combobox","tk.combobox-update"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() box = ttk.Combobox(root, values=[ \"Red\" , \"Green\" , \"Blue\" ], state= \"readonly\" ) box.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) swatch = tk.Label(root, text= \" \" , width= 20 ) swatch.pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) def picked (event): swatch.config(bg=box.get().lower()) box.bind( \"<<ComboboxSelected>>\" , picked) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.combobox-update","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I change the list of options in a Combobox later (dependent dropdowns)?","qh":"How do I change the list of options in a Combobox later (dependent dropdowns)?","a":"<code>box[&quot;values&quot;] = new_list</code> (or <code>box.config(values=...)</code>), then <code>box.set(&quot;&quot;)</code> to clear the old choice.","aka":"update combobox values, dynamic dropdown, second dropdown depends on first, combobox set values","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\ndata = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;Fruit&quot;</i>: [<i class=\"s\">&quot;Apple&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Pear&quot;</i>], <i class=\"s\">&quot;Veg&quot;</i>: [<i class=\"s\">&quot;Carrot&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Leek&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Pea&quot;</i>]}\nroot = tk.Tk()\nkind = ttk.Combobox(root, values=<i class=\"b\">list</i>(data), state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;readonly&quot;</i>)\nitem = ttk.Combobox(root, state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;readonly&quot;</i>)\nkind.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nitem.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">kind_changed</i>(e):\n    item[<i class=\"s\">&quot;values&quot;</i>] = data[kind.get()]\n    item.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\n\nkind.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;&lt;ComboboxSelected&gt;&gt;&quot;</i>, kind_changed)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk data = { \"Fruit\" : [ \"Apple\" , \"Pear\" ], \"Veg\" : [ \"Carrot\" , \"Leek\" , \"Pea\" ]} root = tk.Tk() kind = ttk.Combobox(root, values= list (data), state= \"readonly\" ) item = ttk.Combobox(root, state= \"readonly\" ) kind.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) item.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) def kind_changed (e): item[ \"values\" ] = data[kind.get()] item. set ( \"\" ) kind.bind( \"<<ComboboxSelected>>\" , kind_changed) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.optionmenu","s":"tk-choices","q":"What is OptionMenu and how do I use it?","qh":"What is OptionMenu and how do I use it?","a":"<code>tk.OptionMenu(root, var, *choices)</code> is a simple dropdown button. Note the <code>*</code> before the list.","aka":"optionmenu, tk.OptionMenu, menu button dropdown, simple dropdown","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nchoices = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;Easy&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Normal&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Hard&quot;</i>]\nlevel = tk.StringVar(value=choices[<i class=\"n\">1</i>])\ntk.OptionMenu(root, level, *choices).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nlevel.trace_add(<i class=\"s\">&quot;write&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> *a: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Level:&quot;</i>, level.get()))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>To change its choices later:</p><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nvar = tk.StringVar(value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>)\nom = tk.OptionMenu(root, var, <i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;b&quot;</i>)\nom.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">set_options</i>(options):\n    menu = om[<i class=\"s\">&quot;menu&quot;</i>]\n    menu.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> o <i class=\"k\">in</i> options:\n        menu.add_command(label=o, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i> v=o: var.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(v))\n    var.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(options[<i class=\"n\">0</i>])\n\nset_options([<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;z&quot;</i>])\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.combobox"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() choices = [ \"Easy\" , \"Normal\" , \"Hard\" ] level = tk.StringVar(value=choices[ 1 ]) tk.OptionMenu(root, level, *choices).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) level.trace_add( \"write\" , lambda *a: print ( \"Level:\" , level.get())) root.mainloop() To change its choices later: import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() var = tk.StringVar(value= \"a\" ) om = tk.OptionMenu(root, var, \"a\" , \"b\" ) om.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) def set_options (options): menu = om[ \"menu\" ] menu.delete( 0 , \"end\" ) for o in options: menu.add_command(label=o, command= lambda v=o: var. set (v)) var. set (options[ 0 ]) set_options([ \"x\" , \"y\" , \"z\" ]) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.listbox","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I make a list the user can pick from (Listbox)?","qh":"How do I make a list the user can pick from (Listbox)?","a":"Create <code>tk.Listbox</code>, add items with <code>insert(&quot;end&quot;, item)</code>, and read the selection with <code>curselection()</code>.","aka":"listbox, list widget, list of items, show list, selectable list, tk.Listbox","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlb = tk.Listbox(root, height=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nlb.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> name <i class=\"k\">in</i> [<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Bob&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Cara&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Dev&quot;</i>]:\n    lb.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, name)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">show</i>():\n    sel = lb.curselection()          <i class=\"c\"># a tuple of indexes, e.g. (2,)</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> sel:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Picked:&quot;</i>, lb.get(sel[<i class=\"n\">0</i>]))\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Show&quot;</i>, command=show).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>To fill it from a list in one go: <code>lb.insert(&quot;end&quot;, *my_list)</code>.</p>","see":["tk.listbox-select","tk.listbox-delete","tk.listbox-scroll"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lb = tk.Listbox(root, height= 6 ) lb.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) for name in [ \"Ada\" , \"Bob\" , \"Cara\" , \"Dev\" ]: lb.insert( \"end\" , name) def show (): sel = lb.curselection() # a tuple of indexes, e.g. (2,) if sel: print ( \"Picked:\" , lb.get(sel[ 0 ])) tk.Button(root, text= \"Show\" , command=show).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop() To fill it from a list in one go: lb.insert(\"end\", *my_list) ."},{"id":"tk.listbox-select","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I run code when a Listbox item is clicked or double-clicked?","qh":"How do I run code when a Listbox item is clicked or double-clicked?","a":"Bind <code>&quot;&lt;&lt;ListboxSelect&gt;&gt;&quot;</code> for single selection changes, <code>&quot;&lt;Double-Button-1&gt;&quot;</code> for double-click.","aka":"listbox select event, ListboxSelect, double click listbox, on select listbox, listbox click","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlb = tk.Listbox(root)\nlb.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Paris&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Tokyo&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Lima&quot;</i>)\nlb.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ninfo = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\ninfo.pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">selected</i>(e):\n    sel = lb.curselection()\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> sel:\n        info.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Selected &quot;</i> + lb.get(sel[<i class=\"n\">0</i>]))\n\nlb.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;&lt;ListboxSelect&gt;&gt;&quot;</i>, selected)\nlb.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Double-Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Opened&quot;</i>, lb.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;active&quot;</i>)))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>&lt;&lt;ListboxSelect&gt;&gt;</code> also fires when the selection is cleared, so check <code>curselection()</code> isn't empty.</p>","see":["tk.listbox"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lb = tk.Listbox(root) lb.insert( \"end\" , \"Paris\" , \"Tokyo\" , \"Lima\" ) lb.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) info = tk.Label(root, text= \"\" ) info.pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) def selected (e): sel = lb.curselection() if sel: info.config(text= \"Selected \" + lb.get(sel[ 0 ])) lb.bind( \"<<ListboxSelect>>\" , selected) lb.bind( \"<Double-Button-1>\" , lambda e: print ( \"Opened\" , lb.get( \"active\" ))) root.mainloop() <<ListboxSelect>> also fires when the selection is cleared, so check curselection() isn't empty."},{"id":"tk.listbox-delete","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I delete items from a Listbox (selected or all)?","qh":"How do I delete items from a Listbox (selected or all)?","a":"<code>lb.delete(index)</code> for one item, <code>lb.delete(0, &quot;end&quot;)</code> for all. For the selection, delete in <b>reverse</b> order so the indexes don't shift.","aka":"listbox delete, remove selected item, clear listbox, delete all items listbox","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlb = tk.Listbox(root, selectmode=<i class=\"s\">&quot;extended&quot;</i>)\nlb.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, *[<i class=\"s\">&quot;one&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;two&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;three&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;four&quot;</i>])\nlb.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">remove_selected</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">reversed</i>(lb.curselection()):\n        lb.delete(i)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Delete selected&quot;</i>, command=remove_selected).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Clear&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: lb.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lb = tk.Listbox(root, selectmode= \"extended\" ) lb.insert( \"end\" , *[ \"one\" , \"two\" , \"three\" , \"four\" ]) lb.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) def remove_selected (): for i in reversed (lb.curselection()): lb.delete(i) tk.Button(root, text= \"Delete selected\" , command=remove_selected).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Clear\" , command= lambda : lb.delete( 0 , \"end\" )).pack(side= \"left\" , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.listbox-multi","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I let the user select several items in a Listbox?","qh":"How do I let the user select several items in a Listbox?","a":"<code>selectmode=&quot;multiple&quot;</code> (click toggles) or <code>&quot;extended&quot;</code> (Shift/Ctrl like a file manager). <code>curselection()</code> gives all indexes.","aka":"multiple selection, selectmode, multi select listbox, select many","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlb = tk.Listbox(root, selectmode=<i class=\"s\">&quot;multiple&quot;</i>, exportselection=<i class=\"k\">False</i>)\nlb.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, *[<i class=\"s\">&quot;Mon&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Tue&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Wed&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Thu&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Fri&quot;</i>])\nlb.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Days&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: <i class=\"b\">print</i>([lb.get(i) <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> lb.curselection()])).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>exportselection=False</code> stops the selection vanishing when you select text somewhere else (common with two listboxes).</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lb = tk.Listbox(root, selectmode= \"multiple\" , exportselection= False ) lb.insert( \"end\" , *[ \"Mon\" , \"Tue\" , \"Wed\" , \"Thu\" , \"Fri\" ]) lb.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Days\" , command= lambda : print ([lb.get(i) for i in lb.curselection()])).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop() exportselection=False stops the selection vanishing when you select text somewhere else (common with two listboxes)."},{"id":"tk.listbox-scroll","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I add a scrollbar to a Listbox?","qh":"How do I add a scrollbar to a Listbox?","a":"Put both in a Frame, then connect them: <code>yscrollcommand=sb.set</code> on the listbox and <code>command=lb.yview</code> on the scrollbar.","aka":"listbox scrollbar, scroll list, long listbox","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nframe = tk.Frame(root)\nframe.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nsb = tk.Scrollbar(frame, orient=<i class=\"s\">&quot;vertical&quot;</i>)\nlb = tk.Listbox(frame, yscrollcommand=sb.<i class=\"b\">set</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">8</i>)\nsb.config(command=lb.yview)\nsb.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;right&quot;</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>)\nlb.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nlb.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, *[<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Item </i>{i}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">100</i>)])\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.scrollbar"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() frame = tk.Frame(root) frame.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 , fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) sb = tk.Scrollbar(frame, orient= \"vertical\" ) lb = tk.Listbox(frame, yscrollcommand=sb. set , height= 8 ) sb.config(command=lb.yview) sb.pack(side= \"right\" , fill= \"y\" ) lb.pack(side= \"left\" , fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) lb.insert( \"end\" , *[ f\" Item {i} \" for i in range ( 100 )]) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.listbox-colors","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I change the colour of one Listbox item or edit an item?","qh":"How do I change the colour of one Listbox item or edit an item?","a":"Colour: <code>lb.itemconfig(i, fg=&quot;red&quot;, bg=&quot;#fee&quot;)</code>. Edit: there's no \"replace\", so <code>delete(i)</code> then <code>insert(i, new)</code>.","aka":"listbox item color, itemconfig, update listbox item, edit item listbox","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlb = tk.Listbox(root)\nlb.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;done&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;late&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;todo&quot;</i>)\nlb.itemconfig(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>)\nlb.delete(<i class=\"n\">2</i>)\nlb.insert(<i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;todo (edited)&quot;</i>)\nlb.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lb = tk.Listbox(root) lb.insert( \"end\" , \"done\" , \"late\" , \"todo\" ) lb.itemconfig( 1 , fg= \"white\" , bg= \"#c23b22\" ) lb.delete( 2 ) lb.insert( 2 , \"todo (edited)\" ) lb.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 20 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.scale","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I make a slider?","qh":"How do I make a slider?","a":"<code>tk.Scale(root, from_=0, to=100, orient=&quot;horizontal&quot;)</code>. Read it with <code>.get()</code> or a variable; <code>command=</code> runs while it moves.","aka":"slider, scale, range input, volume slider, ttk.Scale, horizontal slider, value slider","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nvol = tk.IntVar(value=<i class=\"n\">50</i>)\ntk.Scale(root, from_=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, to=<i class=\"n\">100</i>, orient=<i class=\"s\">&quot;horizontal&quot;</i>, variable=vol, length=<i class=\"n\">200</i>, label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Volume&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Label(root, textvariable=vol).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>It's <code>from_</code> with an underscore, because <code>from</code> is a Python keyword. For steps use <code>resolution=0.5</code> or <code>tickinterval=10</code>.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() vol = tk.IntVar(value= 50 ) tk.Scale(root, from_= 0 , to= 100 , orient= \"horizontal\" , variable=vol, length= 200 , label= \"Volume\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) tk.Label(root, textvariable=vol).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop() It's from_ with an underscore, because from is a Python keyword. For steps use resolution=0.5 or tickinterval=10 ."},{"id":"tk.spinbox","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I make a number box with up/down arrows (Spinbox)?","qh":"How do I make a number box with up/down arrows (Spinbox)?","a":"<code>tk.Spinbox(root, from_=1, to=10)</code> or with a list: <code>values=(&quot;S&quot;, &quot;M&quot;, &quot;L&quot;)</code>. Read with <code>.get()</code> (a string).","aka":"spinbox, number picker, up down arrows, increment box, numeric input","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nqty = tk.Spinbox(root, from_=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, to=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nqty.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nsize = tk.Spinbox(root, values=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;S&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;M&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;L&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;XL&quot;</i>), width=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;readonly&quot;</i>)\nsize.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Buy&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">int</i>(qty.get()), size.get())).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() qty = tk.Spinbox(root, from_= 1 , to= 10 , width= 5 ) qty.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) size = tk.Spinbox(root, values=( \"S\" , \"M\" , \"L\" , \"XL\" ), width= 5 , state= \"readonly\" ) size.pack(padx= 20 , pady=( 0 , 10 )) tk.Button(root, text= \"Buy\" , command= lambda : print ( int (qty.get()), size.get())).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.checkbutton-switch","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I make a toggle button (on/off switch)?","qh":"How do I make a toggle button (on/off switch)?","a":"A Checkbutton with <code>indicatoron=False</code> looks like a button that stays pressed. Or change a Button's text in its command.","aka":"toggle button, on off button, switch, indicatoron, push button toggle","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\non = tk.BooleanVar()\ntk.Checkbutton(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Bold&quot;</i>, variable=on, indicatoron=<i class=\"k\">False</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">8</i>, selectcolor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ffcc66&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\nstate = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;on&quot;</i>: <i class=\"k\">False</i>}\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">flip</i>():\n    state[<i class=\"s\">&quot;on&quot;</i>] = <i class=\"k\">not</i> state[<i class=\"s\">&quot;on&quot;</i>]\n    sw.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ON&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> state[<i class=\"s\">&quot;on&quot;</i>] <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;OFF&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#2e7d32&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> state[<i class=\"s\">&quot;on&quot;</i>] <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;#888&quot;</i>)\n\nsw = tk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;OFF&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#888&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">8</i>, command=flip)\nsw.pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() on = tk.BooleanVar() tk.Checkbutton(root, text= \"Bold\" , variable=on, indicatoron= False , width= 8 , selectcolor= \"#ffcc66\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) state = { \"on\" : False } def flip (): state[ \"on\" ] = not state[ \"on\" ] sw.config(text= \"ON\" if state[ \"on\" ] else \"OFF\" , bg= \"#2e7d32\" if state[ \"on\" ] else \"#888\" ) sw = tk.Button(root, text= \"OFF\" , bg= \"#888\" , fg= \"white\" , width= 8 , command=flip) sw.pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.layout-which","s":"tk-layout","q":"pack, grid or place: which should I use?","qh":"pack, grid or place: which should I use?","a":"<b>grid</b> for forms and anything in rows/columns, <b>pack</b> for simple stacks (top-to-bottom or side by side), <b>place</b> only for exact pixel positions. Group widgets in Frames and you can use a different one inside each Frame.","aka":"geometry manager, layout manager, pack vs grid, how to position widgets, arrange widgets","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntop = tk.Frame(root, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ddd&quot;</i>); top.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>)          <i class=\"c\"># pack in root</i>\ntk.Label(top, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Toolbar&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ddd&quot;</i>).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\n\nform = tk.Frame(root); form.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)            <i class=\"c\"># grid inside this frame</i>\ntk.Label(form, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Name&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;e&quot;</i>)\ntk.Entry(form).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\ntk.Label(form, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Email&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;e&quot;</i>)\ntk.Entry(form).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.pack","tk.grid","tk.place","tk.err.pack-grid"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() top = tk.Frame(root, bg= \"#ddd\" ); top.pack(fill= \"x\" ) # pack in root tk.Label(top, text= \"Toolbar\" , bg= \"#ddd\" ).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 6 ) form = tk.Frame(root); form.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) # grid inside this frame tk.Label(form, text= \"Name\" ).grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , sticky= \"e\" ) tk.Entry(form).grid(row= 0 , column= 1 ) tk.Label(form, text= \"Email\" ).grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , sticky= \"e\" ) tk.Entry(form).grid(row= 1 , column= 1 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.pack","s":"tk-layout","q":"How does pack() work? How do I put widgets side by side?","qh":"How does pack() work? How do I put widgets side by side?","a":"Each <code>pack()</code> takes space from the remaining area, from the <code>side</code> you say (default <code>&quot;top&quot;</code>). Use <code>side=&quot;left&quot;</code> to place widgets in a row.","aka":"pack side left, widgets next to each other, horizontal layout, stack widgets, pack order","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> t <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;one&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;two&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;three&quot;</i>):\n    tk.Button(root, text=t).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Mixing sides in one container gets confusing fast. Put each row in its own Frame instead.</p>","see":["tk.pack-fill","tk.padding"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() for t in ( \"one\" , \"two\" , \"three\" ): tk.Button(root, text=t).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 4 , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() Mixing sides in one container gets confusing fast. Put each row in its own Frame instead."},{"id":"tk.pack-fill","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I make a widget fill the window or stretch when it's resized?","qh":"How do I make a widget fill the window or stretch when it's resized?","a":"With pack: <code>fill=&quot;x&quot;</code>/<code>&quot;y&quot;</code>/<code>&quot;both&quot;</code> stretches it across its slot; <code>expand=True</code> makes its slot grow with the window. Usually you want both: <code>pack(fill=&quot;both&quot;, expand=True)</code>.","aka":"fill expand, stretch widget, fill both, expand true, widget resize with window, full width","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;300x220&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;fill x&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>)\ntk.Text(root, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#eee&quot;</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;status bar&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ccc&quot;</i>, anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;bottom&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.grid-weight"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"300x220\" ) tk.Label(root, text= \"fill x\" , bg= \"#fc6\" ).pack(fill= \"x\" ) tk.Text(root, bg= \"#eee\" ).pack(fill= \"both\" , expand= True , padx= 6 , pady= 6 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"status bar\" , bg= \"#ccc\" , anchor= \"w\" ).pack(fill= \"x\" , side= \"bottom\" ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.grid","s":"tk-layout","q":"How does grid() work?","qh":"How does grid() work?","a":"<code>widget.grid(row=r, column=c)</code>. Rows and columns start at 0, are only as big as their widest widget, and empty rows/columns take no space.","aka":"grid row column, table layout, form layout, rows and columns, grid tutorial","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Username&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>)\ntk.Entry(root).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Password&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>)\ntk.Entry(root, show=<i class=\"s\">&quot;*&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Log in&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;e&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Skipping to <code>column=10</code> won't leave a gap: empty columns have no width. Use <code>padx</code> or <code>minsize</code> for gaps.</p>","see":["tk.grid-sticky","tk.grid-span","tk.grid-weight"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"Username\" ).grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , padx= 5 , pady= 5 , sticky= \"w\" ) tk.Entry(root).grid(row= 0 , column= 1 , padx= 5 , pady= 5 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"Password\" ).grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , padx= 5 , pady= 5 , sticky= \"w\" ) tk.Entry(root, show= \"*\" ).grid(row= 1 , column= 1 , padx= 5 , pady= 5 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Log in\" ).grid(row= 2 , column= 1 , sticky= \"e\" , padx= 5 , pady= 5 ) root.mainloop() Skipping to column=10 won't leave a gap: empty columns have no width. Use padx or minsize for gaps."},{"id":"tk.grid-sticky","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I align or stretch a widget inside its grid cell (sticky)?","qh":"How do I align or stretch a widget inside its grid cell (sticky)?","a":"<code>sticky</code> uses compass letters: <code>&quot;w&quot;</code> left, <code>&quot;e&quot;</code> right, <code>&quot;n&quot;</code> top, <code>&quot;s&quot;</code> bottom. <code>&quot;ew&quot;</code> stretches across, <code>&quot;nsew&quot;</code> fills the cell.","aka":"sticky, grid align left, grid stretch, nsew, sticky w e, align in grid cell","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;a long label in column 0&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;e&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;e&quot;</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ew&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">3</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ew&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.grid-weight"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"a long label in column 0\" ).grid(row= 0 , column= 0 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"w\" ).grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , sticky= \"w\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"e\" ).grid(row= 2 , column= 0 , sticky= \"e\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"ew\" ).grid(row= 3 , column= 0 , sticky= \"ew\" ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.grid-span","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I make a widget span several columns or rows?","qh":"How do I make a widget span several columns or rows?","a":"<code>grid(row=2, column=0, columnspan=2)</code>; same with <code>rowspan</code>. Add <code>sticky=&quot;ew&quot;</code> to stretch it over the whole span.","aka":"columnspan, rowspan, merge cells, widget across two columns, full width button grid","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Entry(root).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Entry(root).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Wide button&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, columnspan=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ew&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Entry(root).grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , padx= 4 , pady= 4 ) tk.Entry(root).grid(row= 0 , column= 1 , padx= 4 , pady= 4 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Wide button\" ).grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , columnspan= 2 , sticky= \"ew\" , padx= 4 , pady= 4 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.grid-weight","s":"tk-layout","q":"Why don't my grid widgets stretch when the window is resized?","qh":"Why don't my grid widgets stretch when the window is resized?","a":"Give the row/column a weight: <code>root.columnconfigure(0, weight=1)</code> and <code>root.rowconfigure(0, weight=1)</code>, <b>and</b> use <code>sticky=&quot;nsew&quot;</code> on the widget.","aka":"grid resize, columnconfigure weight, rowconfigure, grid expand, responsive grid, grid fill window","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;320x200&quot;</i>)\nroot.columnconfigure(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, weight=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)        <i class=\"c\"># column 0 takes extra width</i>\nroot.rowconfigure(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, weight=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)           <i class=\"c\"># row 0 takes extra height</i>\ntk.Text(root).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, columnspan=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;nsew&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Entry(root).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ew&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Send&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Weights share extra space: weight 2 gets twice as much as weight 1. <code>uniform=&quot;x&quot;</code> keeps columns the same size.</p>","see":["tk.grid-sticky","tk.pack-fill"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"320x200\" ) root.columnconfigure( 0 , weight= 1 ) # column 0 takes extra width root.rowconfigure( 0 , weight= 1 ) # row 0 takes extra height tk.Text(root).grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , columnspan= 2 , sticky= \"nsew\" , padx= 5 , pady= 5 ) tk.Entry(root).grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , sticky= \"ew\" , padx= 5 , pady= 5 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Send\" ).grid(row= 1 , column= 1 , padx= 5 , pady= 5 ) root.mainloop() Weights share extra space: weight 2 gets twice as much as weight 1. uniform=\"x\" keeps columns the same size."},{"id":"tk.place","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I put a widget at an exact position (x, y)?","qh":"How do I put a widget at an exact position (x, y)?","a":"<code>widget.place(x=50, y=20)</code>. Relative: <code>place(relx=0.5, rely=0.5, anchor=&quot;center&quot;)</code> centres it whatever the window size.","aka":"place, absolute position, x y coordinates widget, relx rely, position widget pixel","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;300x200&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;at 10,10&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>).place(x=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, y=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;center&quot;</i>).place(relx=<i class=\"n\">0.5</i>, rely=<i class=\"n\">0.5</i>, anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;center&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;bottom right&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ccc&quot;</i>).place(relx=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, rely=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;se&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>place</code> doesn't make the window grow to fit, so set a size with <code>geometry()</code>.</p>","see":["tk.center-widget"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"300x200\" ) tk.Label(root, text= \"at 10,10\" , bg= \"#fc6\" ).place(x= 10 , y= 10 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"center\" ).place(relx= 0.5 , rely= 0.5 , anchor= \"center\" ) tk.Label(root, text= \"bottom right\" , bg= \"#ccc\" ).place(relx= 1 , rely= 1 , anchor= \"se\" ) root.mainloop() place doesn't make the window grow to fit, so set a size with geometry() ."},{"id":"tk.center-widget","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I centre a widget in the window?","qh":"How do I centre a widget in the window?","a":"Quickest: <code>place(relx=.5, rely=.5, anchor=&quot;center&quot;)</code>. With pack: <code>pack(expand=True)</code>. With grid: give the row and column weight and no sticky.","aka":"center button, centre label, middle of window, center widget, align center","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;300x200&quot;</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;I&#x27;m in the middle&quot;</i>).pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"300x200\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"I'm in the middle\" ).pack(expand= True ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.padding","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I add space between widgets?","qh":"How do I add space between widgets?","a":"<code>padx</code>/<code>pady</code> in <code>pack()</code>/<code>grid()</code> add space <b>outside</b> the widget; give a pair for different sides: <code>pady=(10, 0)</code>. <code>ipadx</code>/<code>ipady</code> add space <b>inside</b> it.","aka":"padding, margin, padx pady, space between buttons, gap, ipadx, spacing","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;padx=20&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;pady=(20, 0): only above&quot;</i>).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">20</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>))\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ipadx=20 ipady=10: bigger inside&quot;</i>).pack(ipadx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, ipady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Button(root, text= \"padx=20\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"pady=(20, 0): only above\" ).pack(pady=( 20 , 0 )) tk.Button(root, text= \"ipadx=20 ipady=10: bigger inside\" ).pack(ipadx= 20 , ipady= 10 , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.err.pack-grid","s":"tk-layout","q":"TclError: cannot use geometry manager grid inside . which already has slaves managed by pack","qh":"TclError: cannot use geometry manager grid inside . which already has slaves managed by pack","a":"In one container (window or Frame) every child must use the <b>same</b> manager. Use <code>pack</code> for everything in <code>root</code>, or <code>grid</code> for everything; put a Frame in between if you need both.","aka":"cannot use geometry manager, mixing pack and grid, pack and grid together, already has slaves managed by, content managed by","err":"cannot use geometry manager*inside*which already has*managed by","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre>tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Title&quot;</i>).pack()\ntk.Entry(root).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)       <i class=\"c\"># TclError: root already uses pack</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Title&quot;</i>).pack()\nform = tk.Frame(root)\nform.pack()\ntk.Entry(form).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)        <i class=\"c\"># fine: grid is inside the frame</i>\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>The message says <code>.</code> because that's Tk's name for the root window. A name like <code>.!frame</code> means a Frame.</p>","see":["tk.layout-which"],"t":"tk.Label(root, text= \"Title\" ).pack() tk.Entry(root).grid(row= 0 , column= 0 ) # TclError: root already uses pack import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"Title\" ).pack() form = tk.Frame(root) form.pack() tk.Entry(form).grid(row= 0 , column= 0 ) # fine: grid is inside the frame root.mainloop() The message says . because that's Tk's name for the root window. A name like .!frame means a Frame."},{"id":"tk.err.none-grid","s":"tk-layout","q":"AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'get' (or 'config', 'insert'...)","qh":"AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'get' (or 'config', 'insert'...)","a":"You did <code>x = tk.Entry(root).grid(...)</code>. <code>grid()</code>/<code>pack()</code>/<code>place()</code> return <code>None</code>, so <code>x</code> is <code>None</code>. Create the widget first, then lay it out on a <b>separate line</b>.","aka":"nonetype has no attribute get, entry get none, label config none, grid returns none, pack returns none","err":"'NoneType' object has no attribute 'get'|'NoneType' object has no attribute 'config'|'NoneType' object has no attribute 'insert'|'NoneType' object has no attribute 'delete'|'NoneType' object has no attribute 'configure'|'NoneType' object has no attribute","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre>name = tk.Entry(root).pack()\nname.get()          <i class=\"c\"># AttributeError: &#x27;NoneType&#x27; object has no attribute &#x27;get&#x27;</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nname = tk.Entry(root)\nname.pack()\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">repr</i>(name.get()))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Chaining is fine when you never need the widget again: <code>tk.Label(root, text=&quot;Hi&quot;).pack()</code>.</p>","see":["py.err.nonetype"],"t":"name = tk.Entry(root).pack() name.get() # AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'get' import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() name = tk.Entry(root) name.pack() print ( repr (name.get())) root.mainloop() Chaining is fine when you never need the widget again: tk.Label(root, text=\"Hi\").pack() ."},{"id":"tk.frame","s":"tk-layout","q":"What is a Frame and how do I use it to group widgets?","qh":"What is a Frame and how do I use it to group widgets?","a":"A <code>Frame</code> is an invisible box that holds other widgets. Lay out the Frame in the window, then the widgets inside the Frame. <code>LabelFrame</code> adds a border with a title.","aka":"frame, container, group widgets, panel, sections in window, labelframe, border around widgets","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nleft = tk.Frame(root, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#eee&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nleft.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> t <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Home&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Files&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Settings&quot;</i>):\n    tk.Button(left, text=t, width=<i class=\"n\">10</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">2</i>)\n\nbox = tk.LabelFrame(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Details&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nbox.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\ntk.Label(box, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Name:&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>)\ntk.Entry(box).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>An empty Frame shrinks to nothing. To keep a set size: <code>Frame(root, width=200, height=100)</code> plus <code>frame.pack_propagate(False)</code> (or <code>grid_propagate(False)</code>).</p>","see":["tk.layout-which"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() left = tk.Frame(root, bg= \"#eee\" , padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) left.pack(side= \"left\" , fill= \"y\" ) for t in ( \"Home\" , \"Files\" , \"Settings\" ): tk.Button(left, text=t, width= 10 ).pack(pady= 2 ) box = tk.LabelFrame(root, text= \"Details\" , padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) box.pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 10 , pady= 10 , fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) tk.Label(box, text= \"Name:\" ).grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , sticky= \"w\" ) tk.Entry(box).grid(row= 0 , column= 1 ) root.mainloop() An empty Frame shrinks to nothing. To keep a set size: Frame(root, width=200, height=100) plus frame.pack_propagate(False) (or grid_propagate(False) )."},{"id":"tk.frame-size","s":"tk-layout","q":"Why does my Frame ignore its width and height?","qh":"Why does my Frame ignore its width and height?","a":"A Frame shrinks or grows to fit its children. Turn that off with <code>frame.pack_propagate(False)</code> (if children use pack) or <code>grid_propagate(False)</code> (if they use grid).","aka":"frame size ignored, pack_propagate, grid_propagate, frame shrinks, fixed size frame","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nf = tk.Frame(root, width=<i class=\"n\">250</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">120</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>)\nf.pack_propagate(<i class=\"k\">False</i>)\nf.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Label(f, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;fixed 250x120 frame&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>).pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() f = tk.Frame(root, width= 250 , height= 120 , bg= \"#fc6\" ) f.pack_propagate( False ) f.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) tk.Label(f, text= \"fixed 250x120 frame\" , bg= \"#fc6\" ).pack(expand= True ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.scrollbar","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I connect a Scrollbar to a widget?","qh":"How do I connect a Scrollbar to a widget?","a":"Two links: the widget gets <code>yscrollcommand=scrollbar.set</code>, the scrollbar gets <code>command=widget.yview</code>. Works for Text, Listbox, Canvas, Treeview (use <code>x</code> versions for horizontal).","aka":"scrollbar, add scrollbar, scroll, yscrollcommand, xscrollcommand, horizontal scrollbar","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nt = tk.Text(root, wrap=<i class=\"s\">&quot;none&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">8</i>)\nys = tk.Scrollbar(root, orient=<i class=\"s\">&quot;vertical&quot;</i>, command=t.yview)\nxs = tk.Scrollbar(root, orient=<i class=\"s\">&quot;horizontal&quot;</i>, command=t.xview)\nt.config(yscrollcommand=ys.<i class=\"b\">set</i>, xscrollcommand=xs.<i class=\"b\">set</i>)\nt.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;nsew&quot;</i>)\nys.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ns&quot;</i>)\nxs.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ew&quot;</i>)\nroot.rowconfigure(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, weight=<i class=\"n\">1</i>); root.columnconfigure(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, weight=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\nt.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;\\n&quot;</i>.join(<i class=\"s\">&quot;long line &quot;</i> * <i class=\"n\">8</i> + <i class=\"b\">str</i>(i) <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">60</i>)))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.scroll-frame","tk.text-scroll","tk.listbox-scroll"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() t = tk.Text(root, wrap= \"none\" , width= 30 , height= 8 ) ys = tk.Scrollbar(root, orient= \"vertical\" , command=t.yview) xs = tk.Scrollbar(root, orient= \"horizontal\" , command=t.xview) t.config(yscrollcommand=ys. set , xscrollcommand=xs. set ) t.grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , sticky= \"nsew\" ) ys.grid(row= 0 , column= 1 , sticky= \"ns\" ) xs.grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , sticky= \"ew\" ) root.rowconfigure( 0 , weight= 1 ); root.columnconfigure( 0 , weight= 1 ) t.insert( \"1.0\" , \"\\n\" .join( \"long line \" * 8 + str (i) for i in range ( 60 ))) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.scroll-frame","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I make a scrollable Frame (scroll a whole page of widgets)?","qh":"How do I make a scrollable Frame (scroll a whole page of widgets)?","a":"Frames can't scroll. Put a Frame inside a Canvas with <code>create_window</code>, update the canvas <code>scrollregion</code> when the frame changes size, and attach a Scrollbar to the canvas.","aka":"scrollable frame, scroll many widgets, scroll window, scrolling frame canvas, frame with scrollbar, mousewheel scroll frame","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">ScrollFrame</i>(tk.Frame):\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, master, **kw):\n        <i class=\"b\">super</i>().__init__(master, **kw)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas = tk.Canvas(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, highlightthickness=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\n        sb = tk.Scrollbar(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, orient=<i class=\"s\">&quot;vertical&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.yview)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.inner = tk.Frame(<i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.inner.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Configure&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.configure(scrollregion=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.bbox(<i class=\"s\">&quot;all&quot;</i>)))\n        win = <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.create_window((<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>), window=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.inner, anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;nw&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Configure&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.itemconfigure(win, width=e.width))\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.configure(yscrollcommand=sb.<i class=\"b\">set</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n        sb.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;right&quot;</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.inner.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Enter&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.bind_all(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;MouseWheel&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">self</i>._wheel))\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.inner.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Leave&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.unbind_all(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;MouseWheel&gt;&quot;</i>))\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">_wheel</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, e):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.yview_scroll(<i class=\"b\">int</i>(-e.delta / <i class=\"n\">120</i>) <i class=\"k\">or</i> (-<i class=\"n\">1</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> e.delta &gt; <i class=\"n\">0</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"n\">1</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;units&quot;</i>)\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;300x250&quot;</i>)\nsf = ScrollFrame(root)\nsf.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">40</i>):\n    tk.Checkbutton(sf.inner, text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Option </i>{i}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>).pack(anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Put your widgets in <code>sf.inner</code>, not <code>sf</code>. On Linux the wheel sends <code>&lt;Button-4&gt;</code>/<code>&lt;Button-5&gt;</code> instead; see <a href=\"#/a/tk.mousewheel\">How do I use the mouse wheel / scroll wheel?</a>.</p>","see":["tk.mousewheel"],"t":"import tkinter as tk class ScrollFrame (tk.Frame): def __init__ ( self , master, **kw): super ().__init__(master, **kw) self .canvas = tk.Canvas( self , highlightthickness= 0 ) sb = tk.Scrollbar( self , orient= \"vertical\" , command= self .canvas.yview) self .inner = tk.Frame( self .canvas) self .inner.bind( \"<Configure>\" , lambda e: self .canvas.configure(scrollregion= self .canvas.bbox( \"all\" ))) win = self .canvas.create_window(( 0 , 0 ), window= self .inner, anchor= \"nw\" ) self .canvas.bind( \"<Configure>\" , lambda e: self .canvas.itemconfigure(win, width=e.width)) self .canvas.configure(yscrollcommand=sb. set ) self .canvas.pack(side= \"left\" , fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) sb.pack(side= \"right\" , fill= \"y\" ) self .inner.bind( \"<Enter>\" , lambda e: self .canvas.bind_all( \"<MouseWheel>\" , self ._wheel)) self .inner.bind( \"<Leave>\" , lambda e: self .canvas.unbind_all( \"<MouseWheel>\" )) def _wheel ( self , e): self .canvas.yview_scroll( int (-e.delta / 120 ) or (- 1 if e.delta > 0 else 1 ), \"units\" ) root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"300x250\" ) sf = ScrollFrame(root) sf.pack(fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) for i in range ( 40 ): tk.Checkbutton(sf.inner, text= f\" Option {i} \" ).pack(anchor= \"w\" ) root.mainloop() Put your widgets in sf.inner , not sf . On Linux the wheel sends <Button-4> / <Button-5> instead; see How do I use the mouse wheel / scroll wheel? ."},{"id":"tk.paned","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I make a split view with a draggable divider?","qh":"How do I make a split view with a draggable divider?","a":"Use <code>ttk.PanedWindow</code> (or <code>tk.PanedWindow</code>) and <code>add()</code> each side.","aka":"panedwindow, splitter, resizable panels, split pane, draggable divider","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;400x200&quot;</i>)\npw = ttk.PanedWindow(root, orient=<i class=\"s\">&quot;horizontal&quot;</i>)\npw.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\npw.add(tk.Listbox(pw), weight=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\npw.add(tk.Text(pw), weight=<i class=\"n\">3</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"400x200\" ) pw = ttk.PanedWindow(root, orient= \"horizontal\" ) pw.pack(fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) pw.add(tk.Listbox(pw), weight= 1 ) pw.add(tk.Text(pw), weight= 3 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.separator","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I draw a dividing line between widgets?","qh":"How do I draw a dividing line between widgets?","a":"<code>ttk.Separator(root, orient=&quot;horizontal&quot;).pack(fill=&quot;x&quot;)</code>, or a 1-pixel-high Frame with a background colour.","aka":"separator, horizontal line, divider, hr, vertical line between widgets","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Above&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nttk.Separator(root, orient=<i class=\"s\">&quot;horizontal&quot;</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Below&quot;</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Frame(root, height=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"Above\" ).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 5 ) ttk.Separator(root, orient= \"horizontal\" ).pack(fill= \"x\" , padx= 10 , pady= 5 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"Below\" ).pack(pady= 5 ) tk.Frame(root, height= 2 , bg= \"#c23b22\" ).pack(fill= \"x\" , padx= 10 , pady= 5 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.responsive","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I make my layout adapt when the window is resized?","qh":"How do I make my layout adapt when the window is resized?","a":"Use <code>fill</code>/<code>expand</code> (pack) or <code>weight</code> + <code>sticky</code> (grid) so widgets stretch. For bigger changes (e.g. switch to one column when narrow), bind <code>&lt;Configure&gt;</code> and re-grid.","aka":"responsive layout, resize widgets with window, window resize event, scale widgets, adaptive layout","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;500x200&quot;</i>)\nboxes = [tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Panel </i>{i}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>, relief=<i class=\"s\">&quot;solid&quot;</i>, bd=<i class=\"n\">1</i>) <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">3</i>)]\nmode = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;cols&quot;</i>: <i class=\"k\">None</i>}\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">relayout</i>(event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    cols = <i class=\"n\">3</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> root.winfo_width() &gt; <i class=\"n\">400</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> cols == mode[<i class=\"s\">&quot;cols&quot;</i>]:\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n    mode[<i class=\"s\">&quot;cols&quot;</i>] = cols\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> i, b <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">enumerate</i>(boxes):\n        b.grid(row=i // cols, column=i % cols, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;nsew&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> c <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">3</i>):\n        root.columnconfigure(c, weight=<i class=\"n\">1</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> c &lt; cols <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"n\">0</i>)\n\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Configure&gt;&quot;</i>, relayout)\nrelayout()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.pack-fill","tk.grid-weight"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"500x200\" ) boxes = [tk.Label(root, text= f\" Panel {i} \" , bg= \"#fc6\" , relief= \"solid\" , bd= 1 ) for i in range ( 3 )] mode = { \"cols\" : None } def relayout (event= None ): cols = 3 if root.winfo_width() > 400 else 1 if cols == mode[ \"cols\" ]: return mode[ \"cols\" ] = cols for i, b in enumerate (boxes): b.grid(row=i // cols, column=i % cols, sticky= \"nsew\" , padx= 4 , pady= 4 ) for c in range ( 3 ): root.columnconfigure(c, weight= 1 if c < cols else 0 ) root.bind( \"<Configure>\" , relayout) relayout() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.grid-info","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I find which row/column a widget is in, or which widget is at a cell?","qh":"How do I find which row/column a widget is in, or which widget is at a cell?","a":"<code>widget.grid_info()</code> returns a dict with <code>&quot;row&quot;</code> and <code>&quot;column&quot;</code>. <code>parent.grid_slaves(row=r, column=c)</code> returns the widgets at that cell.","aka":"grid_info, grid_slaves, widget at row column, get widget position grid","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> r <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">2</i>):\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> c <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">2</i>):\n        tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{r}<i class=\"s\">,</i>{c}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">6</i>).grid(row=r, column=c)\nw = root.grid_slaves(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)[<i class=\"n\">0</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(w[<i class=\"s\">&quot;text&quot;</i>], w.grid_info()[<i class=\"s\">&quot;row&quot;</i>], w.grid_info()[<i class=\"s\">&quot;column&quot;</i>])\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() for r in range ( 2 ): for c in range ( 2 ): tk.Label(root, text= f\" {r} , {c} \" , width= 6 ).grid(row=r, column=c) w = root.grid_slaves(row= 1 , column= 0 )[ 0 ] print (w[ \"text\" ], w.grid_info()[ \"row\" ], w.grid_info()[ \"column\" ]) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.bind","s":"tk-events","q":"How does bind() work?","qh":"How does bind() work?","a":"<code>widget.bind(&quot;&lt;Event&gt;&quot;, function)</code>. The function receives one <code>event</code> argument with details (<code>event.x</code>, <code>event.keysym</code>, <code>event.widget</code>...).","aka":"bind event, event binding, event handler, bind function, widget.bind, event callback","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Click or type&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#eee&quot;</i>)\nlab.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">clicked</i>(event):\n    lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Click at </i>{event.x}<i class=\"s\">, </i>{event.y}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">typed</i>(event):\n    lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Key: </i>{event.keysym}<i class=\"s\">  char: </i>{event.char!r}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\nlab.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, clicked)\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Key&gt;&quot;</i>, typed)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>A function used for both <code>command=</code> (no argument) and <code>bind</code> (one argument) should be written <code>def f(event=None):</code>.</p>","see":["tk.bind-keys","tk.mouse","tk.event-names"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"Click or type\" , width= 30 , height= 5 , bg= \"#eee\" ) lab.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) def clicked (event): lab.config(text= f\" Click at {event.x} , {event.y} \" ) def typed (event): lab.config(text= f\" Key: {event.keysym} char: {event.char!r} \" ) lab.bind( \"<Button-1>\" , clicked) root.bind( \"<Key>\" , typed) root.mainloop() A function used for both command= (no argument) and bind (one argument) should be written def f(event=None): ."},{"id":"tk.err.bind-missing-arg","s":"tk-events","q":"TypeError: my_function() takes 0 positional arguments but 1 was given (when using bind)","qh":"TypeError: my_function() takes 0 positional arguments but 1 was given (when using bind)","a":"<code>bind</code> always passes an <code>event</code> argument. Add a parameter: <code>def go(event=None):</code>. (In a class method, <code>self</code> is the first one, so you need <code>def go(self, event=None):</code>.)","aka":"bind takes 0 positional arguments, bind missing event argument, function takes no arguments but 1 given tkinter","err":"takes 0 positional arguments but 1 was given|takes 1 positional argument but 2 were given","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">go</i>():\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;go&quot;</i>)\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Return&gt;&quot;</i>, go)     <i class=\"c\"># TypeError when Enter is pressed</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">go</i>(event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;go&quot;</i>)\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Return&gt;&quot;</i>, go)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Go&quot;</i>, command=go).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.bind","py.err.positional-args"],"t":"def go (): print ( \"go\" ) root.bind( \"<Return>\" , go) # TypeError when Enter is pressed import tkinter as tk def go (event= None ): print ( \"go\" ) root = tk.Tk() root.bind( \"<Return>\" , go) tk.Button(root, text= \"Go\" , command=go).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.bind-keys","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I detect key presses (arrow keys, letters, Enter, Escape)?","qh":"How do I detect key presses (arrow keys, letters, Enter, Escape)?","a":"Bind on the window: <code>root.bind(&quot;&lt;Left&gt;&quot;, f)</code>, <code>root.bind(&quot;&lt;Return&gt;&quot;, f)</code>, <code>root.bind(&quot;&lt;Escape&gt;&quot;, f)</code>, <code>root.bind(&quot;a&quot;, f)</code>, or <code>&quot;&lt;Key&gt;&quot;</code> for every key and check <code>event.keysym</code>.","aka":"keyboard input, key press event, arrow keys, keypress, detect key, keyboard controls, wasd, KeyPress","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">240</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">160</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\ndot = c.create_oval(<i class=\"n\">110</i>, <i class=\"n\">70</i>, <i class=\"n\">130</i>, <i class=\"n\">90</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>)\nmoves = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;Left&quot;</i>: (-<i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;Right&quot;</i>: (<i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;Up&quot;</i>: (<i class=\"n\">0</i>, -<i class=\"n\">10</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;Down&quot;</i>: (<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>),\n         <i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>: (-<i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;d&quot;</i>: (<i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>: (<i class=\"n\">0</i>, -<i class=\"n\">10</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;s&quot;</i>: (<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>)}\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">key</i>(event):\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> event.keysym <i class=\"k\">in</i> moves:\n        c.move(dot, *moves[event.keysym])\n\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Key&gt;&quot;</i>, key)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Key events go to the widget that has <b>focus</b>. Binding on <code>root</code> catches keys anywhere in that window. If nothing happens, click the window first.</p>","see":["tk.shortcuts","tk.key-held","tk.event-names"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 240 , height= 160 , bg= \"white\" ) c.pack() dot = c.create_oval( 110 , 70 , 130 , 90 , fill= \"red\" ) moves = { \"Left\" : (- 10 , 0 ), \"Right\" : ( 10 , 0 ), \"Up\" : ( 0 , - 10 ), \"Down\" : ( 0 , 10 ), \"a\" : (- 10 , 0 ), \"d\" : ( 10 , 0 ), \"w\" : ( 0 , - 10 ), \"s\" : ( 0 , 10 )} def key (event): if event.keysym in moves: c.move(dot, *moves[event.keysym]) root.bind( \"<Key>\" , key) root.mainloop() Key events go to the widget that has focus . Binding on root catches keys anywhere in that window. If nothing happens, click the window first."},{"id":"tk.key-held","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I make smooth movement while a key is held down (for games)?","qh":"How do I make smooth movement while a key is held down (for games)?","a":"Don't move on each key event (it stutters and has a repeat delay). Track which keys are down with <code>&lt;KeyPress&gt;</code>/<code>&lt;KeyRelease&gt;</code> in a set, and move in an <a href=\"#/a/tk.after\">after()</a> game loop.","aka":"key held down, smooth movement, keyrelease, multiple keys at once, game controls, key repeat delay","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">300</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">200</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;black&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\nship = c.create_rectangle(<i class=\"n\">140</i>, <i class=\"n\">90</i>, <i class=\"n\">160</i>, <i class=\"n\">110</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;orange&quot;</i>)\nheld = <i class=\"b\">set</i>()\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;KeyPress&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: held.add(e.keysym))\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;KeyRelease&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: held.discard(e.keysym))\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">tick</i>():\n    dx = ((<i class=\"s\">&quot;Right&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">in</i> held) - (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Left&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">in</i> held)) * <i class=\"n\">4</i>\n    dy = ((<i class=\"s\">&quot;Down&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">in</i> held) - (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Up&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">in</i> held)) * <i class=\"n\">4</i>\n    c.move(ship, dx, dy)\n    root.after(<i class=\"n\">16</i>, tick)          <i class=\"c\"># about 60 times a second</i>\n\ntick()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.game-loop"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 300 , height= 200 , bg= \"black\" ) c.pack() ship = c.create_rectangle( 140 , 90 , 160 , 110 , fill= \"orange\" ) held = set () root.bind( \"<KeyPress>\" , lambda e: held.add(e.keysym)) root.bind( \"<KeyRelease>\" , lambda e: held.discard(e.keysym)) def tick (): dx = (( \"Right\" in held) - ( \"Left\" in held)) * 4 dy = (( \"Down\" in held) - ( \"Up\" in held)) * 4 c.move(ship, dx, dy) root.after( 16 , tick) # about 60 times a second tick() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.shortcuts","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I add keyboard shortcuts like Ctrl+S?","qh":"How do I add keyboard shortcuts like Ctrl+S?","a":"<code>root.bind(&quot;&lt;Control-s&gt;&quot;, save)</code>. Shift is a different key: <code>&quot;&lt;Control-S&gt;&quot;</code> (capital) is Ctrl+Shift+S. On macOS use <code>&quot;&lt;Command-s&gt;&quot;</code>.","aka":"keyboard shortcut, hotkey, ctrl s, control key binding, command key mac, accelerator, ctrl+c ctrl+v","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> sys\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nstatus = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Press Ctrl+S or Ctrl+O&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nstatus.pack()\nmod = <i class=\"s\">&quot;Command&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> sys.platform == <i class=\"s\">&quot;darwin&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;Control&quot;</i>\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">save</i>(event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    status.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Saved&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;break&quot;</i>          <i class=\"c\"># stop the key doing anything else</i>\n\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">&lt;</i>{mod}<i class=\"s\">-s&gt;</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>, save)\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">&lt;</i>{mod}<i class=\"s\">-o&gt;</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: status.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Open&quot;</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.menu-accelerator"],"t":"import tkinter as tk import sys root = tk.Tk() status = tk.Label(root, text= \"Press Ctrl+S or Ctrl+O\" , width= 30 , pady= 20 ) status.pack() mod = \"Command\" if sys.platform == \"darwin\" else \"Control\" def save (event= None ): status.config(text= \"Saved\" ) return \"break\" # stop the key doing anything else root.bind( f\" < {mod} -s> \" , save) root.bind( f\" < {mod} -o> \" , lambda e: status.config(text= \"Open\" )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.mouse","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I detect mouse clicks, right-clicks and mouse position?","qh":"How do I detect mouse clicks, right-clicks and mouse position?","a":"<code>&lt;Button-1&gt;</code> left, <code>&lt;Button-2&gt;</code>/<code>&lt;Button-3&gt;</code> middle/right (on macOS right-click is <code>&lt;Button-2&gt;</code>), <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code> when moving. <code>event.x, event.y</code> are relative to the widget; <code>event.x_root, event.y_root</code> to the screen.","aka":"mouse click event, right click, middle click, mouse position, mouse coordinates, Button-1, Button-3, cursor position","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> sys\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">300</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">200</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\npos = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\npos.pack()\nright = <i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-2&gt;&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> sys.platform == <i class=\"s\">&quot;darwin&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-3&gt;&quot;</i>\n\nc.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Motion&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: pos.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">x=</i>{e.x}<i class=\"s\"> y=</i>{e.y}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>))\nc.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: c.create_oval(e.x-<i class=\"n\">4</i>, e.y-<i class=\"n\">4</i>, e.x+<i class=\"n\">4</i>, e.y+<i class=\"n\">4</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;blue&quot;</i>))\nc.bind(right, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: c.create_oval(e.x-<i class=\"n\">4</i>, e.y-<i class=\"n\">4</i>, e.x+<i class=\"n\">4</i>, e.y+<i class=\"n\">4</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>To get the mouse position at any moment: <code>root.winfo_pointerxy()</code> (screen coordinates).</p>","see":["tk.drag","tk.right-click-menu"],"t":"import tkinter as tk import sys root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 300 , height= 200 , bg= \"white\" ) c.pack() pos = tk.Label(root, text= \"\" ) pos.pack() right = \"<Button-2>\" if sys.platform == \"darwin\" else \"<Button-3>\" c.bind( \"<Motion>\" , lambda e: pos.config(text= f\" x= {e.x} y= {e.y} \" )) c.bind( \"<Button-1>\" , lambda e: c.create_oval(e.x- 4 , e.y- 4 , e.x+ 4 , e.y+ 4 , fill= \"blue\" )) c.bind(right, lambda e: c.create_oval(e.x- 4 , e.y- 4 , e.x+ 4 , e.y+ 4 , fill= \"red\" )) root.mainloop() To get the mouse position at any moment: root.winfo_pointerxy() (screen coordinates)."},{"id":"tk.double-click","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I detect a double-click?","qh":"How do I detect a double-click?","a":"Bind <code>&quot;&lt;Double-Button-1&gt;&quot;</code>. Note the single-click handler also runs on the first click.","aka":"double click event, Double-Button-1, double tap","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Double-click me&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#eee&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nlab.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nlab.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Double-Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Double!&quot;</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"Double-click me\" , bg= \"#eee\" , padx= 30 , pady= 30 ) lab.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 20 ) lab.bind( \"<Double-Button-1>\" , lambda e: lab.config(text= \"Double!\" )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.drag","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I drag something with the mouse?","qh":"How do I drag something with the mouse?","a":"On <code>&lt;Button-1&gt;</code> remember the start point; on <code>&lt;B1-Motion&gt;</code> move by the difference and update the start point.","aka":"drag and drop, drag widget, drag item canvas, move with mouse, B1-Motion, click and drag","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;300x200&quot;</i>)\ncard = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;drag me&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\ncard.place(x=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, y=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">start</i>(e):\n    card._x, card._y = e.x, e.y\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">move</i>(e):\n    card.place(x=card.winfo_x() + e.x - card._x, y=card.winfo_y() + e.y - card._y)\n\ncard.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, start)\ncard.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;B1-Motion&gt;&quot;</i>, move)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Dragging <b>files from your desktop</b> into tkinter isn't built in; the <code>tkinterdnd2</code> package adds it.</p>","see":["tk.canvas-drag","tk.drag-window"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"300x200\" ) card = tk.Label(root, text= \"drag me\" , bg= \"#fc6\" , padx= 10 , pady= 6 ) card.place(x= 20 , y= 20 ) def start (e): card._x, card._y = e.x, e.y def move (e): card.place(x=card.winfo_x() + e.x - card._x, y=card.winfo_y() + e.y - card._y) card.bind( \"<Button-1>\" , start) card.bind( \"<B1-Motion>\" , move) root.mainloop() Dragging files from your desktop into tkinter isn't built in; the tkinterdnd2 package adds it."},{"id":"tk.mousewheel","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I use the mouse wheel / scroll wheel?","qh":"How do I use the mouse wheel / scroll wheel?","a":"Bind <code>&lt;MouseWheel&gt;</code> and look at <code>event.delta</code> (positive = up). Older Tk on Linux sends <code>&lt;Button-4&gt;</code> (up) and <code>&lt;Button-5&gt;</code> (down) instead, so bind those too.","aka":"mouse wheel event, scroll wheel, MouseWheel, Button-4 Button-5, scroll linux, zoom with wheel","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nn = tk.IntVar(value=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\ntk.Label(root, textvariable=n, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">30</i>), width=<i class=\"n\">6</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">wheel</i>(event):\n    up = event.num == <i class=\"n\">4</i> <i class=\"k\">or</i> <i class=\"b\">getattr</i>(event, <i class=\"s\">&quot;delta&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>) &gt; <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n    n.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(n.get() + (<i class=\"n\">1</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> up <i class=\"k\">else</i> -<i class=\"n\">1</i>))\n\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;MouseWheel&gt;&quot;</i>, wheel)     <i class=\"c\"># Windows, macOS, Tk 9 on Linux</i>\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-4&gt;&quot;</i>, wheel)       <i class=\"c\"># Linux, Tk 8.6</i>\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-5&gt;&quot;</i>, wheel)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Windows gives <code>delta</code> in steps of 120; macOS gives small numbers like 1 or 3.</p>","see":["tk.scroll-frame"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() n = tk.IntVar(value= 0 ) tk.Label(root, textvariable=n, font=( \"Arial\" , 30 ), width= 6 ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 20 ) def wheel (event): up = event.num == 4 or getattr (event, \"delta\" , 0 ) > 0 n. set (n.get() + ( 1 if up else - 1 )) root.bind( \"<MouseWheel>\" , wheel) # Windows, macOS, Tk 9 on Linux root.bind( \"<Button-4>\" , wheel) # Linux, Tk 8.6 root.bind( \"<Button-5>\" , wheel) root.mainloop() Windows gives delta in steps of 120; macOS gives small numbers like 1 or 3."},{"id":"tk.event-names","s":"tk-events","q":"What event names can I bind?","qh":"What event names can I bind?","a":"Common ones below. Format: <code>&lt;Modifier-Type-Detail&gt;</code>, e.g. <code>&lt;Control-Shift-Key-a&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;Double-Button-1&gt;</code>.","aka":"list of events, event types, bind strings, event sequence, keysym names, event syntax","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code out\" data-tag=\"output\"><pre>&lt;Button-1&gt; &lt;Button-3&gt;        mouse button pressed (1 left, 2 middle, 3 right; mac right = 2)\n&lt;ButtonRelease-1&gt;            mouse button released\n&lt;Double-Button-1&gt;            double-click\n&lt;B1-Motion&gt;                  mouse moved with left button held (dragging)\n&lt;Motion&gt;                     mouse moved\n&lt;Enter&gt; &lt;Leave&gt;              mouse moved into / out of the widget\n&lt;MouseWheel&gt;                 scroll wheel (Linux Tk 8.6: &lt;Button-4&gt;/&lt;Button-5&gt;)\n&lt;Key&gt; &lt;KeyPress&gt;             any key;  &lt;KeyRelease&gt; any key released\n&lt;Return&gt; &lt;Escape&gt; &lt;Tab&gt; &lt;BackSpace&gt; &lt;Delete&gt; &lt;space&gt;\n&lt;Up&gt; &lt;Down&gt; &lt;Left&gt; &lt;Right&gt; &lt;Home&gt; &lt;End&gt; &lt;Prior&gt;(PageUp) &lt;Next&gt;(PageDown)\n&lt;F1&gt; ... &lt;F12&gt;\na   A   1                    a single character (A means Shift+a)\n&lt;Control-s&gt; &lt;Alt-x&gt; &lt;Shift-Up&gt; &lt;Command-s&gt;(mac)\n&lt;FocusIn&gt; &lt;FocusOut&gt;         widget gained / lost keyboard focus\n&lt;Configure&gt;                  widget resized or moved (event.width, event.height)\n&lt;Destroy&gt;                    widget destroyed\n&lt;Map&gt; &lt;Unmap&gt;                shown / hidden\n&lt;&lt;ComboboxSelected&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;ListboxSelect&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;TreeviewSelect&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;NotebookTabChanged&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;Modified&gt;&gt;</pre></figure><p>To find the name of any key, bind <code>&lt;Key&gt;</code> and print <code>event.keysym</code>.</p>","see":["tk.bind"],"t":"<Button-1> <Button-3> mouse button pressed (1 left, 2 middle, 3 right; mac right = 2) <ButtonRelease-1> mouse button released <Double-Button-1> double-click <B1-Motion> mouse moved with left button held (dragging) <Motion> mouse moved <Enter> <Leave> mouse moved into / out of the widget <MouseWheel> scroll wheel (Linux Tk 8.6: <Button-4>/<Button-5>) <Key> <KeyPress> any key; <KeyRelease> any key released <Return> <Escape> <Tab> <BackSpace> <Delete> <space> <Up> <Down> <Left> <Right> <Home> <End> <Prior>(PageUp) <Next>(PageDown) <F1> ... <F12> a A 1 a single character (A means Shift+a) <Control-s> <Alt-x> <Shift-Up> <Command-s>(mac) <FocusIn> <FocusOut> widget gained / lost keyboard focus <Configure> widget resized or moved (event.width, event.height) <Destroy> widget destroyed <Map> <Unmap> shown / hidden <<ComboboxSelected>> <<ListboxSelect>> <<TreeviewSelect>> <<NotebookTabChanged>> <<Modified>> To find the name of any key, bind <Key> and print event.keysym ."},{"id":"tk.event-attrs","s":"tk-events","q":"What information does the event object have?","qh":"What information does the event object have?","a":"The useful fields: <code>widget</code>, <code>x</code>, <code>y</code>, <code>x_root</code>, <code>y_root</code>, <code>keysym</code>, <code>char</code>, <code>keycode</code>, <code>num</code> (mouse button), <code>delta</code> (wheel), <code>width</code>, <code>height</code> (Configure), <code>state</code> (modifier keys).","aka":"event object, event attributes, event.widget, event.keysym, event.char, event.x","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;press a key or click&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nlab.pack()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">show</i>(e):\n    lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">widget=</i>{e.widget.winfo_class()}<i class=\"s\"> keysym=</i>{e.keysym}<i class=\"s\"> char=</i>{e.char!r}<i class=\"s\">\\nx=</i>{e.x}<i class=\"s\"> y=</i>{e.y}<i class=\"s\"> num=</i>{e.num}<i class=\"s\"> state=</i>{e.state}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Key&gt;&quot;</i>, show)\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button&gt;&quot;</i>, show)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>event.widget</code> is the widget it happened on: handy when one function handles many widgets.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"press a key or click\" , width= 40 , height= 4 ) lab.pack() def show (e): lab.config(text= f\" widget= {e.widget.winfo_class()} keysym= {e.keysym} char= {e.char!r} \\nx= {e.x} y= {e.y} num= {e.num} state= {e.state} \" ) root.bind( \"<Key>\" , show) root.bind( \"<Button>\" , show) root.mainloop() event.widget is the widget it happened on: handy when one function handles many widgets."},{"id":"tk.return-break","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I stop a key doing its normal thing (e.g. stop Enter adding a new line in a Text)?","qh":"How do I stop a key doing its normal thing (e.g. stop Enter adding a new line in a Text)?","a":"Return the string <code>&quot;break&quot;</code> from your handler. Tk then skips the widget's built-in behaviour for that event.","aka":"return break, prevent default, block key, stop newline text widget, override key behaviour","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nt = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nt.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">send</i>(event):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Sent:&quot;</i>, t.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end-1c&quot;</i>))\n    t.delete(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;break&quot;</i>           <i class=\"c\"># no newline gets inserted</i>\n\nt.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Return&gt;&quot;</i>, send)\nt.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Shift-Return&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"k\">None</i>)   <i class=\"c\"># Shift+Enter still makes a new line</i>\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() t = tk.Text(root, width= 30 , height= 5 ) t.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) def send (event): print ( \"Sent:\" , t.get( \"1.0\" , \"end-1c\" )) t.delete( \"1.0\" , \"end\" ) return \"break\" # no newline gets inserted t.bind( \"<Return>\" , send) t.bind( \"<Shift-Return>\" , lambda e: None ) # Shift+Enter still makes a new line root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.unbind","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I remove a binding (unbind)?","qh":"How do I remove a binding (unbind)?","a":"<code>widget.unbind(&quot;&lt;Event&gt;&quot;)</code>. To remove only one of several handlers, keep the id that <code>bind(..., add=&quot;+&quot;)</code> returns and pass it as the second argument.","aka":"unbind, remove event, stop listening to key, disable bind","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Click me once&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#eee&quot;</i>)\nlab.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">once</i>(e):\n    lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Clicked (now unbound)&quot;</i>)\n    lab.unbind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>)\n\nlab.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, once)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>bind</code> normally <b>replaces</b> the previous handler for that event; pass <code>add=&quot;+&quot;</code> to keep both.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"Click me once\" , padx= 30 , pady= 30 , bg= \"#eee\" ) lab.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 20 ) def once (e): lab.config(text= \"Clicked (now unbound)\" ) lab.unbind( \"<Button-1>\" ) lab.bind( \"<Button-1>\" , once) root.mainloop() bind normally replaces the previous handler for that event; pass add=\"+\" to keep both."},{"id":"tk.bind-all","s":"tk-events","q":"What's the difference between bind, bind_all and bind_class?","qh":"What's the difference between bind, bind_all and bind_class?","a":"<code>w.bind</code> is for one widget. <code>bind_all</code> fires in every widget of the app. <code>bind_class(&quot;Entry&quot;, ...)</code> affects every widget of that type.","aka":"bind_all, bind_class, global key binding, bind to every widget","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Entry(root).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Entry(root).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nroot.bind_all(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;F1&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Help (from anywhere)&quot;</i>))\nroot.bind_class(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Entry&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;FocusIn&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: e.widget.config(bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ffffe0&quot;</i>), add=<i class=\"s\">&quot;+&quot;</i>)\nroot.bind_class(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Entry&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;FocusOut&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: e.widget.config(bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>), add=<i class=\"s\">&quot;+&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Entry(root).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) tk.Entry(root).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) root.bind_all( \"<F1>\" , lambda e: print ( \"Help (from anywhere)\" )) root.bind_class( \"Entry\" , \"<FocusIn>\" , lambda e: e.widget.config(bg= \"#ffffe0\" ), add= \"+\" ) root.bind_class( \"Entry\" , \"<FocusOut>\" , lambda e: e.widget.config(bg= \"white\" ), add= \"+\" ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.focus","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I tell which widget has focus, or react when it gains/loses focus?","qh":"How do I tell which widget has focus, or react when it gains/loses focus?","a":"<code>root.focus_get()</code> returns the focused widget. Bind <code>&lt;FocusIn&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;FocusOut&gt;</code> to react.","aka":"focus_get, focus in, focus out, FocusIn, FocusOut, focused widget, on blur","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\na = tk.Entry(root); a.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nb = tk.Entry(root); b.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> e <i class=\"k\">in</i> (a, b):\n    e.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;FocusIn&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> ev: ev.widget.config(highlightthickness=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, highlightcolor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;orange&quot;</i>))\na.focus()\nroot.after(<i class=\"n\">100</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;focused:&quot;</i>, root.focus_get() <i class=\"k\">is</i> a))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() a = tk.Entry(root); a.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) b = tk.Entry(root); b.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) for e in (a, b): e.bind( \"<FocusIn>\" , lambda ev: ev.widget.config(highlightthickness= 2 , highlightcolor= \"orange\" )) a.focus() root.after( 100 , lambda : print ( \"focused:\" , root.focus_get() is a)) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.virtual-event","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I create my own event (virtual event) and trigger it?","qh":"How do I create my own event (virtual event) and trigger it?","a":"Bind a name in double angle brackets, <code>&lt;&lt;MyEvent&gt;&gt;</code>, and fire it with <code>widget.event_generate(&quot;&lt;&lt;MyEvent&gt;&gt;&quot;)</code>. <code>event_generate</code> also simulates real events.","aka":"event_generate, custom event, virtual event, trigger event programmatically, simulate key press","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;waiting&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nlab.pack()\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;&lt;Scored&gt;&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Somebody scored!&quot;</i>))\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Score&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: root.event_generate(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;&lt;Scored&gt;&gt;&quot;</i>)).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"waiting\" , padx= 30 , pady= 30 ) lab.pack() root.bind( \"<<Scored>>\" , lambda e: lab.config(text= \"Somebody scored!\" )) tk.Button(root, text= \"Score\" , command= lambda : root.event_generate( \"<<Scored>>\" )).pack(pady= 10 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.configure-event","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I run code when the window is resized?","qh":"How do I run code when the window is resized?","a":"Bind <code>&lt;Configure&gt;</code> on the window. It also fires when the window moves, and for child widgets, so check <code>event.widget</code>.","aka":"window resize event, Configure event, on resize, detect window size change","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;resize me&quot;</i>)\nlab.pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">resized</i>(e):\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> e.widget <i class=\"k\">is</i> root:\n        lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{e.width}<i class=\"s\"> x </i>{e.height}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Configure&gt;&quot;</i>, resized)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"resize me\" ) lab.pack(expand= True ) def resized (e): if e.widget is root: lab.config(text= f\" {e.width} x {e.height} \" ) root.bind( \"<Configure>\" , resized) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.stringvar","s":"tk-vars","q":"What is StringVar and when should I use it?","qh":"What is StringVar and when should I use it?","a":"A <code>StringVar</code> holds a value that widgets can watch. Link it with <code>textvariable=</code> (Label, Entry) or <code>variable=</code> (Checkbutton, Radiobutton, Scale); then <code>var.set(...)</code> updates every linked widget, and <code>var.get()</code> reads it.","aka":"StringVar, textvariable, tkinter variable, IntVar, DoubleVar, BooleanVar, control variable, tk variables","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nname = tk.StringVar(value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;world&quot;</i>)\ntk.Entry(root, textvariable=name).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Label(root, textvariable=name, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">16</i>)).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)     <i class=\"c\"># changes as you type</i>\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Reset&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: name.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;world&quot;</i>)).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>You don't <b>need</b> them; <code>widget.config(text=...)</code> and <code>entry.get()</code> do the same. They're handy when several widgets show one value.</p>","see":["tk.trace","tk.var-types"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() name = tk.StringVar(value= \"world\" ) tk.Entry(root, textvariable=name).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) tk.Label(root, textvariable=name, font=( \"Arial\" , 16 )).pack(pady= 5 ) # changes as you type tk.Button(root, text= \"Reset\" , command= lambda : name. set ( \"world\" )).pack(pady= 5 ) root.mainloop() You don't need them; widget.config(text=...) and entry.get() do the same. They're handy when several widgets show one value."},{"id":"tk.var-types","s":"tk-vars","q":"What's the difference between StringVar, IntVar, DoubleVar and BooleanVar?","qh":"What's the difference between StringVar, IntVar, DoubleVar and BooleanVar?","a":"They differ only in what <code>get()</code> returns: <code>str</code>, <code>int</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>bool</code>. If the linked Entry holds text that isn't a number, <code>IntVar.get()</code> raises <code>TclError</code>.","aka":"IntVar, DoubleVar, BooleanVar, var get type, intvar error, expected floating-point number","err":"TclError: expected floating-point number but got|expected integer but got","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nage = tk.IntVar(value=<i class=\"n\">12</i>)\ne = tk.Entry(root, textvariable=age)\ne.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">check</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Next year:&quot;</i>, age.get() + <i class=\"n\">1</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> tk.TclError:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;That&#x27;s not a whole number&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Check&quot;</i>, command=check).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>For user-typed numbers, a StringVar + your own <code>int()</code> in <code>try</code> is often simpler.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() age = tk.IntVar(value= 12 ) e = tk.Entry(root, textvariable=age) e.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) def check (): try : print ( \"Next year:\" , age.get() + 1 ) except tk.TclError: print ( \"That's not a whole number\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Check\" , command=check).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop() For user-typed numbers, a StringVar + your own int() in try is often simpler."},{"id":"tk.trace","s":"tk-vars","q":"How do I run a function whenever a variable changes (trace)?","qh":"How do I run a function whenever a variable changes (trace)?","a":"<code>var.trace_add(&quot;write&quot;, callback)</code>. The callback receives three arguments you can ignore with <code>*args</code>. (<code>trace(&quot;w&quot;, ...)</code> is the old, deprecated form.)","aka":"trace_add, trace variable, on change, watch variable, trace write, trace_variable deprecated","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nf = tk.DoubleVar(value=<i class=\"n\">32</i>)\nc = tk.StringVar()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">convert</i>(*args):\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        c.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{(f.get() - <i class=\"n\">32</i>) * <i class=\"n\">5</i> / <i class=\"n\">9</i>:<i class=\"s\">.1f</i>}<i class=\"s\"> °C</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> tk.TclError:\n        c.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;?&quot;</i>)\n\nf.trace_add(<i class=\"s\">&quot;write&quot;</i>, convert)\ntk.Scale(root, from_=-<i class=\"n\">40</i>, to=<i class=\"n\">212</i>, orient=<i class=\"s\">&quot;horizontal&quot;</i>, variable=f, length=<i class=\"n\">240</i>, label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;°F&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Label(root, textvariable=c, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">18</i>)).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nconvert()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.entry-changes"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() f = tk.DoubleVar(value= 32 ) c = tk.StringVar() def convert (*args): try : c. set ( f\" {(f.get() - 32 ) * 5 / 9 : .1f } °C \" ) except tk.TclError: c. set ( \"?\" ) f.trace_add( \"write\" , convert) tk.Scale(root, from_=- 40 , to= 212 , orient= \"horizontal\" , variable=f, length= 240 , label= \"°F\" ).pack(padx= 10 ) tk.Label(root, textvariable=c, font=( \"Arial\" , 18 )).pack(pady= 10 ) convert() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.var-gc","s":"tk-vars","q":"Why does my Checkbutton/Radiobutton/Label not show the variable's value (it looks empty or all selected)?","qh":"Why does my Checkbutton/Radiobutton/Label not show the variable's value (it looks empty or all selected)?","a":"The <code>StringVar</code> was created inside a function as a local variable and got deleted when the function ended. Store it somewhere that lives on: a global, <code>self.var</code> in a class, or a list/dict.","aka":"variable garbage collected, variable in function, radiobutton all selected, checkbutton not working in function, StringVar local variable","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">build</i>():\n    choice = tk.StringVar(value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>)      <i class=\"c\"># deleted when build() ends</i>\n    tk.Radiobutton(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;A&quot;</i>, variable=choice, value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>).pack()</pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">App</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, root):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.choice = tk.StringVar(value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>)     <i class=\"c\"># kept alive by self</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">for</i> v <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;abc&quot;</i>:\n            tk.Radiobutton(root, text=v.upper(), variable=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.choice, value=v).pack(anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>)\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\napp = App(root)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.err.image-gone"],"t":"def build (): choice = tk.StringVar(value= \"a\" ) # deleted when build() ends tk.Radiobutton(root, text= \"A\" , variable=choice, value= \"a\" ).pack() import tkinter as tk class App : def __init__ ( self , root): self .choice = tk.StringVar(value= \"a\" ) # kept alive by self for v in \"abc\" : tk.Radiobutton(root, text=v.upper(), variable= self .choice, value=v).pack(anchor= \"w\" ) root = tk.Tk() app = App(root) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.var-before-root","s":"tk-vars","q":"RuntimeError: Too early to create variable: no default root window","qh":"RuntimeError: Too early to create variable: no default root window","a":"You created a <code>StringVar</code>, <code>PhotoImage</code> or <code>Font</code> before <code>root = tk.Tk()</code>. Create the root window first.","aka":"too early to create variable, no default root window, StringVar before Tk, too early to create image, too early to use font","err":"Too early to create variable|no default root window|Too early to create image|Too early to use font","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\nname = tk.StringVar()     <i class=\"c\"># RuntimeError: no window yet</i>\nroot = tk.Tk()</pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nname = tk.StringVar(value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ok&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(name.get())\nroot.destroy()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk name = tk.StringVar() # RuntimeError: no window yet root = tk.Tk() import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() name = tk.StringVar(value= \"ok\" ) print (name.get()) root.destroy()"},{"id":"tk.global-vs-class","s":"tk-vars","q":"How do I share or change values between my functions (global, nonlocal, or a class)?","qh":"How do I share or change values between my functions (global, nonlocal, or a class)?","a":"For small scripts, declare <code>global score</code> in the function that changes it. For anything bigger, keep state on a class (<code>self.score</code>) or a dict; it scales better.","aka":"global variable tkinter, change variable in function, score variable, counter button, UnboundLocalError tkinter, share data between functions","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nscore = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;0&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">24</i>))\nlab.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">add</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> score            <i class=\"c\"># without this: UnboundLocalError</i>\n    score += <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n    lab.config(text=<i class=\"b\">str</i>(score))\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;+1&quot;</i>, command=add).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.app-class","py.global"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() score = 0 lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"0\" , font=( \"Arial\" , 24 )) lab.pack(padx= 40 , pady= 10 ) def add (): global score # without this: UnboundLocalError score += 1 lab.config(text= str (score)) tk.Button(root, text= \"+1\" , command=add).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.booleanvar","s":"tk-vars","q":"How do I use BooleanVar with a Checkbutton?","qh":"How do I use BooleanVar with a Checkbutton?","a":"<code>v = tk.BooleanVar()</code>, <code>Checkbutton(..., variable=v)</code>. <code>v.get()</code> gives <code>True</code>/<code>False</code>; <code>v.set(True)</code> ticks it from code.","aka":"booleanvar, checkbox true false, toggle variable","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ndark = tk.BooleanVar()\narea = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Preview&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">24</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\narea.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">apply</i>():\n    area.config(bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#222&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> dark.get() <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> dark.get() <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;black&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Checkbutton(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Dark mode&quot;</i>, variable=dark, command=apply).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.checkbutton"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() dark = tk.BooleanVar() area = tk.Label(root, text= \"Preview\" , width= 24 , height= 4 , bg= \"white\" ) area.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) def apply (): area.config(bg= \"#222\" if dark.get() else \"white\" , fg= \"white\" if dark.get() else \"black\" ) tk.Checkbutton(root, text= \"Dark mode\" , variable=dark, command=apply).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.after","s":"tk-time","q":"How do I run something after a delay, or repeat it every second?","qh":"How do I run something after a delay, or repeat it every second?","a":"<code>root.after(ms, function)</code> runs the function once after <code>ms</code> milliseconds. To repeat, the function schedules itself again at its end.","aka":"after, timer, delay, schedule, repeat every second, set interval, set timeout, loop in tkinter, run function every x seconds","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ncount = tk.IntVar(value=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\ntk.Label(root, textvariable=count, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">40</i>), width=<i class=\"n\">4</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">tick</i>():\n    count.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(count.get() + <i class=\"n\">1</i>)\n    root.after(<i class=\"n\">1000</i>, tick)        <i class=\"c\"># call me again in 1 second</i>\n\nroot.after(<i class=\"n\">1000</i>, tick)            <i class=\"c\"># first call</i>\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Pass arguments after the function: <code>root.after(500, move, 10, 0)</code> calls <code>move(10, 0)</code>.</p>","see":["tk.after-not-sleep","tk.after-cancel","tk.clock"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() count = tk.IntVar(value= 0 ) tk.Label(root, textvariable=count, font=( \"Arial\" , 40 ), width= 4 ).pack(padx= 30 , pady= 20 ) def tick (): count. set (count.get() + 1 ) root.after( 1000 , tick) # call me again in 1 second root.after( 1000 , tick) # first call root.mainloop() Pass arguments after the function: root.after(500, move, 10, 0) calls move(10, 0) ."},{"id":"tk.after-not-sleep","s":"tk-time","q":"Why does time.sleep() freeze my window (\"Not Responding\")?","qh":"Why does time.sleep() freeze my window (\"Not Responding\")?","a":"<code>sleep</code> (and long loops) block <code>mainloop</code>, so the window can't redraw or respond. Use <a href=\"#/a/tk.after\">after()</a> for delays and repeating steps, or a <a href=\"#/a/tk.thread\">thread</a> for long work.","aka":"time.sleep tkinter, window frozen, not responding, gui freezes, while loop freezes tkinter, sleep in tkinter, label doesn't update until end","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">countdown</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">5</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>, -<i class=\"n\">1</i>):\n        label.config(text=i)\n        time.sleep(<i class=\"n\">1</i>)          <i class=\"c\"># window freezes, shows only the end</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlabel = tk.Label(root, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">40</i>), width=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nlabel.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">countdown</i>(i=<i class=\"n\">5</i>):\n    label.config(text=<i class=\"b\">str</i>(i) <i class=\"k\">if</i> i <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;Go!&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> i &gt; <i class=\"n\">0</i>:\n        root.after(<i class=\"n\">1000</i>, countdown, i - <i class=\"n\">1</i>)\n\ncountdown()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.after","tk.thread","tk.update"],"t":"def countdown (): for i in range ( 5 , 0 , - 1 ): label.config(text=i) time.sleep( 1 ) # window freezes, shows only the end import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() label = tk.Label(root, font=( \"Arial\" , 40 ), width= 4 ) label.pack(padx= 30 , pady= 20 ) def countdown (i= 5 ): label.config(text= str (i) if i else \"Go!\" ) if i > 0 : root.after( 1000 , countdown, i - 1 ) countdown() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.after-cancel","s":"tk-time","q":"How do I stop an after() loop (cancel a timer)?","qh":"How do I stop an after() loop (cancel a timer)?","a":"<code>after()</code> returns an id. Keep it and call <code>root.after_cancel(id)</code>. Or use a flag the loop checks.","aka":"after_cancel, stop timer, stop loop, cancel after, pause timer, stop animation","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nn = tk.IntVar(value=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\njob = <i class=\"k\">None</i>\ntk.Label(root, textvariable=n, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">30</i>)).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">tick</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> job\n    n.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(n.get() + <i class=\"n\">1</i>)\n    job = root.after(<i class=\"n\">200</i>, tick)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">start</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> job <i class=\"k\">is</i> <i class=\"k\">None</i>:\n        tick()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">stop</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> job\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> job <i class=\"k\">is</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> <i class=\"k\">None</i>:\n        root.after_cancel(job)\n        job = <i class=\"k\">None</i>\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Start&quot;</i>, command=start).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Stop&quot;</i>, command=stop).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Pressing Start twice without the <code>if job is None</code> check starts two loops that run at double speed.</p>","see":["tk.recipe-stopwatch"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() n = tk.IntVar(value= 0 ) job = None tk.Label(root, textvariable=n, font=( \"Arial\" , 30 )).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 10 ) def tick (): global job n. set (n.get() + 1 ) job = root.after( 200 , tick) def start (): if job is None : tick() def stop (): global job if job is not None : root.after_cancel(job) job = None tk.Button(root, text= \"Start\" , command=start).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Stop\" , command=stop).pack(side= \"left\" , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() Pressing Start twice without the if job is None check starts two loops that run at double speed."},{"id":"tk.clock","s":"tk-time","q":"How do I show a live clock (current time)?","qh":"How do I show a live clock (current time)?","a":"Update a label with <code>time.strftime(&quot;%H:%M:%S&quot;)</code> every second using <code>after</code>.","aka":"digital clock, show time, current time label, live time, time display, date and time label","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> time\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Clock&quot;</i>)\nclock = tk.Label(root, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Courier&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">48</i>), bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;black&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ffb000&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nclock.pack()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">update</i>():\n    clock.config(text=time.strftime(<i class=\"s\">&quot;%H:%M:%S&quot;</i>))\n    root.after(<i class=\"n\">1000</i>, update)\n\nupdate()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Use <code>%I:%M %p</code> for 12-hour time with AM/PM, <code>%A %d %B %Y</code> for a date like \"Monday 05 June 2026\".</p>","see":["py.datetime-format"],"t":"import tkinter as tk import time root = tk.Tk() root.title( \"Clock\" ) clock = tk.Label(root, font=( \"Courier\" , 48 ), bg= \"black\" , fg= \"#ffb000\" , padx= 20 ) clock.pack() def update (): clock.config(text=time.strftime( \"%H:%M:%S\" )) root.after( 1000 , update) update() root.mainloop() Use %I:%M %p for 12-hour time with AM/PM, %A %d %B %Y for a date like \"Monday 05 June 2026\"."},{"id":"tk.game-loop","s":"tk-time","q":"How do I make a game loop / animation in tkinter?","qh":"How do I make a game loop / animation in tkinter?","a":"One function that updates positions, then calls <code>root.after(16, itself)</code> (about 60 frames/second). Move canvas items with <code>move()</code> or <code>coords()</code>.","aka":"game loop, animation loop, move object continuously, fps, frame rate, animate canvas, bouncing ball","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nW, H = <i class=\"n\">400</i>, <i class=\"n\">260</i>\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=W, height=H, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#111&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\nball = c.create_oval(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">20</i>, <i class=\"n\">20</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ffb000&quot;</i>)\nvx, vy = <i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">frame</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> vx, vy\n    c.move(ball, vx, vy)\n    x1, y1, x2, y2 = c.coords(ball)\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> x1 &lt;= <i class=\"n\">0</i> <i class=\"k\">or</i> x2 &gt;= W:\n        vx = -vx\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> y1 &lt;= <i class=\"n\">0</i> <i class=\"k\">or</i> y2 &gt;= H:\n        vy = -vy\n    root.after(<i class=\"n\">16</i>, frame)\n\nframe()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.canvas-move","tk.key-held","tk.recipe-snake"],"t":"import tkinter as tk W, H = 400 , 260 root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width=W, height=H, bg= \"#111\" ) c.pack() ball = c.create_oval( 0 , 0 , 20 , 20 , fill= \"#ffb000\" ) vx, vy = 4 , 3 def frame (): global vx, vy c.move(ball, vx, vy) x1, y1, x2, y2 = c.coords(ball) if x1 <= 0 or x2 >= W: vx = -vx if y1 <= 0 or y2 >= H: vy = -vy root.after( 16 , frame) frame() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.update","s":"tk-time","q":"What do update() and update_idletasks() do? Should I use them?","qh":"What do update() and update_idletasks() do? Should I use them?","a":"<code>update_idletasks()</code> redraws pending changes now (safe). <code>update()</code> also handles clicks and keys, which can re-enter your code in confusing ways. Prefer <code>after()</code> over loops with <code>update()</code>.","aka":"update, update_idletasks, refresh window, force redraw, root.update loop","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nlab.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">slow_work</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> step <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">6</i>):\n        lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Step </i>{step}<i class=\"s\"> of 5</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n        lab.update_idletasks()        <i class=\"c\"># show the text now</i>\n        <i class=\"b\">sum</i>(<i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">300000</i>))            <i class=\"c\"># pretend work</i>\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Run&quot;</i>, command=slow_work).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.after(<i class=\"n\">100</i>, slow_work)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>The window still can't be moved or clicked during the loop. For real work use a <a href=\"#/a/tk.thread\">thread</a>.</p>","see":["tk.after-not-sleep"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"\" , width= 30 ) lab.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 20 ) def slow_work (): for step in range ( 1 , 6 ): lab.config(text= f\" Step {step} of 5 \" ) lab.update_idletasks() # show the text now sum ( range ( 300000 )) # pretend work tk.Button(root, text= \"Run\" , command=slow_work).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.after( 100 , slow_work) root.mainloop() The window still can't be moved or clicked during the loop. For real work use a thread ."},{"id":"tk.thread","s":"tk-time","q":"How do I run a long task (download, calculation) without freezing the window?","qh":"How do I run a long task (download, calculation) without freezing the window?","a":"Run the work in a <code>threading.Thread</code>. <b>Don't touch widgets from that thread.</b> Send results back through a <code>queue.Queue</code> and read it with <code>after()</code> in the main thread.","aka":"threading tkinter, background task, long running task, freeze during download, worker thread, non blocking, progress while working","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> threading, queue, time\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nstatus = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Idle&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nstatus.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nq = queue.Queue()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">work</i>():                       <i class=\"c\"># runs in the background thread</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>):\n        time.sleep(<i class=\"n\">0.05</i>)          <i class=\"c\"># pretend slow work</i>\n        q.put(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Step </i>{i}<i class=\"s\"> done</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n    q.put(<i class=\"s\">&quot;DONE&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">poll</i>():                       <i class=\"c\"># runs in the main thread</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        <i class=\"k\">while</i> <i class=\"k\">True</i>:\n            msg = q.get_nowait()\n            status.config(text=msg)\n            <i class=\"k\">if</i> msg == <i class=\"s\">&quot;DONE&quot;</i>:\n                btn.config(state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;normal&quot;</i>)\n                <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> queue.Empty:\n        <i class=\"k\">pass</i>\n    root.after(<i class=\"n\">50</i>, poll)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">start</i>():\n    btn.config(state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;disabled&quot;</i>)\n    threading.Thread(target=work, daemon=<i class=\"k\">True</i>).start()\n    poll()\n\nbtn = tk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Start&quot;</i>, command=start)\nbtn.pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nstart()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>daemon=True</code> lets the program quit even if the thread is still running.</p>","see":["tk.err.main-thread","tk.progressbar"],"t":"import tkinter as tk import threading, queue, time root = tk.Tk() status = tk.Label(root, text= \"Idle\" , width= 30 ) status.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) q = queue.Queue() def work (): # runs in the background thread for i in range ( 1 , 4 ): time.sleep( 0.05 ) # pretend slow work q.put( f\" Step {i} done \" ) q.put( \"DONE\" ) def poll (): # runs in the main thread try : while True : msg = q.get_nowait() status.config(text=msg) if msg == \"DONE\" : btn.config(state= \"normal\" ) return except queue.Empty: pass root.after( 50 , poll) def start (): btn.config(state= \"disabled\" ) threading.Thread(target=work, daemon= True ).start() poll() btn = tk.Button(root, text= \"Start\" , command=start) btn.pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) start() root.mainloop() daemon=True lets the program quit even if the thread is still running."},{"id":"tk.err.main-thread","s":"tk-time","q":"RuntimeError: main thread is not in main loop","qh":"RuntimeError: main thread is not in main loop","a":"A background thread touched a widget (or tkinter object) directly. Only the main thread may use tkinter. Pass data back with a queue and <code>after()</code>, as in <a href=\"#/a/tk.thread\">How do I run a long task (download, calculation) without freezing the window?</a>.","aka":"main thread is not in main loop, tkinter thread error, Tcl_AsyncDelete, calling tkinter from thread, update gui from thread","err":"main thread is not in main loop|Tcl_AsyncDelete: async handler deleted by the wrong thread|Calling Tcl from different apartment","h":"<p>A quick alternative for small updates is <code>root.after(0, func)</code> from the thread: it schedules <code>func</code> on the main loop. This usually works in practice, but a queue is the safe way.</p>","see":["tk.thread"],"t":"A quick alternative for small updates is root.after(0, func) from the thread: it schedules func on the main loop. This usually works in practice, but a queue is the safe way."},{"id":"tk.after-idle","s":"tk-time","q":"How do I run code right after the window has appeared?","qh":"How do I run code right after the window has appeared?","a":"<code>root.after(0, func)</code> or <code>root.after_idle(func)</code> runs once <code>mainloop()</code> has started and the window is drawn.","aka":"after_idle, run after window shows, on startup, after window loaded, after mainloop starts","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;loading...&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nlab.pack()\nroot.after(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Window is </i>{root.winfo_width()}<i class=\"s\"> px wide</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"loading...\" , padx= 40 , pady= 30 ) lab.pack() root.after( 0 , lambda : lab.config(text= f\" Window is {root.winfo_width()} px wide \" )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.blink","s":"tk-time","q":"How do I make text blink or flash?","qh":"How do I make text blink or flash?","a":"Toggle a colour with <code>after()</code>.","aka":"blink label, flashing text, flash color, alternate colors","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ALERT&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">24</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;bold&quot;</i>), fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nlab.pack()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">blink</i>(on=<i class=\"k\">True</i>):\n    lab.config(fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> on <i class=\"k\">else</i> root[<i class=\"s\">&quot;bg&quot;</i>])\n    root.after(<i class=\"n\">400</i>, blink, <i class=\"k\">not</i> on)\n\nblink()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"ALERT\" , font=( \"Arial\" , 24 , \"bold\" ), fg= \"red\" , padx= 30 , pady= 20 ) lab.pack() def blink (on= True ): lab.config(fg= \"red\" if on else root[ \"bg\" ]) root.after( 400 , blink, not on) blink() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.menubar","s":"tk-menus","q":"How do I add a menu bar (File, Edit, Help)?","qh":"How do I add a menu bar (File, Edit, Help)?","a":"Create a <code>tk.Menu</code>, add sub-menus with <code>add_cascade</code>, and attach it with <code>root.config(menu=menubar)</code>.","aka":"menu bar, file menu, top menu, menubar, add_cascade, add_command, dropdown menu at top","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;320x180&quot;</i>)\nmenubar = tk.Menu(root)\n\nfile_menu = tk.Menu(menubar, tearoff=<i class=\"k\">False</i>)\nfile_menu.add_command(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;New&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;new&quot;</i>))\nfile_menu.add_command(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Open...&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;open&quot;</i>))\nfile_menu.add_separator()\nfile_menu.add_command(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Exit&quot;</i>, command=root.destroy)\nmenubar.add_cascade(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;File&quot;</i>, menu=file_menu)\n\nhelp_menu = tk.Menu(menubar, tearoff=<i class=\"k\">False</i>)\nhelp_menu.add_command(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;About&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;about&quot;</i>))\nmenubar.add_cascade(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Help&quot;</i>, menu=help_menu)\n\nroot.config(menu=menubar)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>tearoff=False</code> removes the dashed line at the top of each menu. On macOS the menu appears in the screen's top bar, not in the window.</p>","see":["tk.menu-accelerator","tk.right-click-menu","tk.menu-check"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"320x180\" ) menubar = tk.Menu(root) file_menu = tk.Menu(menubar, tearoff= False ) file_menu.add_command(label= \"New\" , command= lambda : print ( \"new\" )) file_menu.add_command(label= \"Open...\" , command= lambda : print ( \"open\" )) file_menu.add_separator() file_menu.add_command(label= \"Exit\" , command=root.destroy) menubar.add_cascade(label= \"File\" , menu=file_menu) help_menu = tk.Menu(menubar, tearoff= False ) help_menu.add_command(label= \"About\" , command= lambda : print ( \"about\" )) menubar.add_cascade(label= \"Help\" , menu=help_menu) root.config(menu=menubar) root.mainloop() tearoff=False removes the dashed line at the top of each menu. On macOS the menu appears in the screen's top bar, not in the window."},{"id":"tk.menu-accelerator","s":"tk-menus","q":"How do I show keyboard shortcuts in a menu (and make them work)?","qh":"How do I show keyboard shortcuts in a menu (and make them work)?","a":"<code>accelerator=&quot;Ctrl+S&quot;</code> only <b>displays</b> the text. You must also <code>bind</code> the key yourself.","aka":"menu shortcut, accelerator, ctrl+s in menu, menu hotkey, underline letter menu","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">save</i>(event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;saved&quot;</i>)\n\nmenubar = tk.Menu(root)\nfm = tk.Menu(menubar, tearoff=<i class=\"k\">False</i>)\nfm.add_command(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Save&quot;</i>, accelerator=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ctrl+S&quot;</i>, command=save, underline=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\nmenubar.add_cascade(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;File&quot;</i>, menu=fm, underline=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)     <i class=\"c\"># Alt+F opens it on Windows</i>\nroot.config(menu=menubar)\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Control-s&gt;&quot;</i>, save)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.shortcuts"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() def save (event= None ): print ( \"saved\" ) menubar = tk.Menu(root) fm = tk.Menu(menubar, tearoff= False ) fm.add_command(label= \"Save\" , accelerator= \"Ctrl+S\" , command=save, underline= 0 ) menubar.add_cascade(label= \"File\" , menu=fm, underline= 0 ) # Alt+F opens it on Windows root.config(menu=menubar) root.bind( \"<Control-s>\" , save) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.submenu","s":"tk-menus","q":"How do I make a sub-menu inside a menu?","qh":"How do I make a sub-menu inside a menu?","a":"Make another <code>tk.Menu</code> whose parent is the menu, and <code>add_cascade</code> it there.","aka":"nested menu, submenu, cascade inside menu, menu inside menu, recent files menu","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nbar = tk.Menu(root)\nfile_menu = tk.Menu(bar, tearoff=<i class=\"k\">False</i>)\nrecent = tk.Menu(file_menu, tearoff=<i class=\"k\">False</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> f <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;notes.txt&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;todo.txt&quot;</i>):\n    recent.add_command(label=f, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i> f=f: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;open&quot;</i>, f))\nfile_menu.add_cascade(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Open recent&quot;</i>, menu=recent)\nbar.add_cascade(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;File&quot;</i>, menu=file_menu)\nroot.config(menu=bar)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() bar = tk.Menu(root) file_menu = tk.Menu(bar, tearoff= False ) recent = tk.Menu(file_menu, tearoff= False ) for f in ( \"notes.txt\" , \"todo.txt\" ): recent.add_command(label=f, command= lambda f=f: print ( \"open\" , f)) file_menu.add_cascade(label= \"Open recent\" , menu=recent) bar.add_cascade(label= \"File\" , menu=file_menu) root.config(menu=bar) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.menu-check","s":"tk-menus","q":"How do I add checkmarks or radio choices to a menu?","qh":"How do I add checkmarks or radio choices to a menu?","a":"<code>add_checkbutton(label=..., variable=bool_var)</code> and <code>add_radiobutton(label=..., variable=var, value=...)</code>.","aka":"checkbutton menu, add_checkbutton, add_radiobutton, menu toggle, view menu options","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nwrap = tk.BooleanVar(value=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nsize = tk.StringVar(value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;M&quot;</i>)\nbar = tk.Menu(root)\nview = tk.Menu(bar, tearoff=<i class=\"k\">False</i>)\nview.add_checkbutton(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Word wrap&quot;</i>, variable=wrap, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;wrap&quot;</i>, wrap.get()))\nview.add_separator()\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> s <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;SML&quot;</i>:\n    view.add_radiobutton(label=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Size </i>{s}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>, variable=size, value=s)\nbar.add_cascade(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;View&quot;</i>, menu=view)\nroot.config(menu=bar)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() wrap = tk.BooleanVar(value= True ) size = tk.StringVar(value= \"M\" ) bar = tk.Menu(root) view = tk.Menu(bar, tearoff= False ) view.add_checkbutton(label= \"Word wrap\" , variable=wrap, command= lambda : print ( \"wrap\" , wrap.get())) view.add_separator() for s in \"SML\" : view.add_radiobutton(label= f\" Size {s} \" , variable=size, value=s) bar.add_cascade(label= \"View\" , menu=view) root.config(menu=bar) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.menu-disable","s":"tk-menus","q":"How do I disable or change a menu item?","qh":"How do I disable or change a menu item?","a":"<code>menu.entryconfig(&quot;Label text&quot;, state=&quot;disabled&quot;)</code> (or use its index). Change the label the same way: <code>entryconfig(0, label=&quot;New name&quot;)</code>.","aka":"grey out menu item, entryconfig, disable menu option, rename menu item","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nbar = tk.Menu(root)\nedit = tk.Menu(bar, tearoff=<i class=\"k\">False</i>)\nedit.add_command(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Undo&quot;</i>)\nedit.add_command(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Paste&quot;</i>)\nedit.entryconfig(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Undo&quot;</i>, state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;disabled&quot;</i>)\nedit.entryconfig(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Paste here&quot;</i>)\nbar.add_cascade(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Edit&quot;</i>, menu=edit)\nroot.config(menu=bar)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() bar = tk.Menu(root) edit = tk.Menu(bar, tearoff= False ) edit.add_command(label= \"Undo\" ) edit.add_command(label= \"Paste\" ) edit.entryconfig( \"Undo\" , state= \"disabled\" ) edit.entryconfig( 1 , label= \"Paste here\" ) bar.add_cascade(label= \"Edit\" , menu=edit) root.config(menu=bar) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.right-click-menu","s":"tk-menus","q":"How do I make a right-click (context) menu?","qh":"How do I make a right-click (context) menu?","a":"Make a <code>tk.Menu</code> and show it with <code>menu.tk_popup(event.x_root, event.y_root)</code> in a right-click handler.","aka":"context menu, popup menu, right click menu, tk_popup, post menu","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> sys\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nt = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nt.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nmenu = tk.Menu(root, tearoff=<i class=\"k\">False</i>)\nmenu.add_command(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Cut&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: t.event_generate(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;&lt;Cut&gt;&gt;&quot;</i>))\nmenu.add_command(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Copy&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: t.event_generate(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;&lt;Copy&gt;&gt;&quot;</i>))\nmenu.add_command(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Paste&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: t.event_generate(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;&lt;Paste&gt;&gt;&quot;</i>))\nmenu.add_separator()\nmenu.add_command(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Select all&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: t.tag_add(<i class=\"s\">&quot;sel&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>))\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">popup</i>(event):\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        menu.tk_popup(event.x_root, event.y_root)\n    <i class=\"k\">finally</i>:\n        menu.grab_release()\n\nt.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-2&gt;&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> sys.platform == <i class=\"s\">&quot;darwin&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-3&gt;&quot;</i>, popup)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.mouse"],"t":"import tkinter as tk import sys root = tk.Tk() t = tk.Text(root, width= 40 , height= 6 ) t.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) menu = tk.Menu(root, tearoff= False ) menu.add_command(label= \"Cut\" , command= lambda : t.event_generate( \"<<Cut>>\" )) menu.add_command(label= \"Copy\" , command= lambda : t.event_generate( \"<<Copy>>\" )) menu.add_command(label= \"Paste\" , command= lambda : t.event_generate( \"<<Paste>>\" )) menu.add_separator() menu.add_command(label= \"Select all\" , command= lambda : t.tag_add( \"sel\" , \"1.0\" , \"end\" )) def popup (event): try : menu.tk_popup(event.x_root, event.y_root) finally : menu.grab_release() t.bind( \"<Button-2>\" if sys.platform == \"darwin\" else \"<Button-3>\" , popup) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.messagebox","s":"tk-menus","q":"How do I show a popup message (info, warning, error)?","qh":"How do I show a popup message (info, warning, error)?","a":"<code>from tkinter import messagebox</code>, then <code>messagebox.showinfo(&quot;Title&quot;, &quot;Message&quot;)</code>. Also <code>showwarning</code> and <code>showerror</code>.","aka":"messagebox, alert, popup message, showinfo, showerror, showwarning, message box, dialog box","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> messagebox\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Info&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: messagebox.showinfo(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Saved&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Your file was saved.&quot;</i>)).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Warning&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: messagebox.showwarning(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Careful&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Low battery&quot;</i>)).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Error&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: messagebox.showerror(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Oops&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Something went wrong&quot;</i>)).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>If you get <code>module &#x27;tkinter&#x27; has no attribute &#x27;messagebox&#x27;</code>, add <code>from tkinter import messagebox</code>. It is a separate module.</p>","see":["tk.messagebox-ask"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import messagebox root = tk.Tk() tk.Button(root, text= \"Info\" , command= lambda : messagebox.showinfo( \"Saved\" , \"Your file was saved.\" )).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 4 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Warning\" , command= lambda : messagebox.showwarning( \"Careful\" , \"Low battery\" )).pack(pady= 4 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Error\" , command= lambda : messagebox.showerror( \"Oops\" , \"Something went wrong\" )).pack(pady= 4 ) root.mainloop() If you get module 'tkinter' has no attribute 'messagebox' , add from tkinter import messagebox . It is a separate module."},{"id":"tk.messagebox-ask","s":"tk-menus","q":"How do I ask a yes/no question in a popup?","qh":"How do I ask a yes/no question in a popup?","a":"<code>messagebox.askyesno(&quot;Title&quot;, &quot;Question?&quot;)</code> returns <code>True</code> or <code>False</code>. <code>askyesnocancel</code> returns <code>True</code>/<code>False</code>/<code>None</code>.","aka":"askyesno, yes no dialog, confirm dialog, askokcancel, askquestion, askyesnocancel, are you sure","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> messagebox\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">delete</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> messagebox.askyesno(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Delete&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Delete all items?&quot;</i>):\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Deleted&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">else</i>:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Kept&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Delete everything&quot;</i>, command=delete).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Return values: <code>askokcancel</code>/<code>askyesno</code>/<code>askretrycancel</code> → <code>True</code>/<code>False</code>; <code>askquestion</code> → the strings <code>&quot;yes&quot;</code>/<code>&quot;no&quot;</code>.</p>","see":["tk.close-protocol"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import messagebox root = tk.Tk() def delete (): if messagebox.askyesno( \"Delete\" , \"Delete all items?\" ): print ( \"Deleted\" ) else : print ( \"Kept\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Delete everything\" , command=delete).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop() Return values: askokcancel / askyesno / askretrycancel → True / False ; askquestion → the strings \"yes\" / \"no\" ."},{"id":"tk.messagebox-no-root","s":"tk-menus","q":"How do I show a messagebox without an empty main window behind it?","qh":"How do I show a messagebox without an empty main window behind it?","a":"Create the root, hide it with <code>withdraw()</code>, then show the box.","aka":"messagebox only, no root window, hide root, popup without window, messagebox from script","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> messagebox\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.withdraw()\nmessagebox.showinfo(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Done&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;The script has finished.&quot;</i>)\nroot.destroy()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import messagebox root = tk.Tk() root.withdraw() messagebox.showinfo( \"Done\" , \"The script has finished.\" ) root.destroy()"},{"id":"tk.filedialog-open","s":"tk-menus","q":"How do I let the user choose a file to open?","qh":"How do I let the user choose a file to open?","a":"<code>from tkinter import filedialog</code>, then <code>path = filedialog.askopenfilename(filetypes=[...])</code>. It returns <code>&quot;&quot;</code> if the user cancels.","aka":"open file dialog, file picker, browse file, askopenfilename, choose file, select file","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> filedialog\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntext = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">50</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntext.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">open_file</i>():\n    path = filedialog.askopenfilename(title=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Open&quot;</i>, filetypes=[(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Text files&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;*.txt&quot;</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;All files&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;*.*&quot;</i>)])\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> path:\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i>                     <i class=\"c\"># cancelled</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(path, encoding=<i class=\"s\">&quot;utf-8&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n        text.delete(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n        text.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, f.read())\n    root.title(path)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Open...&quot;</i>, command=open_file).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>askopenfilenames</code> (with an s) lets them pick several and returns a tuple.</p>","see":["tk.filedialog-save","py.file-read"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import filedialog root = tk.Tk() text = tk.Text(root, width= 50 , height= 10 ) text.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) def open_file (): path = filedialog.askopenfilename(title= \"Open\" , filetypes=[( \"Text files\" , \"*.txt\" ), ( \"All files\" , \"*.*\" )]) if not path: return # cancelled with open (path, encoding= \"utf-8\" ) as f: text.delete( \"1.0\" , \"end\" ) text.insert( \"1.0\" , f.read()) root.title(path) tk.Button(root, text= \"Open...\" , command=open_file).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop() askopenfilenames (with an s) lets them pick several and returns a tuple."},{"id":"tk.filedialog-save","s":"tk-menus","q":"How do I show a \"Save as\" dialog?","qh":"How do I show a \"Save as\" dialog?","a":"<code>filedialog.asksaveasfilename(defaultextension=&quot;.txt&quot;, filetypes=[...])</code>, then write to that path.","aka":"save file dialog, asksaveasfilename, save as, choose where to save, export file","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> filedialog\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntext = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\ntext.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">save_as</i>():\n    path = filedialog.asksaveasfilename(defaultextension=<i class=\"s\">&quot;.txt&quot;</i>, initialfile=<i class=\"s\">&quot;notes.txt&quot;</i>,\n                                        filetypes=[(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Text files&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;*.txt&quot;</i>)])\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> path:\n        <i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(path, <i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>, encoding=<i class=\"s\">&quot;utf-8&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n            f.write(text.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end-1c&quot;</i>))\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Save as...&quot;</i>, command=save_as).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import filedialog root = tk.Tk() text = tk.Text(root, width= 40 , height= 6 ) text.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) def save_as (): path = filedialog.asksaveasfilename(defaultextension= \".txt\" , initialfile= \"notes.txt\" , filetypes=[( \"Text files\" , \"*.txt\" )]) if path: with open (path, \"w\" , encoding= \"utf-8\" ) as f: f.write(text.get( \"1.0\" , \"end-1c\" )) tk.Button(root, text= \"Save as...\" , command=save_as).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.filedialog-folder","s":"tk-menus","q":"How do I let the user pick a folder?","qh":"How do I let the user pick a folder?","a":"<code>filedialog.askdirectory()</code> returns the folder path, or <code>&quot;&quot;</code> if cancelled.","aka":"choose directory, askdirectory, select folder, browse folder","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> filedialog\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> os\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlb = tk.Listbox(root, width=<i class=\"n\">50</i>)\nlb.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">pick</i>():\n    folder = filedialog.askdirectory(title=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Pick a folder&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> folder:\n        lb.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">for</i> name <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">sorted</i>(os.listdir(folder)):\n            lb.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, name)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Choose folder&quot;</i>, command=pick).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["py.listdir"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import filedialog import os root = tk.Tk() lb = tk.Listbox(root, width= 50 ) lb.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) def pick (): folder = filedialog.askdirectory(title= \"Pick a folder\" ) if folder: lb.delete( 0 , \"end\" ) for name in sorted (os.listdir(folder)): lb.insert( \"end\" , name) tk.Button(root, text= \"Choose folder\" , command=pick).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.simpledialog","s":"tk-menus","q":"How do I ask the user to type a value in a popup?","qh":"How do I ask the user to type a value in a popup?","a":"<code>from tkinter import simpledialog</code>, then <code>simpledialog.askstring(&quot;Title&quot;, &quot;Prompt&quot;)</code>. Also <code>askinteger</code> and <code>askfloat</code> (with <code>minvalue</code>/<code>maxvalue</code>). They return <code>None</code> if cancelled.","aka":"input dialog, askstring, askinteger, prompt, simpledialog, input box popup, ask for name popup","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> simpledialog\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hello, stranger&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nlab.pack()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">ask</i>():\n    name = simpledialog.askstring(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Name&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;What&#x27;s your name?&quot;</i>, parent=root)\n    age = simpledialog.askinteger(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Age&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;How old are you?&quot;</i>, minvalue=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, maxvalue=<i class=\"n\">120</i>, parent=root)\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> name:\n        lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Hello, </i>{name}<i class=\"s\"> (</i>{age}<i class=\"s\">)</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ask&quot;</i>, command=ask).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.modal"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import simpledialog root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"Hello, stranger\" , padx= 30 , pady= 20 ) lab.pack() def ask (): name = simpledialog.askstring( \"Name\" , \"What's your name?\" , parent=root) age = simpledialog.askinteger( \"Age\" , \"How old are you?\" , minvalue= 1 , maxvalue= 120 , parent=root) if name: lab.config(text= f\" Hello, {name} ( {age} ) \" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Ask\" , command=ask).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.colorchooser","s":"tk-menus","q":"How do I let the user pick a colour?","qh":"How do I let the user pick a colour?","a":"<code>from tkinter import colorchooser</code>, then <code>rgb, hex_code = colorchooser.askcolor()</code>. Both are <code>None</code> if cancelled.","aka":"color picker, colour picker, askcolor, colorchooser, choose color dialog","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> colorchooser\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nsw = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;  &quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">3</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\nsw.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">pick</i>():\n    rgb, hex_code = colorchooser.askcolor(title=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Pick a colour&quot;</i>, initialcolor=sw[<i class=\"s\">&quot;bg&quot;</i>])\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> hex_code:\n        sw.config(bg=hex_code)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Choose colour&quot;</i>, command=pick).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import colorchooser root = tk.Tk() sw = tk.Label(root, text= \" \" , width= 20 , height= 3 , bg= \"white\" ) sw.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) def pick (): rgb, hex_code = colorchooser.askcolor(title= \"Pick a colour\" , initialcolor=sw[ \"bg\" ]) if hex_code: sw.config(bg=hex_code) tk.Button(root, text= \"Choose colour\" , command=pick).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.fontchooser","s":"tk-menus","q":"Is there a font picker dialog?","qh":"Is there a font picker dialog?","a":"Not in standard tkinter (Tk 8.6+ has one but Python doesn't wrap it nicely). Easiest: a Listbox of <code>tkinter.font.families()</code> with a preview label.","aka":"font dialog, choose font, font picker","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> font\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlb = tk.Listbox(root, height=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nlb.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> fam <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">sorted</i>(<i class=\"b\">set</i>(font.families())):\n    lb.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, fam)\npreview = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;The quick brown fox&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">24</i>)\npreview.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nlb.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;&lt;ListboxSelect&gt;&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: lb.curselection() <i class=\"k\">and</i> preview.config(font=(lb.get(lb.curselection()[<i class=\"n\">0</i>]), <i class=\"n\">16</i>)))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.font-list"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import font root = tk.Tk() lb = tk.Listbox(root, height= 10 , width= 30 ) lb.pack(side= \"left\" , fill= \"y\" , padx= 6 , pady= 6 ) for fam in sorted ( set (font.families())): lb.insert( \"end\" , fam) preview = tk.Label(root, text= \"The quick brown fox\" , width= 24 ) preview.pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 10 ) lb.bind( \"<<ListboxSelect>>\" , lambda e: lb.curselection() and preview.config(font=(lb.get(lb.curselection()[ 0 ]), 16 ))) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.canvas","s":"tk-canvas","q":"How do I draw shapes (lines, rectangles, circles)?","qh":"How do I draw shapes (lines, rectangles, circles)?","a":"Make a <code>tk.Canvas</code> and use <code>create_line</code>, <code>create_rectangle</code>, <code>create_oval</code>, <code>create_polygon</code>, <code>create_arc</code>, <code>create_text</code>. Coordinates are pixels from the top-left corner (y goes <b>down</b>).","aka":"canvas, draw shapes, draw circle, draw rectangle, draw line, create_oval, create_rectangle, create_line, graphics tkinter","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">320</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">220</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\nc.create_line(<i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">310</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">3</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;black&quot;</i>)\nc.create_rectangle(<i class=\"n\">20</i>, <i class=\"n\">30</i>, <i class=\"n\">120</i>, <i class=\"n\">100</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>, outline=<i class=\"s\">&quot;black&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">2</i>)\nc.create_oval(<i class=\"n\">150</i>, <i class=\"n\">30</i>, <i class=\"n\">220</i>, <i class=\"n\">100</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>, outline=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)          <i class=\"c\"># circle: equal width &amp; height</i>\nc.create_polygon(<i class=\"n\">250</i>, <i class=\"n\">100</i>, <i class=\"n\">280</i>, <i class=\"n\">30</i>, <i class=\"n\">310</i>, <i class=\"n\">100</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#2c6a52&quot;</i>)\nc.create_arc(<i class=\"n\">20</i>, <i class=\"n\">120</i>, <i class=\"n\">120</i>, <i class=\"n\">210</i>, start=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, extent=<i class=\"n\">270</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#3f5a7a&quot;</i>)\nc.create_text(<i class=\"n\">220</i>, <i class=\"n\">165</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hello canvas&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">16</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Every <code>create_...</code> returns an <b>id</b> number; keep it to move, change or delete that shape later.</p>","see":["tk.canvas-circle","tk.canvas-move","tk.canvas-text"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 320 , height= 220 , bg= \"white\" ) c.pack() c.create_line( 10 , 10 , 310 , 10 , width= 3 , fill= \"black\" ) c.create_rectangle( 20 , 30 , 120 , 100 , fill= \"#fc6\" , outline= \"black\" , width= 2 ) c.create_oval( 150 , 30 , 220 , 100 , fill= \"#c23b22\" , outline= \"\" ) # circle: equal width & height c.create_polygon( 250 , 100 , 280 , 30 , 310 , 100 , fill= \"#2c6a52\" ) c.create_arc( 20 , 120 , 120 , 210 , start= 0 , extent= 270 , fill= \"#3f5a7a\" ) c.create_text( 220 , 165 , text= \"Hello canvas\" , font=( \"Arial\" , 16 )) root.mainloop() Every create_... returns an id number; keep it to move, change or delete that shape later."},{"id":"tk.canvas-circle","s":"tk-canvas","q":"How do I draw a circle from a centre and radius?","qh":"How do I draw a circle from a centre and radius?","a":"<code>create_oval</code> takes a bounding box, so convert: <code>(x - r, y - r, x + r, y + r)</code>.","aka":"circle center radius, draw circle at point, create_circle","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">circle</i>(canvas, x, y, r, **kw):\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> canvas.create_oval(x - r, y - r, x + r, y + r, **kw)\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">200</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">200</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> r <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">90</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>, -<i class=\"n\">15</i>):\n    circle(c, <i class=\"n\">100</i>, <i class=\"n\">100</i>, r, outline=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">2</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk def circle (canvas, x, y, r, **kw): return canvas.create_oval(x - r, y - r, x + r, y + r, **kw) root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 200 , height= 200 , bg= \"white\" ) c.pack() for r in range ( 90 , 0 , - 15 ): circle(c, 100 , 100 , r, outline= \"#c23b22\" , width= 2 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.canvas-move","s":"tk-canvas","q":"How do I move, change or delete a shape on the Canvas?","qh":"How do I move, change or delete a shape on the Canvas?","a":"<code>c.move(id, dx, dy)</code> moves by an amount; <code>c.coords(id, x1, y1, x2, y2)</code> sets a new position; <code>c.itemconfig(id, fill=&quot;red&quot;)</code> changes options; <code>c.delete(id)</code> removes it (<code>c.delete(&quot;all&quot;)</code> clears everything).","aka":"move shape, canvas move, coords, itemconfig, delete shape, canvas delete all, change color of shape","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">300</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">120</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\nbox = c.create_rectangle(<i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">40</i>, <i class=\"n\">50</i>, <i class=\"n\">80</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Move right&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: c.move(box, <i class=\"n\">20</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>)).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Red&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: c.itemconfig(box, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>)).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Reset&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: c.coords(box, <i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">40</i>, <i class=\"n\">50</i>, <i class=\"n\">80</i>)).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Delete&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: c.delete(box)).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>c.coords(id)</code> with no numbers returns the current position as a list.</p>","see":["tk.game-loop","tk.canvas-tags"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 300 , height= 120 , bg= \"white\" ) c.pack() box = c.create_rectangle( 10 , 40 , 50 , 80 , fill= \"#fc6\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Move right\" , command= lambda : c.move(box, 20 , 0 )).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 4 , pady= 6 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Red\" , command= lambda : c.itemconfig(box, fill= \"red\" )).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 4 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Reset\" , command= lambda : c.coords(box, 10 , 40 , 50 , 80 )).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 4 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Delete\" , command= lambda : c.delete(box)).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 4 ) root.mainloop() c.coords(id) with no numbers returns the current position as a list."},{"id":"tk.canvas-text","s":"tk-canvas","q":"How do I put text on a Canvas and change it?","qh":"How do I put text on a Canvas and change it?","a":"<code>t = c.create_text(x, y, text=&quot;...&quot;, anchor=&quot;nw&quot;)</code>, change with <code>c.itemconfig(t, text=&quot;new&quot;)</code>. <code>anchor</code> says which point of the text sits at (x, y).","aka":"canvas text, create_text, update canvas text, score on canvas, text anchor canvas","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">260</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">100</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#111&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\nscore = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\nlabel = c.create_text(<i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Score: 0&quot;</i>, anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;nw&quot;</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Courier&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">16</i>))\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">point</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> score\n    score += <i class=\"n\">10</i>\n    c.itemconfig(label, text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Score: </i>{score}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;+10&quot;</i>, command=point).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 260 , height= 100 , bg= \"#111\" ) c.pack() score = 0 label = c.create_text( 10 , 10 , text= \"Score: 0\" , anchor= \"nw\" , fill= \"white\" , font=( \"Courier\" , 16 )) def point (): global score score += 10 c.itemconfig(label, text= f\" Score: {score} \" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"+10\" , command=point).pack(pady= 6 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.canvas-click","s":"tk-canvas","q":"How do I tell which shape was clicked on a Canvas?","qh":"How do I tell which shape was clicked on a Canvas?","a":"Either bind on the item itself with <code>c.tag_bind(id, &quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;, f)</code>, or on the canvas and use <code>c.find_closest(event.x, event.y)</code> / <code>c.find_overlapping(...)</code>.","aka":"click on shape, canvas item click, tag_bind, find_closest, find_overlapping, clicked item","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> random\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">300</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">200</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> _ <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">8</i>):\n    x, y = random.randint(<i class=\"n\">20</i>, <i class=\"n\">280</i>), random.randint(<i class=\"n\">20</i>, <i class=\"n\">180</i>)\n    c.create_oval(x - <i class=\"n\">15</i>, y - <i class=\"n\">15</i>, x + <i class=\"n\">15</i>, y + <i class=\"n\">15</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>, tags=<i class=\"s\">&quot;dot&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">clicked</i>(event):\n    hit = c.find_overlapping(event.x, event.y, event.x, event.y)\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> item <i class=\"k\">in</i> hit:\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;dot&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">in</i> c.gettags(item):\n            c.itemconfig(item, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>)\n\nc.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, clicked)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>find_closest</code> always returns *something* (the nearest item), even if you clicked empty space; <code>find_overlapping</code> only returns items under the point.</p>","see":["tk.canvas-tags"],"t":"import tkinter as tk import random root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 300 , height= 200 , bg= \"white\" ) c.pack() for _ in range ( 8 ): x, y = random.randint( 20 , 280 ), random.randint( 20 , 180 ) c.create_oval(x - 15 , y - 15 , x + 15 , y + 15 , fill= \"#fc6\" , tags= \"dot\" ) def clicked (event): hit = c.find_overlapping(event.x, event.y, event.x, event.y) for item in hit: if \"dot\" in c.gettags(item): c.itemconfig(item, fill= \"#c23b22\" ) c.bind( \"<Button-1>\" , clicked) root.mainloop() find_closest always returns *something* (the nearest item), even if you clicked empty space; find_overlapping only returns items under the point."},{"id":"tk.canvas-tags","s":"tk-canvas","q":"What are canvas tags and how do I use them?","qh":"What are canvas tags and how do I use them?","a":"A tag is a name you give to one or many items (<code>tags=&quot;enemy&quot;</code>). Most canvas methods accept a tag instead of an id, so <code>c.move(&quot;enemy&quot;, 5, 0)</code> moves all of them.","aka":"canvas tags, group shapes, move many shapes, tag_bind, delete by tag, addtag","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">320</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">120</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;black&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">5</i>):\n    c.create_rectangle(<i class=\"n\">20</i> + i * <i class=\"n\">50</i>, <i class=\"n\">20</i>, <i class=\"n\">50</i> + i * <i class=\"n\">50</i>, <i class=\"n\">40</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>, tags=<i class=\"s\">&quot;enemy&quot;</i>)\nc.create_rectangle(<i class=\"n\">150</i>, <i class=\"n\">90</i>, <i class=\"n\">170</i>, <i class=\"n\">105</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>, tags=<i class=\"s\">&quot;player&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">step</i>():\n    c.move(<i class=\"s\">&quot;enemy&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> c.coords(c.find_withtag(<i class=\"s\">&quot;enemy&quot;</i>)[<i class=\"n\">0</i>])[<i class=\"n\">3</i>] &lt; <i class=\"n\">90</i>:\n        root.after(<i class=\"n\">30</i>, step)\n\nc.tag_bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;enemy&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: c.delete(<i class=\"s\">&quot;current&quot;</i>))   <i class=\"c\"># &quot;current&quot; = item under mouse</i>\nstep()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 320 , height= 120 , bg= \"black\" ) c.pack() for i in range ( 5 ): c.create_rectangle( 20 + i * 50 , 20 , 50 + i * 50 , 40 , fill= \"#c23b22\" , tags= \"enemy\" ) c.create_rectangle( 150 , 90 , 170 , 105 , fill= \"#fc6\" , tags= \"player\" ) def step (): c.move( \"enemy\" , 0 , 2 ) if c.coords(c.find_withtag( \"enemy\" )[ 0 ])[ 3 ] < 90 : root.after( 30 , step) c.tag_bind( \"enemy\" , \"<Button-1>\" , lambda e: c.delete( \"current\" )) # \"current\" = item under mouse step() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.canvas-drag","s":"tk-canvas","q":"How do I drag shapes around on a Canvas?","qh":"How do I drag shapes around on a Canvas?","a":"On press, find the item under the mouse; on motion, <code>move</code> it by the mouse's change in position.","aka":"drag canvas item, move shape with mouse, drag and drop canvas","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">320</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">220</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\nc.create_rectangle(<i class=\"n\">20</i>, <i class=\"n\">20</i>, <i class=\"n\">80</i>, <i class=\"n\">70</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>, tags=<i class=\"s\">&quot;drag&quot;</i>)\nc.create_oval(<i class=\"n\">120</i>, <i class=\"n\">60</i>, <i class=\"n\">180</i>, <i class=\"n\">120</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>, tags=<i class=\"s\">&quot;drag&quot;</i>)\ndrag = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;item&quot;</i>: <i class=\"k\">None</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">0</i>}\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">press</i>(e):\n    items = c.find_overlapping(e.x, e.y, e.x, e.y)\n    drag[<i class=\"s\">&quot;item&quot;</i>] = items[-<i class=\"n\">1</i>] <i class=\"k\">if</i> items <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"k\">None</i>\n    drag[<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>], drag[<i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>] = e.x, e.y\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">motion</i>(e):\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> drag[<i class=\"s\">&quot;item&quot;</i>]:\n        c.move(drag[<i class=\"s\">&quot;item&quot;</i>], e.x - drag[<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>], e.y - drag[<i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>])\n        c.tag_raise(drag[<i class=\"s\">&quot;item&quot;</i>])\n        drag[<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>], drag[<i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>] = e.x, e.y\n\nc.tag_bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;drag&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;ButtonPress-1&gt;&quot;</i>, press)\nc.tag_bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;drag&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;B1-Motion&gt;&quot;</i>, motion)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.drag"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 320 , height= 220 , bg= \"white\" ) c.pack() c.create_rectangle( 20 , 20 , 80 , 70 , fill= \"#fc6\" , tags= \"drag\" ) c.create_oval( 120 , 60 , 180 , 120 , fill= \"#c23b22\" , tags= \"drag\" ) drag = { \"item\" : None , \"x\" : 0 , \"y\" : 0 } def press (e): items = c.find_overlapping(e.x, e.y, e.x, e.y) drag[ \"item\" ] = items[- 1 ] if items else None drag[ \"x\" ], drag[ \"y\" ] = e.x, e.y def motion (e): if drag[ \"item\" ]: c.move(drag[ \"item\" ], e.x - drag[ \"x\" ], e.y - drag[ \"y\" ]) c.tag_raise(drag[ \"item\" ]) drag[ \"x\" ], drag[ \"y\" ] = e.x, e.y c.tag_bind( \"drag\" , \"<ButtonPress-1>\" , press) c.tag_bind( \"drag\" , \"<B1-Motion>\" , motion) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.collision","s":"tk-canvas","q":"How do I check if two shapes touch (collision detection)?","qh":"How do I check if two shapes touch (collision detection)?","a":"Compare their bounding boxes from <code>c.bbox(id)</code> or <code>c.coords(id)</code>: two rectangles overlap if each one starts before the other ends, on both axes.","aka":"collision, hit detection, overlap, bbox, find_overlapping collision, game collision","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">overlap</i>(a, b):\n    ax1, ay1, ax2, ay2 = a\n    bx1, by1, bx2, by2 = b\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> ax1 &lt; bx2 <i class=\"k\">and</i> bx1 &lt; ax2 <i class=\"k\">and</i> ay1 &lt; by2 <i class=\"k\">and</i> by1 &lt; ay2\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">300</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">100</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\nplayer = c.create_rectangle(<i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">40</i>, <i class=\"n\">30</i>, <i class=\"n\">60</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#3f5a7a&quot;</i>)\nwall = c.create_rectangle(<i class=\"n\">200</i>, <i class=\"n\">20</i>, <i class=\"n\">220</i>, <i class=\"n\">80</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">step</i>():\n    c.move(player, <i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> overlap(c.coords(player), c.coords(wall)):\n        c.itemconfig(player, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;orange&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n    root.after(<i class=\"n\">20</i>, step)\n\nstep()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>For circles compare the distance between centres with the sum of the radii.</p>","see":["tk.game-loop"],"t":"import tkinter as tk def overlap (a, b): ax1, ay1, ax2, ay2 = a bx1, by1, bx2, by2 = b return ax1 < bx2 and bx1 < ax2 and ay1 < by2 and by1 < ay2 root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 300 , height= 100 , bg= \"white\" ) c.pack() player = c.create_rectangle( 10 , 40 , 30 , 60 , fill= \"#3f5a7a\" ) wall = c.create_rectangle( 200 , 20 , 220 , 80 , fill= \"#c23b22\" ) def step (): c.move(player, 3 , 0 ) if overlap(c.coords(player), c.coords(wall)): c.itemconfig(player, fill= \"orange\" ) return root.after( 20 , step) step() root.mainloop() For circles compare the distance between centres with the sum of the radii."},{"id":"tk.canvas-paint","s":"tk-canvas","q":"How do I make a simple drawing (paint) program?","qh":"How do I make a simple drawing (paint) program?","a":"On <code>&lt;B1-Motion&gt;</code> draw a line from the last mouse point to the new one.","aka":"paint app, draw with mouse, freehand drawing, sketch pad, drawing app","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Sketch&quot;</i>)\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">400</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">300</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, cursor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;pencil&quot;</i>)\nc.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nlast = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>: <i class=\"k\">None</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>: <i class=\"k\">None</i>}\ncolour = tk.StringVar(value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;black&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">down</i>(e):\n    last[<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>], last[<i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>] = e.x, e.y\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">drag</i>(e):\n    c.create_line(last[<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>], last[<i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>], e.x, e.y, fill=colour.get(), width=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, capstyle=<i class=\"s\">&quot;round&quot;</i>, smooth=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n    last[<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>], last[<i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>] = e.x, e.y\n\nc.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, down)\nc.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;B1-Motion&gt;&quot;</i>, drag)\nbar = tk.Frame(root); bar.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> col <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;black&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;blue&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;green&quot;</i>):\n    tk.Radiobutton(bar, text=col, value=col, variable=colour).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>)\ntk.Button(bar, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Clear&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: c.delete(<i class=\"s\">&quot;all&quot;</i>)).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;right&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.canvas-save"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.title( \"Sketch\" ) c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 400 , height= 300 , bg= \"white\" , cursor= \"pencil\" ) c.pack(fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) last = { \"x\" : None , \"y\" : None } colour = tk.StringVar(value= \"black\" ) def down (e): last[ \"x\" ], last[ \"y\" ] = e.x, e.y def drag (e): c.create_line(last[ \"x\" ], last[ \"y\" ], e.x, e.y, fill=colour.get(), width= 4 , capstyle= \"round\" , smooth= True ) last[ \"x\" ], last[ \"y\" ] = e.x, e.y c.bind( \"<Button-1>\" , down) c.bind( \"<B1-Motion>\" , drag) bar = tk.Frame(root); bar.pack(fill= \"x\" ) for col in ( \"black\" , \"red\" , \"blue\" , \"green\" ): tk.Radiobutton(bar, text=col, value=col, variable=colour).pack(side= \"left\" ) tk.Button(bar, text= \"Clear\" , command= lambda : c.delete( \"all\" )).pack(side= \"right\" ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.canvas-save","s":"tk-canvas","q":"How do I save what's on a Canvas as an image?","qh":"How do I save what's on a Canvas as an image?","a":"Built in: <code>c.postscript(file=&quot;out.eps&quot;)</code> (vector EPS). For PNG, either draw the same shapes onto a Pillow image as you go, or grab the screen area with <code>PIL.ImageGrab</code>.","aka":"save canvas, export canvas png, canvas to image, postscript, screenshot canvas","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">200</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">120</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\nc.create_oval(<i class=\"n\">40</i>, <i class=\"n\">20</i>, <i class=\"n\">160</i>, <i class=\"n\">100</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>)\nroot.update()\nc.postscript(file=<i class=\"s\">&quot;drawing.eps&quot;</i>, colormode=<i class=\"s\">&quot;color&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;saved drawing.eps&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>With Pillow installed: <code>ImageGrab.grab(bbox=(x, y, x + w, y + h))</code> where <code>x = c.winfo_rootx()</code> etc. (works on Windows/macOS; on Linux needs X11).</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 200 , height= 120 , bg= \"white\" ) c.pack() c.create_oval( 40 , 20 , 160 , 100 , fill= \"#fc6\" ) root.update() c.postscript(file= \"drawing.eps\" , colormode= \"color\" ) print ( \"saved drawing.eps\" ) root.mainloop() With Pillow installed: ImageGrab.grab(bbox=(x, y, x + w, y + h)) where x = c.winfo_rootx() etc. (works on Windows/macOS; on Linux needs X11)."},{"id":"tk.canvas-image","s":"tk-canvas","q":"How do I show an image on a Canvas?","qh":"How do I show an image on a Canvas?","a":"<code>c.create_image(x, y, image=photo, anchor=&quot;nw&quot;)</code>. Keep a reference to the <code>PhotoImage</code> or it disappears.","aka":"canvas image, create_image, background image canvas, sprite, picture on canvas","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">200</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">150</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\nsprite = tk.PhotoImage(file=<i class=\"s\">&quot;photo.png&quot;</i>)\nitem = c.create_image(<i class=\"n\">20</i>, <i class=\"n\">20</i>, image=sprite, anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;nw&quot;</i>)\nc.sprite = sprite                   <i class=\"c\"># keep it alive</i>\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Move&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: c.move(item, <i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">5</i>)).pack()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.photoimage","tk.err.image-gone","tk.bg-image"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 200 , height= 150 , bg= \"white\" ) c.pack() sprite = tk.PhotoImage(file= \"photo.png\" ) item = c.create_image( 20 , 20 , image=sprite, anchor= \"nw\" ) c.sprite = sprite # keep it alive tk.Button(root, text= \"Move\" , command= lambda : c.move(item, 10 , 5 )).pack() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.canvas-scroll","s":"tk-canvas","q":"How do I make a Canvas bigger than the window and scroll it?","qh":"How do I make a Canvas bigger than the window and scroll it?","a":"Set <code>scrollregion=(0, 0, W, H)</code> to the full size and connect scrollbars with <code>xview</code>/<code>yview</code>. Use <code>c.canvasx(event.x)</code> to turn a click into canvas coordinates.","aka":"scroll canvas, scrollregion, large canvas, canvas scrollbar, pan canvas","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">300</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">200</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, scrollregion=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">1000</i>, <i class=\"n\">1000</i>))\nys = tk.Scrollbar(root, command=c.yview)\nxs = tk.Scrollbar(root, orient=<i class=\"s\">&quot;horizontal&quot;</i>, command=c.xview)\nc.config(yscrollcommand=ys.<i class=\"b\">set</i>, xscrollcommand=xs.<i class=\"b\">set</i>)\nc.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>); ys.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ns&quot;</i>); xs.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ew&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">1000</i>, <i class=\"n\">100</i>):\n    c.create_text(i + <i class=\"n\">50</i>, i + <i class=\"n\">50</i>, text=<i class=\"b\">str</i>(i))\nc.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(c.canvasx(e.x), c.canvasy(e.y)))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.scrollbar"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 300 , height= 200 , bg= \"white\" , scrollregion=( 0 , 0 , 1000 , 1000 )) ys = tk.Scrollbar(root, command=c.yview) xs = tk.Scrollbar(root, orient= \"horizontal\" , command=c.xview) c.config(yscrollcommand=ys. set , xscrollcommand=xs. set ) c.grid(row= 0 , column= 0 ); ys.grid(row= 0 , column= 1 , sticky= \"ns\" ); xs.grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , sticky= \"ew\" ) for i in range ( 0 , 1000 , 100 ): c.create_text(i + 50 , i + 50 , text= str (i)) c.bind( \"<Button-1>\" , lambda e: print (c.canvasx(e.x), c.canvasy(e.y))) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.canvas-grid","s":"tk-canvas","q":"How do I draw a grid (board) on a Canvas and know which cell was clicked?","qh":"How do I draw a grid (board) on a Canvas and know which cell was clicked?","a":"Draw rectangles in two loops, size <code>CELL</code>. On click, the cell is <code>event.x // CELL, event.y // CELL</code>.","aka":"grid on canvas, board game, chess board, cells, tile map, which square clicked","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nCELL, N = <i class=\"n\">40</i>, <i class=\"n\">8</i>\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=CELL * N, height=CELL * N)\nc.pack()\ncells = {}\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> row <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(N):\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> col <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(N):\n        colour = <i class=\"s\">&quot;#e9e2d4&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> (row + col) % <i class=\"n\">2</i> == <i class=\"n\">0</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;#6b655a&quot;</i>\n        cells[row, col] = c.create_rectangle(col * CELL, row * CELL, (col + <i class=\"n\">1</i>) * CELL, (row + <i class=\"n\">1</i>) * CELL, fill=colour, outline=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">click</i>(e):\n    col, row = e.x // CELL, e.y // CELL\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> (row, col) <i class=\"k\">in</i> cells:\n        c.itemconfig(cells[row, col], fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>)\n\nc.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, click)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk CELL, N = 40 , 8 root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width=CELL * N, height=CELL * N) c.pack() cells = {} for row in range (N): for col in range (N): colour = \"#e9e2d4\" if (row + col) % 2 == 0 else \"#6b655a\" cells[row, col] = c.create_rectangle(col * CELL, row * CELL, (col + 1 ) * CELL, (row + 1 ) * CELL, fill=colour, outline= \"\" ) def click (e): col, row = e.x // CELL, e.y // CELL if (row, col) in cells: c.itemconfig(cells[row, col], fill= \"#c23b22\" ) c.bind( \"<Button-1>\" , click) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.canvas-layers","s":"tk-canvas","q":"How do I put one shape in front of or behind another?","qh":"How do I put one shape in front of or behind another?","a":"Later items are drawn on top. Change it with <code>c.tag_raise(id)</code> (to front) or <code>c.tag_lower(id)</code> (to back).","aka":"z order, bring to front, send to back, tag_raise, tag_lower, layer order","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">200</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">150</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\na = c.create_rectangle(<i class=\"n\">30</i>, <i class=\"n\">30</i>, <i class=\"n\">120</i>, <i class=\"n\">110</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>)\nb = c.create_rectangle(<i class=\"n\">70</i>, <i class=\"n\">50</i>, <i class=\"n\">170</i>, <i class=\"n\">130</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#3f5a7a&quot;</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Yellow to front&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: c.tag_raise(a)).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Yellow to back&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: c.tag_lower(a)).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 200 , height= 150 , bg= \"white\" ) c.pack() a = c.create_rectangle( 30 , 30 , 120 , 110 , fill= \"#fc6\" ) b = c.create_rectangle( 70 , 50 , 170 , 130 , fill= \"#3f5a7a\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Yellow to front\" , command= lambda : c.tag_raise(a)).pack(side= \"left\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Yellow to back\" , command= lambda : c.tag_lower(a)).pack(side= \"left\" ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.canvas-chart","s":"tk-canvas","q":"How do I draw a simple bar chart?","qh":"How do I draw a simple bar chart?","a":"Scale each value to the canvas height and draw rectangles. For real charts embed matplotlib with <code>FigureCanvasTkAgg</code>.","aka":"bar chart, graph, chart tkinter, plot data, matplotlib tkinter","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\ndata = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;Mon&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Tue&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">7</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Wed&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Thu&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">9</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Fri&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">6</i>}\nW, H, PAD = <i class=\"n\">320</i>, <i class=\"n\">200</i>, <i class=\"n\">24</i>\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=W, height=H, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\ntop = <i class=\"b\">max</i>(data.values())\nbw = (W - <i class=\"n\">2</i> * PAD) / <i class=\"b\">len</i>(data)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> i, (day, v) <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">enumerate</i>(data.items()):\n    x = PAD + i * bw\n    h = (H - <i class=\"n\">2</i> * PAD) * v / top\n    c.create_rectangle(x + <i class=\"n\">6</i>, H - PAD - h, x + bw - <i class=\"n\">6</i>, H - PAD, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>, outline=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\n    c.create_text(x + bw / <i class=\"n\">2</i>, H - PAD + <i class=\"n\">10</i>, text=day)\n    c.create_text(x + bw / <i class=\"n\">2</i>, H - PAD - h - <i class=\"n\">8</i>, text=<i class=\"b\">str</i>(v))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Matplotlib version (needs <code>pip install matplotlib</code>):</p><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> matplotlib.figure <i class=\"k\">import</i> Figure\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> matplotlib.backends.backend_tkagg <i class=\"k\">import</i> FigureCanvasTkAgg\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nfig = Figure(figsize=(<i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>))\nfig.add_subplot().plot([<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>], [<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">9</i>, <i class=\"n\">16</i>])\nFigureCanvasTkAgg(fig, master=root).get_tk_widget().pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk data = { \"Mon\" : 4 , \"Tue\" : 7 , \"Wed\" : 3 , \"Thu\" : 9 , \"Fri\" : 6 } W, H, PAD = 320 , 200 , 24 root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width=W, height=H, bg= \"white\" ) c.pack() top = max (data.values()) bw = (W - 2 * PAD) / len (data) for i, (day, v) in enumerate (data.items()): x = PAD + i * bw h = (H - 2 * PAD) * v / top c.create_rectangle(x + 6 , H - PAD - h, x + bw - 6 , H - PAD, fill= \"#c23b22\" , outline= \"\" ) c.create_text(x + bw / 2 , H - PAD + 10 , text=day) c.create_text(x + bw / 2 , H - PAD - h - 8 , text= str (v)) root.mainloop() Matplotlib version (needs pip install matplotlib ): import tkinter as tk from matplotlib.figure import Figure from matplotlib.backends.backend_tkagg import FigureCanvasTkAgg root = tk.Tk() fig = Figure(figsize=( 4 , 3 )) fig.add_subplot().plot([ 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 ], [ 1 , 4 , 9 , 16 ]) FigureCanvasTkAgg(fig, master=root).get_tk_widget().pack(fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.photoimage","s":"tk-images","q":"How do I show an image (PNG or GIF) in tkinter?","qh":"How do I show an image (PNG or GIF) in tkinter?","a":"<code>img = tk.PhotoImage(file=&quot;photo.png&quot;)</code>, then <code>tk.Label(root, image=img)</code>. PhotoImage reads PNG, GIF and PPM; for JPG use <a href=\"#/a/tk.pillow\">Pillow</a>.","aka":"show image, display picture, PhotoImage, image in label, load png, add image to window","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nimg = tk.PhotoImage(file=<i class=\"s\">&quot;photo.png&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(root, image=img).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>The image stays visible only while the <code>img</code> variable exists. See <a href=\"#/a/tk.err.image-gone\">My image doesn't show (blank space) when I create it inside a function or class. Why?</a>.</p>","see":["tk.err.image-gone","tk.pillow","tk.img-path"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() img = tk.PhotoImage(file= \"photo.png\" ) tk.Label(root, image=img).pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() The image stays visible only while the img variable exists. See My image doesn't show (blank space) when I create it inside a function or class. Why? ."},{"id":"tk.err.image-gone","s":"tk-images","q":"My image doesn't show (blank space) when I create it inside a function or class. Why?","qh":"My image doesn't show (blank space) when I create it inside a function or class. Why?","a":"Python deletes the <code>PhotoImage</code> when the function ends because nothing refers to it any more, and Tk then shows nothing. Keep a reference: <code>label.image = img</code>, or <code>self.img = img</code>.","aka":"image not showing, image disappears, blank image, photoimage garbage collected, image in function not displayed, keep reference image","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">show</i>():\n    img = tk.PhotoImage(file=<i class=\"s\">&quot;photo.png&quot;</i>)\n    tk.Label(root, image=img).pack()      <i class=\"c\"># blank: img is deleted after show()</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">show</i>():\n    img = tk.PhotoImage(file=<i class=\"s\">&quot;photo.png&quot;</i>)\n    lab = tk.Label(root, image=img)\n    lab.image = img           <i class=\"c\"># keep a reference on the label</i>\n    lab.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\nshow()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.photoimage","tk.var-gc"],"t":"def show (): img = tk.PhotoImage(file= \"photo.png\" ) tk.Label(root, image=img).pack() # blank: img is deleted after show() import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() def show (): img = tk.PhotoImage(file= \"photo.png\" ) lab = tk.Label(root, image=img) lab.image = img # keep a reference on the label lab.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) show() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.pillow","s":"tk-images","q":"How do I show a JPG (or resize an image)?","qh":"How do I show a JPG (or resize an image)?","a":"Install Pillow (<code>pip install pillow</code>), open with <code>Image.open</code>, resize, then wrap with <code>ImageTk.PhotoImage</code>.","aka":"jpg tkinter, jpeg, resize image, pillow, PIL ImageTk, scale image, thumbnail, couldn't recognize data in image file","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> PIL <i class=\"k\">import</i> Image, ImageTk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\npic = Image.<i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;photo.jpg&quot;</i>)\npic = pic.resize((<i class=\"n\">200</i>, <i class=\"n\">150</i>))              <i class=\"c\"># exact size (may stretch)</i>\n<i class=\"c\"># pic.thumbnail((200, 200))               # or: fit inside 200x200, keep shape</i>\nphoto = ImageTk.PhotoImage(pic)\nlab = tk.Label(root, image=photo)\nlab.image = photo\nlab.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><figure class=\"code sh\" data-tag=\"terminal\"><pre>pip install pillow</pre></figure><p>You install <code>pillow</code> but import <code>PIL</code>. <code>pip install PIL</code> does not work.</p>","see":["tk.err.couldnt-recognize","tk.err.image-gone"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from PIL import Image, ImageTk root = tk.Tk() pic = Image. open ( \"photo.jpg\" ) pic = pic.resize(( 200 , 150 )) # exact size (may stretch) # pic.thumbnail((200, 200)) # or: fit inside 200x200, keep shape photo = ImageTk.PhotoImage(pic) lab = tk.Label(root, image=photo) lab.image = photo lab.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() pip install pillow You install pillow but import PIL . pip install PIL does not work."},{"id":"tk.err.couldnt-recognize","s":"tk-images","q":"TclError: couldn't recognize data in image file","qh":"TclError: couldn't recognize data in image file","a":"<code>tk.PhotoImage</code> can't read that format (usually JPG/JPEG, or a file that was renamed to <code>.png</code> but isn't really one). Load it through Pillow instead.","aka":"couldn't recognize data in image file, cannot open jpg, image file error, photoimage jpg error","err":"couldn't recognize data in image file","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> PIL <i class=\"k\">import</i> Image, ImageTk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nphoto = ImageTk.PhotoImage(Image.<i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;photo.jpg&quot;</i>))\nlab = tk.Label(root, image=photo)\nlab.image = photo\nlab.pack()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.pillow"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from PIL import Image, ImageTk root = tk.Tk() photo = ImageTk.PhotoImage(Image. open ( \"photo.jpg\" )) lab = tk.Label(root, image=photo) lab.image = photo lab.pack() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.img-path","s":"tk-images","q":"TclError: couldn't open \"image.png\": no such file or directory","qh":"TclError: couldn't open \"image.png\": no such file or directory","a":"The path is relative to the <b>current working directory</b>, which isn't always your script's folder. Build the path from the script's location.","aka":"couldn't open image, no such file or directory image, image path, file not found image, image works in vscode not idle","err":"couldn't open*no such file or directory","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> pathlib <i class=\"k\">import</i> Path\n\nHERE = Path(__file__).parent              <i class=\"c\"># folder this .py file is in</i>\nroot = tk.Tk()\nimg = tk.PhotoImage(file=HERE / <i class=\"s\">&quot;photo.png&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(root, image=img).pack()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["py.err.filenotfound","py.script-dir"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from pathlib import Path HERE = Path(__file__).parent # folder this .py file is in root = tk.Tk() img = tk.PhotoImage(file=HERE / \"photo.png\" ) tk.Label(root, image=img).pack() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.bg-image","s":"tk-images","q":"How do I set a background image for the window?","qh":"How do I set a background image for the window?","a":"Put the image in a Label with <code>place(x=0, y=0, relwidth=1, relheight=1)</code> and create it first, so other widgets sit on top. Or draw it on a Canvas and add widgets with <code>create_window</code>.","aka":"background image, wallpaper, image behind widgets, bg image tkinter","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> PIL <i class=\"k\">import</i> Image, ImageTk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;300x200&quot;</i>)\nbg = ImageTk.PhotoImage(Image.<i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;photo.jpg&quot;</i>).resize((<i class=\"n\">300</i>, <i class=\"n\">200</i>)))\nback = tk.Label(root, image=bg)\nback.image = bg\nback.place(x=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, y=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, relwidth=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, relheight=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;On top of the image&quot;</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">80</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Widgets can't be truly see-through: a Label on top of the image will still have its own background colour. Use canvas text (<code>create_text</code>) for text that sits directly on the picture.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from PIL import Image, ImageTk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"300x200\" ) bg = ImageTk.PhotoImage(Image. open ( \"photo.jpg\" ).resize(( 300 , 200 ))) back = tk.Label(root, image=bg) back.image = bg back.place(x= 0 , y= 0 , relwidth= 1 , relheight= 1 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"On top of the image\" ).pack(pady= 80 ) root.mainloop() Widgets can't be truly see-through: a Label on top of the image will still have its own background colour. Use canvas text ( create_text ) for text that sits directly on the picture."},{"id":"tk.img-resize-window","s":"tk-images","q":"How do I make an image resize with the window?","qh":"How do I make an image resize with the window?","a":"Keep the original Pillow image, and on <code>&lt;Configure&gt;</code> make a resized copy and set it on the label.","aka":"scale image to window, responsive image, fit image to window, image resize on configure","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> PIL <i class=\"k\">import</i> Image, ImageTk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;300x220&quot;</i>)\noriginal = Image.<i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;photo.jpg&quot;</i>)\nlab = tk.Label(root, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;black&quot;</i>)\nlab.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">fit</i>(e):\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> e.width &lt; <i class=\"n\">2</i> <i class=\"k\">or</i> e.height &lt; <i class=\"n\">2</i>:\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n    copy = original.copy()\n    copy.thumbnail((e.width, e.height))\n    lab.photo = ImageTk.PhotoImage(copy)\n    lab.config(image=lab.photo)\n\nlab.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Configure&gt;&quot;</i>, fit)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from PIL import Image, ImageTk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"300x220\" ) original = Image. open ( \"photo.jpg\" ) lab = tk.Label(root, bg= \"black\" ) lab.pack(fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) def fit (e): if e.width < 2 or e.height < 2 : return copy = original.copy() copy.thumbnail((e.width, e.height)) lab.photo = ImageTk.PhotoImage(copy) lab.config(image=lab.photo) lab.bind( \"<Configure>\" , fit) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.img-change","s":"tk-images","q":"How do I change the image shown in a Label (e.g. a slideshow)?","qh":"How do I change the image shown in a Label (e.g. a slideshow)?","a":"<code>label.config(image=new_img)</code> and keep a reference (<code>label.image = new_img</code>).","aka":"change image, swap image, slideshow, next image button, update picture","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> PIL <i class=\"k\">import</i> Image\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\n<i class=\"c\"># make three coloured test images in memory</i>\nimgs = []\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> col <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;#2c6a52&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;#3f5a7a&quot;</i>):\n    Image.new(<i class=\"s\">&quot;RGB&quot;</i>, (<i class=\"n\">160</i>, <i class=\"n\">100</i>), col).save(<i class=\"s\">&quot;tmp.png&quot;</i>)\n    imgs.append(tk.PhotoImage(file=<i class=\"s\">&quot;tmp.png&quot;</i>))\ni = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\nlab = tk.Label(root, image=imgs[<i class=\"n\">0</i>])\nlab.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">next_image</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> i\n    i = (i + <i class=\"n\">1</i>) % <i class=\"b\">len</i>(imgs)\n    lab.config(image=imgs[i])\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Next&quot;</i>, command=next_image).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Here the list <code>imgs</code> keeps all images alive.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from PIL import Image root = tk.Tk() # make three coloured test images in memory imgs = [] for col in ( \"#c23b22\" , \"#2c6a52\" , \"#3f5a7a\" ): Image.new( \"RGB\" , ( 160 , 100 ), col).save( \"tmp.png\" ) imgs.append(tk.PhotoImage(file= \"tmp.png\" )) i = 0 lab = tk.Label(root, image=imgs[ 0 ]) lab.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) def next_image (): global i i = (i + 1 ) % len (imgs) lab.config(image=imgs[i]) tk.Button(root, text= \"Next\" , command=next_image).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop() Here the list imgs keeps all images alive."},{"id":"tk.gif","s":"tk-images","q":"How do I show an animated GIF?","qh":"How do I show an animated GIF?","a":"PhotoImage loads one frame at a time: <code>PhotoImage(file=&quot;a.gif&quot;, format=&quot;gif -index N&quot;)</code>. Load the frames and cycle them with <code>after</code>.","aka":"animated gif, gif animation, gif frames, play gif","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> PIL <i class=\"k\">import</i> Image\n\n<i class=\"c\"># build a small animated gif to show</i>\nframes = [Image.new(<i class=\"s\">&quot;RGB&quot;</i>, (<i class=\"n\">80</i>, <i class=\"n\">80</i>), (<i class=\"n\">40</i> * i, <i class=\"n\">80</i>, <i class=\"n\">200</i> - <i class=\"n\">30</i> * i)) <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">6</i>)]\nframes[<i class=\"n\">0</i>].save(<i class=\"s\">&quot;spin.gif&quot;</i>, save_all=<i class=\"k\">True</i>, append_images=frames[<i class=\"n\">1</i>:], duration=<i class=\"n\">100</i>, loop=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ngif_frames = []\n<i class=\"k\">while</i> <i class=\"k\">True</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        gif_frames.append(tk.PhotoImage(file=<i class=\"s\">&quot;spin.gif&quot;</i>, <i class=\"b\">format</i>=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">gif -index </i>{<i class=\"b\">len</i>(gif_frames)}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>))\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> tk.TclError:\n        <i class=\"k\">break</i>\nlab = tk.Label(root)\nlab.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">play</i>(n=<i class=\"n\">0</i>):\n    lab.config(image=gif_frames[n])\n    root.after(<i class=\"n\">100</i>, play, (n + <i class=\"n\">1</i>) % <i class=\"b\">len</i>(gif_frames))\n\nplay()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from PIL import Image # build a small animated gif to show frames = [Image.new( \"RGB\" , ( 80 , 80 ), ( 40 * i, 80 , 200 - 30 * i)) for i in range ( 6 )] frames[ 0 ].save( \"spin.gif\" , save_all= True , append_images=frames[ 1 :], duration= 100 , loop= 0 ) root = tk.Tk() gif_frames = [] while True : try : gif_frames.append(tk.PhotoImage(file= \"spin.gif\" , format = f\" gif -index { len (gif_frames)} \" )) except tk.TclError: break lab = tk.Label(root) lab.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 20 ) def play (n= 0 ): lab.config(image=gif_frames[n]) root.after( 100 , play, (n + 1 ) % len (gif_frames)) play() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.img-pixels","s":"tk-images","q":"How do I read or set individual pixels, or make an image from scratch?","qh":"How do I read or set individual pixels, or make an image from scratch?","a":"<code>PhotoImage(width=w, height=h)</code> makes a blank image; <code>img.put(&quot;#ff0000&quot;, (x, y))</code> sets a pixel; <code>img.get(x, y)</code> reads one. For anything big, use Pillow, which is far faster.","aka":"pixel color, put pixel, get pixel, generate image, PhotoImage put, draw pixels","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nW, H = <i class=\"n\">120</i>, <i class=\"n\">80</i>\nimg = tk.PhotoImage(width=W, height=H)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> y <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(H):\n    row = <i class=\"s\">&quot;{&quot;</i> + <i class=\"s\">&quot; &quot;</i>.join(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">#</i>{x * <i class=\"n\">2</i>:<i class=\"s\">02x</i>}{y * <i class=\"n\">3</i>:<i class=\"s\">02x</i>}<i class=\"s\">80</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">for</i> x <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(W)) + <i class=\"s\">&quot;}&quot;</i>\n    img.put(row, to=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, y))\ntk.Label(root, image=img).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(img.get(<i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() W, H = 120 , 80 img = tk.PhotoImage(width=W, height=H) for y in range (H): row = \"{\" + \" \" .join( f\" # {x * 2 : 02x }{y * 3 : 02x } 80 \" for x in range (W)) + \"}\" img.put(row, to=( 0 , y)) tk.Label(root, image=img).pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) print (img.get( 10 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.img-zoom","s":"tk-images","q":"How do I make a PhotoImage bigger or smaller without Pillow?","qh":"How do I make a PhotoImage bigger or smaller without Pillow?","a":"<code>img.zoom(2)</code> doubles it, <code>img.subsample(2)</code> halves it (whole numbers only). Good for pixel art.","aka":"zoom, subsample, scale photoimage, shrink image, enlarge pixel art","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nimg = tk.PhotoImage(file=<i class=\"s\">&quot;icon.png&quot;</i>)\nbig = img.zoom(<i class=\"n\">2</i>)\nsmall = img.subsample(<i class=\"n\">2</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> im <i class=\"k\">in</i> (small, img, big):\n    tk.Label(root, image=im).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nroot.imgs = (img, big, small)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() img = tk.PhotoImage(file= \"icon.png\" ) big = img.zoom( 2 ) small = img.subsample( 2 ) for im in (small, img, big): tk.Label(root, image=im).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 6 , pady= 6 ) root.imgs = (img, big, small) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.colors","s":"tk-style","q":"What colours can I use and how do I write them?","qh":"What colours can I use and how do I write them?","a":"Named colours (<code>&quot;red&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;light blue&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;SlateGray3&quot;</code>...) or hex <code>&quot;#rrggbb&quot;</code> like <code>&quot;#ff8800&quot;</code>. Convert RGB numbers with <code>f&quot;#{r:02x}{g:02x}{b:02x}&quot;</code>.","aka":"colour names, color names, hex color, rgb color tkinter, list of colors, color codes","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">rgb</i>(r, g, b):\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">#</i>{r:<i class=\"s\">02x</i>}{g:<i class=\"s\">02x</i>}{b:<i class=\"s\">02x</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> col <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;light blue&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;SlateGray3&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;#ff8800&quot;</i>, rgb(<i class=\"n\">40</i>, <i class=\"n\">120</i>, <i class=\"n\">90</i>)):\n    tk.Label(root, text=col, bg=col, width=<i class=\"n\">20</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(root.winfo_rgb(<i class=\"s\">&quot;orange&quot;</i>))     <i class=\"c\"># (65535, 42405, 0): 16-bit numbers</i>\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>A wrong name gives <code>TclError: unknown color name &quot;....&quot;</code>. Spaces and capitals in names don't matter (<code>&quot;LightBlue&quot;</code> = <code>&quot;light blue&quot;</code>).</p>","see":["tk.bg-color"],"t":"import tkinter as tk def rgb (r, g, b): return f\" # {r: 02x }{g: 02x }{b: 02x } \" root = tk.Tk() for col in ( \"red\" , \"light blue\" , \"SlateGray3\" , \"#ff8800\" , rgb( 40 , 120 , 90 )): tk.Label(root, text=col, bg=col, width= 20 ).pack(pady= 2 , padx= 10 ) print (root.winfo_rgb( \"orange\" )) # (65535, 42405, 0): 16-bit numbers root.mainloop() A wrong name gives TclError: unknown color name \"....\" . Spaces and capitals in names don't matter ( \"LightBlue\" = \"light blue\" )."},{"id":"tk.font-object","s":"tk-style","q":"How do I make one font and reuse it (or change it everywhere at once)?","qh":"How do I make one font and reuse it (or change it everywhere at once)?","a":"<code>from tkinter import font</code>, make <code>f = font.Font(family=&quot;Helvetica&quot;, size=14)</code>, pass <code>font=f</code> to widgets. Changing <code>f.configure(size=20)</code> updates all of them.","aka":"font object, tkinter.font, Font class, change font size everywhere, default font, named font","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> font\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nf = font.Font(family=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, size=<i class=\"n\">14</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> t <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;one&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;two&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;three&quot;</i>):\n    tk.Label(root, text=t, font=f).pack()\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Bigger&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: f.configure(size=f.cget(<i class=\"s\">&quot;size&quot;</i>) + <i class=\"n\">2</i>)).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">8</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(f.measure(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hello&quot;</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;pixels wide&quot;</i>)   <i class=\"c\"># handy for layout</i>\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.label-font","tk.default-font"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import font root = tk.Tk() f = font.Font(family= \"Helvetica\" , size= 14 ) for t in ( \"one\" , \"two\" , \"three\" ): tk.Label(root, text=t, font=f).pack() tk.Button(root, text= \"Bigger\" , command= lambda : f.configure(size=f.cget( \"size\" ) + 2 )).pack(pady= 8 ) print (f.measure( \"Hello\" ), \"pixels wide\" ) # handy for layout root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.default-font","s":"tk-style","q":"How do I change the default font for the whole app?","qh":"How do I change the default font for the whole app?","a":"Change the named font <code>TkDefaultFont</code> (and <code>TkTextFont</code> for Entry/Text). Or use <code>root.option_add(&quot;*Font&quot;, ...)</code> <b>before</b> creating widgets.","aka":"default font, global font, change all fonts, TkDefaultFont, option_add font","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> font\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nfont.nametofont(<i class=\"s\">&quot;TkDefaultFont&quot;</i>).configure(family=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, size=<i class=\"n\">13</i>)\nfont.nametofont(<i class=\"s\">&quot;TkTextFont&quot;</i>).configure(size=<i class=\"n\">13</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Label uses the default font&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Entry(root).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Button too&quot;</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import font root = tk.Tk() font.nametofont( \"TkDefaultFont\" ).configure(family= \"Helvetica\" , size= 13 ) font.nametofont( \"TkTextFont\" ).configure(size= 13 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"Label uses the default font\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) tk.Entry(root).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Button too\" ).pack(pady= 5 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.font-list","s":"tk-style","q":"How do I see which fonts are available?","qh":"How do I see which fonts are available?","a":"<code>tkinter.font.families()</code> (after creating <code>Tk()</code>).","aka":"list fonts, font families, installed fonts, available fonts","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> font\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nnames = <i class=\"b\">sorted</i>(<i class=\"b\">set</i>(font.families()))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">len</i>(names), <i class=\"s\">&quot;fonts, e.g.&quot;</i>, names[:<i class=\"n\">5</i>])\nroot.destroy()</pre></figure><p>Fonts that work almost everywhere: <code>&quot;Helvetica&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;Times&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;Courier&quot;</code> (Tk maps these to similar system fonts). Using a custom <code>.ttf</code> file isn't built in; install the font on the computer first (or use the <code>pyglet</code> package to load it on Windows).</p>","see":["tk.fontchooser"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import font root = tk.Tk() names = sorted ( set (font.families())) print ( len (names), \"fonts, e.g.\" , names[: 5 ]) root.destroy() Fonts that work almost everywhere: \"Helvetica\" , \"Times\" , \"Courier\" (Tk maps these to similar system fonts). Using a custom .ttf file isn't built in; install the font on the computer first (or use the pyglet package to load it on Windows)."},{"id":"tk.ttk-vs-tk","s":"tk-style","q":"What's the difference between tk and ttk widgets?","qh":"What's the difference between tk and ttk widgets?","a":"<code>ttk</code> widgets use the system's native look and are styled with <code>ttk.Style</code>; they don't accept <code>bg</code>, <code>fg</code>, <code>font</code> directly. <code>tk</code> widgets look older but take colour options directly. ttk also adds Combobox, Treeview, Notebook, Progressbar.","aka":"ttk, themed widgets, tk vs ttk, ttk.Button, native look, modern widgets","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;tk.Button&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nttk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ttk.Button&quot;</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\ntk.Entry(root).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nttk.Entry(root).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>ttk.Button(root, bg=&quot;red&quot;)</code> raises <code>TclError: unknown option &quot;-bg&quot;</code>. Use a style instead (next answer).</p>","see":["tk.ttk-style","tk.ttk-theme"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() tk.Button(root, text= \"tk.Button\" , bg= \"#fc6\" ).pack(padx= 30 , pady= 6 ) ttk.Button(root, text= \"ttk.Button\" ).pack(pady= 6 ) tk.Entry(root).pack(pady= 6 ) ttk.Entry(root).pack(pady= 6 ) root.mainloop() ttk.Button(root, bg=\"red\") raises TclError: unknown option \"-bg\" . Use a style instead (next answer)."},{"id":"tk.ttk-style","s":"tk-style","q":"How do I change colours and fonts of ttk widgets (ttk.Style)?","qh":"How do I change colours and fonts of ttk widgets (ttk.Style)?","a":"Create <code>style = ttk.Style()</code>, configure a style name like <code>&quot;Big.TButton&quot;</code> (<code>Name.</code> + widget class), then use <code>style=&quot;Big.TButton&quot;</code>. <code>style.map</code> sets colours for states like hover and pressed.","aka":"ttk style, style configure, ttk button color, ttk background, custom style, TButton, style map hover","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nstyle = ttk.Style()\nstyle.theme_use(<i class=\"s\">&quot;clam&quot;</i>)                    <i class=\"c\"># the default Windows/Mac themes ignore most colours</i>\nstyle.configure(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Go.TButton&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">13</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;bold&quot;</i>), foreground=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, background=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#2e7d32&quot;</i>, padding=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nstyle.<i class=\"b\">map</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Go.TButton&quot;</i>, background=[(<i class=\"s\">&quot;pressed&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;#1b5e20&quot;</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;active&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;#388e3c&quot;</i>)])\nstyle.configure(<i class=\"s\">&quot;TLabel&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">12</i>))            <i class=\"c\"># every ttk.Label</i>\n\nttk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ready?&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">8</i>)\nttk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Go&quot;</i>, style=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Go.TButton&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">16</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Class names: <code>TButton</code>, <code>TLabel</code>, <code>TEntry</code>, <code>TFrame</code>, <code>TCheckbutton</code>, <code>TCombobox</code>, <code>Treeview</code>, <code>TNotebook</code>, <code>Horizontal.TProgressbar</code>. Find a widget's with <code>widget.winfo_class()</code>.</p>","see":["tk.ttk-theme","tk.ttk-vs-tk"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() style = ttk.Style() style.theme_use( \"clam\" ) # the default Windows/Mac themes ignore most colours style.configure( \"Go.TButton\" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 13 , \"bold\" ), foreground= \"white\" , background= \"#2e7d32\" , padding= 10 ) style. map ( \"Go.TButton\" , background=[( \"pressed\" , \"#1b5e20\" ), ( \"active\" , \"#388e3c\" )]) style.configure( \"TLabel\" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 12 )) # every ttk.Label ttk.Label(root, text= \"Ready?\" ).pack(padx= 30 , pady= 8 ) ttk.Button(root, text= \"Go\" , style= \"Go.TButton\" ).pack(padx= 30 , pady=( 0 , 16 )) root.mainloop() Class names: TButton , TLabel , TEntry , TFrame , TCheckbutton , TCombobox , Treeview , TNotebook , Horizontal.TProgressbar . Find a widget's with widget.winfo_class() ."},{"id":"tk.ttk-theme","s":"tk-style","q":"How do I change the ttk theme?","qh":"How do I change the ttk theme?","a":"<code>ttk.Style().theme_names()</code> lists them; <code>theme_use(&quot;clam&quot;)</code> switches. Built-in: <code>clam</code>, <code>alt</code>, <code>default</code>, <code>classic</code>, plus <code>vista</code>/<code>xpnative</code> on Windows and <code>aqua</code> on macOS.","aka":"ttk theme, theme_use, clam alt default classic, vista theme, aqua, themes list, sun valley theme","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nstyle = ttk.Style()\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(style.theme_names(), <i class=\"s\">&quot;current:&quot;</i>, style.theme_use())\nbox = ttk.Combobox(root, values=style.theme_names(), state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;readonly&quot;</i>)\nbox.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(style.theme_use())\nbox.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nbox.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;&lt;ComboboxSelected&gt;&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: style.theme_use(box.get()))\nttk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Button&quot;</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nttk.Checkbutton(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Check&quot;</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nttk.Progressbar(root, value=<i class=\"n\">60</i>, length=<i class=\"n\">180</i>).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.modern-look"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() style = ttk.Style() print (style.theme_names(), \"current:\" , style.theme_use()) box = ttk.Combobox(root, values=style.theme_names(), state= \"readonly\" ) box. set (style.theme_use()) box.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) box.bind( \"<<ComboboxSelected>>\" , lambda e: style.theme_use(box.get())) ttk.Button(root, text= \"Button\" ).pack(pady= 4 ) ttk.Checkbutton(root, text= \"Check\" ).pack(pady= 4 ) ttk.Progressbar(root, value= 60 , length= 180 ).pack(pady=( 4 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.modern-look","s":"tk-style","q":"How do I make my tkinter app look modern?","qh":"How do I make my tkinter app look modern?","a":"Use ttk widgets everywhere, generous padding, one font, and a small colour palette. For a ready-made modern look, the third-party packages <code>sv-ttk</code> (Windows 11 style), <code>ttkbootstrap</code>, or <code>customtkinter</code> restyle everything.","aka":"modern ui, nicer gui, tkinter looks old, ugly tkinter, customtkinter, sv-ttk, sun valley, ttkbootstrap, beautiful tkinter","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Settings&quot;</i>)\nstyle = ttk.Style()\nstyle.theme_use(<i class=\"s\">&quot;clam&quot;</i>)\nBG, INK, ACCENT = <i class=\"s\">&quot;#f4f1ea&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;#1b1a17&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>\nroot.configure(bg=BG)\nstyle.configure(<i class=\"s\">&quot;.&quot;</i>, background=BG, foreground=INK, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">11</i>))\nstyle.configure(<i class=\"s\">&quot;TEntry&quot;</i>, fieldbackground=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, padding=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nstyle.configure(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Accent.TButton&quot;</i>, background=ACCENT, foreground=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, padding=(<i class=\"n\">14</i>, <i class=\"n\">8</i>), borderwidth=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\nstyle.<i class=\"b\">map</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Accent.TButton&quot;</i>, background=[(<i class=\"s\">&quot;active&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;#9e2f1b&quot;</i>)])\n\nfrm = ttk.Frame(root, padding=<i class=\"n\">24</i>)\nfrm.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nttk.Label(frm, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Profile&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">16</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;bold&quot;</i>)).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, columnspan=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">12</i>))\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> r, name <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">enumerate</i>((<i class=\"s\">&quot;Name&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Email&quot;</i>), start=<i class=\"n\">1</i>):\n    ttk.Label(frm, text=name).grid(row=r, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, padx=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">12</i>))\n    ttk.Entry(frm, width=<i class=\"n\">28</i>).grid(row=r, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nttk.Button(frm, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Save&quot;</i>, style=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Accent.TButton&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">3</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;e&quot;</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">12</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><figure class=\"code sh\" data-tag=\"terminal\"><pre>pip install sv-ttk          # then: import sv_ttk; sv_ttk.set_theme(&quot;dark&quot;)\npip install customtkinter   # its own CTk widgets with rounded corners</pre></figure>","see":["tk.ttk-style","tk.dark-mode"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() root.title( \"Settings\" ) style = ttk.Style() style.theme_use( \"clam\" ) BG, INK, ACCENT = \"#f4f1ea\" , \"#1b1a17\" , \"#c23b22\" root.configure(bg=BG) style.configure( \".\" , background=BG, foreground=INK, font=( \"Helvetica\" , 11 )) style.configure( \"TEntry\" , fieldbackground= \"white\" , padding= 6 ) style.configure( \"Accent.TButton\" , background=ACCENT, foreground= \"white\" , padding=( 14 , 8 ), borderwidth= 0 ) style. map ( \"Accent.TButton\" , background=[( \"active\" , \"#9e2f1b\" )]) frm = ttk.Frame(root, padding= 24 ) frm.pack(fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) ttk.Label(frm, text= \"Profile\" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 16 , \"bold\" )).grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , columnspan= 2 , sticky= \"w\" , pady=( 0 , 12 )) for r, name in enumerate (( \"Name\" , \"Email\" ), start= 1 ): ttk.Label(frm, text=name).grid(row=r, column= 0 , sticky= \"w\" , pady= 6 , padx=( 0 , 12 )) ttk.Entry(frm, width= 28 ).grid(row=r, column= 1 , pady= 6 ) ttk.Button(frm, text= \"Save\" , style= \"Accent.TButton\" ).grid(row= 3 , column= 1 , sticky= \"e\" , pady=( 12 , 0 )) root.mainloop() pip install sv-ttk # then: import sv_ttk; sv_ttk.set_theme(\"dark\") pip install customtkinter # its own CTk widgets with rounded corners"},{"id":"tk.dark-mode","s":"tk-style","q":"How do I make a dark mode (and switch between light and dark)?","qh":"How do I make a dark mode (and switch between light and dark)?","a":"Keep your colours in a dict, and write one function that applies a palette to every widget (walk <code>winfo_children()</code> recursively). For ttk, reconfigure the style.","aka":"dark theme, dark mode, night mode, toggle theme, black background","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nTHEMES = {\n    <i class=\"s\">&quot;light&quot;</i>: {<i class=\"s\">&quot;bg&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;#f4f1ea&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;fg&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;#1b1a17&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;field&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>},\n    <i class=\"s\">&quot;dark&quot;</i>:  {<i class=\"s\">&quot;bg&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;#1d1c1a&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;fg&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;#ece6d8&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;field&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;#2d2b28&quot;</i>},\n}\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">apply</i>(widget, t):\n    cls = widget.winfo_class()\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> cls <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Entry&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Text&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Listbox&quot;</i>):\n        widget.config(bg=t[<i class=\"s\">&quot;field&quot;</i>], fg=t[<i class=\"s\">&quot;fg&quot;</i>], insertbackground=t[<i class=\"s\">&quot;fg&quot;</i>])\n    <i class=\"k\">elif</i> cls <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Label&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Button&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Checkbutton&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Radiobutton&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Frame&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Tk&quot;</i>):\n        widget.config(bg=t[<i class=\"s\">&quot;bg&quot;</i>])\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> cls != <i class=\"s\">&quot;Frame&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">and</i> cls != <i class=\"s\">&quot;Tk&quot;</i>:\n            widget.config(fg=t[<i class=\"s\">&quot;fg&quot;</i>])\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> child <i class=\"k\">in</i> widget.winfo_children():\n        apply(child, t)\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Name&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>))\ntk.Entry(root).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nmode = tk.StringVar(value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;light&quot;</i>)\ntk.Checkbutton(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Dark&quot;</i>, onvalue=<i class=\"s\">&quot;dark&quot;</i>, offvalue=<i class=\"s\">&quot;light&quot;</i>, variable=mode,\n               command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: apply(root, THEMES[mode.get()])).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\napply(root, THEMES[<i class=\"s\">&quot;light&quot;</i>])\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>On macOS, <code>tk.Button</code> colours can't be changed (see <a href=\"#/a/tk.button-color\">How do I change a button's colour? (And why doesn't it work on Mac?)</a>).</p>","see":["tk.ttk-style"],"t":"import tkinter as tk THEMES = { \"light\" : { \"bg\" : \"#f4f1ea\" , \"fg\" : \"#1b1a17\" , \"field\" : \"white\" }, \"dark\" : { \"bg\" : \"#1d1c1a\" , \"fg\" : \"#ece6d8\" , \"field\" : \"#2d2b28\" }, } def apply (widget, t): cls = widget.winfo_class() if cls in ( \"Entry\" , \"Text\" , \"Listbox\" ): widget.config(bg=t[ \"field\" ], fg=t[ \"fg\" ], insertbackground=t[ \"fg\" ]) elif cls in ( \"Label\" , \"Button\" , \"Checkbutton\" , \"Radiobutton\" , \"Frame\" , \"Tk\" ): widget.config(bg=t[ \"bg\" ]) if cls != \"Frame\" and cls != \"Tk\" : widget.config(fg=t[ \"fg\" ]) for child in widget.winfo_children(): apply(child, t) root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"Name\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady=( 10 , 0 )) tk.Entry(root).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 6 ) mode = tk.StringVar(value= \"light\" ) tk.Checkbutton(root, text= \"Dark\" , onvalue= \"dark\" , offvalue= \"light\" , variable=mode, command= lambda : apply(root, THEMES[mode.get()])).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) apply(root, THEMES[ \"light\" ]) root.mainloop() On macOS, tk.Button colours can't be changed (see How do I change a button's colour? (And why doesn't it work on Mac?) )."},{"id":"tk.border-color","s":"tk-style","q":"How do I give a widget a coloured border?","qh":"How do I give a widget a coloured border?","a":"Use <code>highlightthickness</code> + <code>highlightbackground</code> (and <code>highlightcolor</code> when focused), or wrap the widget in a Frame with a background colour and padding.","aka":"border colour, highlightbackground, highlightcolor, outline widget, colored border entry","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Entry(root, highlightthickness=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, highlightbackground=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#bbb&quot;</i>, highlightcolor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>, relief=<i class=\"s\">&quot;flat&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nwrap = tk.Frame(root, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">2</i>)\nwrap.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Label(wrap, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;framed label&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>).pack()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Entry(root, highlightthickness= 2 , highlightbackground= \"#bbb\" , highlightcolor= \"#c23b22\" , relief= \"flat\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) wrap = tk.Frame(root, bg= \"#c23b22\" , padx= 2 , pady= 2 ) wrap.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) tk.Label(wrap, text= \"framed label\" , padx= 10 , pady= 6 ).pack() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.rounded","s":"tk-style","q":"Can I make rounded buttons or rounded corners?","qh":"Can I make rounded buttons or rounded corners?","a":"Standard tk/ttk widgets have square corners. Options: draw a rounded shape on a Canvas and bind clicks to it, use an image of a rounded button, or use <code>customtkinter</code> (which has <code>corner_radius</code>).","aka":"rounded corners, round button, border radius tkinter, rounded rectangle canvas","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">round_rect</i>(c, x1, y1, x2, y2, r, **kw):\n    pts = [x1 + r, y1, x2 - r, y1, x2, y1, x2, y1 + r, x2, y2 - r, x2, y2,\n           x2 - r, y2, x1 + r, y2, x1, y2, x1, y2 - r, x1, y1 + r, x1, y1]\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> c.create_polygon(pts, smooth=<i class=\"k\">True</i>, **kw)\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">200</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">80</i>, highlightthickness=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\nc.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nround_rect(c, <i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">190</i>, <i class=\"n\">70</i>, <i class=\"n\">20</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>, tags=<i class=\"s\">&quot;btn&quot;</i>)\nc.create_text(<i class=\"n\">100</i>, <i class=\"n\">40</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Rounded&quot;</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">14</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;bold&quot;</i>), tags=<i class=\"s\">&quot;btn&quot;</i>)\nc.tag_bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;btn&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;clicked&quot;</i>))\nc.config(cursor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;hand2&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk def round_rect (c, x1, y1, x2, y2, r, **kw): pts = [x1 + r, y1, x2 - r, y1, x2, y1, x2, y1 + r, x2, y2 - r, x2, y2, x2 - r, y2, x1 + r, y2, x1, y2, x1, y2 - r, x1, y1 + r, x1, y1] return c.create_polygon(pts, smooth= True , **kw) root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 200 , height= 80 , highlightthickness= 0 ) c.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) round_rect(c, 10 , 10 , 190 , 70 , 20 , fill= \"#c23b22\" , tags= \"btn\" ) c.create_text( 100 , 40 , text= \"Rounded\" , fill= \"white\" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 14 , \"bold\" ), tags= \"btn\" ) c.tag_bind( \"btn\" , \"<Button-1>\" , lambda e: print ( \"clicked\" )) c.config(cursor= \"hand2\" ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.option-add","s":"tk-style","q":"How do I set default options (like colours) for every widget of a type?","qh":"How do I set default options (like colours) for every widget of a type?","a":"<code>root.option_add(&quot;*Button.background&quot;, &quot;#fc6&quot;)</code> before creating widgets. <code>&quot;*Font&quot;</code> sets the font for everything.","aka":"option_add, default bg for all buttons, global style tk widgets, option database","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.option_add(<i class=\"s\">&quot;*Font&quot;</i>, (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">12</i>))\nroot.option_add(<i class=\"s\">&quot;*Button.background&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;#ffcc66&quot;</i>)\nroot.option_add(<i class=\"s\">&quot;*Label.foreground&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;red label&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;yellow button&quot;</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.option_add( \"*Font\" , ( \"Helvetica\" , 12 )) root.option_add( \"*Button.background\" , \"#ffcc66\" ) root.option_add( \"*Label.foreground\" , \"#c23b22\" ) tk.Label(root, text= \"red label\" ).pack(padx= 30 , pady= 4 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"yellow button\" ).pack(pady= 4 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.treeview","s":"tk-adv","q":"How do I show a table (rows and columns)?","qh":"How do I show a table (rows and columns)?","a":"Use <code>ttk.Treeview(root, columns=..., show=&quot;headings&quot;)</code>, set each <code>heading</code> and <code>column</code>, then <code>insert(&quot;&quot;, &quot;end&quot;, values=(...))</code> for each row.","aka":"table, treeview, data grid, spreadsheet view, columns, show data in table, ttk.Treeview, listview","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ncols = (<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;age&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;city&quot;</i>)\ntree = ttk.Treeview(root, columns=cols, show=<i class=\"s\">&quot;headings&quot;</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> c <i class=\"k\">in</i> cols:\n    tree.heading(c, text=c.title())\n    tree.column(c, width=<i class=\"n\">100</i>, anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>)\ntree.column(<i class=\"s\">&quot;age&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">50</i>, anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;center&quot;</i>)\n\npeople = [(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">36</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;London&quot;</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Linus&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">28</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Helsinki&quot;</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Grace&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">45</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;New York&quot;</i>)]\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> p <i class=\"k\">in</i> people:\n    tree.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, values=p)\n\nsb = ttk.Scrollbar(root, orient=<i class=\"s\">&quot;vertical&quot;</i>, command=tree.yview)\ntree.configure(yscrollcommand=sb.<i class=\"b\">set</i>)\ntree.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>, padx=(<i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>), pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nsb.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Without <code>show=&quot;headings&quot;</code> you get an extra empty first column (the tree column <code>#0</code>).</p>","see":["tk.treeview-select","tk.treeview-edit","tk.treeview-sort"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() cols = ( \"name\" , \"age\" , \"city\" ) tree = ttk.Treeview(root, columns=cols, show= \"headings\" , height= 6 ) for c in cols: tree.heading(c, text=c.title()) tree.column(c, width= 100 , anchor= \"w\" ) tree.column( \"age\" , width= 50 , anchor= \"center\" ) people = [( \"Ada\" , 36 , \"London\" ), ( \"Linus\" , 28 , \"Helsinki\" ), ( \"Grace\" , 45 , \"New York\" )] for p in people: tree.insert( \"\" , \"end\" , values=p) sb = ttk.Scrollbar(root, orient= \"vertical\" , command=tree.yview) tree.configure(yscrollcommand=sb. set ) tree.pack(side= \"left\" , fill= \"both\" , expand= True , padx=( 10 , 0 ), pady= 10 ) sb.pack(side= \"left\" , fill= \"y\" , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() Without show=\"headings\" you get an extra empty first column (the tree column #0 )."},{"id":"tk.treeview-select","s":"tk-adv","q":"How do I get the selected row in a Treeview?","qh":"How do I get the selected row in a Treeview?","a":"<code>tree.selection()</code> gives the selected item ids; <code>tree.item(iid, &quot;values&quot;)</code> gives that row's values. Bind <code>&quot;&lt;&lt;TreeviewSelect&gt;&gt;&quot;</code> to react.","aka":"treeview selection, selected row, TreeviewSelect, get values treeview, focus, click row","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntree = ttk.Treeview(root, columns=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;item&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;price&quot;</i>), show=<i class=\"s\">&quot;headings&quot;</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntree.heading(<i class=\"s\">&quot;item&quot;</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Item&quot;</i>); tree.heading(<i class=\"s\">&quot;price&quot;</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Price&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> row <i class=\"k\">in</i> [(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Tea&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">2.5</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Cake&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">3.0</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Soup&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">4.2</i>)]:\n    tree.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, values=row)\ntree.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ninfo = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\ninfo.pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">selected</i>(event):\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> iid <i class=\"k\">in</i> tree.selection():\n        item, price = tree.item(iid, <i class=\"s\">&quot;values&quot;</i>)      <i class=\"c\"># values come back as strings</i>\n        info.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{item}<i class=\"s\">: </i>{<i class=\"b\">float</i>(price):<i class=\"s\">.2f</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\ntree.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;&lt;TreeviewSelect&gt;&gt;&quot;</i>, selected)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() tree = ttk.Treeview(root, columns=( \"item\" , \"price\" ), show= \"headings\" , height= 4 ) tree.heading( \"item\" , text= \"Item\" ); tree.heading( \"price\" , text= \"Price\" ) for row in [( \"Tea\" , 2.5 ), ( \"Cake\" , 3.0 ), ( \"Soup\" , 4.2 )]: tree.insert( \"\" , \"end\" , values=row) tree.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) info = tk.Label(root, text= \"\" ) info.pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) def selected (event): for iid in tree.selection(): item, price = tree.item(iid, \"values\" ) # values come back as strings info.config(text= f\" {item} : { float (price): .2f } \" ) tree.bind( \"<<TreeviewSelect>>\" , selected) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.treeview-edit","s":"tk-adv","q":"How do I update, delete or clear rows in a Treeview?","qh":"How do I update, delete or clear rows in a Treeview?","a":"Update with <code>tree.item(iid, values=new)</code>, delete with <code>tree.delete(iid)</code>, clear all with <code>tree.delete(*tree.get_children())</code>.","aka":"treeview delete, treeview update row, clear treeview, delete all rows, edit row treeview, treeview set","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntree = ttk.Treeview(root, columns=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;task&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;done&quot;</i>), show=<i class=\"s\">&quot;headings&quot;</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntree.heading(<i class=\"s\">&quot;task&quot;</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Task&quot;</i>); tree.heading(<i class=\"s\">&quot;done&quot;</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Done&quot;</i>)\ntree.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> t <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Shop&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Code&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Sleep&quot;</i>):\n    tree.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, values=(t, <i class=\"s\">&quot;no&quot;</i>))\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">mark_done</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> iid <i class=\"k\">in</i> tree.selection():\n        task, _ = tree.item(iid, <i class=\"s\">&quot;values&quot;</i>)\n        tree.item(iid, values=(task, <i class=\"s\">&quot;yes&quot;</i>))\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">remove</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> iid <i class=\"k\">in</i> tree.selection():\n        tree.delete(iid)\n\nbar = tk.Frame(root); bar.pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\ntk.Button(bar, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Done&quot;</i>, command=mark_done).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Button(bar, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Delete&quot;</i>, command=remove).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Button(bar, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Clear&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: tree.delete(*tree.get_children())).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>To edit a cell by double-clicking, place an Entry over it using <code>tree.bbox(iid, column)</code>; there's no built-in cell editor.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() tree = ttk.Treeview(root, columns=( \"task\" , \"done\" ), show= \"headings\" , height= 5 ) tree.heading( \"task\" , text= \"Task\" ); tree.heading( \"done\" , text= \"Done\" ) tree.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) for t in ( \"Shop\" , \"Code\" , \"Sleep\" ): tree.insert( \"\" , \"end\" , values=(t, \"no\" )) def mark_done (): for iid in tree.selection(): task, _ = tree.item(iid, \"values\" ) tree.item(iid, values=(task, \"yes\" )) def remove (): for iid in tree.selection(): tree.delete(iid) bar = tk.Frame(root); bar.pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) tk.Button(bar, text= \"Done\" , command=mark_done).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 4 ) tk.Button(bar, text= \"Delete\" , command=remove).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 4 ) tk.Button(bar, text= \"Clear\" , command= lambda : tree.delete(*tree.get_children())).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 4 ) root.mainloop() To edit a cell by double-clicking, place an Entry over it using tree.bbox(iid, column) ; there's no built-in cell editor."},{"id":"tk.treeview-sort","s":"tk-adv","q":"How do I sort a Treeview by clicking a column heading?","qh":"How do I sort a Treeview by clicking a column heading?","a":"Give each heading a <code>command</code> that reads all rows, sorts them, and moves them into the new order with <code>tree.move</code>.","aka":"sort treeview, sort table column, click heading to sort","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ncols = (<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;score&quot;</i>)\ntree = ttk.Treeview(root, columns=cols, show=<i class=\"s\">&quot;headings&quot;</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> r <i class=\"k\">in</i> [(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">90</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Sam&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">72</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Kim&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">85</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Lee&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">99</i>)]:\n    tree.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, values=r)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">sort_by</i>(col, reverse=<i class=\"k\">False</i>):\n    rows = [(tree.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(iid, col), iid) <i class=\"k\">for</i> iid <i class=\"k\">in</i> tree.get_children(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)]\n    rows.sort(key=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i> t: <i class=\"b\">float</i>(t[<i class=\"n\">0</i>]) <i class=\"k\">if</i> t[<i class=\"n\">0</i>].replace(<i class=\"s\">&quot;.&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>).isdigit() <i class=\"k\">else</i> t[<i class=\"n\">0</i>].lower(), reverse=reverse)\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> index, (_, iid) <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">enumerate</i>(rows):\n        tree.move(iid, <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, index)\n    tree.heading(col, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: sort_by(col, <i class=\"k\">not</i> reverse))\n\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> c <i class=\"k\">in</i> cols:\n    tree.heading(c, text=c.title(), command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i> c=c: sort_by(c))\ntree.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() cols = ( \"name\" , \"score\" ) tree = ttk.Treeview(root, columns=cols, show= \"headings\" , height= 5 ) for r in [( \"Ada\" , 90 ), ( \"Sam\" , 72 ), ( \"Kim\" , 85 ), ( \"Lee\" , 99 )]: tree.insert( \"\" , \"end\" , values=r) def sort_by (col, reverse= False ): rows = [(tree. set (iid, col), iid) for iid in tree.get_children( \"\" )] rows.sort(key= lambda t: float (t[ 0 ]) if t[ 0 ].replace( \".\" , \"\" , 1 ).isdigit() else t[ 0 ].lower(), reverse=reverse) for index, (_, iid) in enumerate (rows): tree.move(iid, \"\" , index) tree.heading(col, command= lambda : sort_by(col, not reverse)) for c in cols: tree.heading(c, text=c.title(), command= lambda c=c: sort_by(c)) tree.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.treeview-tree","s":"tk-adv","q":"How do I make a tree (folders with children) in a Treeview?","qh":"How do I make a tree (folders with children) in a Treeview?","a":"Insert children with the parent's id as the first argument; <code>open=True</code> expands it.","aka":"tree view, hierarchy, nested items, folders tree, parent child items, expand collapse","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntree = ttk.Treeview(root, height=<i class=\"n\">8</i>)\ntree.heading(<i class=\"s\">&quot;#0&quot;</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Files&quot;</i>)\nsrc = tree.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;src&quot;</i>, <i class=\"b\">open</i>=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\ntree.insert(src, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;main.py&quot;</i>)\ntree.insert(src, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;utils.py&quot;</i>)\ndocs = tree.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;docs&quot;</i>)\ntree.insert(docs, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;readme.md&quot;</i>)\ntree.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() tree = ttk.Treeview(root, height= 8 ) tree.heading( \"#0\" , text= \"Files\" ) src = tree.insert( \"\" , \"end\" , text= \"src\" , open = True ) tree.insert(src, \"end\" , text= \"main.py\" ) tree.insert(src, \"end\" , text= \"utils.py\" ) docs = tree.insert( \"\" , \"end\" , text= \"docs\" ) tree.insert(docs, \"end\" , text= \"readme.md\" ) tree.pack(fill= \"both\" , expand= True , padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.treeview-style","s":"tk-adv","q":"How do I change Treeview row height, fonts, or stripe the rows?","qh":"How do I change Treeview row height, fonts, or stripe the rows?","a":"Row height and font via <code>ttk.Style().configure(&quot;Treeview&quot;, rowheight=28, font=...)</code>. Row colours via tags: <code>tree.tag_configure(&quot;odd&quot;, background=&quot;#eee&quot;)</code>.","aka":"treeview row height, treeview font, alternating row colors, striped rows, treeview tag colour","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nstyle = ttk.Style()\nstyle.theme_use(<i class=\"s\">&quot;clam&quot;</i>)\nstyle.configure(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Treeview&quot;</i>, rowheight=<i class=\"n\">26</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">11</i>))\nstyle.configure(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Treeview.Heading&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">11</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;bold&quot;</i>))\ntree = ttk.Treeview(root, columns=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;n&quot;</i>,), show=<i class=\"s\">&quot;headings&quot;</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\ntree.heading(<i class=\"s\">&quot;n&quot;</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Number&quot;</i>)\ntree.tag_configure(<i class=\"s\">&quot;odd&quot;</i>, background=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#efeae0&quot;</i>)\ntree.tag_configure(<i class=\"s\">&quot;big&quot;</i>, foreground=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">8</i>):\n    tags = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;odd&quot;</i>] <i class=\"k\">if</i> i % <i class=\"n\">2</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> []\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> i &gt; <i class=\"n\">5</i>:\n        tags.append(<i class=\"s\">&quot;big&quot;</i>)\n    tree.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, values=(i * <i class=\"n\">111</i>,), tags=tags)\ntree.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() style = ttk.Style() style.theme_use( \"clam\" ) style.configure( \"Treeview\" , rowheight= 26 , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 11 )) style.configure( \"Treeview.Heading\" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 11 , \"bold\" )) tree = ttk.Treeview(root, columns=( \"n\" ,), show= \"headings\" , height= 6 ) tree.heading( \"n\" , text= \"Number\" ) tree.tag_configure( \"odd\" , background= \"#efeae0\" ) tree.tag_configure( \"big\" , foreground= \"#c23b22\" ) for i in range ( 8 ): tags = [ \"odd\" ] if i % 2 else [] if i > 5 : tags.append( \"big\" ) tree.insert( \"\" , \"end\" , values=(i * 111 ,), tags=tags) tree.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.notebook","s":"tk-adv","q":"How do I make tabs?","qh":"How do I make tabs?","a":"<code>nb = ttk.Notebook(root)</code>, make a Frame per tab, then <code>nb.add(frame, text=&quot;Tab name&quot;)</code>.","aka":"tabs, notebook, tabbed window, ttk.Notebook, tab pages, switch tabs","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nnb = ttk.Notebook(root)\nnb.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\nhome = ttk.Frame(nb, padding=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nttk.Label(home, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Welcome home&quot;</i>).pack()\nsettings = ttk.Frame(nb, padding=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nttk.Checkbutton(settings, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Sound on&quot;</i>).pack(anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>)\n\nnb.add(home, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Home&quot;</i>)\nnb.add(settings, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Settings&quot;</i>)\nnb.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;&lt;NotebookTabChanged&gt;&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Tab:&quot;</i>, nb.tab(nb.select(), <i class=\"s\">&quot;text&quot;</i>)))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Switch tabs from code with <code>nb.select(1)</code> or <code>nb.select(settings)</code>; remove one with <code>nb.forget(frame)</code>.</p>","see":["tk.pages"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() nb = ttk.Notebook(root) nb.pack(fill= \"both\" , expand= True , padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) home = ttk.Frame(nb, padding= 20 ) ttk.Label(home, text= \"Welcome home\" ).pack() settings = ttk.Frame(nb, padding= 20 ) ttk.Checkbutton(settings, text= \"Sound on\" ).pack(anchor= \"w\" ) nb.add(home, text= \"Home\" ) nb.add(settings, text= \"Settings\" ) nb.bind( \"<<NotebookTabChanged>>\" , lambda e: print ( \"Tab:\" , nb.tab(nb.select(), \"text\" ))) root.mainloop() Switch tabs from code with nb.select(1) or nb.select(settings) ; remove one with nb.forget(frame) ."},{"id":"tk.progressbar","s":"tk-adv","q":"How do I show a progress bar?","qh":"How do I show a progress bar?","a":"<code>ttk.Progressbar(root, maximum=100, length=200)</code>; set <code>bar[&quot;value&quot;] = n</code>. For \"busy, unknown length\" use <code>mode=&quot;indeterminate&quot;</code> and <code>bar.start()</code>.","aka":"progress bar, loading bar, ttk.Progressbar, percent complete, indeterminate spinner, loading animation","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nbar = ttk.Progressbar(root, maximum=<i class=\"n\">100</i>, length=<i class=\"n\">240</i>)\nbar.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\npct = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;0%&quot;</i>)\npct.pack()\nbusy = ttk.Progressbar(root, mode=<i class=\"s\">&quot;indeterminate&quot;</i>, length=<i class=\"n\">240</i>)\nbusy.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nbusy.start(<i class=\"n\">15</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">step</i>(n=<i class=\"n\">0</i>):\n    bar[<i class=\"s\">&quot;value&quot;</i>] = n\n    pct.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{n}<i class=\"s\">%</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> n &lt; <i class=\"n\">100</i>:\n        root.after(<i class=\"n\">30</i>, step, n + <i class=\"n\">2</i>)\n\nstep()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>If the bar only jumps to the end, your work is blocking the main loop; update it from <code>after()</code> steps or a <a href=\"#/a/tk.thread\">thread</a>.</p>","see":["tk.thread"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() bar = ttk.Progressbar(root, maximum= 100 , length= 240 ) bar.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) pct = tk.Label(root, text= \"0%\" ) pct.pack() busy = ttk.Progressbar(root, mode= \"indeterminate\" , length= 240 ) busy.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) busy.start( 15 ) def step (n= 0 ): bar[ \"value\" ] = n pct.config(text= f\" {n} % \" ) if n < 100 : root.after( 30 , step, n + 2 ) step() root.mainloop() If the bar only jumps to the end, your work is blocking the main loop; update it from after() steps or a thread ."},{"id":"tk.sizegrip-status","s":"tk-adv","q":"How do I add a status bar at the bottom of the window?","qh":"How do I add a status bar at the bottom of the window?","a":"A Label (or Frame) packed with <code>side=&quot;bottom&quot;, fill=&quot;x&quot;</code> before the main content. <code>ttk.Sizegrip</code> adds the resize handle in the corner.","aka":"status bar, footer bar, bottom bar, sizegrip","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;320x200&quot;</i>)\nstatus = tk.Frame(root, relief=<i class=\"s\">&quot;sunken&quot;</i>, bd=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\nstatus.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;bottom&quot;</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>)\nmsg = tk.Label(status, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ready&quot;</i>, anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>)\nmsg.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nttk.Sizegrip(status).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;right&quot;</i>)\ntk.Text(root).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"320x200\" ) status = tk.Frame(root, relief= \"sunken\" , bd= 1 ) status.pack(side= \"bottom\" , fill= \"x\" ) msg = tk.Label(status, text= \"Ready\" , anchor= \"w\" ) msg.pack(side= \"left\" , fill= \"x\" , expand= True ) ttk.Sizegrip(status).pack(side= \"right\" ) tk.Text(root).pack(fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.calendar","s":"tk-adv","q":"Is there a date picker / calendar widget?","qh":"Is there a date picker / calendar widget?","a":"Not built in. Install <code>tkcalendar</code> (<code>pip install tkcalendar</code>) for <code>Calendar</code> and <code>DateEntry</code>, or build simple day/month/year Spinboxes.","aka":"date picker, calendar widget, choose date, tkcalendar, DateEntry","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkcalendar <i class=\"k\">import</i> DateEntry      <i class=\"c\"># pip install tkcalendar</i>\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nd = DateEntry(root, date_pattern=<i class=\"s\">&quot;yyyy-mm-dd&quot;</i>)\nd.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;OK&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(d.get_date())).pack()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>No-install version:</p><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> datetime\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntoday = datetime.date.today()\nday = tk.Spinbox(root, from_=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, to=<i class=\"n\">31</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">3</i>); day.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>); day.insert(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, today.day)\nmonth = tk.Spinbox(root, from_=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, to=<i class=\"n\">12</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">3</i>); month.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>); month.insert(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, today.month)\nyear = tk.Spinbox(root, from_=<i class=\"n\">1900</i>, to=<i class=\"n\">2100</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">5</i>); year.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>); year.insert(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, today.year)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> w <i class=\"k\">in</i> (day, month, year):\n    w.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">read</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(datetime.date(<i class=\"b\">int</i>(year.get()), <i class=\"b\">int</i>(month.get()), <i class=\"b\">int</i>(day.get())))\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> ValueError <i class=\"k\">as</i> e:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Bad date:&quot;</i>, e)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;OK&quot;</i>, command=read).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkcalendar import DateEntry # pip install tkcalendar root = tk.Tk() d = DateEntry(root, date_pattern= \"yyyy-mm-dd\" ) d.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 20 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"OK\" , command= lambda : print (d.get_date())).pack() root.mainloop() No-install version: import tkinter as tk import datetime root = tk.Tk() today = datetime.date.today() day = tk.Spinbox(root, from_= 1 , to= 31 , width= 3 ); day.delete( 0 , \"end\" ); day.insert( 0 , today.day) month = tk.Spinbox(root, from_= 1 , to= 12 , width= 3 ); month.delete( 0 , \"end\" ); month.insert( 0 , today.month) year = tk.Spinbox(root, from_= 1900 , to= 2100 , width= 5 ); year.delete( 0 , \"end\" ); year.insert( 0 , today.year) for w in (day, month, year): w.pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 4 , pady= 10 ) def read (): try : print (datetime.date( int (year.get()), int (month.get()), int (day.get()))) except ValueError as e: print ( \"Bad date:\" , e) tk.Button(root, text= \"OK\" , command=read).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 6 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.app-class","s":"tk-structure","q":"How do I write a tkinter app as a class?","qh":"How do I write a tkinter app as a class?","a":"Subclass <code>tk.Tk</code> (or <code>tk.Frame</code>), build widgets in <code>__init__</code>, store anything you need later on <code>self</code>, and make callbacks methods.","aka":"tkinter class, oop tkinter, class based gui, self in tkinter, inherit Tk, App class, object oriented tkinter","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Counter</i>(tk.Tk):\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"b\">super</i>().__init__()\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Counter&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.count = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.label = tk.Label(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;0&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">32</i>))\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.label.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n        tk.Button(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;+1&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.add).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n        tk.Button(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Reset&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.reset).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;right&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">add</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.count += <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.label.config(text=<i class=\"b\">str</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>.count))\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">reset</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.count = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.label.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;0&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">if</i> __name__ == <i class=\"s\">&quot;__main__&quot;</i>:\n    Counter().mainloop()</pre></figure><p>No <code>global</code> needed: everything lives on <code>self</code>. Remember <code>self.</code> both when storing (<code>self.label = ...</code>) and using (<code>self.label.config</code>).</p>","see":["tk.pages","py.class-basics"],"t":"import tkinter as tk class Counter (tk.Tk): def __init__ ( self ): super ().__init__() self .title( \"Counter\" ) self .count = 0 self .label = tk.Label( self , text= \"0\" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 32 )) self .label.pack(padx= 40 , pady= 10 ) tk.Button( self , text= \"+1\" , command= self .add).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) tk.Button( self , text= \"Reset\" , command= self .reset).pack(side= \"right\" , padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) def add ( self ): self .count += 1 self .label.config(text= str ( self .count)) def reset ( self ): self .count = 0 self .label.config(text= \"0\" ) if __name__ == \"__main__\" : Counter().mainloop() No global needed: everything lives on self . Remember self. both when storing ( self.label = ... ) and using ( self.label.config )."},{"id":"tk.pages","s":"tk-structure","q":"How do I switch between pages/screens (menu screen, game screen...)?","qh":"How do I switch between pages/screens (menu screen, game screen...)?","a":"Make each page a Frame, stack them all in the same grid cell, and call <code>tkraise()</code> on the one to show. Or destroy the old page and build the new one.","aka":"multiple pages, switch frames, change screen, next page button, login then main page, show frame, screens tkinter, navigate pages","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">App</i>(tk.Tk):\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"b\">super</i>().__init__()\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;300x180&quot;</i>)\n        box = tk.Frame(<i class=\"k\">self</i>)\n        box.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n        box.rowconfigure(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, weight=<i class=\"n\">1</i>); box.columnconfigure(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, weight=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.pages = {}\n        <i class=\"k\">for</i> Page <i class=\"k\">in</i> (Home, Game):\n            p = Page(box, <i class=\"k\">self</i>)\n            p.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;nsew&quot;</i>)\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.pages[Page.__name__] = p\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.show(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Home&quot;</i>)\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">show</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, name):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.pages[name].tkraise()\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Home</i>(tk.Frame):\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, parent, app):\n        <i class=\"b\">super</i>().__init__(parent)\n        tk.Label(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Main menu&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">18</i>)).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n        tk.Button(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Play&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: app.show(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Game&quot;</i>)).pack()\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Game</i>(tk.Frame):\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, parent, app):\n        <i class=\"b\">super</i>().__init__(parent, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#203040&quot;</i>)\n        tk.Label(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Game screen&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#203040&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">18</i>)).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n        tk.Button(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Back&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: app.show(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Home&quot;</i>)).pack()\n\nApp().mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.app-class","tk.notebook"],"t":"import tkinter as tk class App (tk.Tk): def __init__ ( self ): super ().__init__() self .geometry( \"300x180\" ) box = tk.Frame( self ) box.pack(fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) box.rowconfigure( 0 , weight= 1 ); box.columnconfigure( 0 , weight= 1 ) self .pages = {} for Page in (Home, Game): p = Page(box, self ) p.grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , sticky= \"nsew\" ) self .pages[Page.__name__] = p self .show( \"Home\" ) def show ( self , name): self .pages[name].tkraise() class Home (tk.Frame): def __init__ ( self , parent, app): super ().__init__(parent) tk.Label( self , text= \"Main menu\" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 18 )).pack(pady= 20 ) tk.Button( self , text= \"Play\" , command= lambda : app.show( \"Game\" )).pack() class Game (tk.Frame): def __init__ ( self , parent, app): super ().__init__(parent, bg= \"#203040\" ) tk.Label( self , text= \"Game screen\" , fg= \"white\" , bg= \"#203040\" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 18 )).pack(pady= 20 ) tk.Button( self , text= \"Back\" , command= lambda : app.show( \"Home\" )).pack() App().mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.pass-data-windows","s":"tk-structure","q":"How do I pass data between windows or pages?","qh":"How do I pass data between windows or pages?","a":"Keep shared data in one place (the App object or a dict), give each page/window a reference to it, and pass callbacks for \"send the answer back\".","aka":"share data between windows, pass variable to another window, return value from toplevel, communicate between frames, access widget from another class","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">App</i>(tk.Tk):\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"b\">super</i>().__init__()\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.username = tk.StringVar(value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;nobody&quot;</i>)\n        tk.Label(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Logged in as:&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">16</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>))\n        tk.Label(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, textvariable=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.username, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">14</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;bold&quot;</i>)).pack()\n        tk.Button(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Change...&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.open_dialog).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">12</i>)\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">open_dialog</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        NameDialog(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, on_done=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.username.<i class=\"b\">set</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">NameDialog</i>(tk.Toplevel):\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, master, on_done):\n        <i class=\"b\">super</i>().__init__(master)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.on_done = on_done\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.entry = tk.Entry(<i class=\"k\">self</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.entry.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n        tk.Button(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;OK&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.ok).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">ok</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.on_done(<i class=\"k\">self</i>.entry.get())      <i class=\"c\"># send the value back</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.destroy()\n\nApp().mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.pages","tk.modal"],"t":"import tkinter as tk class App (tk.Tk): def __init__ ( self ): super ().__init__() self .username = tk.StringVar(value= \"nobody\" ) tk.Label( self , text= \"Logged in as:\" ).pack(padx= 30 , pady=( 16 , 0 )) tk.Label( self , textvariable= self .username, font=( \"Helvetica\" , 14 , \"bold\" )).pack() tk.Button( self , text= \"Change...\" , command= self .open_dialog).pack(pady= 12 ) def open_dialog ( self ): NameDialog( self , on_done= self .username. set ) class NameDialog (tk.Toplevel): def __init__ ( self , master, on_done): super ().__init__(master) self .on_done = on_done self .entry = tk.Entry( self ) self .entry.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) tk.Button( self , text= \"OK\" , command= self .ok).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) def ok ( self ): self .on_done( self .entry.get()) # send the value back self .destroy() App().mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.split-files","s":"tk-structure","q":"How do I split a big tkinter program into several files?","qh":"How do I split a big tkinter program into several files?","a":"Put each page/window class in its own <code>.py</code> file in the same folder and <code>import</code> it. Keep logic (calculations, saving) in files that don't import tkinter at all, so they're easy to test.","aka":"multiple files, modules tkinter, import my own file, organize code, project structure, separate gui and logic","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code out\" data-tag=\"output\"><pre>my_app/\n    main.py          # creates App and calls mainloop()\n    pages.py         # class Home(tk.Frame), class Game(tk.Frame)\n    storage.py       # load_scores(), save_scores()  (no tkinter here)</pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"c\"># main.py</i>\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> pages <i class=\"k\">import</i> Home, Game\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> storage\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">App</i>(tk.Tk):\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"b\">super</i>().__init__()\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.scores = storage.load_scores()\n        ...\n\n<i class=\"k\">if</i> __name__ == <i class=\"s\">&quot;__main__&quot;</i>:\n    App().mainloop()</pre></figure><p>If two files import each other you get a circular import; pass objects in as arguments instead.</p>","see":["py.own-module","py.err.circular-import"],"t":"my_app/ main.py # creates App and calls mainloop() pages.py # class Home(tk.Frame), class Game(tk.Frame) storage.py # load_scores(), save_scores() (no tkinter here) # main.py import tkinter as tk from pages import Home, Game import storage class App (tk.Tk): def __init__ ( self ): super ().__init__() self .scores = storage.load_scores() ... if __name__ == \"__main__\" : App().mainloop() If two files import each other you get a circular import; pass objects in as arguments instead."},{"id":"tk.save-settings","s":"tk-structure","q":"How do I save settings or data between runs?","qh":"How do I save settings or data between runs?","a":"Save a dict to a JSON file when things change (or on close), and load it at start. Handle the first run when the file doesn't exist yet.","aka":"save data, remember settings, persist, json save load, save high score, config file, store data tkinter","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> json\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> pathlib <i class=\"k\">import</i> Path\n\nFILE = Path(<i class=\"s\">&quot;settings.json&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">load</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> json.loads(FILE.read_text())\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> (FileNotFoundError, json.JSONDecodeError):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> {<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;volume&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">50</i>}\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">save</i>():\n    FILE.write_text(json.dumps({<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>: name.get(), <i class=\"s\">&quot;volume&quot;</i>: volume.get()}, indent=<i class=\"n\">2</i>))\n    root.destroy()\n\ndata = load()\nroot = tk.Tk()\nname = tk.StringVar(value=data[<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>])\nvolume = tk.IntVar(value=data[<i class=\"s\">&quot;volume&quot;</i>])\ntk.Entry(root, textvariable=name).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\ntk.Scale(root, from_=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, to=<i class=\"n\">100</i>, orient=<i class=\"s\">&quot;horizontal&quot;</i>, variable=volume).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nroot.protocol(<i class=\"s\">&quot;WM_DELETE_WINDOW&quot;</i>, save)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["py.json","tk.close-protocol"],"t":"import tkinter as tk import json from pathlib import Path FILE = Path( \"settings.json\" ) def load (): try : return json.loads(FILE.read_text()) except (FileNotFoundError, json.JSONDecodeError): return { \"name\" : \"\" , \"volume\" : 50 } def save (): FILE.write_text(json.dumps({ \"name\" : name.get(), \"volume\" : volume.get()}, indent= 2 )) root.destroy() data = load() root = tk.Tk() name = tk.StringVar(value=data[ \"name\" ]) volume = tk.IntVar(value=data[ \"volume\" ]) tk.Entry(root, textvariable=name).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 6 ) tk.Scale(root, from_= 0 , to= 100 , orient= \"horizontal\" , variable=volume).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 6 ) root.protocol( \"WM_DELETE_WINDOW\" , save) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.sqlite","s":"tk-structure","q":"How do I save data in a database (SQLite) from a tkinter app?","qh":"How do I save data in a database (SQLite) from a tkinter app?","a":"Python's built-in <code>sqlite3</code> stores data in one file. Connect once, <code>execute</code> SQL with <code>?</code> placeholders, and <code>commit()</code> after changes.","aka":"sqlite tkinter, database gui, sqlite3, save to database, crud app, contacts app","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> sqlite3\n\ndb = sqlite3.connect(<i class=\"s\">&quot;contacts.db&quot;</i>)\ndb.execute(<i class=\"s\">&quot;CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS contacts (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT, phone TEXT)&quot;</i>)\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nname, phone = tk.Entry(root), tk.Entry(root)\nname.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>); phone.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntree = ttk.Treeview(root, columns=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;phone&quot;</i>), show=<i class=\"s\">&quot;headings&quot;</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\ntree.heading(<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Name&quot;</i>); tree.heading(<i class=\"s\">&quot;phone&quot;</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Phone&quot;</i>)\ntree.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, columnspan=<i class=\"n\">3</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">refresh</i>():\n    tree.delete(*tree.get_children())\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> row <i class=\"k\">in</i> db.execute(<i class=\"s\">&quot;SELECT id, name, phone FROM contacts ORDER BY name&quot;</i>):\n        tree.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, iid=row[<i class=\"n\">0</i>], values=row[<i class=\"n\">1</i>:])\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">add</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> name.get():\n        db.execute(<i class=\"s\">&quot;INSERT INTO contacts (name, phone) VALUES (?, ?)&quot;</i>, (name.get(), phone.get()))\n        db.commit()\n        name.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>); phone.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n        refresh()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">delete</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> iid <i class=\"k\">in</i> tree.selection():\n        db.execute(<i class=\"s\">&quot;DELETE FROM contacts WHERE id = ?&quot;</i>, (iid,))\n    db.commit()\n    refresh()\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Add&quot;</i>, command=add).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Delete selected&quot;</i>, command=delete).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, columnspan=<i class=\"n\">3</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nrefresh()\nroot.mainloop()\ndb.close()</pre></figure><p>Never build SQL with f-strings from user input; always use <code>?</code> placeholders.</p>","see":["tk.treeview","py.sqlite"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk import sqlite3 db = sqlite3.connect( \"contacts.db\" ) db.execute( \"CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS contacts (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT, phone TEXT)\" ) root = tk.Tk() name, phone = tk.Entry(root), tk.Entry(root) name.grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , padx= 4 , pady= 4 ); phone.grid(row= 0 , column= 1 , padx= 4 ) tree = ttk.Treeview(root, columns=( \"name\" , \"phone\" ), show= \"headings\" , height= 6 ) tree.heading( \"name\" , text= \"Name\" ); tree.heading( \"phone\" , text= \"Phone\" ) tree.grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , columnspan= 3 , padx= 4 , pady= 4 ) def refresh (): tree.delete(*tree.get_children()) for row in db.execute( \"SELECT id, name, phone FROM contacts ORDER BY name\" ): tree.insert( \"\" , \"end\" , iid=row[ 0 ], values=row[ 1 :]) def add (): if name.get(): db.execute( \"INSERT INTO contacts (name, phone) VALUES (?, ?)\" , (name.get(), phone.get())) db.commit() name.delete( 0 , \"end\" ); phone.delete( 0 , \"end\" ) refresh() def delete (): for iid in tree.selection(): db.execute( \"DELETE FROM contacts WHERE id = ?\" , (iid,)) db.commit() refresh() tk.Button(root, text= \"Add\" , command=add).grid(row= 0 , column= 2 , padx= 4 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Delete selected\" , command=delete).grid(row= 2 , column= 0 , columnspan= 3 , pady= 4 ) refresh() root.mainloop() db.close() Never build SQL with f-strings from user input; always use ? placeholders."},{"id":"tk.restart-app","s":"tk-structure","q":"How do I reset or restart my app (start the game again)?","qh":"How do I reset or restart my app (start the game again)?","a":"Don't restart Python. Write a <code>reset()</code> that sets your variables back and redraws, or destroy the page's Frame and build a new one.","aka":"restart program, reset game, play again, new game button, restart tkinter","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> random\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Game</i>(tk.Frame):\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, master, on_restart):\n        <i class=\"b\">super</i>().__init__(master, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.secret = random.randint(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n        tk.Label(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Guess 1-10&quot;</i>).pack()\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.e = tk.Entry(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">5</i>); <i class=\"k\">self</i>.e.pack()\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.msg = tk.Label(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>); <i class=\"k\">self</i>.msg.pack()\n        tk.Button(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Guess&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.guess).pack()\n        tk.Button(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;New game&quot;</i>, command=on_restart).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">8</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>))\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">guess</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n            g = <i class=\"b\">int</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>.e.get())\n        <i class=\"k\">except</i> ValueError:\n            <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.msg.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Correct!&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> g == <i class=\"k\">self</i>.secret <i class=\"k\">else</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Higher&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> g &lt; <i class=\"k\">self</i>.secret <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;Lower&quot;</i>))\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ncurrent = <i class=\"k\">None</i>\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">new_game</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> current\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> current:\n        current.destroy()\n    current = Game(root, new_game)\n    current.pack()\n\nnew_game()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk import random class Game (tk.Frame): def __init__ ( self , master, on_restart): super ().__init__(master, padx= 20 , pady= 20 ) self .secret = random.randint( 1 , 10 ) tk.Label( self , text= \"Guess 1-10\" ).pack() self .e = tk.Entry( self , width= 5 ); self .e.pack() self .msg = tk.Label( self , text= \"\" ); self .msg.pack() tk.Button( self , text= \"Guess\" , command= self .guess).pack() tk.Button( self , text= \"New game\" , command=on_restart).pack(pady=( 8 , 0 )) def guess ( self ): try : g = int ( self .e.get()) except ValueError: return self .msg.config(text= \"Correct!\" if g == self .secret else ( \"Higher\" if g < self .secret else \"Lower\" )) root = tk.Tk() current = None def new_game (): global current if current: current.destroy() current = Game(root, new_game) current.pack() new_game() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.widget-from-list","s":"tk-structure","q":"How do I create widgets from a list or dict (and get their values back)?","qh":"How do I create widgets from a list or dict (and get their values back)?","a":"Loop over your data, create each widget, and store it in a dict keyed by name so you can read them all later.","aka":"dynamic widgets, create entries in loop, form from list, generate widgets, list of entries","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nfields = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;Name&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Email&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Phone&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;City&quot;</i>]\nroot = tk.Tk()\nentries = {}\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> row, f <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">enumerate</i>(fields):\n    tk.Label(root, text=f).grid(row=row, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;e&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">3</i>)\n    entries[f] = tk.Entry(root, width=<i class=\"n\">28</i>)\n    entries[f].grid(row=row, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">3</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">submit</i>():\n    data = {f: e.get() <i class=\"k\">for</i> f, e <i class=\"k\">in</i> entries.items()}\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(data)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Submit&quot;</i>, command=submit).grid(row=<i class=\"b\">len</i>(fields), column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;e&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk fields = [ \"Name\" , \"Email\" , \"Phone\" , \"City\" ] root = tk.Tk() entries = {} for row, f in enumerate (fields): tk.Label(root, text=f).grid(row=row, column= 0 , sticky= \"e\" , padx= 6 , pady= 3 ) entries[f] = tk.Entry(root, width= 28 ) entries[f].grid(row=row, column= 1 , padx= 6 , pady= 3 ) def submit (): data = {f: e.get() for f, e in entries.items()} print (data) tk.Button(root, text= \"Submit\" , command=submit).grid(row= len (fields), column= 1 , sticky= \"e\" , padx= 6 , pady= 6 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.mvc","s":"tk-structure","q":"How should I separate the GUI from the program logic?","qh":"How should I separate the GUI from the program logic?","a":"Put rules and data in a plain class with no tkinter, and have the GUI call it and display the results. Then you can test and reuse the logic.","aka":"separate logic and gui, mvc tkinter, model view, clean code tkinter, testable gui","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Bank</i>:                         <i class=\"c\"># logic only: no tkinter</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.balance = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">deposit</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, amount):\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> amount &lt;= <i class=\"n\">0</i>:\n            <i class=\"k\">raise</i> ValueError(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Amount must be positive&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.balance += amount\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">BankView</i>(tk.Frame):           <i class=\"c\"># GUI only</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, master, bank):\n        <i class=\"b\">super</i>().__init__(master, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.bank = bank\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.amount = tk.Entry(<i class=\"k\">self</i>); <i class=\"k\">self</i>.amount.pack()\n        tk.Button(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Deposit&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.deposit).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.out = tk.Label(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Balance: 0&quot;</i>); <i class=\"k\">self</i>.out.pack()\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">deposit</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.bank.deposit(<i class=\"b\">float</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>.amount.get()))\n        <i class=\"k\">except</i> ValueError <i class=\"k\">as</i> e:\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.out.config(text=<i class=\"b\">str</i>(e))\n            <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.out.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Balance: </i>{<i class=\"k\">self</i>.bank.balance:<i class=\"s\">.2f</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nBankView(root, Bank()).pack()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk class Bank : # logic only: no tkinter def __init__ ( self ): self .balance = 0 def deposit ( self , amount): if amount <= 0 : raise ValueError( \"Amount must be positive\" ) self .balance += amount class BankView (tk.Frame): # GUI only def __init__ ( self , master, bank): super ().__init__(master, padx= 20 , pady= 20 ) self .bank = bank self .amount = tk.Entry( self ); self .amount.pack() tk.Button( self , text= \"Deposit\" , command= self .deposit).pack(pady= 6 ) self .out = tk.Label( self , text= \"Balance: 0\" ); self .out.pack() def deposit ( self ): try : self .bank.deposit( float ( self .amount.get())) except ValueError as e: self .out.config(text= str (e)) return self .out.config(text= f\" Balance: { self .bank.balance: .2f } \" ) root = tk.Tk() BankView(root, Bank()).pack() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.err.pyimage","s":"tk-errors","q":"TclError: image \"pyimage1\" doesn't exist","qh":"TclError: image \"pyimage1\" doesn't exist","a":"You have <b>two</b> <code>tk.Tk()</code> windows. An image belongs to the Tk it was made in. Make the second window a <code>tk.Toplevel</code> instead, or pass <code>master=</code> when creating the image.","aka":"pyimage doesn't exist, pyimage1, pyimage2, image doesn't exist toplevel, image in second window","err":"image \"pyimage*\" doesn't exist","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre>root = tk.Tk()\nwin = tk.Tk()                          <i class=\"c\"># second Tk: wrong</i>\nimg = tk.PhotoImage(file=<i class=\"s\">&quot;photo.png&quot;</i>)  <i class=\"c\"># belongs to root (the first Tk)</i>\ntk.Label(win, image=img).pack()        <i class=\"c\"># TclError: image &quot;pyimage1&quot; doesn&#x27;t exist</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nwin = tk.Toplevel(root)\nimg = tk.PhotoImage(file=<i class=\"s\">&quot;photo.png&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(win, image=img).pack()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>It also happens in IDLE or Jupyter after re-running a script without closing the previous window.</p>","see":["tk.toplevel"],"t":"root = tk.Tk() win = tk.Tk() # second Tk: wrong img = tk.PhotoImage(file= \"photo.png\" ) # belongs to root (the first Tk) tk.Label(win, image=img).pack() # TclError: image \"pyimage1\" doesn't exist import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() win = tk.Toplevel(root) img = tk.PhotoImage(file= \"photo.png\" ) tk.Label(win, image=img).pack() root.mainloop() It also happens in IDLE or Jupyter after re-running a script without closing the previous window."},{"id":"tk.err.unknown-option","s":"tk-errors","q":"TclError: unknown option \"-something\"","qh":"TclError: unknown option \"-something\"","a":"That widget doesn't have that option. Usually: using <code>bg</code>/<code>fg</code>/<code>font</code> on a <b>ttk</b> widget (use a Style), a typo (<code>txt=</code> instead of <code>text=</code>), or an option meant for <code>pack</code>/<code>grid</code> passed to the widget (or the other way round).","aka":"unknown option, bad option, -bg unknown option ttk, -font unknown option, -command unknown option","err":"unknown option \"-*\"","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre>ttk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hi&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>)       <i class=\"c\"># ttk: no -bg</i>\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hi&quot;</i>, padding=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)       <i class=\"c\"># tk.Label has padx/pady, not padding</i>\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hi&quot;</i>).pack(sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>) <i class=\"c\"># sticky is a grid option</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hi&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">sorted</i>(lab.keys()))     <i class=\"c\"># every option a widget accepts</i>\nroot.destroy()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.ttk-style"],"t":"ttk.Button(root, text= \"Hi\" , bg= \"red\" ) # ttk: no -bg tk.Label(root, text= \"Hi\" , padding= 10 ) # tk.Label has padx/pady, not padding tk.Button(root, text= \"Hi\" ).pack(sticky= \"w\" ) # sticky is a grid option import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"Hi\" ) print ( sorted (lab.keys())) # every option a widget accepts root.destroy()"},{"id":"tk.err.invalid-command","s":"tk-errors","q":"TclError: invalid command name \".!button\" (or \"12345callback\")","qh":"TclError: invalid command name \".!button\" (or \"12345callback\")","a":"Your code used a widget (or an <code>after</code> loop kept running) <b>after the window was destroyed</b>. Cancel <code>after</code> jobs before closing, and don't touch widgets after <code>destroy()</code>.","aka":"invalid command name, can't invoke command, application has been destroyed, error after closing window, after callback after destroy","err":"invalid command name*|can't invoke \"*\" command: application has been destroyed|application has been destroyed","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;0&quot;</i>)\nlab.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\njob = <i class=\"k\">None</i>\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">tick</i>(n=<i class=\"n\">0</i>):\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> job\n    lab.config(text=<i class=\"b\">str</i>(n))\n    job = root.after(<i class=\"n\">100</i>, tick, n + <i class=\"n\">1</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">close</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> job:\n        root.after_cancel(job)\n    root.destroy()\n\nroot.protocol(<i class=\"s\">&quot;WM_DELETE_WINDOW&quot;</i>, close)\ntick()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Messages like this printed when you close the window, with no other problem, are harmless but this is how to silence them.</p>","see":["tk.after-cancel","tk.close-protocol"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"0\" ) lab.pack(padx= 40 , pady= 20 ) job = None def tick (n= 0 ): global job lab.config(text= str (n)) job = root.after( 100 , tick, n + 1 ) def close (): if job: root.after_cancel(job) root.destroy() root.protocol( \"WM_DELETE_WINDOW\" , close) tick() root.mainloop() Messages like this printed when you close the window, with no other problem, are harmless but this is how to silence them."},{"id":"tk.err.bad-window-path","s":"tk-errors","q":"TclError: bad window path name \".!frame.!label\"","qh":"TclError: bad window path name \".!frame.!label\"","a":"You're calling a method on a widget that has already been destroyed (often after clearing a frame or switching pages). Check with <code>widget.winfo_exists()</code> or stop keeping old references.","aka":"bad window path name, widget destroyed error, window path","err":"bad window path name","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;temp&quot;</i>)\nlab.pack()\nlab.destroy()\n<i class=\"k\">if</i> lab.winfo_exists():\n    lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;safe&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">else</i>:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;label is gone&quot;</i>)\nroot.destroy()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"temp\" ) lab.pack() lab.destroy() if lab.winfo_exists(): lab.config(text= \"safe\" ) else : print ( \"label is gone\" ) root.destroy()"},{"id":"tk.err.no-display","s":"tk-errors","q":"TclError: no display name and no $DISPLAY environment variable","qh":"TclError: no display name and no $DISPLAY environment variable","a":"The computer running the code has no screen (a server, SSH session, online IDE, WSL without a GUI). Run it on a desktop, enable a GUI (WSLg, X forwarding with <code>ssh -X</code>), or use a virtual display like <code>xvfb-run</code> for testing.","aka":"no display name, couldn't connect to display, tkinter on server, tkinter ssh, tkinter replit, tkinter codespaces, headless","err":"no display name and no $DISPLAY|couldn't connect to display","h":"<figure class=\"code sh\" data-tag=\"terminal\"><pre>xvfb-run python3 my_app.py     # Linux: runs with an invisible screen\nssh -X user@host                # forward windows over SSH</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"xvfb-run python3 my_app.py # Linux: runs with an invisible screen ssh -X user@host # forward windows over SSH"},{"id":"tk.err.wrong-args","s":"tk-errors","q":"TypeError: __init__() got multiple values for argument / takes from 1 to 2 positional arguments","qh":"TypeError: __init__() got multiple values for argument / takes from 1 to 2 positional arguments","a":"Options must be passed by <b>name</b>. <code>tk.Label(root, &quot;Hello&quot;)</code> passes <code>&quot;Hello&quot;</code> as the second positional argument. Write <code>tk.Label(root, text=&quot;Hello&quot;)</code>.","aka":"got multiple values for argument master, positional arguments widget, Label() takes, widget arguments wrong","err":"got multiple values for argument|takes from 1 to 2 positional arguments but*were given","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre>tk.Label(root, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Hello&quot;</i>)\ntk.Button(root, <i class=\"s\">&quot;OK&quot;</i>, ok)</pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hello&quot;</i>).pack()\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;OK&quot;</i>, command=root.destroy).pack()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"tk.Label(root, \"Hello\" ) tk.Button(root, \"OK\" , ok) import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"Hello\" ).pack() tk.Button(root, text= \"OK\" , command=root.destroy).pack() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.err.str-not-callable-tk","s":"tk-errors","q":"TypeError: 'str' object is not callable (in tkinter)","qh":"TypeError: 'str' object is not callable (in tkinter)","a":"You gave a variable the same name as a function or method, e.g. <code>root.title = &quot;My app&quot;</code> (now <code>title</code> is a string), or <code>label = ...</code> shadowing a function called <code>label</code>. Use <code>root.title(&quot;My app&quot;)</code> and different names.","aka":"str object is not callable tkinter, label not callable, title not callable, overwrote function","err":"'str' object is not callable|'Label' object is not callable|'Button' object is not callable","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre>root.title = <i class=\"s\">&quot;My app&quot;</i>\nroot.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Other&quot;</i>)        <i class=\"c\"># TypeError: &#x27;str&#x27; object is not callable</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;My app&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["py.err.not-callable"],"t":"root.title = \"My app\" root.title( \"Other\" ) # TypeError: 'str' object is not callable import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.title( \"My app\" ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.err.no-mainloop-idle","s":"tk-errors","q":"My tkinter program behaves differently in IDLE / Thonny / VS Code / Jupyter","qh":"My tkinter program behaves differently in IDLE / Thonny / VS Code / Jupyter","a":"IDLE itself is a tkinter app, so <code>root.quit()</code> may not close windows and leftover windows stay around between runs. Always end with <code>root.mainloop()</code>, close with <code>root.destroy()</code>, and if things act strangely, restart the shell (Shell &gt; Restart Shell) or run from a terminal.","aka":"tkinter idle, tkinter jupyter, window doesnt close idle, idle freezes tkinter, works in idle not when double clicked","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code sh\" data-tag=\"terminal\"><pre>python my_app.py      # Windows (or: py my_app.py)\npython3 my_app.py     # macOS / Linux</pre></figure>","see":["tk.quit-vs-destroy","tk.err.pyimage"],"t":"python my_app.py # Windows (or: py my_app.py) python3 my_app.py # macOS / Linux"},{"id":"tk.err.color","s":"tk-errors","q":"TclError: unknown color name \"...\"","qh":"TclError: unknown color name \"...\"","a":"The name isn't a Tk colour, or a hex code is malformed. Hex needs <code>#</code> and 3, 6 or 12 hex digits: <code>&quot;#f80&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;#ff8800&quot;</code>.","aka":"unknown color name, invalid color, bad colour, colour not found","err":"unknown color name","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> c <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;light green&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;#ff8800&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;#f80&quot;</i>):\n    tk.Label(root, text=c, bg=c).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n    tk.Label(root, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ff8800&quot;</i>)        <i class=\"c\"># missing #</i>\n<i class=\"k\">except</i> tk.TclError <i class=\"k\">as</i> e:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(e)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.colors"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() for c in ( \"light green\" , \"#ff8800\" , \"#f80\" ): tk.Label(root, text=c, bg=c).pack(fill= \"x\" ) try : tk.Label(root, bg= \"ff8800\" ) # missing # except tk.TclError as e: print (e) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.err.recursion-after","s":"tk-errors","q":"RecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded (in a tkinter loop)","qh":"RecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded (in a tkinter loop)","a":"A function calls <b>itself directly</b> to loop (<code>def loop(): ...; loop()</code>). Schedule the next call with <code>root.after(ms, loop)</code> instead: that isn't recursion, so it can run forever.","aka":"recursion error tkinter, loop function calls itself, game loop recursion","err":"maximum recursion depth exceeded","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">loop</i>():\n    move()\n    loop()               <i class=\"c\"># RecursionError after ~1000 calls (and the window freezes)</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nn = tk.IntVar()\ntk.Label(root, textvariable=n).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">loop</i>():\n    n.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(n.get() + <i class=\"n\">1</i>)\n    root.after(<i class=\"n\">10</i>, loop)\n\nloop()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.after","py.err.recursion"],"t":"def loop (): move() loop() # RecursionError after ~1000 calls (and the window freezes) import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() n = tk.IntVar() tk.Label(root, textvariable=n).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 20 ) def loop (): n. set (n.get() + 1 ) root.after( 10 , loop) loop() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.err.grid-columnspan-str","s":"tk-errors","q":"TclError: expected integer but got \"...\" (in grid, after or a widget option)","qh":"TclError: expected integer but got \"...\" (in grid, after or a widget option)","a":"An option that needs a number got text, e.g. <code>grid(row=&quot;1&quot;)</code> from an Entry, or <code>width=&quot;20px&quot;</code>. Convert with <code>int()</code> and give plain numbers (pixels or characters).","aka":"expected integer but got, bad screen distance, expected floating point, grid row string","err":"expected integer but got|bad screen distance","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nrow_text = <i class=\"s\">&quot;2&quot;</i>\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;here&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"b\">int</i>(row_text), column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\ntk.Frame(root, width=<i class=\"n\">100</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">3</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() row_text = \"2\" tk.Label(root, text= \"here\" ).grid(row= int (row_text), column= 0 ) tk.Frame(root, width= 100 , height= 20 , bg= \"#fc6\" ).grid(row= 3 , column= 0 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.recipe-calculator","s":"tk-recipes","q":"How do I make a calculator in tkinter?","qh":"How do I make a calculator in tkinter?","a":"A display Entry plus a grid of buttons. Each button appends its character; <code>=</code> evaluates the expression safely.","aka":"calculator app, calculator gui, simple calculator, button calculator, calculator project","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> ast, operator\n\nOPS = {ast.Add: operator.add, ast.Sub: operator.sub, ast.Mult: operator.mul, ast.Div: operator.truediv, ast.USub: operator.neg}\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">calc</i>(expr):\n    <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;&quot;Evaluate + - * / and brackets without using eval().&quot;&quot;&quot;</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">ev</i>(node):\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"b\">isinstance</i>(node, ast.Expression):\n            <i class=\"k\">return</i> ev(node.body)\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"b\">isinstance</i>(node, ast.Constant) <i class=\"k\">and</i> <i class=\"b\">isinstance</i>(node.value, (<i class=\"b\">int</i>, <i class=\"b\">float</i>)):\n            <i class=\"k\">return</i> node.value\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"b\">isinstance</i>(node, ast.BinOp) <i class=\"k\">and</i> <i class=\"b\">type</i>(node.op) <i class=\"k\">in</i> OPS:\n            <i class=\"k\">return</i> OPS[<i class=\"b\">type</i>(node.op)](ev(node.left), ev(node.right))\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"b\">isinstance</i>(node, ast.UnaryOp) <i class=\"k\">and</i> <i class=\"b\">type</i>(node.op) <i class=\"k\">in</i> OPS:\n            <i class=\"k\">return</i> OPS[<i class=\"b\">type</i>(node.op)](ev(node.operand))\n        <i class=\"k\">raise</i> ValueError(<i class=\"s\">&quot;bad input&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> ev(ast.parse(expr, mode=<i class=\"s\">&quot;eval&quot;</i>))\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Calculator&quot;</i>)\ndisplay = tk.Entry(root, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">22</i>), justify=<i class=\"s\">&quot;right&quot;</i>, bd=<i class=\"n\">8</i>, relief=<i class=\"s\">&quot;flat&quot;</i>)\ndisplay.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, columnspan=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ew&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">press</i>(ch):\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> ch == <i class=\"s\">&quot;C&quot;</i>:\n        display.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">elif</i> ch == <i class=\"s\">&quot;=&quot;</i>:\n        <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n            result = calc(display.get())\n            display.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n            display.insert(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{result:<i class=\"s\">g</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">except</i> (ValueError, SyntaxError, ZeroDivisionError):\n            display.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n            display.insert(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Error&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">else</i>:\n        display.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, ch)\n\nkeys = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;7&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;8&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;9&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;/&quot;</i>,\n        <i class=\"s\">&quot;4&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;5&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;6&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;*&quot;</i>,\n        <i class=\"s\">&quot;1&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;2&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;3&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;-&quot;</i>,\n        <i class=\"s\">&quot;C&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;=&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;+&quot;</i>]\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> i, k <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">enumerate</i>(keys):\n    tk.Button(root, text=k, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">16</i>), width=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">2</i>,\n              command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i> k=k: press(k)).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i> + i // <i class=\"n\">4</i>, column=i % <i class=\"n\">4</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;nsew&quot;</i>)\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Return&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: press(<i class=\"s\">&quot;=&quot;</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Using <code>eval()</code> on typed text would also work but can run any Python code; the small <code>calc</code> above only allows arithmetic.</p>","see":["tk.button-grid","tk.lambda-loop"],"t":"import tkinter as tk import ast, operator OPS = {ast.Add: operator.add, ast.Sub: operator.sub, ast.Mult: operator.mul, ast.Div: operator.truediv, ast.USub: operator.neg} def calc (expr): \"\"\"Evaluate + - * / and brackets without using eval().\"\"\" def ev (node): if isinstance (node, ast.Expression): return ev(node.body) if isinstance (node, ast.Constant) and isinstance (node.value, ( int , float )): return node.value if isinstance (node, ast.BinOp) and type (node.op) in OPS: return OPS[ type (node.op)](ev(node.left), ev(node.right)) if isinstance (node, ast.UnaryOp) and type (node.op) in OPS: return OPS[ type (node.op)](ev(node.operand)) raise ValueError( \"bad input\" ) return ev(ast.parse(expr, mode= \"eval\" )) root = tk.Tk() root.title( \"Calculator\" ) display = tk.Entry(root, font=( \"Helvetica\" , 22 ), justify= \"right\" , bd= 8 , relief= \"flat\" ) display.grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , columnspan= 4 , sticky= \"ew\" , padx= 6 , pady= 6 ) def press (ch): if ch == \"C\" : display.delete( 0 , \"end\" ) elif ch == \"=\" : try : result = calc(display.get()) display.delete( 0 , \"end\" ) display.insert( 0 , f\" {result: g } \" ) except (ValueError, SyntaxError, ZeroDivisionError): display.delete( 0 , \"end\" ) display.insert( 0 , \"Error\" ) else : display.insert( \"end\" , ch) keys = [ \"7\" , \"8\" , \"9\" , \"/\" , \"4\" , \"5\" , \"6\" , \"*\" , \"1\" , \"2\" , \"3\" , \"-\" , \"C\" , \"0\" , \"=\" , \"+\" ] for i, k in enumerate (keys): tk.Button(root, text=k, font=( \"Helvetica\" , 16 ), width= 4 , height= 2 , command= lambda k=k: press(k)).grid(row= 1 + i // 4 , column=i % 4 , padx= 2 , pady= 2 , sticky= \"nsew\" ) root.bind( \"<Return>"},{"id":"tk.recipe-todo","s":"tk-recipes","q":"How do I make a to-do list app (that saves)?","qh":"How do I make a to-do list app (that saves)?","a":"An Entry + Listbox; Add, Done and Delete buttons; save the list to a JSON file on every change.","aka":"todo app, to do list, task list app, checklist app, todo project","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> json\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> pathlib <i class=\"k\">import</i> Path\n\nFILE = Path(<i class=\"s\">&quot;todo.json&quot;</i>)\ntasks = json.loads(FILE.read_text()) <i class=\"k\">if</i> FILE.exists() <i class=\"k\">else</i> []\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">save</i>():\n    FILE.write_text(json.dumps(tasks))\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">refresh</i>():\n    lb.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> t <i class=\"k\">in</i> tasks:\n        lb.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, (<i class=\"s\">&quot;✓ &quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> t[<i class=\"s\">&quot;done&quot;</i>] <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;   &quot;</i>) + t[<i class=\"s\">&quot;text&quot;</i>])\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">add</i>(event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    text = entry.get().strip()\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> text:\n        tasks.append({<i class=\"s\">&quot;text&quot;</i>: text, <i class=\"s\">&quot;done&quot;</i>: <i class=\"k\">False</i>})\n        entry.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n        save(); refresh()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">toggle</i>(event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> lb.curselection():\n        tasks[i][<i class=\"s\">&quot;done&quot;</i>] = <i class=\"k\">not</i> tasks[i][<i class=\"s\">&quot;done&quot;</i>]\n    save(); refresh()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">delete</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">reversed</i>(lb.curselection()):\n        tasks.pop(i)\n    save(); refresh()\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;To-do&quot;</i>)\nentry = tk.Entry(root, width=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nentry.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nentry.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Return&gt;&quot;</i>, add)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Add&quot;</i>, command=add).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nlb = tk.Listbox(root, width=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">12</i>))\nlb.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, columnspan=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nlb.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Double-Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, toggle)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Done / undo&quot;</i>, command=toggle).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Delete&quot;</i>, command=delete).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nrefresh()\nentry.focus()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.listbox","tk.save-settings"],"t":"import tkinter as tk import json from pathlib import Path FILE = Path( \"todo.json\" ) tasks = json.loads(FILE.read_text()) if FILE.exists() else [] def save (): FILE.write_text(json.dumps(tasks)) def refresh (): lb.delete( 0 , \"end\" ) for t in tasks: lb.insert( \"end\" , ( \"✓ \" if t[ \"done\" ] else \" \" ) + t[ \"text\" ]) def add (event= None ): text = entry.get().strip() if text: tasks.append({ \"text\" : text, \"done\" : False }) entry.delete( 0 , \"end\" ) save(); refresh() def toggle (event= None ): for i in lb.curselection(): tasks[i][ \"done\" ] = not tasks[i][ \"done\" ] save(); refresh() def delete (): for i in reversed (lb.curselection()): tasks.pop(i) save(); refresh() root = tk.Tk() root.title( \"To-do\" ) entry = tk.Entry(root, width= 30 ) entry.grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , padx= 6 , pady= 6 ) entry.bind( \"<Return>\" , add) tk.Button(root, text= \"Add\" , command=add).grid(row= 0 , column= 1 , padx= 6 ) lb = tk.Listbox(root, width= 40 , height= 10 , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 12 )) lb.grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , columnspan= 2 , padx= 6 ) lb.bind( \"<Double-Button-1>\" , toggle) tk.Button(root, text= \"Done / undo\" , command=toggle).grid(row= 2 , column= 0 , sticky= \"w\" , padx= 6 , pady= 6 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Delete\" , command=delete).grid(row= 2 , column= 1 , padx= 6 , pady= 6 ) refresh() entry.focus() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.recipe-stopwatch","s":"tk-recipes","q":"How do I make a stopwatch or countdown timer?","qh":"How do I make a stopwatch or countdown timer?","a":"Store the start time with <code>time.perf_counter()</code>, and refresh the display with <code>after()</code>. Measuring real time (instead of counting ticks) keeps it accurate.","aka":"stopwatch, timer app, countdown timer, start stop reset, pomodoro timer, egg timer","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> time\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Stopwatch</i>(tk.Tk):\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"b\">super</i>().__init__()\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Stopwatch&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.elapsed, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.started, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.job = <i class=\"n\">0.0</i>, <i class=\"k\">None</i>, <i class=\"k\">None</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.display = tk.Label(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;00:00.0&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Courier&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">40</i>), padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.display.pack()\n        bar = tk.Frame(<i class=\"k\">self</i>); bar.pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">8</i>)\n        tk.Button(bar, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Start&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">8</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.start).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\n        tk.Button(bar, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Stop&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">8</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.stop).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\n        tk.Button(bar, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Reset&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">8</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.reset).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">total</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>.elapsed + (time.perf_counter() - <i class=\"k\">self</i>.started <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>.started <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"n\">0</i>)\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">show</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        t = <i class=\"k\">self</i>.total()\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.display.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{<i class=\"b\">int</i>(t // <i class=\"n\">60</i>):<i class=\"s\">02d</i>}<i class=\"s\">:</i>{t % <i class=\"n\">60</i>:<i class=\"s\">04.1f</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.job = <i class=\"k\">self</i>.after(<i class=\"n\">50</i>, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.show)\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">start</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>.started <i class=\"k\">is</i> <i class=\"k\">None</i>:\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.started = time.perf_counter()\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.show()\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">stop</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>.started <i class=\"k\">is</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> <i class=\"k\">None</i>:\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.elapsed = <i class=\"k\">self</i>.total()\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.started = <i class=\"k\">None</i>\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.after_cancel(<i class=\"k\">self</i>.job)\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">reset</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.stop()\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.elapsed = <i class=\"n\">0.0</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.display.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;00:00.0&quot;</i>)\n\nStopwatch().mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Countdown version: show <code>limit - total()</code> and stop when it reaches 0.</p>","see":["tk.after","tk.after-cancel"],"t":"import tkinter as tk import time class Stopwatch (tk.Tk): def __init__ ( self ): super ().__init__() self .title( \"Stopwatch\" ) self .elapsed, self .started, self .job = 0.0 , None , None self .display = tk.Label( self , text= \"00:00.0\" , font=( \"Courier\" , 40 ), padx= 20 ) self .display.pack() bar = tk.Frame( self ); bar.pack(pady= 8 ) tk.Button(bar, text= \"Start\" , width= 8 , command= self .start).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 4 ) tk.Button(bar, text= \"Stop\" , width= 8 , command= self .stop).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 4 ) tk.Button(bar, text= \"Reset\" , width= 8 , command= self .reset).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 4 ) def total ( self ): return self .elapsed + (time.perf_counter() - self .started if self .started else 0 ) def show ( self ): t = self .total() self .display.config(text= f\" { int (t // 60 ): 02d } : {t % 60 : 04.1f } \" ) self .job = self .after( 50 , self .show) def start ( self ): if self .started is None : self .started = time.perf_counter() self .show() def stop ( self ): if self .started is not None : self .elapsed = self .total() self .started = None self .after_cancel( self .job) def reset ( self ): self .stop() self .elapsed = 0.0 self .display.config(text= \"00:00.0\" ) Stopwatch().mainloop() Countdown version: show limit - total() and stop when it reaches 0."},{"id":"tk.recipe-login","s":"tk-recipes","q":"How do I make a login form?","qh":"How do I make a login form?","a":"Two Entries (password with <code>show=&quot;*&quot;</code>), a button that checks them, and a message label. Never store plain passwords; store a salted hash.","aka":"login screen, login page, username password form, sign in gui, login system tkinter","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> hashlib, os\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">hash_pw</i>(password, salt):\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac(<i class=\"s\">&quot;sha256&quot;</i>, password.encode(), salt, <i class=\"n\">100_000</i>).hex()\n\nSALT = os.urandom(<i class=\"n\">16</i>)\nUSERS = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;ada&quot;</i>: hash_pw(<i class=\"s\">&quot;secret123&quot;</i>, SALT)}      <i class=\"c\"># normally loaded from a file/database</i>\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Log in&quot;</i>)\nfrm = tk.Frame(root, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nfrm.pack()\ntk.Label(frm, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Username&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>)\nuser = tk.Entry(frm); user.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Label(frm, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Password&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>)\npw = tk.Entry(frm, show=<i class=\"s\">&quot;*&quot;</i>); pw.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nmsg = tk.Label(frm, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>); msg.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">3</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, columnspan=<i class=\"n\">2</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">login</i>(event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    name = user.get().strip().lower()\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> name <i class=\"k\">in</i> USERS <i class=\"k\">and</i> USERS[name] == hash_pw(pw.get(), SALT):\n        frm.destroy()\n        tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Welcome, </i>{name}<i class=\"s\">!</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">18</i>), padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>).pack()\n    <i class=\"k\">else</i>:\n        msg.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Wrong username or password&quot;</i>)\n        pw.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Button(frm, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Log in&quot;</i>, command=login).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;e&quot;</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Return&gt;&quot;</i>, login)\nuser.focus()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.entry-password","py.hash-password"],"t":"import tkinter as tk import hashlib, os def hash_pw (password, salt): return hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac( \"sha256\" , password.encode(), salt, 100_000 ).hex() SALT = os.urandom( 16 ) USERS = { \"ada\" : hash_pw( \"secret123\" , SALT)} # normally loaded from a file/database root = tk.Tk() root.title( \"Log in\" ) frm = tk.Frame(root, padx= 20 , pady= 20 ) frm.pack() tk.Label(frm, text= \"Username\" ).grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , sticky= \"w\" ) user = tk.Entry(frm); user.grid(row= 0 , column= 1 , pady= 4 ) tk.Label(frm, text= \"Password\" ).grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , sticky= \"w\" ) pw = tk.Entry(frm, show= \"*\" ); pw.grid(row= 1 , column= 1 , pady= 4 ) msg = tk.Label(frm, text= \"\" , fg= \"red\" ); msg.grid(row= 3 , column= 0 , columnspan= 2 ) def login (event= None ): name = user.get().strip().lower() if name in USERS and USERS[name] == hash_pw(pw.get(), SALT): frm.destroy() tk.Label(root, text= f\" Welcome, {name} ! \" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 18 ), padx= 40 , pady= 40 ).pack() else : msg.config(text= \"Wrong username or password\" ) pw.delete( 0 , \"end\" ) tk.Button(frm, text= \"Log in\" , command=login).grid(row= 2 , column= 1 , sticky= \"e\" , pady= 6 ) root.bind( \"<Return>\" , login) user.focus() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.recipe-quiz","s":"tk-recipes","q":"How do I make a quiz app?","qh":"How do I make a quiz app?","a":"Keep questions in a list of dicts, show one at a time with Radiobuttons, check the answer, then move on and show the score at the end.","aka":"quiz game, multiple choice quiz, quiz gui, questions and answers app, trivia","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nQUESTIONS = [\n    {<i class=\"s\">&quot;q&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;What does len(&#x27;cat&#x27;) return?&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;options&quot;</i>: [<i class=\"s\">&quot;2&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;3&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;&#x27;cat&#x27;&quot;</i>], <i class=\"s\">&quot;answer&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;3&quot;</i>},\n    {<i class=\"s\">&quot;q&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;Which is a tkinter widget?&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;options&quot;</i>: [<i class=\"s\">&quot;Label&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Printer&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Window()&quot;</i>], <i class=\"s\">&quot;answer&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;Label&quot;</i>},\n    {<i class=\"s\">&quot;q&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;What starts the tkinter event loop?&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;options&quot;</i>: [<i class=\"s\">&quot;run()&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;mainloop()&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;start()&quot;</i>], <i class=\"s\">&quot;answer&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;mainloop()&quot;</i>},\n]\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Quiz</i>(tk.Tk):\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"b\">super</i>().__init__()\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Quiz&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.i, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.score = <i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.choice = tk.StringVar()\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.q = tk.Label(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">14</i>), wraplength=<i class=\"n\">320</i>, justify=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.q.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">20</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>), anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.opts = [tk.Radiobutton(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, variable=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.choice, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">12</i>)) <i class=\"k\">for</i> _ <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">3</i>)]\n        <i class=\"k\">for</i> r <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>.opts:\n            r.pack(anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.feedback = tk.Label(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.feedback.pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.btn = tk.Button(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Answer&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.answer)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.btn.pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">20</i>))\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.show()\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">show</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        item = QUESTIONS[<i class=\"k\">self</i>.i]\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.q.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{<i class=\"k\">self</i>.i + <i class=\"n\">1</i>}<i class=\"s\">. </i>{item[<i class=\"s\">&#x27;q&#x27;</i>]}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.choice.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(<i class=\"k\">None</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">for</i> r, opt <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">zip</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>.opts, item[<i class=\"s\">&quot;options&quot;</i>]):\n            r.config(text=opt, value=opt)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.feedback.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">answer</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>.choice.get() <i class=\"k\">or</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>.choice.get() == <i class=\"s\">&quot;None&quot;</i>:\n            <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n        right = QUESTIONS[<i class=\"k\">self</i>.i][<i class=\"s\">&quot;answer&quot;</i>]\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>.choice.get() == right:\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.score += <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.feedback.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Correct&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;green&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">else</i>:\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.feedback.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">No, it was </i>{right}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.btn.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Next&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.<i class=\"b\">next</i>)\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">next</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.i += <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>.i == <i class=\"b\">len</i>(QUESTIONS):\n            <i class=\"k\">for</i> w <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>.winfo_children():\n                w.destroy()\n            tk.Label(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Score: </i>{<i class=\"k\">self</i>.score}<i class=\"s\">/</i>{<i class=\"b\">len</i>(QUESTIONS)}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">22</i>), padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>).pack()\n            <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.btn.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Answer&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.answer)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.show()\n\nQuiz().mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.radiobutton"],"t":"import tkinter as tk QUESTIONS = [ { \"q\" : \"What does len('cat') return?\" , \"options\" : [ \"2\" , \"3\" , \"'cat'\" ], \"answer\" : \"3\" }, { \"q\" : \"Which is a tkinter widget?\" , \"options\" : [ \"Label\" , \"Printer\" , \"Window()\" ], \"answer\" : \"Label\" }, { \"q\" : \"What starts the tkinter event loop?\" , \"options\" : [ \"run()\" , \"mainloop()\" , \"start()\" ], \"answer\" : \"mainloop()\" }, ] class Quiz (tk.Tk): def __init__ ( self ): super ().__init__() self .title( \"Quiz\" ) self .i, self .score = 0 , 0 self .choice = tk.StringVar() self .q = tk.Label( self , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 14 ), wraplength= 320 , justify= \"left\" ) self .q.pack(padx= 20 , pady=( 20 , 10 ), anchor= \"w\" ) self .opts = [tk.Radiobutton( self , variable= self .choice, font=( \"Helvetica\" , 12 )) for _ in range ( 3 )] for r in self .opts: r.pack(anchor= \"w\" , padx= 30 ) self .feedback = tk.Label( self , text= \"\" ) self .feedback.pack(pady= 6 ) self .btn = tk.Button( self , text= \"Answer\" , command= self .answer) self .btn.pack(pady=( 0 , 20 )) self .show() def show ( self ): item = QUESTIONS[ self .i] self .q.config(text= f\" { self .i + 1 } . {item[ 'q' ]} \" ) self .choice. set ( None ) for r, opt in zip ( self .opts, item[ \"options\" ]): r.config(text=opt, value=opt) self .feedback.config(text= \"\" ) def answer ( self ): if not self .choice.get() or self .choice.get() == \"None\" : return right = QUESTIONS[ self .i][ \"answer\" ] if self .choice.get() == right: self .score += 1 self .feedback.config(text= \"Correct\" , fg= \"green\" ) else : self .feedback.config(text= f\" No, it was {right} \" , fg= \"red\" ) self .btn.config(text= \"Next\" , co"},{"id":"tk.recipe-tictactoe","s":"tk-recipes","q":"How do I make tic-tac-toe (noughts and crosses)?","qh":"How do I make tic-tac-toe (noughts and crosses)?","a":"A 3x3 grid of buttons, a list of the 8 winning lines, and a turn variable.","aka":"tic tac toe, noughts and crosses, x and o game, 3x3 game, board game tkinter","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nLINES = [(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>), (<i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">5</i>), (<i class=\"n\">6</i>, <i class=\"n\">7</i>, <i class=\"n\">8</i>), (<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">6</i>), (<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">7</i>), (<i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">5</i>, <i class=\"n\">8</i>), (<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">8</i>), (<i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">6</i>)]\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Tic-tac-toe&quot;</i>)\nboard = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>] * <i class=\"n\">9</i>\nturn = <i class=\"s\">&quot;X&quot;</i>\nstatus = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;X to play&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">14</i>))\nstatus.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">3</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, columnspan=<i class=\"n\">3</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\ncells = []\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">winner</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> a, b, c <i class=\"k\">in</i> LINES:\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> board[a] <i class=\"k\">and</i> board[a] == board[b] == board[c]:\n            <i class=\"k\">return</i> board[a]\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;draw&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"b\">all</i>(board) <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"k\">None</i>\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">play</i>(i):\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> turn\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> board[i] <i class=\"k\">or</i> winner():\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n    board[i] = turn\n    cells[i].config(text=turn, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> turn == <i class=\"s\">&quot;X&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;#3f5a7a&quot;</i>)\n    w = winner()\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> w:\n        status.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Draw!&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> w == <i class=\"s\">&quot;draw&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{w}<i class=\"s\"> wins!</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">else</i>:\n        turn = <i class=\"s\">&quot;O&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> turn == <i class=\"s\">&quot;X&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;X&quot;</i>\n        status.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{turn}<i class=\"s\"> to play</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">reset</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> turn\n    turn = <i class=\"s\">&quot;X&quot;</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">9</i>):\n        board[i] = <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>\n        cells[i].config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\n    status.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;X to play&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">9</i>):\n    b = tk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">28</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;bold&quot;</i>), width=<i class=\"n\">3</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i> i=i: play(i))\n    b.grid(row=i // <i class=\"n\">3</i>, column=i % <i class=\"n\">3</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">2</i>)\n    cells.append(b)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;New game&quot;</i>, command=reset).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, columnspan=<i class=\"n\">3</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">8</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.button-grid"],"t":"import tkinter as tk LINES = [( 0 , 1 , 2 ), ( 3 , 4 , 5 ), ( 6 , 7 , 8 ), ( 0 , 3 , 6 ), ( 1 , 4 , 7 ), ( 2 , 5 , 8 ), ( 0 , 4 , 8 ), ( 2 , 4 , 6 )] root = tk.Tk() root.title( \"Tic-tac-toe\" ) board = [ \"\" ] * 9 turn = \"X\" status = tk.Label(root, text= \"X to play\" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 14 )) status.grid(row= 3 , column= 0 , columnspan= 3 , pady= 6 ) cells = [] def winner (): for a, b, c in LINES: if board[a] and board[a] == board[b] == board[c]: return board[a] return \"draw\" if all (board) else None def play (i): global turn if board[i] or winner(): return board[i] = turn cells[i].config(text=turn, fg= \"#c23b22\" if turn == \"X\" else \"#3f5a7a\" ) w = winner() if w: status.config(text= \"Draw!\" if w == \"draw\" else f\" {w} wins! \" ) else : turn = \"O\" if turn == \"X\" else \"X\" status.config(text= f\" {turn} to play \" ) def reset (): global turn turn = \"X\" for i in range ( 9 ): board[i] = \"\" cells[i].config(text= \"\" ) status.config(text= \"X to play\" ) for i in range ( 9 ): b = tk.Button(root, text= \"\" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 28 , \"bold\" ), width= 3 , height= 1 , command= lambda i=i: play(i)) b.grid(row=i // 3 , column=i % 3 , padx= 2 , pady= 2 ) cells.append(b) tk.Button(root, text= \"New game\" , command=reset).grid(row= 4 , column= 0 , columnspan= 3 , pady=( 0 , 8 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.recipe-snake","s":"tk-recipes","q":"How do I make Snake in tkinter?","qh":"How do I make Snake in tkinter?","a":"A Canvas grid, a list of segment positions, an <code>after()</code> loop that adds a new head and drops the tail (unless food was eaten), and arrow keys to turn.","aka":"snake game, snake tkinter, canvas game, arcade game tkinter, simple game","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> random\n\nCELL, COLS, ROWS, SPEED = <i class=\"n\">20</i>, <i class=\"n\">20</i>, <i class=\"n\">15</i>, <i class=\"n\">120</i>\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Snake&quot;</i>)\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=CELL * COLS, height=CELL * ROWS, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#111&quot;</i>, highlightthickness=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\nc.pack()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">new_game</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> snake, direction, food, score, alive\n    snake = [(<i class=\"n\">5</i>, <i class=\"n\">7</i>), (<i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">7</i>), (<i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">7</i>)]\n    direction, score, alive = (<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>), <i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"k\">True</i>\n    food = place_food()\n    step()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">place_food</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">while</i> <i class=\"k\">True</i>:\n        p = (random.randrange(COLS), random.randrange(ROWS))\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> p <i class=\"k\">not</i> <i class=\"k\">in</i> snake:\n            <i class=\"k\">return</i> p\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">turn</i>(dx, dy):\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> direction\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> (dx, dy) != (-direction[<i class=\"n\">0</i>], -direction[<i class=\"n\">1</i>]):      <i class=\"c\"># no reversing into yourself</i>\n        direction = (dx, dy)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">draw</i>():\n    c.delete(<i class=\"s\">&quot;all&quot;</i>)\n    fx, fy = food\n    c.create_oval(fx * CELL + <i class=\"n\">3</i>, fy * CELL + <i class=\"n\">3</i>, (fx + <i class=\"n\">1</i>) * CELL - <i class=\"n\">3</i>, (fy + <i class=\"n\">1</i>) * CELL - <i class=\"n\">3</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>, outline=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> x, y <i class=\"k\">in</i> snake:\n        c.create_rectangle(x * CELL + <i class=\"n\">1</i>, y * CELL + <i class=\"n\">1</i>, (x + <i class=\"n\">1</i>) * CELL - <i class=\"n\">1</i>, (y + <i class=\"n\">1</i>) * CELL - <i class=\"n\">1</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ffb000&quot;</i>, outline=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\n    c.create_text(<i class=\"n\">6</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Score </i>{score}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>, anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;nw&quot;</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> alive:\n        c.create_text(CELL * COLS / <i class=\"n\">2</i>, CELL * ROWS / <i class=\"n\">2</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Game over: press Space&quot;</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">16</i>))\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">step</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> food, score, alive\n    hx, hy = snake[<i class=\"n\">0</i>]\n    head = (hx + direction[<i class=\"n\">0</i>], hy + direction[<i class=\"n\">1</i>])\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> head <i class=\"k\">in</i> snake <i class=\"k\">or</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> (<i class=\"n\">0</i> &lt;= head[<i class=\"n\">0</i>] &lt; COLS <i class=\"k\">and</i> <i class=\"n\">0</i> &lt;= head[<i class=\"n\">1</i>] &lt; ROWS):\n        alive = <i class=\"k\">False</i>\n        draw()\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n    snake.insert(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, head)\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> head == food:\n        score += <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n        food = place_food()\n    <i class=\"k\">else</i>:\n        snake.pop()\n    draw()\n    root.after(SPEED, step)\n\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> key, d <i class=\"k\">in</i> {<i class=\"s\">&quot;Up&quot;</i>: (<i class=\"n\">0</i>, -<i class=\"n\">1</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;Down&quot;</i>: (<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;Left&quot;</i>: (-<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;Right&quot;</i>: (<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>)}.items():\n    root.bind(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">&lt;</i>{key}<i class=\"s\">&gt;</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e, d=d: turn(*d))\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;space&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"k\">None</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> alive <i class=\"k\">else</i> new_game())\nnew_game()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.game-loop","tk.bind-keys"],"t":"import tkinter as tk import random CELL, COLS, ROWS, SPEED = 20 , 20 , 15 , 120 root = tk.Tk() root.title( \"Snake\" ) c = tk.Canvas(root, width=CELL * COLS, height=CELL * ROWS, bg= \"#111\" , highlightthickness= 0 ) c.pack() def new_game (): global snake, direction, food, score, alive snake = [( 5 , 7 ), ( 4 , 7 ), ( 3 , 7 )] direction, score, alive = ( 1 , 0 ), 0 , True food = place_food() step() def place_food (): while True : p = (random.randrange(COLS), random.randrange(ROWS)) if p not in snake: return p def turn (dx, dy): global direction if (dx, dy) != (-direction[ 0 ], -direction[ 1 ]): # no reversing into yourself direction = (dx, dy) def draw (): c.delete( \"all\" ) fx, fy = food c.create_oval(fx * CELL + 3 , fy * CELL + 3 , (fx + 1 ) * CELL - 3 , (fy + 1 ) * CELL - 3 , fill= \"#c23b22\" , outline= \"\" ) for x, y in snake: c.create_rectangle(x * CELL + 1 , y * CELL + 1 , (x + 1 ) * CELL - 1 , (y + 1 ) * CELL - 1 , fill= \"#ffb000\" , outline= \"\" ) c.create_text( 6 , 4 , text= f\" Score {score} \" , anchor= \"nw\" , fill= \"white\" ) if not alive: c.create_text(CELL * COLS / 2 , CELL * ROWS / 2 , text= \"Game over: press Space\" , fill= \"white\" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 16 )) def step (): global food, score, alive hx, hy = snake[ 0 ] head = (hx + direction[ 0 ], hy + direction[ 1 ]) if head in snake or not ( 0 <= head[ 0 ] < COLS and 0 <= head[ 1 ] < ROWS): alive = False draw() return snake.insert( 0 , head) if head == food: score += 1 food = place_food() else : snake.pop() draw() root.after(SPEED, step) for key, d in { \"Up\" : ( 0 , - 1 ), \"Down\" : ( 0 , 1 ), \"Left\" : (- 1 , 0 ), \"Right"},{"id":"tk.recipe-notepad","s":"tk-recipes","q":"How do I make a simple text editor (Notepad)?","qh":"How do I make a simple text editor (Notepad)?","a":"A Text widget with scrollbar, a File menu with New/Open/Save/Save As, keyboard shortcuts, and a title showing the file name.","aka":"text editor, notepad clone, open save file editor, editor project","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> filedialog, messagebox, scrolledtext\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Editor</i>(tk.Tk):\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"b\">super</i>().__init__()\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.path = <i class=\"k\">None</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;600x400&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.text = scrolledtext.ScrolledText(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, wrap=<i class=\"s\">&quot;word&quot;</i>, undo=<i class=\"k\">True</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Courier&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">12</i>))\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.text.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n        bar = tk.Menu(<i class=\"k\">self</i>)\n        fm = tk.Menu(bar, tearoff=<i class=\"k\">False</i>)\n        fm.add_command(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;New&quot;</i>, accelerator=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ctrl+N&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.new)\n        fm.add_command(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Open...&quot;</i>, accelerator=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ctrl+O&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.<i class=\"b\">open</i>)\n        fm.add_command(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Save&quot;</i>, accelerator=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ctrl+S&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.save)\n        fm.add_command(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Save as...&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.save_as)\n        fm.add_separator()\n        fm.add_command(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Exit&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.quit_app)\n        bar.add_cascade(label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;File&quot;</i>, menu=fm)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.config(menu=bar)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Control-n&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"k\">self</i>.new())\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Control-o&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"k\">self</i>.<i class=\"b\">open</i>())\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Control-s&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"k\">self</i>.save())\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.protocol(<i class=\"s\">&quot;WM_DELETE_WINDOW&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.quit_app)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.update_title()\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">update_title</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.title((<i class=\"k\">self</i>.path <i class=\"k\">or</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;Untitled&quot;</i>) + <i class=\"s\">&quot; - Editor&quot;</i>)\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">new</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.text.delete(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>); <i class=\"k\">self</i>.path = <i class=\"k\">None</i>; <i class=\"k\">self</i>.update_title()\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">open</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        p = filedialog.askopenfilename(filetypes=[(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Text&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;*.txt&quot;</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;All&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;*.*&quot;</i>)])\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> p:\n            <i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(p, encoding=<i class=\"s\">&quot;utf-8&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n                <i class=\"k\">self</i>.text.delete(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>); <i class=\"k\">self</i>.text.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, f.read())\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.path = p; <i class=\"k\">self</i>.update_title(); <i class=\"k\">self</i>.text.edit_modified(<i class=\"k\">False</i>)\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">save</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>.path:\n            <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>.save_as()\n        <i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>.path, <i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>, encoding=<i class=\"s\">&quot;utf-8&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n            f.write(<i class=\"k\">self</i>.text.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end-1c&quot;</i>))\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.text.edit_modified(<i class=\"k\">False</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"k\">True</i>\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">save_as</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        p = filedialog.asksaveasfilename(defaultextension=<i class=\"s\">&quot;.txt&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> p:\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.path = p; <i class=\"k\">self</i>.update_title()\n            <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>.save()\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">quit_app</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>.text.edit_modified():\n            ans = messagebox.askyesnocancel(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Save?&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Save changes first?&quot;</i>)\n            <i class=\"k\">if</i> ans <i class=\"k\">is</i> <i class=\"k\">None</i>:\n                <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n            <i class=\"k\">if</i> ans <i class=\"k\">and</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>.save():\n                <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.destroy()\n\nEditor().mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.filedialog-open","tk.filedialog-save","tk.menubar"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import filedialog, messagebox, scrolledtext class Editor (tk.Tk): def __init__ ( self ): super ().__init__() self .path = None self .geometry( \"600x400\" ) self .text = scrolledtext.ScrolledText( self , wrap= \"word\" , undo= True , font=( \"Courier\" , 12 )) self .text.pack(fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) bar = tk.Menu( self ) fm = tk.Menu(bar, tearoff= False ) fm.add_command(label= \"New\" , accelerator= \"Ctrl+N\" , command= self .new) fm.add_command(label= \"Open...\" , accelerator= \"Ctrl+O\" , command= self . open ) fm.add_command(label= \"Save\" , accelerator= \"Ctrl+S\" , command= self .save) fm.add_command(label= \"Save as...\" , command= self .save_as) fm.add_separator() fm.add_command(label= \"Exit\" , command= self .quit_app) bar.add_cascade(label= \"File\" , menu=fm) self .config(menu=bar) self .bind( \"<Control-n>\" , lambda e: self .new()) self .bind( \"<Control-o>\" , lambda e: self . open ()) self .bind( \"<Control-s>\" , lambda e: self .save()) self .protocol( \"WM_DELETE_WINDOW\" , self .quit_app) self .update_title() def update_title ( self ): self .title(( self .path or \"Untitled\" ) + \" - Editor\" ) def new ( self ): self .text.delete( \"1.0\" , \"end\" ); self .path = None ; self .update_title() def open ( self ): p = filedialog.askopenfilename(filetypes=[( \"Text\" , \"*.txt\" ), ( \"All\" , \"*.*\" )]) if p: with open (p, encoding= \"utf-8\" ) as f: self .text.delete( \"1.0\" , \"end\" ); self .text.insert( \"1.0\" , f.read()) self .path = p; self .update_title(); self .text.edit_modified( False ) def save ( self ): if not self .path: return self .save_as() with open ( "},{"id":"tk.recipe-converter","s":"tk-recipes","q":"How do I make a unit converter (temperature, distance)?","qh":"How do I make a unit converter (temperature, distance)?","a":"An Entry, a Combobox of conversions, and a dict of formulas; update the result live with <code>trace_add</code>.","aka":"unit converter, temperature converter, celsius fahrenheit converter, km miles converter, currency converter gui","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nCONVERSIONS = {\n    <i class=\"s\">&quot;°C → °F&quot;</i>: <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> x: x * <i class=\"n\">9</i> / <i class=\"n\">5</i> + <i class=\"n\">32</i>,\n    <i class=\"s\">&quot;°F → °C&quot;</i>: <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> x: (x - <i class=\"n\">32</i>) * <i class=\"n\">5</i> / <i class=\"n\">9</i>,\n    <i class=\"s\">&quot;km → miles&quot;</i>: <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> x: x * <i class=\"n\">0.621371</i>,\n    <i class=\"s\">&quot;miles → km&quot;</i>: <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> x: x / <i class=\"n\">0.621371</i>,\n    <i class=\"s\">&quot;kg → lb&quot;</i>: <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> x: x * <i class=\"n\">2.20462</i>,\n}\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Converter&quot;</i>)\nvalue = tk.StringVar(value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;100&quot;</i>)\nkind = ttk.Combobox(root, values=<i class=\"b\">list</i>(CONVERSIONS), state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;readonly&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">14</i>)\nkind.current(<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\nresult = tk.Label(root, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">18</i>), width=<i class=\"n\">14</i>)\ntk.Entry(root, textvariable=value, width=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">14</i>)).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">8</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nkind.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">8</i>)\nresult.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, columnspan=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">update</i>(*args):\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        result.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{CONVERSIONS[kind.get()](<i class=\"b\">float</i>(value.get())):<i class=\"s\">.2f</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> ValueError:\n        result.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;-&quot;</i>)\n\nvalue.trace_add(<i class=\"s\">&quot;write&quot;</i>, update)\nkind.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;&lt;ComboboxSelected&gt;&gt;&quot;</i>, update)\nupdate()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.trace","tk.combobox"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk CONVERSIONS = { \"°C → °F\" : lambda x: x * 9 / 5 + 32 , \"°F → °C\" : lambda x: (x - 32 ) * 5 / 9 , \"km → miles\" : lambda x: x * 0.621371 , \"miles → km\" : lambda x: x / 0.621371 , \"kg → lb\" : lambda x: x * 2.20462 , } root = tk.Tk() root.title( \"Converter\" ) value = tk.StringVar(value= \"100\" ) kind = ttk.Combobox(root, values= list (CONVERSIONS), state= \"readonly\" , width= 14 ) kind.current( 0 ) result = tk.Label(root, font=( \"Helvetica\" , 18 ), width= 14 ) tk.Entry(root, textvariable=value, width= 10 , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 14 )).grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , padx= 8 , pady= 10 ) kind.grid(row= 0 , column= 1 , padx= 8 ) result.grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , columnspan= 2 , pady=( 0 , 10 )) def update (*args): try : result.config(text= f\" {CONVERSIONS[kind.get()]( float (value.get())): .2f } \" ) except ValueError: result.config(text= \"-\" ) value.trace_add( \"write\" , update) kind.bind( \"<<ComboboxSelected>>\" , update) update() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.recipe-guess","s":"tk-recipes","q":"How do I make a number guessing game with a GUI?","qh":"How do I make a number guessing game with a GUI?","a":"Pick <code>random.randint</code>, read guesses from an Entry, say higher/lower, count tries.","aka":"guess the number, guessing game gui, higher lower game, random number game tkinter","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> random\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Guess&quot;</i>)\nstate = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;secret&quot;</i>: random.randint(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">100</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;tries&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">0</i>}\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;I&#x27;m thinking of a number from 1 to 100&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">16</i>, <i class=\"n\">6</i>))\nguess = tk.Entry(root, width=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">16</i>), justify=<i class=\"s\">&quot;center&quot;</i>)\nguess.pack()\nmsg = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">13</i>))\nmsg.pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">check</i>(event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        g = <i class=\"b\">int</i>(guess.get())\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> ValueError:\n        msg.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Type a whole number&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n    state[<i class=\"s\">&quot;tries&quot;</i>] += <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n    guess.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> g &lt; state[<i class=\"s\">&quot;secret&quot;</i>]:\n        msg.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{g}<i class=\"s\">: higher</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">elif</i> g &gt; state[<i class=\"s\">&quot;secret&quot;</i>]:\n        msg.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{g}<i class=\"s\">: lower</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">else</i>:\n        msg.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Yes! </i>{g}<i class=\"s\"> in </i>{state[<i class=\"s\">&#x27;tries&#x27;</i>]}<i class=\"s\"> tries. New number chosen.</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n        state[<i class=\"s\">&quot;secret&quot;</i>], state[<i class=\"s\">&quot;tries&quot;</i>] = random.randint(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">100</i>), <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Guess&quot;</i>, command=check).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">16</i>))\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Return&gt;&quot;</i>, check)\nguess.focus()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk import random root = tk.Tk() root.title( \"Guess\" ) state = { \"secret\" : random.randint( 1 , 100 ), \"tries\" : 0 } tk.Label(root, text= \"I'm thinking of a number from 1 to 100\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady=( 16 , 6 )) guess = tk.Entry(root, width= 6 , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 16 ), justify= \"center\" ) guess.pack() msg = tk.Label(root, text= \"\" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 13 )) msg.pack(pady= 6 ) def check (event= None ): try : g = int (guess.get()) except ValueError: msg.config(text= \"Type a whole number\" ) return state[ \"tries\" ] += 1 guess.delete( 0 , \"end\" ) if g < state[ \"secret\" ]: msg.config(text= f\" {g} : higher \" ) elif g > state[ \"secret\" ]: msg.config(text= f\" {g} : lower \" ) else : msg.config(text= f\" Yes! {g} in {state[ 'tries' ]} tries. New number chosen. \" ) state[ \"secret\" ], state[ \"tries\" ] = random.randint( 1 , 100 ), 0 tk.Button(root, text= \"Guess\" , command=check).pack(pady=( 0 , 16 )) root.bind( \"<Return>\" , check) guess.focus() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.recipe-image-viewer","s":"tk-recipes","q":"How do I make an image viewer (open and browse images in a folder)?","qh":"How do I make an image viewer (open and browse images in a folder)?","a":"Pick a folder, list its image files, and show one at a time scaled to fit with Pillow; Left/Right keys move between them.","aka":"image viewer, photo viewer, picture browser, next previous image","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> filedialog\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> pathlib <i class=\"k\">import</i> Path\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> PIL <i class=\"k\">import</i> Image, ImageTk\n\nEXTS = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;.png&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;.jpg&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;.jpeg&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;.gif&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;.bmp&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;.webp&quot;</i>}\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;520x420&quot;</i>)\nview = tk.Label(root, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;black&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Open a folder&quot;</i>)\nview.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nfiles, index = [], <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">show</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> files:\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n    img = Image.<i class=\"b\">open</i>(files[index])\n    img.thumbnail((<i class=\"b\">max</i>(view.winfo_width(), <i class=\"n\">50</i>), <i class=\"b\">max</i>(view.winfo_height(), <i class=\"n\">50</i>)))\n    view.photo = ImageTk.PhotoImage(img)\n    view.config(image=view.photo, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\n    root.title(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{files[index].name}<i class=\"s\">  (</i>{index + <i class=\"n\">1</i>}<i class=\"s\">/</i>{<i class=\"b\">len</i>(files)}<i class=\"s\">)</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">open_folder</i>(folder=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> files, index\n    folder = folder <i class=\"k\">or</i> filedialog.askdirectory()\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> folder:\n        files = <i class=\"b\">sorted</i>(p <i class=\"k\">for</i> p <i class=\"k\">in</i> Path(folder).iterdir() <i class=\"k\">if</i> p.suffix.lower() <i class=\"k\">in</i> EXTS)\n        index = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n        show()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">move</i>(step):\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> index\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> files:\n        index = (index + step) % <i class=\"b\">len</i>(files)\n        show()\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Open folder&quot;</i>, command=open_folder).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>)\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Left&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: move(-<i class=\"n\">1</i>))\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Right&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: move(<i class=\"n\">1</i>))\nroot.after(<i class=\"n\">100</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: open_folder(<i class=\"s\">&quot;.&quot;</i>))     <i class=\"c\"># start with the current folder</i>\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.pillow","tk.filedialog-folder"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import filedialog from pathlib import Path from PIL import Image, ImageTk EXTS = { \".png\" , \".jpg\" , \".jpeg\" , \".gif\" , \".bmp\" , \".webp\" } root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"520x420\" ) view = tk.Label(root, bg= \"black\" , fg= \"white\" , text= \"Open a folder\" ) view.pack(fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) files, index = [], 0 def show (): if not files: return img = Image. open (files[index]) img.thumbnail(( max (view.winfo_width(), 50 ), max (view.winfo_height(), 50 ))) view.photo = ImageTk.PhotoImage(img) view.config(image=view.photo, text= \"\" ) root.title( f\" {files[index].name} ( {index + 1 } / { len (files)} ) \" ) def open_folder (folder= None ): global files, index folder = folder or filedialog.askdirectory() if folder: files = sorted (p for p in Path(folder).iterdir() if p.suffix.lower() in EXTS) index = 0 show() def move (step): global index if files: index = (index + step) % len (files) show() tk.Button(root, text= \"Open folder\" , command=open_folder).pack(side= \"left\" ) root.bind( \"<Left>\" , lambda e: move(- 1 )) root.bind( \"<Right>\" , lambda e: move( 1 )) root.after( 100 , lambda : open_folder( \".\" )) # start with the current folder root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.pyinstaller","s":"tk-extras","q":"How do I turn my tkinter program into an .exe (or app) to share?","qh":"How do I turn my tkinter program into an .exe (or app) to share?","a":"Install PyInstaller and run <code>pyinstaller --onefile --windowed app.py</code>. The program appears in the <code>dist</code> folder. <code>--windowed</code> (<code>-w</code>) hides the black console window.","aka":"exe, executable, pyinstaller, compile python, share program, standalone app, no console window, auto-py-to-exe, py to exe","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code sh\" data-tag=\"terminal\"><pre>pip install pyinstaller\npyinstaller --onefile --windowed --icon=app.ico app.py</pre></figure><ul><li>Build on the same system you're targeting: a Windows <code>.exe</code> must be built on Windows, a Mac app on a Mac.</li><li>Antivirus programs sometimes flag one-file exes; <code>--onedir</code> (the default) is flagged less.</li><li>Images and data files must be added with <code>--add-data</code> and found with <a href=\"#/a/tk.resource-path\">My images/files are missing after building with PyInstaller. How do I find them?</a>.</li><li><code>auto-py-to-exe</code> (<code>pip install auto-py-to-exe</code>) is a GUI for the same thing.</li></ul>","see":["tk.resource-path"],"t":"pip install pyinstaller pyinstaller --onefile --windowed --icon=app.ico app.py Build on the same system you're targeting: a Windows .exe must be built on Windows, a Mac app on a Mac. Antivirus programs sometimes flag one-file exes; --onedir (the default) is flagged less. Images and data files must be added with --add-data and found with My images/files are missing after building with PyInstaller. How do I find them? . auto-py-to-exe ( pip install auto-py-to-exe ) is a GUI for the same thing."},{"id":"tk.resource-path","s":"tk-extras","q":"My images/files are missing after building with PyInstaller. How do I find them?","qh":"My images/files are missing after building with PyInstaller. How do I find them?","a":"Add them with <code>--add-data &quot;photo.png:.&quot;</code> (on Windows use <code>;</code> instead of <code>:</code> in older versions), and build paths with a helper that checks <code>sys._MEIPASS</code>.","aka":"pyinstaller images not found, add-data, sys._MEIPASS, resource path, bundle files pyinstaller","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> sys\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> pathlib <i class=\"k\">import</i> Path\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">resource</i>(name):\n    base = Path(<i class=\"b\">getattr</i>(sys, <i class=\"s\">&quot;_MEIPASS&quot;</i>, Path(__file__).parent))\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> base / name\n\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(resource(<i class=\"s\">&quot;photo.png&quot;</i>))</pre></figure><figure class=\"code sh\" data-tag=\"terminal\"><pre>pyinstaller --onefile --windowed --add-data &quot;photo.png:.&quot; app.py</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import sys from pathlib import Path def resource (name): base = Path( getattr (sys, \"_MEIPASS\" , Path(__file__).parent)) return base / name print (resource( \"photo.png\" )) pyinstaller --onefile --windowed --add-data \"photo.png:.\" app.py"},{"id":"tk.sound","s":"tk-extras","q":"How do I play a sound or beep in a tkinter app?","qh":"How do I play a sound or beep in a tkinter app?","a":"A beep: <code>root.bell()</code>. Wav files on Windows: <code>winsound.PlaySound(path, winsound.SND_ASYNC)</code>. Cross-platform and for mp3: <code>pygame.mixer</code> (<code>pip install pygame</code>).","aka":"play sound, beep, audio, music, winsound, playsound, pygame mixer, sound effect","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> sys\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Beep&quot;</i>, command=root.bell).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">play_wav</i>(path):\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> sys.platform == <i class=\"s\">&quot;win32&quot;</i>:\n        <i class=\"k\">import</i> winsound\n        winsound.PlaySound(path, winsound.SND_FILENAME | winsound.SND_ASYNC)\n\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> pygame          <i class=\"c\"># pip install pygame</i>\npygame.mixer.init()\npygame.mixer.Sound(<i class=\"s\">&quot;click.wav&quot;</i>).play()          <i class=\"c\"># sound effect</i>\npygame.mixer.music.load(<i class=\"s\">&quot;song.mp3&quot;</i>)             <i class=\"c\"># background music</i>\npygame.mixer.music.play(-<i class=\"n\">1</i>)                     <i class=\"c\"># -1 = loop forever</i></pre></figure><p>Use <code>SND_ASYNC</code> (or pygame) so the window doesn't freeze while the sound plays.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk import sys root = tk.Tk() tk.Button(root, text= \"Beep\" , command=root.bell).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 20 ) def play_wav (path): if sys.platform == \"win32\" : import winsound winsound.PlaySound(path, winsound.SND_FILENAME | winsound.SND_ASYNC) root.mainloop() import pygame # pip install pygame pygame.mixer.init() pygame.mixer.Sound( \"click.wav\" ).play() # sound effect pygame.mixer.music.load( \"song.mp3\" ) # background music pygame.mixer.music.play(- 1 ) # -1 = loop forever Use SND_ASYNC (or pygame) so the window doesn't freeze while the sound plays."},{"id":"tk.clipboard","s":"tk-extras","q":"How do I copy text to the clipboard (or read it)?","qh":"How do I copy text to the clipboard (or read it)?","a":"<code>root.clipboard_clear()</code>, then <code>root.clipboard_append(text)</code>. Read with <code>root.clipboard_get()</code> (raises <code>TclError</code> if the clipboard is empty or not text).","aka":"clipboard, copy to clipboard, paste from clipboard, clipboard_append, clipboard_get","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ne = tk.Entry(root, width=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\ne.insert(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;copy me&quot;</i>)\ne.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">copy</i>():\n    root.clipboard_clear()\n    root.clipboard_append(e.get())\n    root.update()                  <i class=\"c\"># keeps it on the clipboard after the app closes</i>\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">paste</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        e.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n        e.insert(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, root.clipboard_get())\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> tk.TclError:\n        <i class=\"k\">pass</i>\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Copy&quot;</i>, command=copy).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Paste&quot;</i>, command=paste).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() e = tk.Entry(root, width= 30 ) e.insert( 0 , \"copy me\" ) e.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) def copy (): root.clipboard_clear() root.clipboard_append(e.get()) root.update() # keeps it on the clipboard after the app closes def paste (): try : e.delete( 0 , \"end\" ) e.insert( 0 , root.clipboard_get()) except tk.TclError: pass tk.Button(root, text= \"Copy\" , command=copy).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 20 , pady=( 0 , 10 )) tk.Button(root, text= \"Paste\" , command=paste).pack(side= \"left\" , pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.open-link","s":"tk-extras","q":"How do I open a website link from my app?","qh":"How do I open a website link from my app?","a":"<code>import webbrowser; webbrowser.open(&quot;https://...&quot;)</code>. Make a Label look like a link and bind a click to it.","aka":"hyperlink, open url, webbrowser, clickable link label, open browser","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> webbrowser\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlink = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;python.org&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;blue&quot;</i>, cursor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;hand2&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">12</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;underline&quot;</i>))\nlink.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nlink.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: webbrowser.<i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;https://www.python.org&quot;</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk import webbrowser root = tk.Tk() link = tk.Label(root, text= \"python.org\" , fg= \"blue\" , cursor= \"hand2\" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 12 , \"underline\" )) link.pack(padx= 40 , pady= 30 ) link.bind( \"<Button-1>\" , lambda e: webbrowser. open ( \"https://www.python.org\" )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.open-file-default-app","s":"tk-extras","q":"How do I open a file with its normal program (PDF reader, Notepad...)?","qh":"How do I open a file with its normal program (PDF reader, Notepad...)?","a":"Windows: <code>os.startfile(path)</code>. macOS: <code>subprocess.run([&quot;open&quot;, path])</code>. Linux: <code>subprocess.run([&quot;xdg-open&quot;, path])</code>.","aka":"open file default program, os.startfile, open pdf, xdg-open, launch file","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> os, sys, subprocess\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">open_with_default_app</i>(path):\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> sys.platform == <i class=\"s\">&quot;win32&quot;</i>:\n        os.startfile(path)\n    <i class=\"k\">elif</i> sys.platform == <i class=\"s\">&quot;darwin&quot;</i>:\n        subprocess.run([<i class=\"s\">&quot;open&quot;</i>, path])\n    <i class=\"k\">else</i>:\n        subprocess.run([<i class=\"s\">&quot;xdg-open&quot;</i>, path])\n\nopen_with_default_app(<i class=\"s\">&quot;notes.txt&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import os, sys, subprocess def open_with_default_app (path): if sys.platform == \"win32\" : os.startfile(path) elif sys.platform == \"darwin\" : subprocess.run([ \"open\" , path]) else : subprocess.run([ \"xdg-open\" , path]) open_with_default_app( \"notes.txt\" )"},{"id":"tk.run-command","s":"tk-extras","q":"How do I run another program or command from a button and show its output?","qh":"How do I run another program or command from a button and show its output?","a":"Use <code>subprocess.run([...], capture_output=True, text=True)</code> and put <code>result.stdout</code> in a Text widget. For long commands, run it in a thread.","aka":"subprocess tkinter, run command, run script from gui, terminal output in gui, os.system","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> subprocess, sys\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nout = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">60</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nout.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">run</i>():\n    r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, <i class=\"s\">&quot;--version&quot;</i>], capture_output=<i class=\"k\">True</i>, text=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n    out.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, r.stdout + r.stderr)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Run python --version&quot;</i>, command=run).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.thread"],"t":"import tkinter as tk import subprocess, sys root = tk.Tk() out = tk.Text(root, width= 60 , height= 10 ) out.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) def run (): r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"--version\" ], capture_output= True , text= True ) out.insert( \"end\" , r.stdout + r.stderr) tk.Button(root, text= \"Run python --version\" , command=run).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.tk-version","s":"tk-extras","q":"Which version of tkinter / Tk do I have?","qh":"Which version of tkinter / Tk do I have?","a":"<code>tkinter.TkVersion</code> (e.g. 8.6 or 9.0). <code>python -m tkinter</code> opens a test window showing it.","aka":"tkinter version, tk version, TkVersion, tcl version, check tkinter installed","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Tk&quot;</i>, tkinter.TkVersion, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Tcl&quot;</i>, tkinter.TclVersion)</pre></figure><figure class=\"code sh\" data-tag=\"terminal\"><pre>python -m tkinter</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter print ( \"Tk\" , tkinter.TkVersion, \"Tcl\" , tkinter.TclVersion) python -m tkinter"},{"id":"tk.fullscreen-game-scale","s":"tk-extras","q":"How do I make my game/canvas scale to any window size?","qh":"How do I make my game/canvas scale to any window size?","a":"Draw everything in your own fixed \"world\" units, and on <code>&lt;Configure&gt;</code> compute a scale factor and use <code>canvas.scale(&quot;all&quot;, 0, 0, sx, sy)</code>, or redraw using <code>x * scale</code>.","aka":"scale canvas, resize canvas content, zoom canvas, fit game to window, responsive canvas","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nW, H = <i class=\"n\">400</i>, <i class=\"n\">300</i>                                   <i class=\"c\"># world size</i>\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=W, height=H, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, highlightthickness=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\nc.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\ncurrent = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;s&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">1.0</i>}\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">draw</i>():\n    c.delete(<i class=\"s\">&quot;all&quot;</i>)\n    s = current[<i class=\"s\">&quot;s&quot;</i>]\n    c.create_rectangle(<i class=\"n\">50</i> * s, <i class=\"n\">50</i> * s, <i class=\"n\">150</i> * s, <i class=\"n\">120</i> * s, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>)\n    c.create_oval(<i class=\"n\">250</i> * s, <i class=\"n\">150</i> * s, <i class=\"n\">350</i> * s, <i class=\"n\">250</i> * s, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>)\n    c.create_text(<i class=\"n\">200</i> * s, <i class=\"n\">20</i> * s, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;scales with the window&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"b\">max</i>(<i class=\"n\">6</i>, <i class=\"b\">int</i>(<i class=\"n\">12</i> * s))))\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">resized</i>(e):\n    current[<i class=\"s\">&quot;s&quot;</i>] = <i class=\"b\">min</i>(e.width / W, e.height / H)\n    draw()\n\nc.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Configure&gt;&quot;</i>, resized)\ndraw()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk W, H = 400 , 300 # world size root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width=W, height=H, bg= \"white\" , highlightthickness= 0 ) c.pack(fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) current = { \"s\" : 1.0 } def draw (): c.delete( \"all\" ) s = current[ \"s\" ] c.create_rectangle( 50 * s, 50 * s, 150 * s, 120 * s, fill= \"#fc6\" ) c.create_oval( 250 * s, 150 * s, 350 * s, 250 * s, fill= \"#c23b22\" ) c.create_text( 200 * s, 20 * s, text= \"scales with the window\" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , max ( 6 , int ( 12 * s)))) def resized (e): current[ \"s\" ] = min (e.width / W, e.height / H) draw() c.bind( \"<Configure>\" , resized) draw() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.screenshot","s":"tk-extras","q":"How do I take a screenshot of my window?","qh":"How do I take a screenshot of my window?","a":"With Pillow: <code>ImageGrab.grab(bbox=(x, y, x + w, y + h))</code> using the window's <code>winfo_rootx/rooty/width/height</code>. Works on Windows and macOS (Linux needs X11).","aka":"screenshot, capture window, save window as image, ImageGrab","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> PIL <i class=\"k\">import</i> ImageGrab\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Snap me&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">24</i>), padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>).pack()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">snap</i>():\n    root.update()\n    x, y = root.winfo_rootx(), root.winfo_rooty()\n    w, h = root.winfo_width(), root.winfo_height()\n    ImageGrab.grab(bbox=(x, y, x + w, y + h)).save(<i class=\"s\">&quot;window.png&quot;</i>)\n\nroot.after(<i class=\"n\">500</i>, snap)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from PIL import ImageGrab root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"Snap me\" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 24 ), padx= 40 , pady= 40 ).pack() def snap (): root.update() x, y = root.winfo_rootx(), root.winfo_rooty() w, h = root.winfo_width(), root.winfo_height() ImageGrab.grab(bbox=(x, y, x + w, y + h)).save( \"window.png\" ) root.after( 500 , snap) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.other-gui","s":"tk-extras","q":"Should I use tkinter or something else (PyQt, Kivy, CustomTkinter)?","qh":"Should I use tkinter or something else (PyQt, Kivy, CustomTkinter)?","a":"tkinter is built in, simple, and fine for tools, forms and small games. Pick something else when you need: a modern look with little effort (CustomTkinter), very large professional apps (PySide6/PyQt6), phones (Kivy, BeeWare), or 2D games with sound and sprites (pygame).","aka":"tkinter alternatives, pyqt vs tkinter, kivy, pyside, customtkinter vs tkinter, best python gui, flet, dearpygui","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code out\" data-tag=\"output\"><pre>tkinter        built in, simplest, older look (ttk helps)\ncustomtkinter  tkinter with modern rounded widgets       pip install customtkinter\nPySide6/PyQt6  big, powerful, professional desktop apps   pip install pyside6\nKivy           touch apps, also Android/iOS              pip install kivy\npygame         games: sprites, sound, joystick           pip install pygame\nFlet           Flutter-style apps in Python              pip install flet</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"tkinter built in, simplest, older look (ttk helps) customtkinter tkinter with modern rounded widgets pip install customtkinter PySide6/PyQt6 big, powerful, professional desktop apps pip install pyside6 Kivy touch apps, also Android/iOS pip install kivy pygame games: sprites, sound, joystick pip install pygame Flet Flutter-style apps in Python pip install flet"},{"id":"py.print","s":"py-basics","q":"How do I print several things, without a new line, or with a separator?","qh":"How do I print several things, without a new line, or with a separator?","a":"<code>print(a, b)</code> puts a space between items. <code>sep=</code> changes the separator, <code>end=</code> changes what comes after (default <code>&quot;\\n&quot;</code>).","aka":"print, print without newline, end parameter, sep parameter, print on same line, print multiple variables","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>name, age = <i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">36</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Name:&quot;</i>, name, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Age:&quot;</i>, age)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;b&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;c&quot;</i>, sep=<i class=\"s\">&quot;-&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Loading&quot;</i>, end=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;...&quot;</i>, end=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot; done&quot;</i>)</pre></figure><figure class=\"code out\" data-tag=\"output\"><pre>Name: Ada Age: 36\na-b-c\nLoading... done</pre></figure>","see":["py.fstring"],"t":"name, age = \"Ada\" , 36 print ( \"Name:\" , name, \"Age:\" , age) print ( \"a\" , \"b\" , \"c\" , sep= \"-\" ) print ( \"Loading\" , end= \"\" ) print ( \"...\" , end= \"\" ) print ( \" done\" ) Name: Ada Age: 36 a-b-c Loading... done"},{"id":"py.input","s":"py-basics","q":"How do I ask the user for input?","qh":"How do I ask the user for input?","a":"<code>answer = input(&quot;Question? &quot;)</code>. It always returns a <b>string</b>; convert with <code>int()</code> or <code>float()</code> for numbers.","aka":"input, user input, read from keyboard, ask question, raw_input, get input from user","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>name = <i class=\"b\">input</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;What&#x27;s your name? &quot;</i>)\nage = <i class=\"b\">int</i>(<i class=\"b\">input</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;How old are you? &quot;</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Hi </i>{name}<i class=\"s\">, next year you&#x27;ll be </i>{age + <i class=\"n\">1</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)</pre></figure><p><code>raw_input()</code> is Python 2. In Python 3 it's just <code>input()</code>.</p>","see":["py.input-number","py.err.valueerror-int"],"t":"name = input ( \"What's your name? \" ) age = int ( input ( \"How old are you? \" )) print ( f\" Hi {name} , next year you'll be {age + 1 } \" ) raw_input() is Python 2. In Python 3 it's just input() ."},{"id":"py.input-number","s":"py-basics","q":"How do I keep asking until the user types a valid number?","qh":"How do I keep asking until the user types a valid number?","a":"Loop forever, <code>try</code> to convert, and <code>break</code> (or <code>return</code>) when it works.","aka":"validate input, input loop, ask again, input must be number, try except input, input validation","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">ask_int</i>(prompt, low=<i class=\"k\">None</i>, high=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    <i class=\"k\">while</i> <i class=\"k\">True</i>:\n        text = <i class=\"b\">input</i>(prompt)\n        <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n            n = <i class=\"b\">int</i>(text)\n        <i class=\"k\">except</i> ValueError:\n            <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Please type a whole number.&quot;</i>)\n            <i class=\"k\">continue</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> (low <i class=\"k\">is</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> <i class=\"k\">None</i> <i class=\"k\">and</i> n &lt; low) <i class=\"k\">or</i> (high <i class=\"k\">is</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> <i class=\"k\">None</i> <i class=\"k\">and</i> n &gt; high):\n            <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Pick between </i>{low}<i class=\"s\"> and </i>{high}<i class=\"s\">.</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n            <i class=\"k\">continue</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> n\n\nage = ask_int(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Age: &quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">120</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;OK:&quot;</i>, age)</pre></figure>","see":["py.try-except"],"t":"def ask_int (prompt, low= None , high= None ): while True : text = input (prompt) try : n = int (text) except ValueError: print ( \"Please type a whole number.\" ) continue if (low is not None and n < low) or (high is not None and n > high): print ( f\" Pick between {low} and {high} . \" ) continue return n age = ask_int( \"Age: \" , 1 , 120 ) print ( \"OK:\" , age)"},{"id":"py.variables","s":"py-basics","q":"How do variables work in Python?","qh":"How do variables work in Python?","a":"<code>name = value</code> creates or updates a variable; no type declaration needed. Names use letters, digits and <code>_</code>, can't start with a digit, and are case-sensitive.","aka":"variable, assign, create variable, variable names, multiple assignment, swap variables, constants","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>score = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\nscore = score + <i class=\"n\">10</i>        <i class=\"c\"># or: score += 10</i>\nx, y = <i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>               <i class=\"c\"># assign two at once</i>\nx, y = y, x               <i class=\"c\"># swap</i>\nMAX_LIVES = <i class=\"n\">3</i>             <i class=\"c\"># CAPITALS = a constant by convention</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(score, x, y, MAX_LIVES)</pre></figure>","see":["py.types"],"t":"score = 0 score = score + 10 # or: score += 10 x, y = 3 , 4 # assign two at once x, y = y, x # swap MAX_LIVES = 3 # CAPITALS = a constant by convention print (score, x, y, MAX_LIVES)"},{"id":"py.types","s":"py-basics","q":"What are the basic data types, and how do I check a value's type?","qh":"What are the basic data types, and how do I check a value's type?","a":"<code>int</code> (whole numbers), <code>float</code> (decimals), <code>str</code> (text), <code>bool</code> (<code>True</code>/<code>False</code>), <code>None</code>, plus <code>list</code>, <code>tuple</code>, <code>dict</code>, <code>set</code>. Check with <code>type(x)</code> or <code>isinstance(x, int)</code>.","aka":"data types, type(), isinstance, int float str bool, check type, what type is this","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">for</i> value <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"n\">42</i>, <i class=\"n\">3.14</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;hi&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">True</i>, <i class=\"k\">None</i>, [<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>], (<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>), {<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">1</i>}, {<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>}):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">repr</i>(value), <i class=\"b\">type</i>(value).__name__)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">isinstance</i>(<i class=\"n\">5</i>, (<i class=\"b\">int</i>, <i class=\"b\">float</i>)))</pre></figure>","see":["py.convert"],"t":"for value in ( 42 , 3.14 , \"hi\" , True , None , [ 1 , 2 ], ( 1 , 2 ), { \"a\" : 1 }, { 1 , 2 }): print ( repr (value), type (value).__name__) print ( isinstance ( 5 , ( int , float )))"},{"id":"py.convert","s":"py-basics","q":"How do I convert between strings and numbers?","qh":"How do I convert between strings and numbers?","a":"<code>int(&quot;42&quot;)</code>, <code>float(&quot;3.5&quot;)</code>, <code>str(42)</code>. <code>int(3.9)</code> cuts off the decimals (gives 3); use <code>round()</code> to round.","aka":"convert string to int, int to string, str(), int(), float(), cast, type conversion, number to text","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">int</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;42&quot;</i>) + <i class=\"n\">1</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">float</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;3.5&quot;</i>) * <i class=\"n\">2</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Score: &quot;</i> + <i class=\"b\">str</i>(<i class=\"n\">99</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">int</i>(<i class=\"n\">3.9</i>), <i class=\"b\">round</i>(<i class=\"n\">3.9</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">int</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;ff&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">16</i>), bin(<i class=\"n\">10</i>), hex(<i class=\"n\">255</i>))    <i class=\"c\"># other bases</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">int</i>(<i class=\"b\">float</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;3.0&quot;</i>)))                     <i class=\"c\"># &quot;3.0&quot; -&gt; 3 needs float() first</i></pre></figure>","see":["py.err.valueerror-int","py.err.str-int-concat"],"t":"print ( int ( \"42\" ) + 1 ) print ( float ( \"3.5\" ) * 2 ) print ( \"Score: \" + str ( 99 )) print ( int ( 3.9 ), round ( 3.9 )) print ( int ( \"ff\" , 16 ), bin( 10 ), hex( 255 )) # other bases print ( int ( float ( \"3.0\" ))) # \"3.0\" -> 3 needs float() first"},{"id":"py.fstring","s":"py-basics","q":"How do I put variables inside a string (f-strings, format)?","qh":"How do I put variables inside a string (f-strings, format)?","a":"Put <code>f</code> before the quotes and variables in <code>{}</code>: <code>f&quot;Hi {name}&quot;</code>. Expressions work too: <code>f&quot;{price * 2}&quot;</code>.","aka":"f string, format string, string formatting, string interpolation, .format(), % formatting, insert variable in string","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>name, score, price = <i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">7</i>, <i class=\"n\">3.5</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{name}<i class=\"s\"> scored </i>{score}<i class=\"s\"> points</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Twice the price: </i>{price * <i class=\"n\">2</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{name=}<i class=\"s\">, </i>{score=}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)                     <i class=\"c\"># handy for debugging</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;{} scored {}&quot;</i>.<i class=\"b\">format</i>(name, score))       <i class=\"c\"># older style</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;%s scored %d&quot;</i> % (name, score))           <i class=\"c\"># oldest style</i></pre></figure>","see":["py.format-decimals"],"t":"name, score, price = \"Ada\" , 7 , 3.5 print ( f\" {name} scored {score} points \" ) print ( f\" Twice the price: {price * 2 } \" ) print ( f\" {name=} , {score=} \" ) # handy for debugging print ( \"{} scored {}\" . format (name, score)) # older style print ( \"%s scored %d\" % (name, score)) # oldest style"},{"id":"py.format-decimals","s":"py-basics","q":"How do I show a number with 2 decimal places, commas, or padding?","qh":"How do I show a number with 2 decimal places, commas, or padding?","a":"Use a format spec after <code>:</code> in an f-string: <code>{x:.2f}</code> (2 decimals), <code>{n:,}</code> (commas), <code>{p:.0%}</code> (percent), <code>{n:03d}</code> (leading zeros), <code>{s:&gt;10}</code> (right-align).","aka":"2 decimal places, round to 2 decimals, format float, thousands separator, comma number, percentage, leading zeros, pad number, money format","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>x, big, part, n = <i class=\"n\">3.14159</i>, <i class=\"n\">1234567.891</i>, <i class=\"n\">0.256</i>, <i class=\"n\">7</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{x:<i class=\"s\">.2f</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)        <i class=\"c\"># 3.14</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{big:<i class=\"s\">,.2f</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)     <i class=\"c\"># 1,234,567.89</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{part:<i class=\"s\">.1%</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)     <i class=\"c\"># 25.6%</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{n:<i class=\"s\">03d</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)        <i class=\"c\"># 007</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">[</i>{<i class=\"s\">&#x27;left&#x27;</i>:<i class=\"s\">&lt;8</i>}<i class=\"s\">][</i>{<i class=\"s\">&#x27;right&#x27;</i>:<i class=\"s\">&gt;8</i>}<i class=\"s\">][</i>{<i class=\"s\">&#x27;mid&#x27;</i>:<i class=\"s\">^8</i>}<i class=\"s\">]</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">£</i>{<i class=\"n\">19.5</i>:<i class=\"s\">.2f</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)   <i class=\"c\"># £19.50</i></pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"x, big, part, n = 3.14159 , 1234567.891 , 0.256 , 7 print ( f\" {x: .2f } \" ) # 3.14 print ( f\" {big: ,.2f } \" ) # 1,234,567.89 print ( f\" {part: .1% } \" ) # 25.6% print ( f\" {n: 03d } \" ) # 007 print ( f\" [ { 'left' : <8 } ][ { 'right' : >8 } ][ { 'mid' : ^8 } ] \" ) print ( f\" £ { 19.5 : .2f } \" ) # £19.50"},{"id":"py.if","s":"py-basics","q":"How do if, elif and else work?","qh":"How do if, elif and else work?","a":"Python checks each condition from top to bottom and runs the first block that is true. Blocks are marked by <b>indentation</b> (4 spaces) and every line before a block ends with <code>:</code>.","aka":"if statement, elif, else, conditions, if else, multiple conditions, nested if","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>temp = <i class=\"n\">18</i>\n<i class=\"k\">if</i> temp &gt;= <i class=\"n\">25</i>:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hot&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">elif</i> temp &gt;= <i class=\"n\">15</i>:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Mild&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">else</i>:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Cold&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":["py.and-or","py.comparisons"],"t":"temp = 18 if temp >= 25 : print ( \"Hot\" ) elif temp >= 15 : print ( \"Mild\" ) else : print ( \"Cold\" )"},{"id":"py.comparisons","s":"py-basics","q":"What comparison operators are there (==, !=, <, in...)?","qh":"What comparison operators are there (==, !=, &lt;, in...)?","a":"<code>==</code> equal, <code>!=</code> not equal, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>, <code>in</code>, <code>not in</code>. A single <code>=</code> assigns; <code>==</code> compares. You can chain: <code>0 &lt;= x &lt; 10</code>.","aka":"comparison operators, equals, not equal, greater than, double equals, == vs =, compare strings","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>age = <i class=\"n\">15</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(age == <i class=\"n\">15</i>, age != <i class=\"n\">15</i>, <i class=\"n\">13</i> &lt;= age &lt;= <i class=\"n\">19</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;cat&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i> <i class=\"k\">in</i> [<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>], <i class=\"s\">&quot;z&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;cat&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;apple&quot;</i> &lt; <i class=\"s\">&quot;banana&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Zebra&quot;</i> &lt; <i class=\"s\">&quot;apple&quot;</i>)     <i class=\"c\"># strings compare by character codes</i></pre></figure>","see":["py.is-vs-eq"],"t":"age = 15 print (age == 15 , age != 15 , 13 <= age <= 19 ) print ( \"a\" in \"cat\" , 3 in [ 1 , 2 , 3 ], \"z\" not in \"cat\" ) print ( \"apple\" < \"banana\" , \"Zebra\" < \"apple\" ) # strings compare by character codes"},{"id":"py.and-or","s":"py-basics","q":"How do I combine conditions with and, or, not?","qh":"How do I combine conditions with and, or, not?","a":"<code>and</code> (both true), <code>or</code> (at least one), <code>not</code> (flip). Write the full comparison on <b>each side</b>: <code>x == 1 or x == 2</code>, or better <code>x in (1, 2)</code>.","aka":"and or not, multiple conditions, logical operators, if x or y, x == 1 or 2 bug","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre>answer = <i class=\"s\">&quot;no&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"k\">if</i> answer == <i class=\"s\">&quot;yes&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">or</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>:      <i class=\"c\"># always True! &quot;y&quot; on its own is truthy</i>\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;oops&quot;</i>)</pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre>answer = <i class=\"s\">&quot;no&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"k\">if</i> answer == <i class=\"s\">&quot;yes&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">or</i> answer == <i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;yes&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">if</i> answer <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;yes&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;yes&quot;</i>)\nage, member = <i class=\"n\">20</i>, <i class=\"k\">True</i>\n<i class=\"k\">if</i> age &gt;= <i class=\"n\">18</i> <i class=\"k\">and</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> member:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;adult guest&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"answer = \"no\" if answer == \"yes\" or \"y\" : # always True! \"y\" on its own is truthy print ( \"oops\" ) answer = \"no\" if answer == \"yes\" or answer == \"y\" : print ( \"yes\" ) if answer in ( \"yes\" , \"y\" ): print ( \"yes\" ) age, member = 20 , True if age >= 18 and not member: print ( \"adult guest\" )"},{"id":"py.truthy","s":"py-basics","q":"What counts as True or False (truthiness)?","qh":"What counts as True or False (truthiness)?","a":"These are false: <code>False</code>, <code>None</code>, <code>0</code>, <code>0.0</code>, <code>&quot;&quot;</code>, <code>[]</code>, <code>{}</code>, <code>()</code>, <code>set()</code>. Everything else is true. So <code>if items:</code> means \"if the list isn't empty\".","aka":"truthy falsy, empty list false, if list, if not string, bool of zero","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">for</i> v <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;hi&quot;</i>, [], [<i class=\"n\">0</i>], <i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">repr</i>(v), <i class=\"b\">bool</i>(v))\nname = <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> name:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;name is empty&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"for v in ( 0 , 1 , \"\" , \"hi\" , [], [ 0 ], None ): print ( repr (v), bool (v)) name = \"\" if not name: print ( \"name is empty\" )"},{"id":"py.ternary","s":"py-basics","q":"How do I write an if/else on one line?","qh":"How do I write an if/else on one line?","a":"<code>result = a if condition else b</code>.","aka":"ternary operator, inline if, conditional expression, one line if else","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>age = <i class=\"n\">20</i>\nstatus = <i class=\"s\">&quot;adult&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> age &gt;= <i class=\"n\">18</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;child&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(status)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;even&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"n\">7</i> % <i class=\"n\">2</i> == <i class=\"n\">0</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;odd&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"age = 20 status = \"adult\" if age >= 18 else \"child\" print (status) print ( \"even\" if 7 % 2 == 0 else \"odd\" )"},{"id":"py.for","s":"py-basics","q":"How do for loops and range() work?","qh":"How do for loops and range() work?","a":"<code>for item in something:</code> runs once per item. <code>range(5)</code> gives 0-4, <code>range(1, 6)</code> gives 1-5, <code>range(10, 0, -2)</code> counts down in twos.","aka":"for loop, range, loop n times, loop through list, range start stop step, count down, loop backwards","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">3</i>):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;hello&quot;</i>, i)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>, -<i class=\"n\">3</i>):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(n, end=<i class=\"s\">&quot; &quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>()\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> letter <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;abc&quot;</i>:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(letter.upper())\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> fruit <i class=\"k\">in</i> [<i class=\"s\">&quot;apple&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;pear&quot;</i>]:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(fruit)</pre></figure>","see":["py.enumerate","py.while"],"t":"for i in range ( 3 ): print ( \"hello\" , i) for n in range ( 10 , 0 , - 3 ): print (n, end= \" \" ) print () for letter in \"abc\" : print (letter.upper()) for fruit in [ \"apple\" , \"pear\" ]: print (fruit)"},{"id":"py.while","s":"py-basics","q":"How do while loops work (and how do I stop an infinite loop)?","qh":"How do while loops work (and how do I stop an infinite loop)?","a":"<code>while condition:</code> repeats as long as the condition is true. With <code>while True:</code> use <code>break</code> to leave. Stop a runaway program with <b>Ctrl+C</b>.","aka":"while loop, while true, infinite loop, loop until, stop loop, ctrl c, loop forever","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>count = <i class=\"n\">3</i>\n<i class=\"k\">while</i> count &gt; <i class=\"n\">0</i>:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(count)\n    count -= <i class=\"n\">1</i>          <i class=\"c\"># forget this and it never ends</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Lift off!&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">while</i> <i class=\"k\">True</i>:\n    word = <i class=\"b\">input</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Type quit: &quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> word == <i class=\"s\">&quot;quit&quot;</i>:\n        <i class=\"k\">break</i></pre></figure>","see":["py.break-continue"],"t":"count = 3 while count > 0 : print (count) count -= 1 # forget this and it never ends print ( \"Lift off!\" ) while True : word = input ( \"Type quit: \" ) if word == \"quit\" : break"},{"id":"py.break-continue","s":"py-basics","q":"What do break, continue and pass do?","qh":"What do break, continue and pass do?","a":"<code>break</code> leaves the loop now; <code>continue</code> skips to the next round; <code>pass</code> does nothing (a placeholder where a block is required).","aka":"break, continue, pass, exit loop, skip iteration, loop else","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">10</i>):\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> n == <i class=\"n\">6</i>:\n        <i class=\"k\">break</i>           <i class=\"c\"># stop at 6</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> n % <i class=\"n\">2</i> == <i class=\"n\">0</i>:\n        <i class=\"k\">continue</i>        <i class=\"c\"># skip even numbers</i>\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(n)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">todo</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">pass</i>                <i class=\"c\"># write this later</i></pre></figure><p>A loop's <code>else:</code> block runs only if the loop <b>didn't</b> <code>break</code>: handy for \"not found\".</p>","see":[],"t":"for n in range ( 10 ): if n == 6 : break # stop at 6 if n % 2 == 0 : continue # skip even numbers print (n) def todo (): pass # write this later A loop's else: block runs only if the loop didn't break : handy for \"not found\"."},{"id":"py.nested-break","s":"py-basics","q":"How do I break out of two nested loops at once?","qh":"How do I break out of two nested loops at once?","a":"Put the loops in a function and <code>return</code>, or use a flag variable.","aka":"break nested loop, exit both loops, break outer loop","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>grid = [[<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>], [<i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">99</i>, <i class=\"n\">6</i>], [<i class=\"n\">7</i>, <i class=\"n\">8</i>, <i class=\"n\">9</i>]]\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">find</i>(target):\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> r, row <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">enumerate</i>(grid):\n        <i class=\"k\">for</i> c, value <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">enumerate</i>(row):\n            <i class=\"k\">if</i> value == target:\n                <i class=\"k\">return</i> r, c\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"k\">None</i>\n\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(find(<i class=\"n\">99</i>))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"grid = [[ 1 , 2 , 3 ], [ 4 , 99 , 6 ], [ 7 , 8 , 9 ]] def find (target): for r, row in enumerate (grid): for c, value in enumerate (row): if value == target: return r, c return None print (find( 99 ))"},{"id":"py.function","s":"py-basics","q":"How do I write and call a function?","qh":"How do I write and call a function?","a":"<code>def name(parameters):</code> then the indented body. Call it with <code>name(arguments)</code>. Use <code>return</code> to send a value back.","aka":"def, function, define function, call function, parameters, arguments, how functions work","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">area</i>(width, height):\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> width * height\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">greet</i>(name):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Hello, </i>{name}<i class=\"s\">!</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\na = area(<i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(a)\ngreet(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Sam&quot;</i>)</pre></figure><p>A function must be defined (its <code>def</code> must have run) before the line that calls it runs.</p>","see":["py.return-vs-print","py.default-args"],"t":"def area (width, height): return width * height def greet (name): print ( f\" Hello, {name} ! \" ) a = area( 3 , 4 ) print (a) greet( \"Sam\" ) A function must be defined (its def must have run) before the line that calls it runs."},{"id":"py.return-vs-print","s":"py-basics","q":"What's the difference between return and print? Why does my function give None?","qh":"What's the difference between return and print? Why does my function give None?","a":"<code>print</code> shows something on the screen; <code>return</code> hands a value back to the code that called the function. A function without <code>return</code> gives back <code>None</code>.","aka":"return vs print, function returns none, none printed, function output none, return value","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">add_print</i>(a, b):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(a + b)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">add_return</i>(a, b):\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> a + b\n\nx = add_print(<i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>)     <i class=\"c\"># shows 5, but x is None</i>\ny = add_return(<i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>)    <i class=\"c\"># shows nothing, y is 5</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(x, y)</pre></figure>","see":["py.err.nonetype"],"t":"def add_print (a, b): print (a + b) def add_return (a, b): return a + b x = add_print( 2 , 3 ) # shows 5, but x is None y = add_return( 2 , 3 ) # shows nothing, y is 5 print (x, y)"},{"id":"py.default-args","s":"py-basics","q":"How do I give a function default arguments or keyword arguments?","qh":"How do I give a function default arguments or keyword arguments?","a":"<code>def f(name, greeting=&quot;Hello&quot;):</code> makes <code>greeting</code> optional. Call with names in any order: <code>f(greeting=&quot;Hi&quot;, name=&quot;Ada&quot;)</code>. Never use a list or dict as a default; use <code>None</code>.","aka":"default parameter, optional argument, keyword arguments, named arguments, mutable default argument","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">greet</i>(name, greeting=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hello&quot;</i>, punct=<i class=\"s\">&quot;!&quot;</i>):\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{greeting}<i class=\"s\">, </i>{name}{punct}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>\n\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(greet(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(greet(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Hi&quot;</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(greet(punct=<i class=\"s\">&quot;?&quot;</i>, name=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Sam&quot;</i>))\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">add_item</i>(item, items=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):     <i class=\"c\"># not items=[] (that list is shared between calls)</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> items <i class=\"k\">is</i> <i class=\"k\">None</i>:\n        items = []\n    items.append(item)\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> items\n\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(add_item(<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>), add_item(<i class=\"s\">&quot;b&quot;</i>))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"def greet (name, greeting= \"Hello\" , punct= \"!\" ): return f\" {greeting} , {name}{punct} \" print (greet( \"Ada\" )) print (greet( \"Ada\" , \"Hi\" )) print (greet(punct= \"?\" , name= \"Sam\" )) def add_item (item, items= None ): # not items=[] (that list is shared between calls) if items is None : items = [] items.append(item) return items print (add_item( \"a\" ), add_item( \"b\" ))"},{"id":"py.args-kwargs","s":"py-basics","q":"What are *args and **kwargs?","qh":"What are *args and **kwargs?","a":"<code>*args</code> collects extra positional arguments into a tuple; <code>**kwargs</code> collects extra keyword arguments into a dict. At a call, <code>*list</code> and <code>**dict</code> unpack.","aka":"args kwargs, variable number of arguments, star args, unpack arguments, splat","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">total</i>(*numbers):\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"b\">sum</i>(numbers)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">describe</i>(**info):\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> k, v <i class=\"k\">in</i> info.items():\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{k}<i class=\"s\">: </i>{v}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(total(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>))\ndescribe(name=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>, job=<i class=\"s\">&quot;programmer&quot;</i>)\nnums = [<i class=\"n\">5</i>, <i class=\"n\">6</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(total(*nums))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"def total (*numbers): return sum (numbers) def describe (**info): for k, v in info.items(): print ( f\" {k} : {v} \" ) print (total( 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 )) describe(name= \"Ada\" , job= \"programmer\" ) nums = [ 5 , 6 ] print (total(*nums))"},{"id":"py.return-multiple","s":"py-basics","q":"How do I return several values from a function?","qh":"How do I return several values from a function?","a":"Return them separated by commas (a tuple) and unpack them when calling.","aka":"return two values, return multiple, tuple return, unpack return","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">min_max</i>(numbers):\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"b\">min</i>(numbers), <i class=\"b\">max</i>(numbers)\n\nlow, high = min_max([<i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">9</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">7</i>])\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(low, high)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"def min_max (numbers): return min (numbers), max (numbers) low, high = min_max([ 4 , 9 , 1 , 7 ]) print (low, high)"},{"id":"py.global","s":"py-basics","q":"How do I change a global variable inside a function?","qh":"How do I change a global variable inside a function?","a":"Reading a global inside a function works. <b>Assigning</b> to it needs <code>global name</code> at the top of the function, otherwise Python makes a new local variable. Often better: pass values in and <code>return</code> them.","aka":"global keyword, global variable, change variable in function, UnboundLocalError, scope, local variable, nonlocal","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>counter = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">bump</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> counter\n    counter += <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n\nbump(); bump()\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(counter)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">bump_pure</i>(n):           <i class=\"c\"># usually cleaner</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> n + <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n\ncounter = bump_pure(counter)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(counter)</pre></figure><p><code>nonlocal</code> does the same for a variable of an enclosing function.</p>","see":["py.err.unboundlocal"],"t":"counter = 0 def bump (): global counter counter += 1 bump(); bump() print (counter) def bump_pure (n): # usually cleaner return n + 1 counter = bump_pure(counter) print (counter) nonlocal does the same for a variable of an enclosing function."},{"id":"py.is-vs-eq","s":"py-basics","q":"What's the difference between == and is?","qh":"What's the difference between == and is?","a":"<code>==</code> asks \"same value?\"; <code>is</code> asks \"the very same object?\". Use <code>is</code> only for <code>None</code> (<code>if x is None:</code>), and <code>==</code> for everything else.","aka":"is vs ==, is none, identity, equality, is operator","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>a = [<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>]\nb = [<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(a == b, a <i class=\"k\">is</i> b)\nc = a\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(c <i class=\"k\">is</i> a)\nx = <i class=\"k\">None</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(x <i class=\"k\">is</i> <i class=\"k\">None</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"a = [ 1 , 2 ] b = [ 1 , 2 ] print (a == b, a is b) c = a print (c is a) x = None print (x is None )"},{"id":"py.math-ops","s":"py-basics","q":"How do maths operators work (division, power, remainder)?","qh":"How do maths operators work (division, power, remainder)?","a":"<code>/</code> always gives a float, <code>//</code> divides and rounds down, <code>%</code> is the remainder, <code>**</code> is power. Square root: <code>x ** 0.5</code> or <code>math.sqrt(x)</code>.","aka":"division, integer division, floor division, modulo, remainder, power, exponent, square root, //, %","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> math\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"n\">7</i> / <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">7</i> // <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">7</i> % <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i> ** <i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(math.sqrt(<i class=\"n\">16</i>), <i class=\"n\">27</i> ** (<i class=\"n\">1</i> / <i class=\"n\">3</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(-<i class=\"n\">7</i> // <i class=\"n\">2</i>)        <i class=\"c\"># -4: rounds down, not toward zero</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"n\">10</i> % <i class=\"n\">3</i> == <i class=\"n\">1</i>)    <i class=\"c\"># check remainders: even/odd, every nth item</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">abs</i>(-<i class=\"n\">5</i>), <i class=\"b\">max</i>(<i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">9</i>), <i class=\"b\">min</i>(<i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">9</i>))</pre></figure>","see":["py.float-precision","py.round"],"t":"import math print ( 7 / 2 , 7 // 2 , 7 % 2 , 2 ** 10 ) print (math.sqrt( 16 ), 27 ** ( 1 / 3 )) print (- 7 // 2 ) # -4: rounds down, not toward zero print ( 10 % 3 == 1 ) # check remainders: even/odd, every nth item print ( abs (- 5 ), max ( 3 , 9 ), min ( 3 , 9 ))"},{"id":"py.round","s":"py-basics","q":"How do I round numbers (and why does round(2.5) give 2)?","qh":"How do I round numbers (and why does round(2.5) give 2)?","a":"<code>round(x, 2)</code> rounds to 2 decimals. Python rounds exact halves to the <b>even</b> number (<code>round(2.5)</code> is 2, <code>round(3.5)</code> is 4). Use <code>math.ceil</code>/<code>math.floor</code> to always go up/down, or <code>Decimal</code> for school-style rounding.","aka":"round, round up, round down, ceil, floor, banker's rounding, round half up","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> math\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> decimal <i class=\"k\">import</i> Decimal, ROUND_HALF_UP\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">round</i>(<i class=\"n\">3.14159</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>), <i class=\"b\">round</i>(<i class=\"n\">2.5</i>), <i class=\"b\">round</i>(<i class=\"n\">3.5</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(math.ceil(<i class=\"n\">2.1</i>), math.floor(<i class=\"n\">2.9</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(Decimal(<i class=\"s\">&quot;2.5&quot;</i>).quantize(Decimal(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1&quot;</i>), rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP))</pre></figure>","see":["py.float-precision"],"t":"import math from decimal import Decimal, ROUND_HALF_UP print ( round ( 3.14159 , 2 ), round ( 2.5 ), round ( 3.5 )) print (math.ceil( 2.1 ), math.floor( 2.9 )) print (Decimal( \"2.5\" ).quantize(Decimal( \"1\" ), rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP))"},{"id":"py.float-precision","s":"py-basics","q":"Why does 0.1 + 0.2 give 0.30000000000000004?","qh":"Why does 0.1 + 0.2 give 0.30000000000000004?","a":"Computers store decimals in binary, which can't hold 0.1 exactly. Round when <b>displaying</b> (<code>f&quot;{x:.2f}&quot;</code>), compare with <code>math.isclose</code>, and use <code>Decimal</code> (or whole pennies as ints) for money.","aka":"float precision, floating point error, 0.1+0.2, decimal inaccurate, compare floats","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> math\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> decimal <i class=\"k\">import</i> Decimal\nx = <i class=\"n\">0.1</i> + <i class=\"n\">0.2</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(x, <i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{x:<i class=\"s\">.2f</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(x == <i class=\"n\">0.3</i>, math.isclose(x, <i class=\"n\">0.3</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(Decimal(<i class=\"s\">&quot;0.1&quot;</i>) + Decimal(<i class=\"s\">&quot;0.2&quot;</i>))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import math from decimal import Decimal x = 0.1 + 0.2 print (x, f\" {x: .2f } \" ) print (x == 0.3 , math.isclose(x, 0.3 )) print (Decimal( \"0.1\" ) + Decimal( \"0.2\" ))"},{"id":"py.comments","s":"py-basics","q":"How do I write comments (and multi-line comments)?","qh":"How do I write comments (and multi-line comments)?","a":"<code>#</code> comments out the rest of the line. Python has no block comment; put <code>#</code> on each line (most editors do it with Ctrl+/ or Alt+3 in IDLE). Triple-quoted strings under a <code>def</code> are docstrings.","aka":"comment, comments, multiline comment, docstring, hash","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"c\"># this whole line is a comment</i>\nx = <i class=\"n\">5</i>  <i class=\"c\"># a comment after code</i>\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">area</i>(r):\n    <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;&quot;Return the area of a circle with radius r.&quot;&quot;&quot;</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"n\">3.14159</i> * r * r\n\n<i class=\"b\">help</i>(area)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"# this whole line is a comment x = 5 # a comment after code def area (r): \"\"\"Return the area of a circle with radius r.\"\"\" return 3.14159 * r * r help (area)"},{"id":"py.match","s":"py-basics","q":"Is there a switch/case statement in Python?","qh":"Is there a switch/case statement in Python?","a":"Python 3.10+ has <code>match</code>/<code>case</code>. Before that, use <code>if</code>/<code>elif</code> or a dict of functions.","aka":"switch case, match case, switch statement, pattern matching","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>command = <i class=\"s\">&quot;go north&quot;</i>\nmatch command.split():\n    case [<i class=\"s\">&quot;go&quot;</i>, direction]:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Going&quot;</i>, direction)\n    case [<i class=\"s\">&quot;quit&quot;</i>] | [<i class=\"s\">&quot;exit&quot;</i>]:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Bye&quot;</i>)\n    case _:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Unknown command&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"command = \"go north\" match command.split(): case [ \"go\" , direction]: print ( \"Going\" , direction) case [ \"quit\" ] | [ \"exit\" ]: print ( \"Bye\" ) case _: print ( \"Unknown command\" )"},{"id":"py.indentation","s":"py-basics","q":"How does indentation work in Python?","qh":"How does indentation work in Python?","a":"The lines inside an <code>if</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>def</code>, <code>class</code>... are indented by the same amount (4 spaces is standard). The block ends where the indentation goes back. Don't mix tabs and spaces.","aka":"indentation, tabs vs spaces, indent block, colon, code block, spaces","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">2</i>):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;inside the loop&quot;</i>, i)\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> i == <i class=\"n\">1</i>:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;inside the if&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;after the loop&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":["py.err.indentation"],"t":"for i in range ( 2 ): print ( \"inside the loop\" , i) if i == 1 : print ( \"inside the if\" ) print ( \"after the loop\" )"},{"id":"py.none","s":"py-basics","q":"What is None?","qh":"What is None?","a":"<code>None</code> means \"no value\". Functions return it by default; use it as a placeholder (\"not set yet\"), and test it with <code>is None</code>.","aka":"None, null, nil, nothing value, NoneType","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>best = <i class=\"k\">None</i>\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> score <i class=\"k\">in</i> [<i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">9</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>]:\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> best <i class=\"k\">is</i> <i class=\"k\">None</i> <i class=\"k\">or</i> score &gt; best:\n        best = score\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(best)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"best = None for score in [ 3 , 9 , 4 ]: if best is None or score > best: best = score print (best)"},{"id":"py.walrus","s":"py-basics","q":"What does := (the walrus operator) do?","qh":"What does := (the walrus operator) do?","a":"It assigns <b>and</b> gives the value in one expression (Python 3.8+). Handy in <code>while</code> loops and comprehensions.","aka":"walrus operator, assignment expression, colon equals","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>data = [<i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">8</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">9</i>]\n<i class=\"k\">if</i> (n := <i class=\"b\">len</i>(data)) &gt; <i class=\"n\">3</i>:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">long list (</i>{n}<i class=\"s\"> items)</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>([y <i class=\"k\">for</i> x <i class=\"k\">in</i> data <i class=\"k\">if</i> (y := x * <i class=\"n\">10</i>) &gt; <i class=\"n\">30</i>])</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"data = [ 3 , 8 , 1 , 9 ] if (n := len (data)) > 3 : print ( f\" long list ( {n} items) \" ) print ([y for x in data if (y := x * 10 ) > 30 ])"},{"id":"py.str-basics","s":"py-strings","q":"How do I get one character or part of a string (indexing and slicing)?","qh":"How do I get one character or part of a string (indexing and slicing)?","a":"<code>s[0]</code> is the first character, <code>s[-1]</code> the last, <code>s[2:5]</code> characters 2 to 4, <code>s[:3]</code> the first three, <code>s[3:]</code> from 3 to the end.","aka":"string index, slice string, substring, first character, last character, string slicing, characters of string","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>s = <i class=\"s\">&quot;Python&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(s[<i class=\"n\">0</i>], s[-<i class=\"n\">1</i>])        <i class=\"c\"># P n</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(s[<i class=\"n\">1</i>:<i class=\"n\">4</i>])             <i class=\"c\"># yth</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(s[:<i class=\"n\">2</i>], s[<i class=\"n\">2</i>:])       <i class=\"c\"># Py thon</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(s[::<i class=\"n\">2</i>])             <i class=\"c\"># Pto (every 2nd)</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">len</i>(s))</pre></figure><p>Strings can't be changed in place (<code>s[0] = &quot;J&quot;</code> fails). Build a new one: <code>&quot;J&quot; + s[1:]</code>.</p>","see":["py.reverse-str"],"t":"s = \"Python\" print (s[ 0 ], s[- 1 ]) # P n print (s[ 1 : 4 ]) # yth print (s[: 2 ], s[ 2 :]) # Py thon print (s[:: 2 ]) # Pto (every 2nd) print ( len (s)) Strings can't be changed in place ( s[0] = \"J\" fails). Build a new one: \"J\" + s[1:] ."},{"id":"py.reverse-str","s":"py-strings","q":"How do I reverse a string (or check for a palindrome)?","qh":"How do I reverse a string (or check for a palindrome)?","a":"<code>s[::-1]</code> reverses it.","aka":"reverse string, palindrome, backwards string, string backwards","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>word = <i class=\"s\">&quot;racecar&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(word[::-<i class=\"n\">1</i>])\nclean = <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>.join(ch.lower() <i class=\"k\">for</i> ch <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;Never odd or even&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> ch.isalnum())\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(clean == clean[::-<i class=\"n\">1</i>])      <i class=\"c\"># palindrome check ignoring spaces/case</i></pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"word = \"racecar\" print (word[::- 1 ]) clean = \"\" .join(ch.lower() for ch in \"Never odd or even\" if ch.isalnum()) print (clean == clean[::- 1 ]) # palindrome check ignoring spaces/case"},{"id":"py.split","s":"py-strings","q":"How do I split a string into words or parts?","qh":"How do I split a string into words or parts?","a":"<code>s.split()</code> splits on any whitespace; <code>s.split(&quot;,&quot;)</code> on commas; <code>s.splitlines()</code> into lines. <code>maxsplit</code> limits how many splits.","aka":"split string, string to list, split by comma, split lines, split words, separate string, csv line split","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;the cat sat&quot;</i>.split())\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;a,b,,c&quot;</i>.split(<i class=\"s\">&quot;,&quot;</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;key=value=x&quot;</i>.split(<i class=\"s\">&quot;=&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;line1\\nline2&quot;</i>.splitlines())\nname, age = <i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada,36&quot;</i>.split(<i class=\"s\">&quot;,&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(name, <i class=\"b\">int</i>(age))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">list</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;abc&quot;</i>))                  <i class=\"c\"># into characters</i></pre></figure>","see":["py.join"],"t":"print ( \"the cat sat\" .split()) print ( \"a,b,,c\" .split( \",\" )) print ( \"key=value=x\" .split( \"=\" , 1 )) print ( \"line1\\nline2\" .splitlines()) name, age = \"Ada,36\" .split( \",\" ) print (name, int (age)) print ( list ( \"abc\" )) # into characters"},{"id":"py.join","s":"py-strings","q":"How do I join a list of strings into one string?","qh":"How do I join a list of strings into one string?","a":"<code>&quot;, &quot;.join(list_of_strings)</code>. The items must be strings; convert numbers first.","aka":"join, list to string, combine strings, concatenate list, join with comma","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>words = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;green&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;blue&quot;</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;, &quot;</i>.join(words))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>.join([<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;b&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;c&quot;</i>]))\nnums = [<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot; + &quot;</i>.join(<i class=\"b\">str</i>(n) <i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> nums), <i class=\"s\">&quot;=&quot;</i>, <i class=\"b\">sum</i>(nums))</pre></figure>","see":["py.split"],"t":"words = [ \"red\" , \"green\" , \"blue\" ] print ( \", \" .join(words)) print ( \"\" .join([ \"a\" , \"b\" , \"c\" ])) nums = [ 1 , 2 , 3 ] print ( \" + \" .join( str (n) for n in nums), \"=\" , sum (nums))"},{"id":"py.strip","s":"py-strings","q":"How do I remove spaces or newlines from the start/end of a string?","qh":"How do I remove spaces or newlines from the start/end of a string?","a":"<code>s.strip()</code> removes whitespace from both ends; <code>lstrip()</code>/<code>rstrip()</code> one end. Give characters to remove others: <code>s.strip(&quot;.!&quot;)</code>.","aka":"strip, trim, remove whitespace, remove newline, rstrip, lstrip, remove \\n","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>line = <i class=\"s\">&quot;   hello world \\n&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">repr</i>(line.strip()))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">repr</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;...wow!!!&quot;</i>.strip(<i class=\"s\">&quot;.!&quot;</i>)))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">repr</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;file.txt&quot;</i>.removesuffix(<i class=\"s\">&quot;.txt&quot;</i>)))      <i class=\"c\"># Python 3.9+</i></pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"line = \" hello world \\n\" print ( repr (line.strip())) print ( repr ( \"...wow!!!\" .strip( \".!\" ))) print ( repr ( \"file.txt\" .removesuffix( \".txt\" ))) # Python 3.9+"},{"id":"py.replace","s":"py-strings","q":"How do I replace text in a string?","qh":"How do I replace text in a string?","a":"<code>s.replace(&quot;old&quot;, &quot;new&quot;)</code> returns a new string (the original doesn't change). Remove characters by replacing with <code>&quot;&quot;</code>.","aka":"replace, string replace, remove character from string, substitute, change word in string","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>s = <i class=\"s\">&quot;I like cats. cats are great.&quot;</i>\ns2 = s.replace(<i class=\"s\">&quot;cats&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;dogs&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(s2)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(s.replace(<i class=\"s\">&quot;cats&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;dogs&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>))      <i class=\"c\"># only the first</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1,234,567&quot;</i>.replace(<i class=\"s\">&quot;,&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>))</pre></figure><p><code>s.replace(...)</code> alone does nothing: you must store the result, <code>s = s.replace(...)</code>.</p>","see":["py.regex"],"t":"s = \"I like cats. cats are great.\" s2 = s.replace( \"cats\" , \"dogs\" ) print (s2) print (s.replace( \"cats\" , \"dogs\" , 1 )) # only the first print ( \"1,234,567\" .replace( \",\" , \"\" )) s.replace(...) alone does nothing: you must store the result, s = s.replace(...) ."},{"id":"py.case","s":"py-strings","q":"How do I change upper/lower case, or compare ignoring case?","qh":"How do I change upper/lower case, or compare ignoring case?","a":"<code>upper()</code>, <code>lower()</code>, <code>title()</code>, <code>capitalize()</code>, <code>swapcase()</code>. Compare with both sides lowered (or <code>casefold()</code>).","aka":"uppercase, lowercase, capitalize, title case, case insensitive compare, upper lower","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>s = <i class=\"s\">&quot;hello World&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(s.upper(), s.lower(), s.title(), s.capitalize())\nanswer = <i class=\"s\">&quot;YES&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"k\">if</i> answer.lower() == <i class=\"s\">&quot;yes&quot;</i>:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;matched&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"s = \"hello World\" print (s.upper(), s.lower(), s.title(), s.capitalize()) answer = \"YES\" if answer.lower() == \"yes\" : print ( \"matched\" )"},{"id":"py.find","s":"py-strings","q":"How do I check if a string contains something, or find where it is?","qh":"How do I check if a string contains something, or find where it is?","a":"<code>&quot;cat&quot; in s</code> (True/False), <code>s.find(&quot;cat&quot;)</code> (position or -1), <code>s.count(&quot;a&quot;)</code>, <code>s.startswith(&quot;http&quot;)</code>, <code>s.endswith(&quot;.py&quot;)</code>.","aka":"string contains, substring in string, find, index of substring, startswith, endswith, count occurrences","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>s = <i class=\"s\">&quot;the cat sat on the mat&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;cat&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">in</i> s, <i class=\"s\">&quot;dog&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">in</i> s)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(s.find(<i class=\"s\">&quot;sat&quot;</i>), s.find(<i class=\"s\">&quot;dog&quot;</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(s.count(<i class=\"s\">&quot;at&quot;</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(s.startswith(<i class=\"s\">&quot;the&quot;</i>), s.endswith((<i class=\"s\">&quot;.py&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;.txt&quot;</i>)))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"s = \"the cat sat on the mat\" print ( \"cat\" in s, \"dog\" in s) print (s.find( \"sat\" ), s.find( \"dog\" )) print (s.count( \"at\" )) print (s.startswith( \"the\" ), s.endswith(( \".py\" , \".txt\" )))"},{"id":"py.str-checks","s":"py-strings","q":"How do I check if a string is a number, letters, uppercase...?","qh":"How do I check if a string is a number, letters, uppercase...?","a":"<code>isdigit()</code> (only 0-9), <code>isalpha()</code>, <code>isalnum()</code>, <code>isupper()</code>, <code>islower()</code>, <code>isspace()</code>. For negatives/decimals, try <code>float()</code> in <code>try</code>.","aka":"isdigit, isnumeric, isalpha, isalnum, isupper, check if string is number, is integer string","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">for</i> s <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;123&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;-5&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;3.5&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;abc&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;a1&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">repr</i>(s), s.isdigit(), s.isalpha(), s.isalnum())\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">is_number</i>(s):\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        <i class=\"b\">float</i>(s)\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"k\">True</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> ValueError:\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"k\">False</i>\n\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(is_number(<i class=\"s\">&quot;-3.5&quot;</i>), is_number(<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"for s in ( \"123\" , \"-5\" , \"3.5\" , \"abc\" , \"a1\" , \"\" ): print ( repr (s), s.isdigit(), s.isalpha(), s.isalnum()) def is_number (s): try : float (s) return True except ValueError: return False print (is_number( \"-3.5\" ), is_number( \"x\" ))"},{"id":"py.multiline-str","s":"py-strings","q":"How do I write a string over several lines, or include quotes and special characters?","qh":"How do I write a string over several lines, or include quotes and special characters?","a":"Triple quotes <code>&quot;&quot;&quot;...&quot;&quot;&quot;</code> for multi-line text. <code>\\n</code> newline, <code>\\t</code> tab, <code>\\\\</code> backslash, <code>\\&quot;</code> quote. Prefix <code>r</code> for raw strings (Windows paths, regex).","aka":"multiline string, triple quotes, escape characters, newline character, quotes in string, backslash, raw string, tab character","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>poem = <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;&quot;Roses are red,\nviolets are blue.&quot;&quot;&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(poem)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;She said \\&quot;hi\\&quot;\\tand left.\\n(new line)&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&#x27;It\\&#x27;s fine&#x27;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;It&#x27;s also fine&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">r&quot;C:\\new\\folder&quot;</i>)</pre></figure><p><code>&quot;C:\\new&quot;</code> without <code>r</code> contains a newline character (<code>\\n</code>). Use <code>r&quot;...&quot;</code> or forward slashes for Windows paths.</p>","see":[],"t":"poem = \"\"\"Roses are red, violets are blue.\"\"\" print (poem) print ( \"She said \\\"hi\\\"\\tand left.\\n(new line)\" ) print ( 'It\\'s fine' , \"It's also fine\" ) print ( r\"C:\\new\\folder\" ) \"C:\\new\" without r contains a newline character ( \\n ). Use r\"...\" or forward slashes for Windows paths."},{"id":"py.str-repeat","s":"py-strings","q":"How do I repeat a string or make a line of characters?","qh":"How do I repeat a string or make a line of characters?","a":"Multiply it: <code>&quot;-&quot; * 20</code>.","aka":"repeat string, multiply string, line of dashes, string times number","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;-&quot;</i> * <i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;ha&quot;</i> * <i class=\"n\">3</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">5</i>):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;*&quot;</i> * i)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"print ( \"-\" * 20 ) print ( \"ha\" * 3 ) for i in range ( 1 , 5 ): print ( \"*\" * i)"},{"id":"py.count-words","s":"py-strings","q":"How do I count words or letters in text?","qh":"How do I count words or letters in text?","a":"<code>len(text.split())</code> counts words. <code>collections.Counter</code> counts how often each one appears.","aka":"word count, count letters, character frequency, most common word, counter","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">from</i> collections <i class=\"k\">import</i> Counter\ntext = <i class=\"s\">&quot;the cat and the hat and the bat&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">len</i>(text.split()), <i class=\"s\">&quot;words&quot;</i>)\nc = Counter(text.split())\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(c.most_common(<i class=\"n\">2</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(Counter(<i class=\"s\">&quot;mississippi&quot;</i>))</pre></figure>","see":["py.counter"],"t":"from collections import Counter text = \"the cat and the hat and the bat\" print ( len (text.split()), \"words\" ) c = Counter(text.split()) print (c.most_common( 2 )) print (Counter( \"mississippi\" ))"},{"id":"py.str-chars","s":"py-strings","q":"How do I get the ASCII/Unicode code of a character (and back)?","qh":"How do I get the ASCII/Unicode code of a character (and back)?","a":"<code>ord(&quot;A&quot;)</code> gives 65, <code>chr(65)</code> gives <code>&quot;A&quot;</code>.","aka":"ord, chr, ascii code, unicode, character code, caesar cipher","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">ord</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;A&quot;</i>), <i class=\"b\">chr</i>(<i class=\"n\">97</i>))\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">caesar</i>(text, shift):\n    out = <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> ch <i class=\"k\">in</i> text:\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> ch.isalpha():\n            base = <i class=\"b\">ord</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;A&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">if</i> ch.isupper() <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"b\">ord</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>)\n            out += <i class=\"b\">chr</i>((<i class=\"b\">ord</i>(ch) - base + shift) % <i class=\"n\">26</i> + base)\n        <i class=\"k\">else</i>:\n            out += ch\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> out\n\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(caesar(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hello, World&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"print ( ord ( \"A\" ), chr ( 97 )) def caesar (text, shift): out = \"\" for ch in text: if ch.isalpha(): base = ord ( \"A\" ) if ch.isupper() else ord ( \"a\" ) out += chr (( ord (ch) - base + shift) % 26 + base) else : out += ch return out print (caesar( \"Hello, World\" , 3 ))"},{"id":"py.regex","s":"py-strings","q":"How do I use regular expressions (find patterns, emails, numbers in text)?","qh":"How do I use regular expressions (find patterns, emails, numbers in text)?","a":"<code>import re</code>. <code>re.findall(pattern, text)</code> returns all matches; <code>re.search</code> the first; <code>re.sub</code> replaces; <code>re.fullmatch</code> checks the whole string. Write patterns as raw strings <code>r&quot;...&quot;</code>.","aka":"regex, re module, regular expression, find all numbers, pattern matching, re.findall, re.sub, validate email","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> re\ntext = <i class=\"s\">&quot;Call 555-1234 or 555-9876 before 5pm. Email: ada@example.com&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(re.findall(<i class=\"s\">r&quot;\\d{3}-\\d{4}&quot;</i>, text))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(re.findall(<i class=\"s\">r&quot;\\d+&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;a1b22c333&quot;</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(re.sub(<i class=\"s\">r&quot;\\s+&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot; &quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;too    many   spaces&quot;</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">bool</i>(re.fullmatch(<i class=\"s\">r&quot;[\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[\\w.]+&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;ada@example.com&quot;</i>)))\nm = re.search(<i class=\"s\">r&quot;(\\w+)@(\\w+)&quot;</i>, text)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(m.group(<i class=\"n\">1</i>), m.group(<i class=\"n\">2</i>))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import re text = \"Call 555-1234 or 555-9876 before 5pm. Email: ada@example.com\" print (re.findall( r\"\\d{3}-\\d{4}\" , text)) print (re.findall( r\"\\d+\" , \"a1b22c333\" )) print (re.sub( r\"\\s+\" , \" \" , \"too many spaces\" )) print ( bool (re.fullmatch( r\"[\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[\\w.]+\" , \"ada@example.com\" ))) m = re.search( r\"(\\w+)@(\\w+)\" , text) print (m.group( 1 ), m.group( 2 ))"},{"id":"py.str-pad","s":"py-strings","q":"How do I line up text in columns (padding)?","qh":"How do I line up text in columns (padding)?","a":"f-string alignment <code>{s:&lt;10}</code> / <code>{s:&gt;10}</code> / <code>{s:^10}</code>, or <code>ljust</code>, <code>rjust</code>, <code>center</code>, <code>zfill</code>.","aka":"pad string, align columns, ljust, rjust, center text, table output, zfill","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>rows = [(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Apple&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">1.5</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Watermelon&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">3.25</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Fig&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">0.4</i>)]\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> name, price <i class=\"k\">in</i> rows:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{name:<i class=\"s\">&lt;12</i>}{price:<i class=\"s\">&gt;6.2f</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;7&quot;</i>.zfill(<i class=\"n\">3</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;hi&quot;</i>.center(<i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;*&quot;</i>))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"rows = [( \"Apple\" , 1.5 ), ( \"Watermelon\" , 3.25 ), ( \"Fig\" , 0.4 )] for name, price in rows: print ( f\" {name: <12 }{price: >6.2f } \" ) print ( \"7\" .zfill( 3 ), \"hi\" .center( 10 , \"*\" ))"},{"id":"py.str-empty","s":"py-strings","q":"How do I check if a string is empty?","qh":"How do I check if a string is empty?","a":"<code>if not s:</code> is True for <code>&quot;&quot;</code>. To also treat spaces as empty: <code>if not s.strip():</code>.","aka":"empty string, blank string, check empty input, string is empty","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">for</i> s <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;  &quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;hi&quot;</i>):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">repr</i>(s), <i class=\"s\">&quot;empty&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> s <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;not empty&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;| blank&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> s.strip() <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"for s in ( \"\" , \" \" , \"hi\" ): print ( repr (s), \"empty\" if not s else \"not empty\" , \"| blank\" if not s.strip() else \"\" )"},{"id":"py.list-basics","s":"py-lists","q":"How do I make a list and get items from it?","qh":"How do I make a list and get items from it?","a":"<code>items = [1, 2, 3]</code>. <code>items[0]</code> is the first, <code>items[-1]</code> the last, <code>len(items)</code> the count, <code>items[1:3]</code> a slice.","aka":"list, array, create list, list index, access list item, first item, last item, list length","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>fruits = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;apple&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;banana&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;cherry&quot;</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(fruits[<i class=\"n\">0</i>], fruits[-<i class=\"n\">1</i>], <i class=\"b\">len</i>(fruits))\nfruits[<i class=\"n\">1</i>] = <i class=\"s\">&quot;blueberry&quot;</i>          <i class=\"c\"># lists can be changed</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(fruits)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(fruits[:<i class=\"n\">2</i>])</pre></figure>","see":["py.list-add","py.list-remove"],"t":"fruits = [ \"apple\" , \"banana\" , \"cherry\" ] print (fruits[ 0 ], fruits[- 1 ], len (fruits)) fruits[ 1 ] = \"blueberry\" # lists can be changed print (fruits) print (fruits[: 2 ])"},{"id":"py.list-add","s":"py-lists","q":"How do I add items to a list (append, insert, extend)?","qh":"How do I add items to a list (append, insert, extend)?","a":"<code>append(x)</code> adds one item at the end; <code>insert(i, x)</code> at a position; <code>extend(other)</code> (or <code>+=</code>) adds every item from another list.","aka":"append, add to list, insert, extend, add item, push, join two lists, concatenate lists","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>nums = [<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>]\nnums.append(<i class=\"n\">3</i>)\nnums.insert(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>)\nnums.extend([<i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">5</i>])\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(nums)\nboth = [<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>] + [<i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(both)</pre></figure><p><code>append([4, 5])</code> adds <b>one</b> item that is itself a list: <code>[..., [4, 5]]</code>.</p>","see":[],"t":"nums = [ 1 , 2 ] nums.append( 3 ) nums.insert( 0 , 0 ) nums.extend([ 4 , 5 ]) print (nums) both = [ 1 , 2 ] + [ 3 , 4 ] print (both) append([4, 5]) adds one item that is itself a list: [..., [4, 5]] ."},{"id":"py.list-remove","s":"py-lists","q":"How do I remove items from a list (remove, pop, del, clear)?","qh":"How do I remove items from a list (remove, pop, del, clear)?","a":"<code>remove(x)</code> removes the first matching value; <code>pop(i)</code> removes by position and returns it (<code>pop()</code> = last); <code>del items[i]</code> deletes; <code>clear()</code> empties it.","aka":"remove from list, delete item, pop, del, clear list, remove by index, remove by value, remove all occurrences","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>items = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;b&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;c&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;b&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;d&quot;</i>]\nitems.remove(<i class=\"s\">&quot;b&quot;</i>)          <i class=\"c\"># first &quot;b&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(items)\nlast = items.pop()\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(last, items)\n<i class=\"k\">del</i> items[<i class=\"n\">0</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(items)\nitems = [x <i class=\"k\">for</i> x <i class=\"k\">in</i> items <i class=\"k\">if</i> x != <i class=\"s\">&quot;b&quot;</i>]     <i class=\"c\"># remove all &quot;b&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(items)</pre></figure><p><code>remove</code> raises <code>ValueError</code> if the value isn't there; check with <code>if x in items:</code> first.</p>","see":["py.list-remove-loop"],"t":"items = [ \"a\" , \"b\" , \"c\" , \"b\" , \"d\" ] items.remove( \"b\" ) # first \"b\" print (items) last = items.pop() print (last, items) del items[ 0 ] print (items) items = [x for x in items if x != \"b\" ] # remove all \"b\" print (items) remove raises ValueError if the value isn't there; check with if x in items: first."},{"id":"py.list-remove-loop","s":"py-lists","q":"Why does removing items while looping over a list skip some?","qh":"Why does removing items while looping over a list skip some?","a":"Removing shifts the remaining items left, so the loop jumps over the next one. Build a new list instead, or loop over a copy.","aka":"remove while iterating, modify list during loop, list skips items, delete in for loop","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre>nums = [<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>]\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> nums:\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> n == <i class=\"n\">2</i>:\n        nums.remove(n)     <i class=\"c\"># leaves [1, 2, 3]</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre>nums = [<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>]\nnums = [n <i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> nums <i class=\"k\">if</i> n != <i class=\"n\">2</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(nums)\nnums = [<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>]\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> nums[:]:          <i class=\"c\"># loop over a copy</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> n == <i class=\"n\">2</i>:\n        nums.remove(n)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(nums)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"nums = [ 1 , 2 , 2 , 3 ] for n in nums: if n == 2 : nums.remove(n) # leaves [1, 2, 3] nums = [ 1 , 2 , 2 , 3 ] nums = [n for n in nums if n != 2 ] print (nums) nums = [ 1 , 2 , 2 , 3 ] for n in nums[:]: # loop over a copy if n == 2 : nums.remove(n) print (nums)"},{"id":"py.list-find","s":"py-lists","q":"How do I check if an item is in a list, or find its position?","qh":"How do I check if an item is in a list, or find its position?","a":"<code>x in items</code> (True/False); <code>items.index(x)</code> gives the first position (raises <code>ValueError</code> if missing); <code>items.count(x)</code> counts.","aka":"in list, list contains, index of item, find in list, list search, position in list","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>names = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;ann&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;bob&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;cat&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;bob&quot;</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;bob&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">in</i> names, names.index(<i class=\"s\">&quot;bob&quot;</i>), names.count(<i class=\"s\">&quot;bob&quot;</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>([i <i class=\"k\">for</i> i, n <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">enumerate</i>(names) <i class=\"k\">if</i> n == <i class=\"s\">&quot;bob&quot;</i>])      <i class=\"c\"># all positions</i></pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"names = [ \"ann\" , \"bob\" , \"cat\" , \"bob\" ] print ( \"bob\" in names, names.index( \"bob\" ), names.count( \"bob\" )) print ([i for i, n in enumerate (names) if n == \"bob\" ]) # all positions"},{"id":"py.sort","s":"py-lists","q":"How do I sort a list (and what's the difference between sort and sorted)?","qh":"How do I sort a list (and what's the difference between sort and sorted)?","a":"<code>items.sort()</code> sorts in place and returns <code>None</code>; <code>sorted(items)</code> returns a new list. Add <code>reverse=True</code> for descending, <code>key=</code> to sort by something else.","aka":"sort list, sorted, reverse sort, descending, sort by key, sort by second item, sort by length, sort list of dicts","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>nums = [<i class=\"n\">5</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">9</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">sorted</i>(nums), <i class=\"b\">sorted</i>(nums, reverse=<i class=\"k\">True</i>))\nnums.sort()\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(nums)\nwords = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;banana&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Fig&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;apple&quot;</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">sorted</i>(words, key=<i class=\"b\">str</i>.lower))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">sorted</i>(words, key=<i class=\"b\">len</i>))\npeople = [(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">36</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Bob&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">25</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Cy&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">41</i>)]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">sorted</i>(people, key=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i> p: p[<i class=\"n\">1</i>]))\nscores = [{<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;A&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;pts&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">3</i>}, {<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;B&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;pts&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">9</i>}]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">max</i>(scores, key=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i> d: d[<i class=\"s\">&quot;pts&quot;</i>])[<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>])</pre></figure><p><code>nums = nums.sort()</code> makes <code>nums</code> None. Use one or the other.</p>","see":["py.lambda"],"t":"nums = [ 5 , 2 , 9 , 1 ] print ( sorted (nums), sorted (nums, reverse= True )) nums.sort() print (nums) words = [ \"banana\" , \"Fig\" , \"apple\" ] print ( sorted (words, key= str .lower)) print ( sorted (words, key= len )) people = [( \"Ada\" , 36 ), ( \"Bob\" , 25 ), ( \"Cy\" , 41 )] print ( sorted (people, key= lambda p: p[ 1 ])) scores = [{ \"name\" : \"A\" , \"pts\" : 3 }, { \"name\" : \"B\" , \"pts\" : 9 }] print ( max (scores, key= lambda d: d[ \"pts\" ])[ \"name\" ]) nums = nums.sort() makes nums None. Use one or the other."},{"id":"py.list-reverse","s":"py-lists","q":"How do I reverse a list?","qh":"How do I reverse a list?","a":"<code>items.reverse()</code> (in place), <code>items[::-1]</code> or <code>list(reversed(items))</code> (new list).","aka":"reverse list, backwards list, reversed","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>a = [<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(a[::-<i class=\"n\">1</i>])\na.reverse()\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(a)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> x <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">reversed</i>([<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>]):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(x, end=<i class=\"s\">&quot; &quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"a = [ 1 , 2 , 3 ] print (a[::- 1 ]) a.reverse() print (a) for x in reversed ([ 1 , 2 , 3 ]): print (x, end= \" \" )"},{"id":"py.list-math","s":"py-lists","q":"How do I get the sum, average, max or min of a list?","qh":"How do I get the sum, average, max or min of a list?","a":"<code>sum(nums)</code>, <code>max(nums)</code>, <code>min(nums)</code>, average = <code>sum(nums) / len(nums)</code> (or <code>statistics.mean</code>).","aka":"sum of list, average, mean, max, min, largest number, smallest, total","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> statistics\nnums = [<i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">8</i>, <i class=\"n\">15</i>, <i class=\"n\">16</i>, <i class=\"n\">23</i>, <i class=\"n\">42</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">sum</i>(nums), <i class=\"b\">max</i>(nums), <i class=\"b\">min</i>(nums))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">sum</i>(nums) / <i class=\"b\">len</i>(nums), statistics.mean(nums), statistics.median(nums))</pre></figure><p>Empty lists: <code>max([])</code> raises <code>ValueError</code>; use <code>max(nums, default=0)</code>.</p>","see":[],"t":"import statistics nums = [ 4 , 8 , 15 , 16 , 23 , 42 ] print ( sum (nums), max (nums), min (nums)) print ( sum (nums) / len (nums), statistics.mean(nums), statistics.median(nums)) Empty lists: max([]) raises ValueError ; use max(nums, default=0) ."},{"id":"py.enumerate","s":"py-lists","q":"How do I loop over a list and also get the index?","qh":"How do I loop over a list and also get the index?","a":"<code>for i, item in enumerate(items):</code> gives position and item together. <code>enumerate(items, start=1)</code> counts from 1.","aka":"enumerate, index in for loop, loop with counter, for i in range(len())","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>fruits = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;apple&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;banana&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;cherry&quot;</i>]\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> i, f <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">enumerate</i>(fruits, start=<i class=\"n\">1</i>):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{i}<i class=\"s\">. </i>{f}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"fruits = [ \"apple\" , \"banana\" , \"cherry\" ] for i, f in enumerate (fruits, start= 1 ): print ( f\" {i} . {f} \" )"},{"id":"py.zip","s":"py-lists","q":"How do I loop over two lists at the same time?","qh":"How do I loop over two lists at the same time?","a":"<code>for a, b in zip(list1, list2):</code>. It stops at the shorter list. <code>dict(zip(keys, values))</code> builds a dict.","aka":"zip, loop two lists, parallel lists, combine lists into pairs, lists to dict","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>names = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Bob&quot;</i>]\nscores = [<i class=\"n\">90</i>, <i class=\"n\">72</i>]\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> n, s <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">zip</i>(names, scores):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(n, s)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">dict</i>(<i class=\"b\">zip</i>(names, scores)))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"names = [ \"Ada\" , \"Bob\" ] scores = [ 90 , 72 ] for n, s in zip (names, scores): print (n, s) print ( dict ( zip (names, scores)))"},{"id":"py.list-comp","s":"py-lists","q":"What is a list comprehension?","qh":"What is a list comprehension?","a":"<code>[expression for item in iterable if condition]</code> builds a list in one line.","aka":"list comprehension, one line for loop, create list in one line, filter list, map list, comprehension with if","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>nums = <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">11</i>)\nsquares = [n * n <i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> nums]\nevens = [n <i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> nums <i class=\"k\">if</i> n % <i class=\"n\">2</i> == <i class=\"n\">0</i>]\nlabels = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;even&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> n % <i class=\"n\">2</i> == <i class=\"n\">0</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;odd&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">4</i>)]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(squares, evens, labels)\nwords = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hi&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;there&quot;</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>([w.upper() <i class=\"k\">for</i> w <i class=\"k\">in</i> words])</pre></figure>","see":["py.map-filter"],"t":"nums = range ( 1 , 11 ) squares = [n * n for n in nums] evens = [n for n in nums if n % 2 == 0 ] labels = [ \"even\" if n % 2 == 0 else \"odd\" for n in range ( 4 )] print (squares, evens, labels) words = [ \"Hi\" , \"there\" ] print ([w.upper() for w in words])"},{"id":"py.copy-list","s":"py-lists","q":"Why did changing one list change the other? How do I copy a list?","qh":"Why did changing one list change the other? How do I copy a list?","a":"<code>b = a</code> doesn't copy; both names point to the same list. Copy with <code>a.copy()</code>, <code>a[:]</code> or <code>list(a)</code>; for lists inside lists use <code>copy.deepcopy(a)</code>.","aka":"copy list, list aliasing, list changes both, shallow copy, deep copy, b = a list","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> copy\na = [<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>]\nb = a\nb.append(<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(a)                  <i class=\"c\"># [1, 2, 3, 4]: same list</i>\nc = a.copy()\nc.append(<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(a, c)\ngrid = [[<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>], [<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>]]\ndeep = copy.deepcopy(grid)\ndeep[<i class=\"n\">0</i>][<i class=\"n\">0</i>] = <i class=\"n\">9</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(grid, deep)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import copy a = [ 1 , 2 , 3 ] b = a b.append( 4 ) print (a) # [1, 2, 3, 4]: same list c = a.copy() c.append( 5 ) print (a, c) grid = [[ 0 , 0 ], [ 0 , 0 ]] deep = copy.deepcopy(grid) deep[ 0 ][ 0 ] = 9 print (grid, deep)"},{"id":"py.2d-list","s":"py-lists","q":"How do I make a 2D list (grid), and why does changing one row change them all?","qh":"How do I make a 2D list (grid), and why does changing one row change them all?","a":"Build each row separately with a comprehension: <code>[[0] * cols for _ in range(rows)]</code>. <code>[[0] * 3] * 3</code> repeats the <b>same</b> row 3 times.","aka":"2d list, list of lists, grid, matrix, nested list, [[0]*3]*3 bug, board","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre>grid = [[<i class=\"n\">0</i>] * <i class=\"n\">3</i>] * <i class=\"n\">3</i>\ngrid[<i class=\"n\">0</i>][<i class=\"n\">0</i>] = <i class=\"n\">1</i>            <i class=\"c\"># [[1,0,0],[1,0,0],[1,0,0]]</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre>rows, cols = <i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>\ngrid = [[<i class=\"n\">0</i>] * cols <i class=\"k\">for</i> _ <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(rows)]\ngrid[<i class=\"n\">0</i>][<i class=\"n\">0</i>] = <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> row <i class=\"k\">in</i> grid:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(row)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(grid[<i class=\"n\">0</i>][<i class=\"n\">0</i>])          <i class=\"c\"># row 0, column 0</i></pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"grid = [[ 0 ] * 3 ] * 3 grid[ 0 ][ 0 ] = 1 # [[1,0,0],[1,0,0],[1,0,0]] rows, cols = 3 , 4 grid = [[ 0 ] * cols for _ in range (rows)] grid[ 0 ][ 0 ] = 1 for row in grid: print (row) print (grid[ 0 ][ 0 ]) # row 0, column 0"},{"id":"py.dedupe","s":"py-lists","q":"How do I remove duplicates from a list?","qh":"How do I remove duplicates from a list?","a":"<code>list(set(items))</code> (order is lost) or <code>list(dict.fromkeys(items))</code> (keeps the first-seen order).","aka":"remove duplicates, unique values, distinct, dedupe list, keep order unique","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>items = [<i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">list</i>(<i class=\"b\">set</i>(items)))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">list</i>(<i class=\"b\">dict</i>.fromkeys(items)))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"items = [ 3 , 1 , 3 , 2 , 1 ] print ( list ( set (items))) print ( list ( dict .fromkeys(items)))"},{"id":"py.flatten","s":"py-lists","q":"How do I flatten a list of lists?","qh":"How do I flatten a list of lists?","a":"<code>[x for row in nested for x in row]</code>, or <code>itertools.chain.from_iterable(nested)</code>.","aka":"flatten list, nested list to flat, list of lists to list","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> itertools\nnested = [[<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>], [<i class=\"n\">3</i>], [<i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">5</i>]]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>([x <i class=\"k\">for</i> row <i class=\"k\">in</i> nested <i class=\"k\">for</i> x <i class=\"k\">in</i> row])\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">list</i>(itertools.chain.from_iterable(nested)))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import itertools nested = [[ 1 , 2 ], [ 3 ], [ 4 , 5 ]] print ([x for row in nested for x in row]) print ( list (itertools.chain.from_iterable(nested)))"},{"id":"py.random-choice","s":"py-lists","q":"How do I pick a random item, shuffle a list, or pick several?","qh":"How do I pick a random item, shuffle a list, or pick several?","a":"<code>random.choice(items)</code>, <code>random.shuffle(items)</code> (in place), <code>random.sample(items, k)</code> (k different items), <code>random.choices(items, k=5)</code> (with repeats).","aka":"random choice, shuffle, random sample, random item from list, random element","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> random\ncolours = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;green&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;blue&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;yellow&quot;</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(random.choice(colours))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(random.sample(colours, <i class=\"n\">2</i>))\nrandom.shuffle(colours)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(colours)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(random.choices([<i class=\"s\">&quot;H&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;T&quot;</i>], k=<i class=\"n\">5</i>))</pre></figure>","see":["py.random"],"t":"import random colours = [ \"red\" , \"green\" , \"blue\" , \"yellow\" ] print (random.choice(colours)) print (random.sample(colours, 2 )) random.shuffle(colours) print (colours) print (random.choices([ \"H\" , \"T\" ], k= 5 ))"},{"id":"py.tuple","s":"py-lists","q":"What's a tuple and how is it different from a list?","qh":"What's a tuple and how is it different from a list?","a":"A tuple <code>(1, 2, 3)</code> is like a list that can't be changed. Use it for fixed groups (coordinates, RGB) and as dict keys. A one-item tuple needs a comma: <code>(5,)</code>.","aka":"tuple, immutable list, tuple vs list, one item tuple, unpack tuple","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>point = (<i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nx, y = point\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(x, y)\nsingle = (<i class=\"n\">5</i>,)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">type</i>(single), <i class=\"b\">type</i>((<i class=\"n\">5</i>)))\nlocations = {(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>): <i class=\"s\">&quot;home&quot;</i>}\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(locations[(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>)])</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"point = ( 3 , 4 ) x, y = point print (x, y) single = ( 5 ,) print ( type (single), type (( 5 ))) locations = {( 0 , 0 ): \"home\" } print (locations[( 0 , 0 )])"},{"id":"py.list-chunks","s":"py-lists","q":"How do I split a list into chunks (groups of n)?","qh":"How do I split a list into chunks (groups of n)?","a":"Slice in steps: <code>[items[i:i + n] for i in range(0, len(items), n)]</code>.","aka":"chunks, split list into groups, batch list, groups of 3","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>items = <i class=\"b\">list</i>(<i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nn = <i class=\"n\">3</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>([items[i:i + n] <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"b\">len</i>(items), n)])</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"items = list ( range ( 10 )) n = 3 print ([items[i:i + n] for i in range ( 0 , len (items), n)])"},{"id":"py.list-any-all","s":"py-lists","q":"How do I check if any or all items match a condition?","qh":"How do I check if any or all items match a condition?","a":"<code>any(cond for x in items)</code> / <code>all(cond for x in items)</code>.","aka":"any, all, check all items, every item, at least one","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>scores = [<i class=\"n\">55</i>, <i class=\"n\">72</i>, <i class=\"n\">91</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">any</i>(s &gt; <i class=\"n\">90</i> <i class=\"k\">for</i> s <i class=\"k\">in</i> scores), <i class=\"b\">all</i>(s &gt;= <i class=\"n\">50</i> <i class=\"k\">for</i> s <i class=\"k\">in</i> scores))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"scores = [ 55 , 72 , 91 ] print ( any (s > 90 for s in scores), all (s >= 50 for s in scores))"},{"id":"py.list-index-error","s":"py-lists","q":"How do I safely get an item when the list might be too short?","qh":"How do I safely get an item when the list might be too short?","a":"Check the length first, or use a slice (slices never raise errors).","aka":"safe index, default if index missing, list get default","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>items = [<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>]\ni = <i class=\"n\">5</i>\nvalue = items[i] <i class=\"k\">if</i> i &lt; <i class=\"b\">len</i>(items) <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"k\">None</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(value)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(items[<i class=\"n\">5</i>:<i class=\"n\">6</i>])      <i class=\"c\"># [] instead of an error</i></pre></figure>","see":["py.err.indexerror"],"t":"items = [ 1 , 2 ] i = 5 value = items[i] if i < len (items) else None print (value) print (items[ 5 : 6 ]) # [] instead of an error"},{"id":"py.dict-basics","s":"py-dicts","q":"How do I make a dictionary and get, add or change values?","qh":"How do I make a dictionary and get, add or change values?","a":"<code>d = {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;Ada&quot;, &quot;age&quot;: 36}</code>. Read <code>d[&quot;name&quot;]</code>, add or change with <code>d[&quot;city&quot;] = &quot;London&quot;</code>.","aka":"dictionary, dict, key value, create dict, add to dict, update dict, access dict, hashmap, lookup table","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>person = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;age&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">36</i>}\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(person[<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>])\nperson[<i class=\"s\">&quot;city&quot;</i>] = <i class=\"s\">&quot;London&quot;</i>        <i class=\"c\"># add</i>\nperson[<i class=\"s\">&quot;age&quot;</i>] += <i class=\"n\">1</i>               <i class=\"c\"># change</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(person, <i class=\"b\">len</i>(person))</pre></figure><p>Keys must be unchangeable types (str, int, tuple). Values can be anything.</p>","see":["py.dict-get","py.dict-loop"],"t":"person = { \"name\" : \"Ada\" , \"age\" : 36 } print (person[ \"name\" ]) person[ \"city\" ] = \"London\" # add person[ \"age\" ] += 1 # change print (person, len (person)) Keys must be unchangeable types (str, int, tuple). Values can be anything."},{"id":"py.dict-get","s":"py-dicts","q":"How do I get a value without a KeyError if the key is missing?","qh":"How do I get a value without a KeyError if the key is missing?","a":"<code>d.get(key)</code> returns <code>None</code> if missing, <code>d.get(key, default)</code> returns your default. Check with <code>if key in d:</code>.","aka":"dict get default, check key exists, key in dict, KeyError dictionary, setdefault, safe dict access","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>stock = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;apples&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">5</i>}\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(stock.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;pears&quot;</i>), stock.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;pears&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>))\n<i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;apples&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">in</i> stock:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;we have apples&quot;</i>)\nstock.setdefault(<i class=\"s\">&quot;pears&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>)     <i class=\"c\"># add only if missing</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(stock)</pre></figure>","see":["py.err.keyerror"],"t":"stock = { \"apples\" : 5 } print (stock.get( \"pears\" ), stock.get( \"pears\" , 0 )) if \"apples\" in stock: print ( \"we have apples\" ) stock.setdefault( \"pears\" , 0 ) # add only if missing print (stock)"},{"id":"py.dict-loop","s":"py-dicts","q":"How do I loop through a dictionary's keys and values?","qh":"How do I loop through a dictionary's keys and values?","a":"<code>for key, value in d.items():</code>. Looping over <code>d</code> directly gives just the keys; <code>d.values()</code> gives just the values.","aka":"loop dict, iterate dictionary, items, keys, values, for key value in dict","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>prices = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;tea&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">2.5</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;cake&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">3.0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;soup&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">4.2</i>}\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> item, price <i class=\"k\">in</i> prices.items():\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{item}<i class=\"s\">: </i>{price:<i class=\"s\">.2f</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">list</i>(prices.keys()), <i class=\"b\">sum</i>(prices.values()))</pre></figure><p>Don't add or delete keys while looping over the dict (<code>RuntimeError: dictionary changed size during iteration</code>); loop over <code>list(d)</code> instead.</p>","see":[],"t":"prices = { \"tea\" : 2.5 , \"cake\" : 3.0 , \"soup\" : 4.2 } for item, price in prices.items(): print ( f\" {item} : {price: .2f } \" ) print ( list (prices.keys()), sum (prices.values())) Don't add or delete keys while looping over the dict ( RuntimeError: dictionary changed size during iteration ); loop over list(d) instead."},{"id":"py.dict-remove","s":"py-dicts","q":"How do I delete a key from a dictionary?","qh":"How do I delete a key from a dictionary?","a":"<code>del d[key]</code> (error if missing) or <code>d.pop(key, None)</code> (safe, returns the value).","aka":"delete key, remove from dict, pop dict, del dict key","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>d = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;b&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;c&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">3</i>}\n<i class=\"k\">del</i> d[<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(d.pop(<i class=\"s\">&quot;b&quot;</i>), d.pop(<i class=\"s\">&quot;zzz&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">None</i>), d)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"d = { \"a\" : 1 , \"b\" : 2 , \"c\" : 3 } del d[ \"a\" ] print (d.pop( \"b\" ), d.pop( \"zzz\" , None ), d)"},{"id":"py.dict-sort","s":"py-dicts","q":"How do I sort a dictionary by value (or by key)?","qh":"How do I sort a dictionary by value (or by key)?","a":"<code>sorted(d.items(), key=lambda kv: kv[1], reverse=True)</code> gives a list of <code>(key, value)</code> pairs, highest first. Wrap in <code>dict(...)</code> for a dict again.","aka":"sort dict by value, sort dictionary, highest value in dict, leaderboard, top scores","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>scores = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">90</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Bob&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">72</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Cy&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">99</i>}\nranked = <i class=\"b\">sorted</i>(scores.items(), key=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i> kv: kv[<i class=\"n\">1</i>], reverse=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> name, s <i class=\"k\">in</i> ranked:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(name, s)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">max</i>(scores, key=scores.get))      <i class=\"c\"># key with the highest value</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">dict</i>(<i class=\"b\">sorted</i>(scores.items())))     <i class=\"c\"># by key</i></pre></figure>","see":["py.sort"],"t":"scores = { \"Ada\" : 90 , \"Bob\" : 72 , \"Cy\" : 99 } ranked = sorted (scores.items(), key= lambda kv: kv[ 1 ], reverse= True ) for name, s in ranked: print (name, s) print ( max (scores, key=scores.get)) # key with the highest value print ( dict ( sorted (scores.items()))) # by key"},{"id":"py.counter","s":"py-dicts","q":"How do I count how many times each thing appears?","qh":"How do I count how many times each thing appears?","a":"<code>collections.Counter(items)</code> does it in one go; or use a dict with <code>d[x] = d.get(x, 0) + 1</code>.","aka":"count occurrences, frequency, tally, Counter, count items in list, most common, histogram","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">from</i> collections <i class=\"k\">import</i> Counter\nvotes = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;blue&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;green&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;blue&quot;</i>]\nc = Counter(votes)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(c, c[<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>], c.most_common(<i class=\"n\">1</i>))\n\ncounts = {}\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> v <i class=\"k\">in</i> votes:\n    counts[v] = counts.get(v, <i class=\"n\">0</i>) + <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(counts)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"from collections import Counter votes = [ \"red\" , \"blue\" , \"red\" , \"green\" , \"red\" , \"blue\" ] c = Counter(votes) print (c, c[ \"red\" ], c.most_common( 1 )) counts = {} for v in votes: counts[v] = counts.get(v, 0 ) + 1 print (counts)"},{"id":"py.defaultdict","s":"py-dicts","q":"How do I group items into lists by a key?","qh":"How do I group items into lists by a key?","a":"<code>collections.defaultdict(list)</code> creates an empty list for new keys automatically.","aka":"group by, defaultdict, dict of lists, categorize, append to dict list","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">from</i> collections <i class=\"k\">import</i> defaultdict\nwords = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;apple&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;avocado&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;banana&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;blueberry&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;cherry&quot;</i>]\nby_letter = defaultdict(<i class=\"b\">list</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> w <i class=\"k\">in</i> words:\n    by_letter[w[<i class=\"n\">0</i>]].append(w)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">dict</i>(by_letter))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"from collections import defaultdict words = [ \"apple\" , \"avocado\" , \"banana\" , \"blueberry\" , \"cherry\" ] by_letter = defaultdict( list ) for w in words: by_letter[w[ 0 ]].append(w) print ( dict (by_letter))"},{"id":"py.dict-merge","s":"py-dicts","q":"How do I merge two dictionaries?","qh":"How do I merge two dictionaries?","a":"<code>merged = a | b</code> (Python 3.9+) or <code>{**a, **b}</code>. <code>a.update(b)</code> changes <code>a</code> in place. Values from <code>b</code> win on duplicate keys.","aka":"merge dicts, combine dictionaries, update dict, join dicts","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>defaults = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;volume&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">5</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;theme&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;light&quot;</i>}\nuser = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;theme&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;dark&quot;</i>}\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(defaults | user)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>({**defaults, **user})</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"defaults = { \"volume\" : 5 , \"theme\" : \"light\" } user = { \"theme\" : \"dark\" } print (defaults | user) print ({**defaults, **user})"},{"id":"py.nested-dict","s":"py-dicts","q":"How do I work with nested dictionaries (dicts inside dicts or lists of dicts)?","qh":"How do I work with nested dictionaries (dicts inside dicts or lists of dicts)?","a":"Chain the lookups: <code>data[&quot;users&quot;][0][&quot;name&quot;]</code>. Loop over the outer level and index into each inner one.","aka":"nested dict, dict of dicts, list of dicts, json like data, access nested value","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>data = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;users&quot;</i>: [{<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;langs&quot;</i>: [<i class=\"s\">&quot;Python&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;C&quot;</i>]},\n                  {<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;Bob&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;langs&quot;</i>: [<i class=\"s\">&quot;JS&quot;</i>]}]}\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(data[<i class=\"s\">&quot;users&quot;</i>][<i class=\"n\">0</i>][<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>])\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> u <i class=\"k\">in</i> data[<i class=\"s\">&quot;users&quot;</i>]:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(u[<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>], <i class=\"s\">&quot;, &quot;</i>.join(u[<i class=\"s\">&quot;langs&quot;</i>]))\ndata[<i class=\"s\">&quot;users&quot;</i>][<i class=\"n\">1</i>][<i class=\"s\">&quot;langs&quot;</i>].append(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Python&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>([u[<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>] <i class=\"k\">for</i> u <i class=\"k\">in</i> data[<i class=\"s\">&quot;users&quot;</i>] <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;Python&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">in</i> u[<i class=\"s\">&quot;langs&quot;</i>]])</pre></figure>","see":["py.json"],"t":"data = { \"users\" : [{ \"name\" : \"Ada\" , \"langs\" : [ \"Python\" , \"C\" ]}, { \"name\" : \"Bob\" , \"langs\" : [ \"JS\" ]}]} print (data[ \"users\" ][ 0 ][ \"name\" ]) for u in data[ \"users\" ]: print (u[ \"name\" ], \", \" .join(u[ \"langs\" ])) data[ \"users\" ][ 1 ][ \"langs\" ].append( \"Python\" ) print ([u[ \"name\" ] for u in data[ \"users\" ] if \"Python\" in u[ \"langs\" ]])"},{"id":"py.dict-comp","s":"py-dicts","q":"What is a dictionary comprehension?","qh":"What is a dictionary comprehension?","a":"<code>{key_expr: value_expr for item in iterable}</code>.","aka":"dict comprehension, create dict one line, invert dictionary, swap keys and values","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>names = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;ada&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;bob&quot;</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>({n: <i class=\"b\">len</i>(n) <i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> names})\nd = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;b&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">2</i>}\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>({v: k <i class=\"k\">for</i> k, v <i class=\"k\">in</i> d.items()})        <i class=\"c\"># swap keys and values</i></pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"names = [ \"ada\" , \"bob\" ] print ({n: len (n) for n in names}) d = { \"a\" : 1 , \"b\" : 2 } print ({v: k for k, v in d.items()}) # swap keys and values"},{"id":"py.set","s":"py-dicts","q":"What is a set and how do I use it (union, intersection, difference)?","qh":"What is a set and how do I use it (union, intersection, difference)?","a":"A set holds unique items with no order. <code>a | b</code> union, <code>a &amp; b</code> in both, <code>a - b</code> in a but not b, <code>a ^ b</code> in exactly one.","aka":"set, unique values, set operations, union, intersection, difference, common elements, in both lists","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>a = {<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>}\nb = {<i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">5</i>}\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(a | b, a &amp; b, a - b, a ^ b)\ns = <i class=\"b\">set</i>()                  <i class=\"c\"># empty set ({} is an empty dict)</i>\ns.add(<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>); s.add(<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(s, <i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">in</i> s)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">set</i>([<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>]) == {<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>})</pre></figure><p>Checking <code>x in some_set</code> is very fast, even with millions of items.</p>","see":[],"t":"a = { 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 } b = { 3 , 4 , 5 } print (a | b, a & b, a - b, a ^ b) s = set () # empty set ({} is an empty dict) s.add( \"x\" ); s.add( \"x\" ) print (s, \"x\" in s) print ( set ([ 1 , 1 , 2 ]) == { 1 , 2 }) Checking x in some_set is very fast, even with millions of items."},{"id":"py.dict-key-from-value","s":"py-dicts","q":"How do I find the key for a given value?","qh":"How do I find the key for a given value?","a":"Loop: <code>[k for k, v in d.items() if v == target]</code>. If you do it often, keep a reversed dict.","aka":"key from value, reverse lookup dict, find key by value","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>capitals = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;France&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;Paris&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Japan&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;Tokyo&quot;</i>}\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>([k <i class=\"k\">for</i> k, v <i class=\"k\">in</i> capitals.items() <i class=\"k\">if</i> v == <i class=\"s\">&quot;Tokyo&quot;</i>])\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">next</i>((k <i class=\"k\">for</i> k, v <i class=\"k\">in</i> capitals.items() <i class=\"k\">if</i> v == <i class=\"s\">&quot;Rome&quot;</i>), <i class=\"k\">None</i>))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"capitals = { \"France\" : \"Paris\" , \"Japan\" : \"Tokyo\" } print ([k for k, v in capitals.items() if v == \"Tokyo\" ]) print ( next ((k for k, v in capitals.items() if v == \"Rome\" ), None ))"},{"id":"py.file-read","s":"py-files","q":"How do I read a text file?","qh":"How do I read a text file?","a":"<code>with open(&quot;notes.txt&quot;, encoding=&quot;utf-8&quot;) as f:</code> then <code>f.read()</code> (all text) or loop <code>for line in f:</code> (line by line). <code>with</code> closes the file for you.","aka":"read file, open file, read lines, read text file, file contents, with open, readlines","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;notes.txt&quot;</i>, encoding=<i class=\"s\">&quot;utf-8&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n    text = f.read()\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(text)\n\n<i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;notes.txt&quot;</i>, encoding=<i class=\"s\">&quot;utf-8&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> line <i class=\"k\">in</i> f:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(line.rstrip(<i class=\"s\">&quot;\\n&quot;</i>))\n\nlines = <i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;notes.txt&quot;</i>, encoding=<i class=\"s\">&quot;utf-8&quot;</i>).read().splitlines()   <i class=\"c\"># list without \\n</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(lines)</pre></figure>","see":["py.err.filenotfound","py.script-dir"],"t":"with open ( \"notes.txt\" , encoding= \"utf-8\" ) as f: text = f.read() print (text) with open ( \"notes.txt\" , encoding= \"utf-8\" ) as f: for line in f: print (line.rstrip( \"\\n\" )) lines = open ( \"notes.txt\" , encoding= \"utf-8\" ).read().splitlines() # list without \\n print (lines)"},{"id":"py.file-write","s":"py-files","q":"How do I write or append to a text file?","qh":"How do I write or append to a text file?","a":"<code>open(path, &quot;w&quot;)</code> creates/overwrites; <code>&quot;a&quot;</code> appends to the end. Write with <code>f.write(text)</code>; add <code>&quot;\\n&quot;</code> yourself.","aka":"write file, save to file, create file, append to file, write lines, mode w a","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;out.txt&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>, encoding=<i class=\"s\">&quot;utf-8&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n    f.write(<i class=\"s\">&quot;first line\\n&quot;</i>)\n    f.write(<i class=\"s\">&quot;second line\\n&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;out.txt&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>, encoding=<i class=\"s\">&quot;utf-8&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n    f.write(<i class=\"s\">&quot;added later\\n&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;out.txt&quot;</i>, encoding=<i class=\"s\">&quot;utf-8&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(f.read())</pre></figure><p><code>print(..., file=f)</code> also writes to a file and adds the newline.</p>","see":[],"t":"with open ( \"out.txt\" , \"w\" , encoding= \"utf-8\" ) as f: f.write( \"first line\\n\" ) f.write( \"second line\\n\" ) with open ( \"out.txt\" , \"a\" , encoding= \"utf-8\" ) as f: f.write( \"added later\\n\" ) with open ( \"out.txt\" , encoding= \"utf-8\" ) as f: print (f.read()) print(..., file=f) also writes to a file and adds the newline."},{"id":"py.file-modes","s":"py-files","q":"What do the file modes r, w, a, x, b and + mean?","qh":"What do the file modes r, w, a, x, b and + mean?","a":"<code>&quot;r&quot;</code> read (default), <code>&quot;w&quot;</code> write (erases first), <code>&quot;a&quot;</code> append, <code>&quot;x&quot;</code> create only if new, <code>&quot;b&quot;</code> binary (images, etc.), <code>&quot;+&quot;</code> read and write.","aka":"file modes, open modes, rb wb, read write mode, binary mode","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code out\" data-tag=\"output\"><pre>&quot;r&quot;   read text                 error if the file doesn&#x27;t exist\n&quot;w&quot;   write text                creates the file, or ERASES it\n&quot;a&quot;   append text               creates the file, writes at the end\n&quot;x&quot;   create                    error if the file already exists\n&quot;rb&quot;  read bytes                images, zip, any non-text file\n&quot;wb&quot;  write bytes\n&quot;r+&quot;  read and write            file must exist</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"\"r\" read text error if the file doesn't exist \"w\" write text creates the file, or ERASES it \"a\" append text creates the file, writes at the end \"x\" create error if the file already exists \"rb\" read bytes images, zip, any non-text file \"wb\" write bytes \"r+\" read and write file must exist"},{"id":"py.csv","s":"py-files","q":"How do I read and write CSV files?","qh":"How do I read and write CSV files?","a":"Use the <code>csv</code> module. <code>csv.reader</code> gives lists, <code>csv.DictReader</code> gives dicts using the header row. Open with <code>newline=&quot;&quot;</code>.","aka":"csv, read csv, write csv, spreadsheet file, comma separated, DictReader, excel csv","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> csv\n\n<i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;scores.csv&quot;</i>, newline=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, encoding=<i class=\"s\">&quot;utf-8&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> row <i class=\"k\">in</i> csv.DictReader(f):\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(row[<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>], <i class=\"b\">int</i>(row[<i class=\"s\">&quot;score&quot;</i>]))\n\nrows = [[<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;score&quot;</i>], [<i class=\"s\">&quot;Kim&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">88</i>], [<i class=\"s\">&quot;Lee&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">95</i>]]\n<i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;new.csv&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>, newline=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, encoding=<i class=\"s\">&quot;utf-8&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n    csv.writer(f).writerows(rows)\n\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;new.csv&quot;</i>).read())</pre></figure><p>Without <code>newline=&quot;&quot;</code>, Windows gets an empty line between rows. For Excel files (<code>.xlsx</code>) use <code>openpyxl</code> or <code>pandas</code>.</p>","see":[],"t":"import csv with open ( \"scores.csv\" , newline= \"\" , encoding= \"utf-8\" ) as f: for row in csv.DictReader(f): print (row[ \"name\" ], int (row[ \"score\" ])) rows = [[ \"name\" , \"score\" ], [ \"Kim\" , 88 ], [ \"Lee\" , 95 ]] with open ( \"new.csv\" , \"w\" , newline= \"\" , encoding= \"utf-8\" ) as f: csv.writer(f).writerows(rows) print ( open ( \"new.csv\" ).read()) Without newline=\"\" , Windows gets an empty line between rows. For Excel files ( .xlsx ) use openpyxl or pandas ."},{"id":"py.json","s":"py-files","q":"How do I save and load data as JSON?","qh":"How do I save and load data as JSON?","a":"<code>json.dump(data, f)</code> writes; <code>json.load(f)</code> reads. <code>json.dumps</code>/<code>json.loads</code> (with s) work with strings.","aka":"json, save dict to file, load json, json dump, json load, store data, serialize","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> json\n\ndata = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;scores&quot;</i>: [<i class=\"n\">90</i>, <i class=\"n\">85</i>], <i class=\"s\">&quot;member&quot;</i>: <i class=\"k\">True</i>}\n<i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;data.json&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>, encoding=<i class=\"s\">&quot;utf-8&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n    json.dump(data, f, indent=<i class=\"n\">2</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;data.json&quot;</i>, encoding=<i class=\"s\">&quot;utf-8&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n    loaded = json.load(f)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(loaded[<i class=\"s\">&quot;scores&quot;</i>], loaded == data)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(json.dumps({<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">1</i>}))</pre></figure><p>JSON keys are always strings: an int key <code>{1: &quot;x&quot;}</code> comes back as <code>{&quot;1&quot;: &quot;x&quot;}</code>. Tuples come back as lists.</p>","see":["tk.save-settings"],"t":"import json data = { \"name\" : \"Ada\" , \"scores\" : [ 90 , 85 ], \"member\" : True } with open ( \"data.json\" , \"w\" , encoding= \"utf-8\" ) as f: json.dump(data, f, indent= 2 ) with open ( \"data.json\" , encoding= \"utf-8\" ) as f: loaded = json.load(f) print (loaded[ \"scores\" ], loaded == data) print (json.dumps({ \"a\" : 1 })) JSON keys are always strings: an int key {1: \"x\"} comes back as {\"1\": \"x\"} . Tuples come back as lists."},{"id":"py.script-dir","s":"py-files","q":"Why can't Python find my file when it's in the same folder as my script?","qh":"Why can't Python find my file when it's in the same folder as my script?","a":"Relative paths start from the <b>current working directory</b>, which depends on how the program was started (VS Code, IDLE, double-click). Build paths from the script's own folder: <code>Path(__file__).parent / &quot;data.txt&quot;</code>.","aka":"file not found same folder, relative path, working directory, os.getcwd, __file__, path of script, file not found vscode","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">from</i> pathlib <i class=\"k\">import</i> Path\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> os\n\nHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;working directory:&quot;</i>, os.getcwd())\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;script folder:    &quot;</i>, HERE)\npath = HERE / <i class=\"s\">&quot;notes.txt&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(path.exists())</pre></figure>","see":["py.err.filenotfound","py.pathlib"],"t":"from pathlib import Path import os HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent print ( \"working directory:\" , os.getcwd()) print ( \"script folder: \" , HERE) path = HERE / \"notes.txt\" print (path.exists())"},{"id":"py.pathlib","s":"py-files","q":"How do I work with file paths (join, file name, extension, parent folder)?","qh":"How do I work with file paths (join, file name, extension, parent folder)?","a":"<code>pathlib.Path</code> handles paths on every system: <code>/</code> joins, <code>.name</code>, <code>.stem</code>, <code>.suffix</code>, <code>.parent</code>, <code>.exists()</code>.","aka":"pathlib, os.path, join path, file extension, file name from path, parent directory, path separator windows","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">from</i> pathlib <i class=\"k\">import</i> Path\np = Path(<i class=\"s\">&quot;projects&quot;</i>) / <i class=\"s\">&quot;game&quot;</i> / <i class=\"s\">&quot;score.txt&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(p, p.name, p.stem, p.suffix, p.parent)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(p.with_suffix(<i class=\"s\">&quot;.json&quot;</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(Path.home())\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(Path(<i class=\"s\">&quot;notes.txt&quot;</i>).resolve())</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"from pathlib import Path p = Path( \"projects\" ) / \"game\" / \"score.txt\" print (p, p.name, p.stem, p.suffix, p.parent) print (p.with_suffix( \".json\" )) print (Path.home()) print (Path( \"notes.txt\" ).resolve())"},{"id":"py.file-exists","s":"py-files","q":"How do I check if a file or folder exists, and create or delete them?","qh":"How do I check if a file or folder exists, and create or delete them?","a":"<code>Path(p).exists()</code>, <code>Path(p).mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)</code>, <code>Path(p).unlink()</code> (delete file), <code>Path(p).rename(new)</code>, <code>shutil.rmtree(folder)</code> (delete folder and contents).","aka":"file exists, os.path.exists, create folder, mkdir, delete file, remove file, rename file, makedirs","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">from</i> pathlib <i class=\"k\">import</i> Path\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> shutil\n\nfolder = Path(<i class=\"s\">&quot;backup/2026&quot;</i>)\nfolder.mkdir(parents=<i class=\"k\">True</i>, exist_ok=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nf = folder / <i class=\"s\">&quot;a.txt&quot;</i>\nf.write_text(<i class=\"s\">&quot;hello&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(f.exists(), f.is_file(), folder.is_dir())\nf.rename(folder / <i class=\"s\">&quot;b.txt&quot;</i>)\n(folder / <i class=\"s\">&quot;b.txt&quot;</i>).unlink()\nshutil.rmtree(<i class=\"s\">&quot;backup&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(Path(<i class=\"s\">&quot;backup&quot;</i>).exists())</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"from pathlib import Path import shutil folder = Path( \"backup/2026\" ) folder.mkdir(parents= True , exist_ok= True ) f = folder / \"a.txt\" f.write_text( \"hello\" ) print (f.exists(), f.is_file(), folder.is_dir()) f.rename(folder / \"b.txt\" ) (folder / \"b.txt\" ).unlink() shutil.rmtree( \"backup\" ) print (Path( \"backup\" ).exists())"},{"id":"py.listdir","s":"py-files","q":"How do I list the files in a folder (or find all .txt files)?","qh":"How do I list the files in a folder (or find all .txt files)?","a":"<code>Path(&quot;.&quot;).iterdir()</code> lists everything; <code>Path(&quot;.&quot;).glob(&quot;*.txt&quot;)</code> matches a pattern; <code>rglob</code> searches subfolders too.","aka":"list files, os.listdir, files in directory, glob, find files, walk folders, recursive","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">from</i> pathlib <i class=\"k\">import</i> Path\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> p <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">sorted</i>(Path(<i class=\"s\">&quot;.&quot;</i>).iterdir()):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>((<i class=\"s\">&quot;DIR &quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> p.is_dir() <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;    &quot;</i>) + p.name)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>([p.name <i class=\"k\">for</i> p <i class=\"k\">in</i> Path(<i class=\"s\">&quot;.&quot;</i>).glob(<i class=\"s\">&quot;*.txt&quot;</i>)])\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">len</i>(<i class=\"b\">list</i>(Path(<i class=\"s\">&quot;.&quot;</i>).rglob(<i class=\"s\">&quot;*.py&quot;</i>))), <i class=\"s\">&quot;python files here and below&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"from pathlib import Path for p in sorted (Path( \".\" ).iterdir()): print (( \"DIR \" if p.is_dir() else \" \" ) + p.name) print ([p.name for p in Path( \".\" ).glob( \"*.txt\" )]) print ( len ( list (Path( \".\" ).rglob( \"*.py\" ))), \"python files here and below\" )"},{"id":"py.file-lines-count","s":"py-files","q":"How do I read a file into a list of numbers (one per line)?","qh":"How do I read a file into a list of numbers (one per line)?","a":"Read lines, strip them, skip blanks, convert each.","aka":"read numbers from file, file to list, parse file, load numbers","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;nums.txt&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n    f.write(<i class=\"s\">&quot;4\\n8\\n\\n15\\n&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;nums.txt&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n    nums = [<i class=\"b\">int</i>(line) <i class=\"k\">for</i> line <i class=\"k\">in</i> f <i class=\"k\">if</i> line.strip()]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(nums, <i class=\"b\">sum</i>(nums))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"with open ( \"nums.txt\" , \"w\" ) as f: f.write( \"4\\n8\\n\\n15\\n\" ) with open ( \"nums.txt\" ) as f: nums = [ int (line) for line in f if line.strip()] print (nums, sum (nums))"},{"id":"py.encoding","s":"py-files","q":"UnicodeDecodeError: 'charmap' codec can't decode byte ... How do I fix it?","qh":"UnicodeDecodeError: 'charmap' codec can't decode byte ... How do I fix it?","a":"Say which encoding to use: <code>open(path, encoding=&quot;utf-8&quot;)</code>. Windows otherwise uses an older default. If the file really isn't UTF-8, try <code>encoding=&quot;cp1252&quot;</code> or <code>errors=&quot;replace&quot;</code>.","aka":"unicodedecodeerror, charmap codec, utf-8 error, encoding error, can't decode byte, emoji in file, special characters file","err":"UnicodeDecodeError|UnicodeEncodeError|'charmap' codec can't","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;u.txt&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>, encoding=<i class=\"s\">&quot;utf-8&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n    f.write(<i class=\"s\">&quot;café, naïve, emoji ✓\\n&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;u.txt&quot;</i>, encoding=<i class=\"s\">&quot;utf-8&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(f.read())\n<i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;u.txt&quot;</i>, encoding=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ascii&quot;</i>, errors=<i class=\"s\">&quot;replace&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(f.read())</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"with open ( \"u.txt\" , \"w\" , encoding= \"utf-8\" ) as f: f.write( \"café, naïve, emoji ✓\\n\" ) with open ( \"u.txt\" , encoding= \"utf-8\" ) as f: print (f.read()) with open ( \"u.txt\" , encoding= \"ascii\" , errors= \"replace\" ) as f: print (f.read())"},{"id":"py.pickle","s":"py-files","q":"How do I save any Python object (not just JSON-friendly data)?","qh":"How do I save any Python object (not just JSON-friendly data)?","a":"<code>pickle.dump(obj, f)</code> / <code>pickle.load(f)</code> with files opened in binary (<code>&quot;wb&quot;</code>/<code>&quot;rb&quot;</code>). Only load pickles you created yourself; a malicious pickle can run code.","aka":"pickle, save object, serialize class, save game state","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> pickle\nstate = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;level&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;items&quot;</i>: {<i class=\"s\">&quot;sword&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;map&quot;</i>}, <i class=\"s\">&quot;pos&quot;</i>: (<i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">7</i>)}\n<i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;save.pkl&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;wb&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n    pickle.dump(state, f)\n<i class=\"k\">with</i> <i class=\"b\">open</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;save.pkl&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;rb&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">as</i> f:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(pickle.load(f))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import pickle state = { \"level\" : 3 , \"items\" : { \"sword\" , \"map\" }, \"pos\" : ( 4 , 7 )} with open ( \"save.pkl\" , \"wb\" ) as f: pickle.dump(state, f) with open ( \"save.pkl\" , \"rb\" ) as f: print (pickle.load(f))"},{"id":"py.sqlite","s":"py-files","q":"How do I use an SQLite database?","qh":"How do I use an SQLite database?","a":"<code>sqlite3.connect(&quot;file.db&quot;)</code>, then <code>execute</code> SQL with <code>?</code> placeholders; <code>commit()</code> after changes; <code>fetchall()</code> to read results.","aka":"sqlite3, database, sql python, create table, insert, select, query database","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> sqlite3\ndb = sqlite3.connect(<i class=\"s\">&quot;:memory:&quot;</i>)          <i class=\"c\"># or &quot;app.db&quot; for a file</i>\ndb.execute(<i class=\"s\">&quot;CREATE TABLE scores (name TEXT, points INTEGER)&quot;</i>)\ndb.executemany(<i class=\"s\">&quot;INSERT INTO scores VALUES (?, ?)&quot;</i>, [(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">90</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Bob&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">72</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Cy&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">99</i>)])\ndb.commit()\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> name, pts <i class=\"k\">in</i> db.execute(<i class=\"s\">&quot;SELECT name, points FROM scores WHERE points &gt; ? ORDER BY points DESC&quot;</i>, (<i class=\"n\">80</i>,)):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(name, pts)\ndb.close()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.sqlite"],"t":"import sqlite3 db = sqlite3.connect( \":memory:\" ) # or \"app.db\" for a file db.execute( \"CREATE TABLE scores (name TEXT, points INTEGER)\" ) db.executemany( \"INSERT INTO scores VALUES (?, ?)\" , [( \"Ada\" , 90 ), ( \"Bob\" , 72 ), ( \"Cy\" , 99 )]) db.commit() for name, pts in db.execute( \"SELECT name, points FROM scores WHERE points > ? ORDER BY points DESC\" , ( 80 ,)): print (name, pts) db.close()"},{"id":"py.read-traceback","s":"py-errors","q":"How do I read an error message (traceback)?","qh":"How do I read an error message (traceback)?","a":"Read it <b>from the bottom up</b>. The last line is the error type and message. The line above it shows the exact code and line number that failed. Lines higher up show how the program got there.","aka":"traceback, read error, understand error, stack trace, what does this error mean, error line number","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\" data-tag=\"error\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">average</i>(nums):\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"b\">sum</i>(nums) / <i class=\"b\">len</i>(nums)\n\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(average([]))</pre></figure><figure class=\"code out\" data-tag=\"output\"><pre>Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;example.py&quot;, line 4, in &lt;module&gt;\n    print(average([]))\n  File &quot;example.py&quot;, line 2, in average\n    return sum(nums) / len(nums)\nZeroDivisionError: division by zero</pre></figure><p>Here: dividing by <code>len(nums)</code>, which was 0, on line 2, inside <code>average</code>, called from line 4. Paste the <b>last line</b> into the search box above.</p>","see":["py.try-except"],"t":"def average (nums): return sum (nums) / len (nums) print (average([])) Traceback (most recent call last): File \"example.py\", line 4, in <module> print(average([])) File \"example.py\", line 2, in average return sum(nums) / len(nums) ZeroDivisionError: division by zero Here: dividing by len(nums) , which was 0, on line 2, inside average , called from line 4. Paste the last line into the search box above."},{"id":"py.try-except","s":"py-errors","q":"How do I catch errors so my program doesn't crash (try/except)?","qh":"How do I catch errors so my program doesn't crash (try/except)?","a":"Put risky code in <code>try:</code> and handle a <b>specific</b> error in <code>except ErrorType:</code>. <code>else:</code> runs if nothing failed; <code>finally:</code> always runs.","aka":"try except, catch error, error handling, exception handling, handle exception, finally, else, except Exception as e","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">safe_divide</i>(a, b):\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        result = a / b\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> ZeroDivisionError:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Can&#x27;t divide by zero&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"k\">None</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> TypeError <i class=\"k\">as</i> e:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Bad types:&quot;</i>, e)\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"k\">None</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">else</i>:\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> result\n    <i class=\"k\">finally</i>:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;(done)&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(safe_divide(<i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(safe_divide(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(safe_divide(<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>))</pre></figure><p>Avoid a bare <code>except:</code>: it also hides typos and Ctrl+C. At most use <code>except Exception as e:</code> and print <code>e</code>.</p>","see":["py.raise"],"t":"def safe_divide (a, b): try : result = a / b except ZeroDivisionError: print ( \"Can't divide by zero\" ) return None except TypeError as e: print ( \"Bad types:\" , e) return None else : return result finally : print ( \"(done)\" ) print (safe_divide( 10 , 2 )) print (safe_divide( 1 , 0 )) print (safe_divide( \"a\" , 2 )) Avoid a bare except: : it also hides typos and Ctrl+C. At most use except Exception as e: and print e ."},{"id":"py.raise","s":"py-errors","q":"How do I raise my own error (or make a custom exception)?","qh":"How do I raise my own error (or make a custom exception)?","a":"<code>raise ValueError(&quot;message&quot;)</code>. For your own kind, subclass <code>Exception</code>.","aka":"raise, throw exception, custom exception, raise ValueError, assert","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">OutOfStock</i>(Exception):\n    <i class=\"k\">pass</i>\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">buy</i>(stock, qty):\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> qty &lt;= <i class=\"n\">0</i>:\n        <i class=\"k\">raise</i> ValueError(<i class=\"s\">&quot;quantity must be positive&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> qty &gt; stock:\n        <i class=\"k\">raise</i> OutOfStock(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">only </i>{stock}<i class=\"s\"> left</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> stock - qty\n\n<i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n    buy(<i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">5</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">except</i> OutOfStock <i class=\"k\">as</i> e:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Sorry:&quot;</i>, e)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"class OutOfStock (Exception): pass def buy (stock, qty): if qty <= 0 : raise ValueError( \"quantity must be positive\" ) if qty > stock: raise OutOfStock( f\" only {stock} left \" ) return stock - qty try : buy( 3 , 5 ) except OutOfStock as e: print ( \"Sorry:\" , e)"},{"id":"py.err.syntax","s":"py-errors","q":"SyntaxError: invalid syntax. What's wrong?","qh":"SyntaxError: invalid syntax. What's wrong?","a":"Python couldn't understand the code's shape. Check the line shown <b>and the line before it</b> for: a missing <code>:</code> after <code>if</code>/<code>for</code>/<code>def</code>, unclosed brackets or quotes, <code>=</code> instead of <code>==</code>, or a missing comma.","aka":"syntax error, invalid syntax, unexpected eof, expected ':', unterminated string, unmatched bracket, was never closed, cannot assign to","err":"SyntaxError|invalid syntax|expected ':'|was never closed|unterminated string literal|unexpected EOF while parsing|cannot assign to","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre>if x = 5            # needs ==  and a colon\nprint(&quot;hi&quot;          # bracket never closed\nname = &quot;Ada         # quote never closed\nfor i in range(3)   # missing colon</pre></figure><figure class=\"code out\" data-tag=\"output\"><pre>SyntaxError: expected &#x27;:&#x27;                     -&gt; add the colon at the end of the line\nSyntaxError: &#x27;(&#x27; was never closed             -&gt; count your brackets\nSyntaxError: unterminated string literal      -&gt; a quote is missing\nSyntaxError: invalid syntax. Maybe you meant &#x27;==&#x27; or &#x27;:=&#x27; instead of &#x27;=&#x27;?</pre></figure><p>The arrow <code>^</code> points to where Python gave up, which is often just <b>after</b> the real mistake.</p>","see":[],"t":"if x = 5 # needs == and a colon print(\"hi\" # bracket never closed name = \"Ada # quote never closed for i in range(3) # missing colon SyntaxError: expected ':' -> add the colon at the end of the line SyntaxError: '(' was never closed -> count your brackets SyntaxError: unterminated string literal -> a quote is missing SyntaxError: invalid syntax. Maybe you meant '==' or ':=' instead of '='? The arrow ^ points to where Python gave up, which is often just after the real mistake."},{"id":"py.err.indentation","s":"py-errors","q":"IndentationError: expected an indented block / unexpected indent / unindent does not match","qh":"IndentationError: expected an indented block / unexpected indent / unindent does not match","a":"The spaces at the start of lines don't line up. Every line in a block needs the same indentation (4 spaces), a line ending in <code>:</code> must be followed by an indented line, and tabs and spaces must not be mixed.","aka":"indentation error, unexpected indent, expected an indented block, unindent does not match any outer indentation level, inconsistent use of tabs and spaces, TabError","err":"IndentationError|expected an indented block|unexpected indent|unindent does not match|TabError|inconsistent use of tabs and spaces","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">greet</i>():\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;hi&quot;</i>)          <i class=\"c\"># expected an indented block</i>\n\nx = <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n    y = <i class=\"n\">2</i>            <i class=\"c\"># unexpected indent</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">greet</i>():\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;hi&quot;</i>)\n\nx = <i class=\"n\">1</i>\ny = <i class=\"n\">2</i>\ngreet()</pre></figure><p>In IDLE: select all and use Format &gt; Untabify Region. In VS Code: \"Convert Indentation to Spaces\".</p>","see":["py.indentation"],"t":"def greet (): print ( \"hi\" ) # expected an indented block x = 1 y = 2 # unexpected indent def greet (): print ( \"hi\" ) x = 1 y = 2 greet() In IDLE: select all and use Format > Untabify Region. In VS Code: \"Convert Indentation to Spaces\"."},{"id":"py.err.nameerror","s":"py-errors","q":"NameError: name 'x' is not defined","qh":"NameError: name 'x' is not defined","a":"Python doesn't know that name at that moment. Usually: a typo (or wrong capitals), using a variable before assigning it, a missing <code>import</code>, a variable that only exists inside another function, or text that needs quotes.","aka":"name is not defined, nameerror, variable not defined, undefined variable, function not defined","err":"NameError: name '*' is not defined|is not defined","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre><i class=\"b\">print</i>(score)              <i class=\"c\"># never assigned</i>\nPrint(<i class=\"s\">&quot;hi&quot;</i>)               <i class=\"c\"># capital P</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(hello)              <i class=\"c\"># meant the text &quot;hello&quot;</i>\nx = math.sqrt(<i class=\"n\">4</i>)          <i class=\"c\"># forgot: import math</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> math\nscore = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(score, <i class=\"s\">&quot;hello&quot;</i>, math.sqrt(<i class=\"n\">4</i>))</pre></figure>","see":["py.err.unboundlocal"],"t":"print (score) # never assigned Print( \"hi\" ) # capital P print (hello) # meant the text \"hello\" x = math.sqrt( 4 ) # forgot: import math import math score = 0 print (score, \"hello\" , math.sqrt( 4 ))"},{"id":"py.err.str-int-concat","s":"py-errors","q":"TypeError: can only concatenate str (not \"int\") to str","qh":"TypeError: can only concatenate str (not \"int\") to str","a":"You used <code>+</code> between text and a number. Convert the number with <code>str()</code>, or use an f-string. If the text came from <code>input()</code>, convert it with <code>int()</code> to do maths.","aka":"can only concatenate str, must be str not int, unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'int' and 'str', add string and number, concatenate int and string","err":"can only concatenate str (not \"*\") to str|unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'int' and 'str'|must be str, not int","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre>age = <i class=\"n\">12</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Age: &quot;</i> + age)</pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre>age = <i class=\"n\">12</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Age: &quot;</i> + <i class=\"b\">str</i>(age))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Age: </i>{age}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\nyears = <i class=\"b\">int</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;5&quot;</i>)             <i class=\"c\"># e.g. from input()</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(years + <i class=\"n\">1</i>)</pre></figure>","see":["py.fstring","py.convert"],"t":"age = 12 print ( \"Age: \" + age) age = 12 print ( \"Age: \" + str (age)) print ( f\" Age: {age} \" ) years = int ( \"5\" ) # e.g. from input() print (years + 1 )"},{"id":"py.err.valueerror-int","s":"py-errors","q":"ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10","qh":"ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10","a":"<code>int()</code> got text that isn't a whole number: letters, an empty string <code>&#x27;&#x27;</code>, a decimal like <code>&#x27;3.5&#x27;</code>, or hidden spaces/newlines. Check the value shown in quotes in the message, then validate the input or catch the error.","aka":"invalid literal for int, valueerror int, could not convert string to float, int input error, convert text to number fails","err":"invalid literal for int() with base 10|could not convert string to float","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">for</i> text <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;42&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot; 7\\n&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;3.5&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;abc&quot;</i>):\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">repr</i>(text), <i class=\"s\">&quot;-&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"b\">int</i>(text))\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> ValueError:\n        <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n            <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">repr</i>(text), <i class=\"s\">&quot;-&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"b\">int</i>(<i class=\"b\">float</i>(text)), <i class=\"s\">&quot;(via float)&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">except</i> ValueError:\n            <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">repr</i>(text), <i class=\"s\">&quot;-&gt; not a number&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":["py.input-number"],"t":"for text in ( \"42\" , \" 7\\n\" , \"3.5\" , \"\" , \"abc\" ): try : print ( repr (text), \"->\" , int (text)) except ValueError: try : print ( repr (text), \"->\" , int ( float (text)), \"(via float)\" ) except ValueError: print ( repr (text), \"-> not a number\" )"},{"id":"py.err.indexerror","s":"py-errors","q":"IndexError: list index out of range","qh":"IndexError: list index out of range","a":"You asked for a position that doesn't exist. A list of length 3 has indexes 0, 1, 2. Common causes: <code>range(len(x) + 1)</code>, using <code>x[len(x)]</code> for the last item (use <code>x[-1]</code>), or an empty list.","aka":"list index out of range, index error, string index out of range, tuple index out of range, off by one","err":"index out of range","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre>items = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;b&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;c&quot;</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(items[<i class=\"n\">3</i>])               <i class=\"c\"># only 0..2</i>\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"b\">len</i>(items) + <i class=\"n\">1</i>):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(items[i])</pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre>items = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;b&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;c&quot;</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(items[-<i class=\"n\">1</i>])\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> item <i class=\"k\">in</i> items:            <i class=\"c\"># no index needed</i>\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(item)\n<i class=\"k\">if</i> items:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(items[<i class=\"n\">0</i>])</pre></figure>","see":["py.list-index-error"],"t":"items = [ \"a\" , \"b\" , \"c\" ] print (items[ 3 ]) # only 0..2 for i in range ( len (items) + 1 ): print (items[i]) items = [ \"a\" , \"b\" , \"c\" ] print (items[- 1 ]) for item in items: # no index needed print (item) if items: print (items[ 0 ])"},{"id":"py.err.keyerror","s":"py-errors","q":"KeyError: 'something'","qh":"KeyError: 'something'","a":"The dictionary has no such key. Check spelling and capitals, or use <code>d.get(key, default)</code> / <code>if key in d:</code>. Print <code>d.keys()</code> to see what's really there.","aka":"keyerror, key not found, dictionary key error, missing key","err":"KeyError","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>d = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;Name&quot;</i>: <i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>}\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(d.keys())\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(d.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;not found&quot;</i>))       <i class=\"c\"># lowercase key isn&#x27;t there</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(d[<i class=\"s\">&quot;Name&quot;</i>])</pre></figure>","see":["py.dict-get"],"t":"d = { \"Name\" : \"Ada\" } print (d.keys()) print (d.get( \"name\" , \"not found\" )) # lowercase key isn't there print (d[ \"Name\" ])"},{"id":"py.err.attributeerror","s":"py-errors","q":"AttributeError: 'X' object has no attribute 'y'","qh":"AttributeError: 'X' object has no attribute 'y'","a":"That type doesn't have that method/property. Usually a typo, the variable holds a different type than you think (print <code>type(x)</code>), or a method from another type (lists have <code>append</code>, strings don't).","aka":"has no attribute, attribute error, object has no attribute, str has no attribute, list has no attribute append","err":"object has no attribute|has no attribute","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>name = <i class=\"s\">&quot;ada&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">type</i>(name), [m <i class=\"k\">for</i> m <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">dir</i>(name) <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> m.startswith(<i class=\"s\">&quot;_&quot;</i>)][:<i class=\"n\">8</i>])\nwords = name.split()          <i class=\"c\"># strings have no append(); make a list first</i>\nwords.append(<i class=\"s\">&quot;lovelace&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(words)</pre></figure>","see":["py.err.nonetype","tk.err.none-grid"],"t":"name = \"ada\" print ( type (name), [m for m in dir (name) if not m.startswith( \"_\" )][: 8 ]) words = name.split() # strings have no append(); make a list first words.append( \"lovelace\" ) print (words)"},{"id":"py.err.nonetype","s":"py-errors","q":"TypeError: 'NoneType' object is not subscriptable (or not iterable / has no attribute)","qh":"TypeError: 'NoneType' object is not subscriptable (or not iterable / has no attribute)","a":"A variable you expected to hold something is <code>None</code>. Usually: a function with no <code>return</code> (or a path that doesn't reach it), or storing the result of an in-place method like <code>x = list.sort()</code> or <code>x = list.append(...)</code>.","aka":"nonetype, none type error, nonetype object is not subscriptable, nonetype object is not iterable, cannot unpack non-iterable NoneType","err":"'NoneType' object is not subscriptable|'NoneType' object is not iterable|cannot unpack non-iterable NoneType object","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre>nums = [<i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>]\nnums = nums.sort()          <i class=\"c\"># sort() returns None</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(nums[<i class=\"n\">0</i>])</pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre>nums = [<i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>]\nnums.sort()                 <i class=\"c\"># sorts in place</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(nums[<i class=\"n\">0</i>])\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">find</i>(name):\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> name == <i class=\"s\">&quot;ada&quot;</i>:\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> {<i class=\"s\">&quot;age&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">36</i>}\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> {}               <i class=\"c\"># always return something useful</i>\n\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(find(<i class=\"s\">&quot;bob&quot;</i>).get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;age&quot;</i>))</pre></figure>","see":["py.return-vs-print","tk.err.none-grid"],"t":"nums = [ 3 , 1 , 2 ] nums = nums.sort() # sort() returns None print (nums[ 0 ]) nums = [ 3 , 1 , 2 ] nums.sort() # sorts in place print (nums[ 0 ]) def find (name): if name == \"ada\" : return { \"age\" : 36 } return {} # always return something useful print (find( \"bob\" ).get( \"age\" ))"},{"id":"py.err.not-callable","s":"py-errors","q":"TypeError: 'int' object is not callable (or 'str', 'list'...)","qh":"TypeError: 'int' object is not callable (or 'str', 'list'...)","a":"You put <code>()</code> after something that isn't a function. Usually you reused a built-in name as a variable (<code>list = [...]</code>, <code>str = &quot;...&quot;</code>, <code>sum = 0</code>, <code>max = 5</code>), or forgot an operator (<code>2(3 + 4)</code> instead of <code>2 * (3 + 4)</code>).","aka":"object is not callable, int not callable, str not callable, list not callable, shadowed builtin","err":"object is not callable","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre><i class=\"b\">sum</i> = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\ntotal = <i class=\"b\">sum</i>([<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>])      <i class=\"c\"># sum is now an int</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"n\">2</i>(<i class=\"n\">3</i> + <i class=\"n\">4</i>))</pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre>total_so_far = <i class=\"n\">0</i>                 <i class=\"c\"># don&#x27;t name it sum</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">sum</i>([<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>]), <i class=\"n\">2</i> * (<i class=\"n\">3</i> + <i class=\"n\">4</i>))</pre></figure><p>In IDLE or a notebook, restart after fixing so the old variable is gone.</p>","see":[],"t":"sum = 0 total = sum ([ 1 , 2 , 3 ]) # sum is now an int print ( 2 ( 3 + 4 )) total_so_far = 0 # don't name it sum print ( sum ([ 1 , 2 , 3 ]), 2 * ( 3 + 4 )) In IDLE or a notebook, restart after fixing so the old variable is gone."},{"id":"py.err.not-subscriptable","s":"py-errors","q":"TypeError: 'int' object is not subscriptable","qh":"TypeError: 'int' object is not subscriptable","a":"You used <code>[ ]</code> on something that isn't a list/str/dict. Check the variable really holds what you think, and whether you meant <code>( )</code> for a function call.","aka":"not subscriptable, int not subscriptable, function object is not subscriptable, type object is not subscriptable","err":"object is not subscriptable","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>n = <i class=\"n\">1234</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">str</i>(n)[<i class=\"n\">0</i>])              <i class=\"c\"># digits: convert to str first</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>([<i class=\"b\">int</i>(d) <i class=\"k\">for</i> d <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">str</i>(n)])\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">get_items</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> [<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(get_items()[<i class=\"n\">0</i>])         <i class=\"c\"># call it with () first, then index</i></pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"n = 1234 print ( str (n)[ 0 ]) # digits: convert to str first print ([ int (d) for d in str (n)]) def get_items (): return [ 1 , 2 ] print (get_items()[ 0 ]) # call it with () first, then index"},{"id":"py.err.not-iterable","s":"py-errors","q":"TypeError: 'int' object is not iterable","qh":"TypeError: 'int' object is not iterable","a":"A <code>for</code> loop (or <code>sum</code>, <code>list()</code>, unpacking) got a single number. To loop a number of times use <code>range(n)</code>.","aka":"not iterable, int is not iterable, for loop int, cannot unpack non-iterable int","err":"object is not iterable|cannot unpack non-iterable","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre><i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"n\">5</i>:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(i)</pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">5</i>):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(i, end=<i class=\"s\">&quot; &quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"for i in 5 : print (i) for i in range ( 5 ): print (i, end= \" \" )"},{"id":"py.err.positional-args","s":"py-errors","q":"TypeError: f() takes 1 positional argument but 2 were given / missing 1 required positional argument","qh":"TypeError: f() takes 1 positional argument but 2 were given / missing 1 required positional argument","a":"You called the function with the wrong number of arguments. In a <b>class</b>, every method needs <code>self</code> as its first parameter, and you call it on an object (<code>obj.method()</code>), not on the class.","aka":"takes positional arguments but were given, missing required positional argument, missing self, wrong number of arguments","err":"takes * positional argument* but * were given|missing * required positional argument","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre><i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Dog</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">bark</i>():                 <i class=\"c\"># missing self</i>\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;woof&quot;</i>)\nDog().bark()                    <i class=\"c\"># takes 0 positional arguments but 1 was given</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Dog</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">bark</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;woof&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">area</i>(w, h):\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> w * h\n\nDog().bark()\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(area(<i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>))</pre></figure>","see":["tk.err.bind-missing-arg","py.class-basics"],"t":"class Dog : def bark (): # missing self print ( \"woof\" ) Dog().bark() # takes 0 positional arguments but 1 was given class Dog : def bark ( self ): print ( \"woof\" ) def area (w, h): return w * h Dog().bark() print (area( 3 , 4 ))"},{"id":"py.err.zerodivision","s":"py-errors","q":"ZeroDivisionError: division by zero","qh":"ZeroDivisionError: division by zero","a":"You divided by 0 (often <code>len()</code> of an empty list, or a counter that never increased). Check first.","aka":"division by zero, zerodivisionerror, divide by zero, modulo by zero","err":"ZeroDivisionError|division by zero|integer modulo by zero","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>scores = []\naverage = <i class=\"b\">sum</i>(scores) / <i class=\"b\">len</i>(scores) <i class=\"k\">if</i> scores <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(average)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"scores = [] average = sum (scores) / len (scores) if scores else 0 print (average)"},{"id":"py.err.modulenotfound","s":"py-errors","q":"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'x'","qh":"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'x'","a":"The package isn't installed <b>for the Python you're running</b>. Install with <code>python -m pip install x</code> using the same <code>python</code> command you run your script with. Some names differ: <code>pip install pillow</code> gives <code>import PIL</code>, <code>pip install opencv-python</code> gives <code>import cv2</code>, <code>beautifulsoup4</code> gives <code>bs4</code>.","aka":"no module named, module not found, import error, pip installed but not found, cannot import name","err":"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named|ImportError: cannot import name","h":"<figure class=\"code sh\" data-tag=\"terminal\"><pre>python -m pip install requests          # Windows: py -m pip install requests\npython3 -m pip install requests         # macOS / Linux\npython -c &quot;import sys; print(sys.executable)&quot;   # which Python am I running?</pre></figure><p>In VS Code, check the interpreter shown in the bottom-right corner matches the one you installed into. <code>cannot import name &#x27;X&#x27;</code> means the module exists but has no <code>X</code> (typo, wrong version, or a circular import).</p>","see":["py.pip","tk.install"],"t":"python -m pip install requests # Windows: py -m pip install requests python3 -m pip install requests # macOS / Linux python -c \"import sys; print(sys.executable)\" # which Python am I running? In VS Code, check the interpreter shown in the bottom-right corner matches the one you installed into. cannot import name 'X' means the module exists but has no X (typo, wrong version, or a circular import)."},{"id":"py.err.filenotfound","s":"py-errors","q":"FileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory","qh":"FileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory","a":"The path is wrong <b>from where Python is running</b>. Print <code>os.getcwd()</code> to see that folder, use the script's own folder (<code>Path(__file__).parent</code>), check the exact name and extension (Windows can hide <code>.txt</code>), and use <code>r&quot;...&quot;</code> for Windows paths.","aka":"no such file or directory, file not found, errno 2, can't open file, path wrong","err":"FileNotFoundError|No such file or directory|Errno 2","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">from</i> pathlib <i class=\"k\">import</i> Path\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> os\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Running in:&quot;</i>, os.getcwd())\ntarget = Path(__file__).parent / <i class=\"s\">&quot;notes.txt&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(target, <i class=\"s\">&quot;exists&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> target.exists() <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;MISSING&quot;</i>)</pre></figure><p><code>python: can&#x27;t open file &#x27;x.py&#x27;</code> is the same problem in the terminal: <code>cd</code> into the script's folder first.</p>","see":["py.script-dir","py.file-exists"],"t":"from pathlib import Path import os print ( \"Running in:\" , os.getcwd()) target = Path(__file__).parent / \"notes.txt\" print (target, \"exists\" if target.exists() else \"MISSING\" ) python: can't open file 'x.py' is the same problem in the terminal: cd into the script's folder first."},{"id":"py.err.unboundlocal","s":"py-errors","q":"UnboundLocalError: cannot access local variable 'x' where it is not associated with a value","qh":"UnboundLocalError: cannot access local variable 'x' where it is not associated with a value","a":"Inside a function you assign to a variable that also exists outside. Python then treats it as local everywhere in the function, so reading it first fails. Add <code>global x</code> (or <code>nonlocal x</code>), or pass it in and return it.","aka":"unboundlocalerror, local variable referenced before assignment, cannot access local variable, global variable in function error","err":"UnboundLocalError|referenced before assignment|cannot access local variable","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre>count = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">add</i>():\n    count += <i class=\"n\">1</i>          <i class=\"c\"># UnboundLocalError</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre>count = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">add</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> count\n    count += <i class=\"n\">1</i>\nadd()\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(count)</pre></figure>","see":["py.global"],"t":"count = 0 def add (): count += 1 # UnboundLocalError count = 0 def add (): global count count += 1 add() print (count)"},{"id":"py.err.recursion","s":"py-errors","q":"RecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded","qh":"RecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded","a":"A function calls itself without ever stopping (missing or unreachable base case), or recursion is just too deep (over ~1000 levels). Fix the base case or use a loop.","aka":"recursion error, maximum recursion depth, infinite recursion, stack overflow python","err":"RecursionError|maximum recursion depth exceeded","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">countdown</i>(n):\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> n &lt;= <i class=\"n\">0</i>:                <i class=\"c\"># base case: stops the recursion</i>\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Lift off&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(n)\n    countdown(n - <i class=\"n\">1</i>)\n\ncountdown(<i class=\"n\">3</i>)</pre></figure>","see":["py.recursion","tk.err.recursion-after"],"t":"def countdown (n): if n <= 0 : # base case: stops the recursion print ( \"Lift off\" ) return print (n) countdown(n - 1 ) countdown( 3 )"},{"id":"py.err.unhashable","s":"py-errors","q":"TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'","qh":"TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'","a":"Lists, dicts and sets can't be dict keys or set items because they can change. Use a tuple instead of a list.","aka":"unhashable type list, unhashable dict, list as dict key, list in set","err":"unhashable type","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>seen = <i class=\"b\">set</i>()\nseen.add((<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>))           <i class=\"c\"># tuple, not [1, 2]</i>\ngrid = {(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>): <i class=\"s\">&quot;start&quot;</i>}\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(seen, grid)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"seen = set () seen.add(( 1 , 2 )) # tuple, not [1, 2] grid = {( 0 , 0 ): \"start\" } print (seen, grid)"},{"id":"py.err.circular-import","s":"py-errors","q":"ImportError: cannot import name 'X' from partially initialized module (circular import)","qh":"ImportError: cannot import name 'X' from partially initialized module (circular import)","a":"Either two of your files import each other, or your file has the same name as the module you import (e.g. <code>random.py</code>, <code>tkinter.py</code>). Rename your file, or move the shared code into a third file both can import.","aka":"circular import, partially initialized module, most likely due to a circular import, import each other","err":"partially initialized module|most likely due to a circular import","h":"<figure class=\"code out\" data-tag=\"output\"><pre>game.py  imports  ui.py\nui.py    imports  game.py      &lt;- circular\n\nfix: put the shared bits in  common.py,  and have both import it</pre></figure><p>Also delete any <code>__pycache__</code> folder after renaming.</p>","see":["tk.file-named-tkinter","py.own-module"],"t":"game.py imports ui.py ui.py imports game.py <- circular fix: put the shared bits in common.py, and have both import it Also delete any __pycache__ folder after renaming."},{"id":"py.err.eof","s":"py-errors","q":"EOFError: EOF when reading a line","qh":"EOFError: EOF when reading a line","a":"<code>input()</code> was called but there's no keyboard to read from: the program runs where input isn't connected (some online editors, a tkinter app started without a console, or a test runner). Run it in a terminal, or get input from a GUI widget instead.","aka":"eoferror, eof when reading a line, input not working, input in online ide","err":"EOFError","h":"","see":[],"t":""},{"id":"py.err.keyboardinterrupt","s":"py-errors","q":"KeyboardInterrupt (after pressing Ctrl+C)","qh":"KeyboardInterrupt (after pressing Ctrl+C)","a":"That's you stopping the program with Ctrl+C: not a bug. If it was stuck, look at the line shown to find the loop that never ended.","aka":"keyboardinterrupt, ctrl c, stop program, stop infinite loop","err":"KeyboardInterrupt","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> time\n<i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">3</i>):\n        time.sleep(<i class=\"n\">0.01</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">except</i> KeyboardInterrupt:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Stopped by user&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import time try : for i in range ( 3 ): time.sleep( 0.01 ) except KeyboardInterrupt: print ( \"Stopped by user\" )"},{"id":"py.err.index-str-assign","s":"py-errors","q":"TypeError: 'str' object does not support item assignment","qh":"TypeError: 'str' object does not support item assignment","a":"Strings and tuples can't be changed in place. Build a new one (or convert to a list, change it, and join).","aka":"str does not support item assignment, change character in string, tuple does not support item assignment, immutable","err":"does not support item assignment","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>word = <i class=\"s\">&quot;cat&quot;</i>\nword = <i class=\"s\">&quot;b&quot;</i> + word[<i class=\"n\">1</i>:]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(word)\nletters = <i class=\"b\">list</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;hello&quot;</i>)\nletters[<i class=\"n\">0</i>] = <i class=\"s\">&quot;j&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>.join(letters))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"word = \"cat\" word = \"b\" + word[ 1 :] print (word) letters = list ( \"hello\" ) letters[ 0 ] = \"j\" print ( \"\" .join(letters))"},{"id":"py.err.too-many-values","s":"py-errors","q":"ValueError: too many values to unpack (expected 2)","qh":"ValueError: too many values to unpack (expected 2)","a":"The number of names on the left doesn't match the number of items on the right. Common when looping a dict without <code>.items()</code>, or splitting a line that has more parts than expected.","aka":"too many values to unpack, not enough values to unpack, unpack error, expected 2 got 3","err":"too many values to unpack|not enough values to unpack","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>d = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>: <i class=\"n\">1</i>}\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> key, value <i class=\"k\">in</i> d.items():           <i class=\"c\"># not: for key, value in d</i>\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(key, value)\nname, *rest = <i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada Byron Lovelace&quot;</i>.split()\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(name, rest)\nfirst, second = <i class=\"s\">&quot;x=1=2&quot;</i>.split(<i class=\"s\">&quot;=&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(first, second)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"d = { \"a\" : 1 } for key, value in d.items(): # not: for key, value in d print (key, value) name, *rest = \"Ada Byron Lovelace\" .split() print (name, rest) first, second = \"x=1=2\" .split( \"=\" , 1 ) print (first, second)"},{"id":"py.err.missing-self-attr","s":"py-errors","q":"AttributeError: 'MyClass' object has no attribute 'x' (inside my class)","qh":"AttributeError: 'MyClass' object has no attribute 'x' (inside my class)","a":"The attribute was never set on <code>self</code>: you wrote <code>x = ...</code> instead of <code>self.x = ...</code> in <code>__init__</code>, misspelled <code>__init__</code> (needs two underscores each side), or used it before it was set.","aka":"object has no attribute class, self attribute missing, forgot self, init not called","err":"object has no attribute","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Player</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, name):      <i class=\"c\"># __init__, two underscores each side</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.name = name           <i class=\"c\"># not just: name = name</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.score = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n\np = Player(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(p.name, p.score)</pre></figure>","see":["py.class-basics"],"t":"class Player : def __init__ ( self , name): # __init__, two underscores each side self .name = name # not just: name = name self .score = 0 p = Player( \"Ada\" ) print (p.name, p.score)"},{"id":"py.err.permission","s":"py-errors","q":"PermissionError: [Errno 13] Permission denied","qh":"PermissionError: [Errno 13] Permission denied","a":"The file is open in another program (Excel locks files), the path is a folder not a file, or you're writing somewhere protected (like <code>C:\\Program Files</code>). Close the file, check the path, or save into your own folders.","aka":"permission denied, errno 13, can't write file, access denied","err":"PermissionError|Permission denied|Errno 13","h":"","see":[],"t":""},{"id":"py.err.oserror-path","s":"py-errors","q":"OSError: [Errno 22] Invalid argument (Windows path)","qh":"OSError: [Errno 22] Invalid argument (Windows path)","a":"Backslashes in Windows paths become escape codes (<code>\\n</code>, <code>\\t</code>, <code>\\U</code>). Use a raw string <code>r&quot;C:\\Users\\me\\file.txt&quot;</code>, forward slashes, or <code>pathlib</code>.","aka":"errno 22 invalid argument, unicode escape error path, windows path backslash, truncated \\UXXXXXXXX escape","err":"Errno 22|Invalid argument|truncated*escape|unicodeescape","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>path1 = <i class=\"s\">r&quot;C:\\Users\\me\\new_folder\\test.txt&quot;</i>\npath2 = <i class=\"s\">&quot;C:/Users/me/new_folder/test.txt&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(path1, path2)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"path1 = r\"C:\\Users\\me\\new_folder\\test.txt\" path2 = \"C:/Users/me/new_folder/test.txt\" print (path1, path2)"},{"id":"py.err.infinite-loop","s":"py-errors","q":"My program just hangs or never ends. How do I find out why?","qh":"My program just hangs or never ends. How do I find out why?","a":"Usually a <code>while</code> loop whose condition never becomes false (the variable never changes), or <code>input()</code> waiting for you. Press Ctrl+C to see where it stopped, or add a <code>print</code> inside the loop.","aka":"program hangs, infinite loop, stuck, nothing happens, frozen program, not responding","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>n = <i class=\"n\">10</i>\nsteps = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n<i class=\"k\">while</i> n != <i class=\"n\">1</i>:\n    n = n // <i class=\"n\">2</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> n % <i class=\"n\">2</i> == <i class=\"n\">0</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"n\">3</i> * n + <i class=\"n\">1</i>    <i class=\"c\"># make sure the loop variable changes</i>\n    steps += <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;finished in&quot;</i>, steps, <i class=\"s\">&quot;steps&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"n = 10 steps = 0 while n != 1 : n = n // 2 if n % 2 == 0 else 3 * n + 1 # make sure the loop variable changes steps += 1 print ( \"finished in\" , steps, \"steps\" )"},{"id":"py.class-basics","s":"py-classes","q":"How do I make a class (and what is self)?","qh":"How do I make a class (and what is self)?","a":"A class is a blueprint; each object made from it has its own data. <code>__init__</code> runs when an object is created; <code>self</code> is \"this particular object\", so <code>self.name</code> is that object's name.","aka":"class, object, oop, self, __init__, constructor, instance, create object, methods, object oriented","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Dog</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, name, age):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.name = name\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.age = age\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">bark</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{<i class=\"k\">self</i>.name}<i class=\"s\"> says woof</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">birthday</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.age += <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n\nrex = Dog(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Rex&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>)\nfido = Dog(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Fido&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nrex.birthday()\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(rex.bark(), rex.age, fido.age)</pre></figure><p>You write <code>self</code> in every method's definition but <b>don't</b> pass it when calling: <code>rex.bark()</code>.</p>","see":["py.inheritance","py.err.missing-self-attr"],"t":"class Dog : def __init__ ( self , name, age): self .name = name self .age = age def bark ( self ): return f\" { self .name} says woof \" def birthday ( self ): self .age += 1 rex = Dog( \"Rex\" , 3 ) fido = Dog( \"Fido\" , 5 ) rex.birthday() print (rex.bark(), rex.age, fido.age) You write self in every method's definition but don't pass it when calling: rex.bark() ."},{"id":"py.inheritance","s":"py-classes","q":"How does inheritance work (and what does super() do)?","qh":"How does inheritance work (and what does super() do)?","a":"<code>class Cat(Animal):</code> gets everything <code>Animal</code> has. Redefine a method to override it; call the parent's version with <code>super().method()</code>.","aka":"inheritance, subclass, parent class, child class, super, override method, extends","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Animal</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, name):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.name = name\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">speak</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;...&quot;</i>\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">intro</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">I&#x27;m </i>{<i class=\"k\">self</i>.name}<i class=\"s\"> and I say </i>{<i class=\"k\">self</i>.speak()}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Cat</i>(Animal):\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, name, indoor):\n        <i class=\"b\">super</i>().__init__(name)         <i class=\"c\"># let Animal set the name</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.indoor = indoor\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">speak</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;meow&quot;</i>\n\nc = Cat(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Tom&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(c.intro(), c.indoor, <i class=\"b\">isinstance</i>(c, Animal))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"class Animal : def __init__ ( self , name): self .name = name def speak ( self ): return \"...\" def intro ( self ): return f\" I'm { self .name} and I say { self .speak()} \" class Cat (Animal): def __init__ ( self , name, indoor): super ().__init__(name) # let Animal set the name self .indoor = indoor def speak ( self ): return \"meow\" c = Cat( \"Tom\" , True ) print (c.intro(), c.indoor, isinstance (c, Animal))"},{"id":"py.str-method","s":"py-classes","q":"How do I control what print() shows for my object (__str__ / __repr__)?","qh":"How do I control what print() shows for my object (__str__ / __repr__)?","a":"Define <code>__str__</code> (for people, used by <code>print</code>) and/or <code>__repr__</code> (for debugging, used in lists and the shell).","aka":"__str__, __repr__, print object, object at 0x, custom print, string representation","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Point</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, x, y):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.x, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.y = x, y\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__str__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">(</i>{<i class=\"k\">self</i>.x}<i class=\"s\">, </i>{<i class=\"k\">self</i>.y}<i class=\"s\">)</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__repr__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Point(</i>{<i class=\"k\">self</i>.x}<i class=\"s\">, </i>{<i class=\"k\">self</i>.y}<i class=\"s\">)</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>\n\np = Point(<i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(p)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>([p, Point(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>)])</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"class Point : def __init__ ( self , x, y): self .x, self .y = x, y def __str__ ( self ): return f\" ( { self .x} , { self .y} ) \" def __repr__ ( self ): return f\" Point( { self .x} , { self .y} ) \" p = Point( 2 , 3 ) print (p) print ([p, Point( 0 , 0 )])"},{"id":"py.class-vs-instance","s":"py-classes","q":"What's the difference between class attributes and instance attributes?","qh":"What's the difference between class attributes and instance attributes?","a":"Attributes set on <code>self</code> belong to one object. Attributes written directly in the class body are shared by all objects (careful with lists there).","aka":"class variable, instance variable, shared between objects, static variable","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Player</i>:\n    count = <i class=\"n\">0</i>                     <i class=\"c\"># shared by every Player</i>\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, name):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.name = name          <i class=\"c\"># this player&#x27;s own</i>\n        Player.count += <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n\na, b = Player(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>), Player(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Bob&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(Player.count, a.name, b.name)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"class Player : count = 0 # shared by every Player def __init__ ( self , name): self .name = name # this player's own Player.count += 1 a, b = Player( \"Ada\" ), Player( \"Bob\" ) print (Player.count, a.name, b.name)"},{"id":"py.dataclass","s":"py-classes","q":"Is there a shorter way to write a class that just holds data?","qh":"Is there a shorter way to write a class that just holds data?","a":"<code>@dataclass</code> writes <code>__init__</code>, <code>__repr__</code> and <code>==</code> for you.","aka":"dataclass, data class, record, struct, namedtuple","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">from</i> dataclasses <i class=\"k\">import</i> dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Item</i>:\n    name: <i class=\"b\">str</i>\n    price: <i class=\"b\">float</i>\n    tags: <i class=\"b\">list</i> = field(default_factory=<i class=\"b\">list</i>)\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">total</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, qty):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>.price * qty\n\npen = Item(<i class=\"s\">&quot;pen&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">1.5</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(pen, pen.total(<i class=\"n\">4</i>), pen == Item(<i class=\"s\">&quot;pen&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">1.5</i>))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"from dataclasses import dataclass, field @dataclass class Item : name: str price: float tags: list = field(default_factory= list ) def total ( self , qty): return self .price * qty pen = Item( \"pen\" , 1.5 ) print (pen, pen.total( 4 ), pen == Item( \"pen\" , 1.5 ))"},{"id":"py.property","s":"py-classes","q":"How do I make a computed attribute or validate a value when it's set (@property)?","qh":"How do I make a computed attribute or validate a value when it's set (@property)?","a":"<code>@property</code> makes a method read like an attribute; <code>@name.setter</code> runs code when it's assigned.","aka":"property, getter setter, computed attribute, validate attribute","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Temperature</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, celsius):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.celsius = celsius\n\n    @property\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">fahrenheit</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>.celsius * <i class=\"n\">9</i> / <i class=\"n\">5</i> + <i class=\"n\">32</i>\n\n    @property\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">celsius</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>._c\n\n    @celsius.setter\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">celsius</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, value):\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> value &lt; -<i class=\"n\">273.15</i>:\n            <i class=\"k\">raise</i> ValueError(<i class=\"s\">&quot;below absolute zero&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>._c = value\n\nt = Temperature(<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(t.fahrenheit)\nt.celsius = <i class=\"n\">100</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(t.fahrenheit)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"class Temperature : def __init__ ( self , celsius): self .celsius = celsius @property def fahrenheit ( self ): return self .celsius * 9 / 5 + 32 @property def celsius ( self ): return self ._c @celsius.setter def celsius ( self , value): if value < - 273.15 : raise ValueError( \"below absolute zero\" ) self ._c = value t = Temperature( 20 ) print (t.fahrenheit) t.celsius = 100 print (t.fahrenheit)"},{"id":"py.static-classmethod","s":"py-classes","q":"What are @staticmethod and @classmethod?","qh":"What are @staticmethod and @classmethod?","a":"A <code>@staticmethod</code> is a plain function kept inside the class (no <code>self</code>). A <code>@classmethod</code> receives the class as <code>cls</code>, often used as another way to create objects.","aka":"staticmethod, classmethod, alternative constructor, cls","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Date</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, y, m, d):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.y, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.m, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.d = y, m, d\n\n    @classmethod\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">from_string</i>(cls, text):\n        y, m, d = <i class=\"b\">map</i>(<i class=\"b\">int</i>, text.split(<i class=\"s\">&quot;-&quot;</i>))\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> cls(y, m, d)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">is_leap</i>(year):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> year % <i class=\"n\">4</i> == <i class=\"n\">0</i> <i class=\"k\">and</i> (year % <i class=\"n\">100</i> != <i class=\"n\">0</i> <i class=\"k\">or</i> year % <i class=\"n\">400</i> == <i class=\"n\">0</i>)\n\nd = Date.from_string(<i class=\"s\">&quot;2026-10-16&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(d.y, d.m, d.d, Date.is_leap(<i class=\"n\">2024</i>))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"class Date : def __init__ ( self , y, m, d): self .y, self .m, self .d = y, m, d @classmethod def from_string (cls, text): y, m, d = map ( int , text.split( \"-\" )) return cls(y, m, d) @staticmethod def is_leap (year): return year % 4 == 0 and (year % 100 != 0 or year % 400 == 0 ) d = Date.from_string( \"2026-10-16\" ) print (d.y, d.m, d.d, Date.is_leap( 2024 ))"},{"id":"py.objects-list","s":"py-classes","q":"How do I keep many objects in a list and find one?","qh":"How do I keep many objects in a list and find one?","a":"Store them in a list; loop or use <code>next(...)</code> to find one; sort with <code>key=lambda o: o.attr</code>.","aka":"list of objects, find object by attribute, loop objects, sort objects","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Student</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, name, grade):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.name, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.grade = name, grade\n\nstudents = [Student(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">91</i>), Student(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Bob&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">78</i>), Student(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Cy&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">85</i>)]\nbest = <i class=\"b\">max</i>(students, key=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i> s: s.grade)\nbob = <i class=\"b\">next</i>((s <i class=\"k\">for</i> s <i class=\"k\">in</i> students <i class=\"k\">if</i> s.name == <i class=\"s\">&quot;Bob&quot;</i>), <i class=\"k\">None</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(best.name, bob.grade)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> s <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">sorted</i>(students, key=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i> s: s.name):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(s.name, s.grade)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"class Student : def __init__ ( self , name, grade): self .name, self .grade = name, grade students = [Student( \"Ada\" , 91 ), Student( \"Bob\" , 78 ), Student( \"Cy\" , 85 )] best = max (students, key= lambda s: s.grade) bob = next ((s for s in students if s.name == \"Bob\" ), None ) print (best.name, bob.grade) for s in sorted (students, key= lambda s: s.name): print (s.name, s.grade)"},{"id":"py.dunder","s":"py-classes","q":"How do I make my objects work with +, ==, <, len() or for loops?","qh":"How do I make my objects work with +, ==, &lt;, len() or for loops?","a":"Define special methods: <code>__add__</code> (+), <code>__eq__</code> (==), <code>__lt__</code> (&lt;, used by sorting), <code>__len__</code> (len), <code>__iter__</code> (for loops), <code>__getitem__</code> (<code>obj[i]</code>).","aka":"operator overloading, magic methods, dunder methods, __eq__, __add__, __len__, __lt__, __iter__","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Vector</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, x, y):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.x, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.y = x, y\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__add__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, other):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> Vector(<i class=\"k\">self</i>.x + other.x, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.y + other.y)\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__eq__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, other):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> (<i class=\"k\">self</i>.x, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.y) == (other.x, other.y)\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__repr__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Vector(</i>{<i class=\"k\">self</i>.x}<i class=\"s\">, </i>{<i class=\"k\">self</i>.y}<i class=\"s\">)</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Deck</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.cards = [r + s <i class=\"k\">for</i> s <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;♠♥&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">for</i> r <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;AKQ&quot;</i>]\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__len__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"b\">len</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>.cards)\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__iter__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"b\">iter</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>.cards)\n\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(Vector(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>) + Vector(<i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>), Vector(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>) == Vector(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>))\nd = Deck()\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">len</i>(d), <i class=\"b\">list</i>(d))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"class Vector : def __init__ ( self , x, y): self .x, self .y = x, y def __add__ ( self , other): return Vector( self .x + other.x, self .y + other.y) def __eq__ ( self , other): return ( self .x, self .y) == (other.x, other.y) def __repr__ ( self ): return f\" Vector( { self .x} , { self .y} ) \" class Deck : def __init__ ( self ): self .cards = [r + s for s in \"♠♥\" for r in \"AKQ\" ] def __len__ ( self ): return len ( self .cards) def __iter__ ( self ): return iter ( self .cards) print (Vector( 1 , 2 ) + Vector( 3 , 4 ), Vector( 1 , 1 ) == Vector( 1 , 1 )) d = Deck() print ( len (d), list (d))"},{"id":"py.private","s":"py-classes","q":"How do I make attributes private?","qh":"How do I make attributes private?","a":"Python has no real private. By convention <code>_name</code> means \"internal, don't touch\". <code>__name</code> (two underscores) gets renamed to avoid clashes in subclasses.","aka":"private variable, underscore, double underscore, name mangling, encapsulation","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Account</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>._balance = <i class=\"n\">0</i>          <i class=\"c\"># internal by convention</i>\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">deposit</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, n):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>._balance += n\n\n    @property\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">balance</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>._balance\n\na = Account()\na.deposit(<i class=\"n\">50</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(a.balance)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"class Account : def __init__ ( self ): self ._balance = 0 # internal by convention def deposit ( self , n): self ._balance += n @property def balance ( self ): return self ._balance a = Account() a.deposit( 50 ) print (a.balance)"},{"id":"py.import","s":"py-modules","q":"How does import work (import x, from x import y, as)?","qh":"How does import work (import x, from x import y, as)?","a":"<code>import math</code> then <code>math.sqrt(4)</code>; <code>from math import sqrt</code> then <code>sqrt(4)</code>; <code>import numpy as np</code> gives a short name.","aka":"import, from import, import as, import module, import function","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> math\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> random <i class=\"k\">import</i> randint, choice\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> datetime <i class=\"k\">as</i> dt\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(math.pi, randint(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">6</i>), choice(<i class=\"s\">&quot;abc&quot;</i>), dt.date.today().year)</pre></figure>","see":["py.own-module","py.err.modulenotfound"],"t":"import math from random import randint, choice import datetime as dt print (math.pi, randint( 1 , 6 ), choice( \"abc\" ), dt.date.today().year)"},{"id":"py.pip","s":"py-modules","q":"How do I install a package with pip?","qh":"How do I install a package with pip?","a":"In a terminal (not inside Python): <code>python -m pip install package</code>. On Windows <code>py -m pip install package</code>; on macOS/Linux <code>python3 -m pip install package</code>.","aka":"pip install, install module, install library, pip not recognized, python -m pip, upgrade pip, pip list","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code sh\" data-tag=\"terminal\"><pre>py -m pip install requests              # Windows\npython3 -m pip install requests         # macOS / Linux\npython -m pip install --upgrade pip\npython -m pip list                       # what&#x27;s installed\npython -m pip uninstall requests</pre></figure><p><code>&#x27;pip&#x27; is not recognized</code> means pip isn't on PATH; <code>python -m pip</code> avoids that. Typing <code>pip install</code> <b>inside</b> the <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> prompt gives a SyntaxError: use the terminal/command prompt.</p>","see":["py.err.modulenotfound","py.venv"],"t":"py -m pip install requests # Windows python3 -m pip install requests # macOS / Linux python -m pip install --upgrade pip python -m pip list # what's installed python -m pip uninstall requests 'pip' is not recognized means pip isn't on PATH; python -m pip avoids that. Typing pip install inside the >>> prompt gives a SyntaxError: use the terminal/command prompt."},{"id":"py.venv","s":"py-modules","q":"What is a virtual environment (venv) and how do I make one?","qh":"What is a virtual environment (venv) and how do I make one?","a":"A folder with its own Python and packages, so projects don't clash. Create it, activate it, then pip installs go into it.","aka":"venv, virtual environment, virtualenv, activate venv, requirements.txt, isolate packages","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code sh\" data-tag=\"terminal\"><pre>python -m venv .venv\n.venv\\Scripts\\activate          # Windows\nsource .venv/bin/activate       # macOS / Linux\npip install requests\npip freeze &gt; requirements.txt   # save the list\npip install -r requirements.txt # recreate it elsewhere\ndeactivate</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"python -m venv .venv .venv\\Scripts\\activate # Windows source .venv/bin/activate # macOS / Linux pip install requests pip freeze > requirements.txt # save the list pip install -r requirements.txt # recreate it elsewhere deactivate"},{"id":"py.own-module","s":"py-modules","q":"How do I import my own file (module)?","qh":"How do I import my own file (module)?","a":"Put <code>helpers.py</code> in the same folder as your main script and <code>import helpers</code> (no <code>.py</code>). Then call <code>helpers.some_function()</code>.","aka":"import my own file, import another python file, my module, call function from another file, module not found own file","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"c\"># helpers.py</i>\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">shout</i>(text):\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> text.upper() + <i class=\"s\">&quot;!&quot;</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"c\"># main.py  (same folder)</i>\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> helpers\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> helpers <i class=\"k\">import</i> shout\n\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(helpers.shout(<i class=\"s\">&quot;hi&quot;</i>), shout(<i class=\"s\">&quot;there&quot;</i>))</pre></figure><p>Don't name your file after an existing module (<code>random.py</code>, <code>math.py</code>, <code>tkinter.py</code>...), or Python will import yours instead.</p>","see":["py.main-guard","py.err.circular-import"],"t":"# helpers.py def shout (text): return text.upper() + \"!\" # main.py (same folder) import helpers from helpers import shout print (helpers.shout( \"hi\" ), shout( \"there\" )) Don't name your file after an existing module ( random.py , math.py , tkinter.py ...), or Python will import yours instead."},{"id":"py.main-guard","s":"py-modules","q":"What does if __name__ == \"__main__\": mean?","qh":"What does if __name__ == \"__main__\": mean?","a":"Code inside it runs only when you run that file directly, not when another file imports it. Use it so importing your module doesn't start the program.","aka":"__name__ == __main__, main function, if name main, run only when executed","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">main</i>():\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Running the program&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">if</i> __name__ == <i class=\"s\">&quot;__main__&quot;</i>:\n    main()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"def main (): print ( \"Running the program\" ) if __name__ == \"__main__\" : main()"},{"id":"py.random","s":"py-modules","q":"How do I make random numbers?","qh":"How do I make random numbers?","a":"<code>random.randint(1, 6)</code> (both ends included), <code>random.random()</code> (0 to 1), <code>random.uniform(a, b)</code> (float), <code>random.choice(seq)</code>. <code>random.seed(n)</code> repeats the same sequence.","aka":"random number, randint, random float, random module, dice roll, seed, random between","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> random\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(random.randint(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">6</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">round</i>(random.uniform(<i class=\"n\">1.5</i>, <i class=\"n\">2.5</i>), <i class=\"n\">2</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(random.random())\nrandom.seed(<i class=\"n\">42</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>([random.randint(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>) <i class=\"k\">for</i> _ <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">5</i>)])</pre></figure><p>For passwords or tokens, use the <code>secrets</code> module instead (<code>secrets.token_hex(16)</code>).</p>","see":["py.random-choice"],"t":"import random print (random.randint( 1 , 6 )) print ( round (random.uniform( 1.5 , 2.5 ), 2 )) print (random.random()) random.seed( 42 ) print ([random.randint( 1 , 10 ) for _ in range ( 5 )]) For passwords or tokens, use the secrets module instead ( secrets.token_hex(16) )."},{"id":"py.sleep","s":"py-modules","q":"How do I pause or wait for a few seconds?","qh":"How do I pause or wait for a few seconds?","a":"<code>import time</code> then <code>time.sleep(2)</code> waits 2 seconds (decimals work: <code>0.5</code>). In tkinter use <code>after()</code> instead.","aka":"sleep, wait, delay, pause program, time.sleep, wait seconds","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> time\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>, -<i class=\"n\">1</i>):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(i)\n    time.sleep(<i class=\"n\">0.01</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Go!&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":["tk.after-not-sleep"],"t":"import time for i in range ( 3 , 0 , - 1 ): print (i) time.sleep( 0.01 ) print ( \"Go!\" )"},{"id":"py.datetime-format","s":"py-modules","q":"How do I get the current date and time, and format it?","qh":"How do I get the current date and time, and format it?","a":"<code>datetime.now()</code> and <code>.strftime(&quot;%d/%m/%Y %H:%M&quot;)</code>. Common codes: <code>%Y</code> year, <code>%m</code> month, <code>%d</code> day, <code>%H</code> hour (24h), <code>%I</code> hour (12h), <code>%M</code> minute, <code>%S</code> second, <code>%p</code> AM/PM, <code>%A</code> weekday, <code>%B</code> month name.","aka":"datetime, current date, current time, now, today, strftime, format date, date string, timestamp","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">from</i> datetime <i class=\"k\">import</i> datetime, date\nnow = datetime.now()\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(now)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(now.strftime(<i class=\"s\">&quot;%d/%m/%Y %H:%M&quot;</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(now.strftime(<i class=\"s\">&quot;%A %d %B %Y, %I:%M %p&quot;</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(date.today().isoformat())\nd = datetime.strptime(<i class=\"s\">&quot;16/10/2026&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;%d/%m/%Y&quot;</i>)      <i class=\"c\"># text -&gt; date</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(d.year, d.month, d.day)</pre></figure>","see":["py.date-math"],"t":"from datetime import datetime, date now = datetime.now() print (now) print (now.strftime( \"%d/%m/%Y %H:%M\" )) print (now.strftime( \"%A %d %B %Y, %I:%M %p\" )) print (date.today().isoformat()) d = datetime.strptime( \"16/10/2026\" , \"%d/%m/%Y\" ) # text -> date print (d.year, d.month, d.day)"},{"id":"py.date-math","s":"py-modules","q":"How do I add days to a date or find the difference between dates?","qh":"How do I add days to a date or find the difference between dates?","a":"Subtract dates to get a <code>timedelta</code>; add <code>timedelta(days=7)</code> to move a date.","aka":"date difference, days between dates, add days, timedelta, age from birthday, countdown days","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">from</i> datetime <i class=\"k\">import</i> date, timedelta\ntoday = date(<i class=\"n\">2026</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">16</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(today + timedelta(days=<i class=\"n\">30</i>))\nbirthday = date(<i class=\"n\">2010</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">5</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>((today - birthday).days, <i class=\"s\">&quot;days old&quot;</i>)\nage = today.year - birthday.year - ((today.month, today.day) &lt; (birthday.month, birthday.day))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;age&quot;</i>, age)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>((date(<i class=\"n\">2026</i>, <i class=\"n\">12</i>, <i class=\"n\">25</i>) - today).days, <i class=\"s\">&quot;days to go&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"from datetime import date, timedelta today = date( 2026 , 10 , 16 ) print (today + timedelta(days= 30 )) birthday = date( 2010 , 3 , 5 ) print ((today - birthday).days, \"days old\" ) age = today.year - birthday.year - ((today.month, today.day) < (birthday.month, birthday.day)) print ( \"age\" , age) print ((date( 2026 , 12 , 25 ) - today).days, \"days to go\" )"},{"id":"py.timing","s":"py-modules","q":"How do I time how long my code takes?","qh":"How do I time how long my code takes?","a":"Record <code>time.perf_counter()</code> before and after.","aka":"time code, measure speed, benchmark, perf_counter, timeit, execution time","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> time\nstart = time.perf_counter()\ntotal = <i class=\"b\">sum</i>(i * i <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">200_000</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">took </i>{time.perf_counter() - start:<i class=\"s\">.4f</i>}<i class=\"s\"> seconds</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import time start = time.perf_counter() total = sum (i * i for i in range ( 200_000 )) print ( f\" took {time.perf_counter() - start: .4f } seconds \" )"},{"id":"py.os-sys","s":"py-modules","q":"What are the os and sys modules for?","qh":"What are the os and sys modules for?","a":"<code>sys</code>: the running Python (<code>sys.argv</code> command-line arguments, <code>sys.exit()</code>, <code>sys.platform</code>, <code>sys.version</code>). <code>os</code>: the operating system (<code>os.getcwd()</code>, <code>os.environ</code>, <code>os.listdir()</code>).","aka":"os module, sys module, sys.argv, environment variables, sys.exit, os.environ, platform","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> os, sys\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(sys.version.split()[<i class=\"n\">0</i>], sys.platform)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(sys.argv)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(os.getcwd())\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(os.environ.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;HOME&quot;</i>) <i class=\"k\">or</i> os.environ.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;USERPROFILE&quot;</i>))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import os, sys print (sys.version.split()[ 0 ], sys.platform) print (sys.argv) print (os.getcwd()) print (os.environ.get( \"HOME\" ) or os.environ.get( \"USERPROFILE\" ))"},{"id":"py.exit","s":"py-modules","q":"How do I stop / exit the program?","qh":"How do I stop / exit the program?","a":"<code>sys.exit()</code> (optionally with a message or code). In a function, <code>return</code> leaves just that function. In tkinter, <code>root.destroy()</code>.","aka":"exit program, quit, sys.exit, stop script, end program, terminate","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\" data-tag=\"error\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> sys\nanswer = <i class=\"s\">&quot;no&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"k\">if</i> answer != <i class=\"s\">&quot;yes&quot;</i>:\n    sys.<i class=\"b\">exit</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Stopping: user said no&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;never reached&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":["tk.quit-vs-destroy"],"t":"import sys answer = \"no\" if answer != \"yes\" : sys. exit ( \"Stopping: user said no\" ) print ( \"never reached\" )"},{"id":"py.math-module","s":"py-modules","q":"What's in the math module (pi, sqrt, sin, factorial...)?","qh":"What's in the math module (pi, sqrt, sin, factorial...)?","a":"<code>math.pi</code>, <code>math.sqrt</code>, <code>math.sin/cos/tan</code> (radians), <code>math.radians</code>, <code>math.factorial</code>, <code>math.gcd</code>, <code>math.log</code>, <code>math.hypot</code>, <code>math.inf</code>.","aka":"math module, pi, square root, trigonometry, sin cos, factorial, gcd, log","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> math\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(math.pi, math.sqrt(<i class=\"n\">2</i>), math.factorial(<i class=\"n\">5</i>), math.gcd(<i class=\"n\">12</i>, <i class=\"n\">18</i>))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(math.sin(math.radians(<i class=\"n\">30</i>)), math.hypot(<i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>), math.log(<i class=\"n\">100</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import math print (math.pi, math.sqrt( 2 ), math.factorial( 5 ), math.gcd( 12 , 18 )) print (math.sin(math.radians( 30 )), math.hypot( 3 , 4 ), math.log( 100 , 10 ))"},{"id":"py.hash-password","s":"py-modules","q":"How do I store passwords safely?","qh":"How do I store passwords safely?","a":"Never store the password itself. Store a slow salted hash (<code>hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac</code>, or the <code>bcrypt</code> package), and compare hashes when logging in.","aka":"hash password, password hashing, store password, hashlib, bcrypt, salt","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> hashlib, os, hmac\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">make_hash</i>(password):\n    salt = os.urandom(<i class=\"n\">16</i>)\n    digest = hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac(<i class=\"s\">&quot;sha256&quot;</i>, password.encode(), salt, <i class=\"n\">200_000</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> salt.hex() + <i class=\"s\">&quot;:&quot;</i> + digest.hex()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">check</i>(password, stored):\n    salt_hex, digest_hex = stored.split(<i class=\"s\">&quot;:&quot;</i>)\n    digest = hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac(<i class=\"s\">&quot;sha256&quot;</i>, password.encode(), bytes.fromhex(salt_hex), <i class=\"n\">200_000</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> hmac.compare_digest(digest.hex(), digest_hex)\n\nstored = make_hash(<i class=\"s\">&quot;hunter2&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(check(<i class=\"s\">&quot;hunter2&quot;</i>, stored), check(<i class=\"s\">&quot;wrong&quot;</i>, stored))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import hashlib, os, hmac def make_hash (password): salt = os.urandom( 16 ) digest = hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac( \"sha256\" , password.encode(), salt, 200_000 ) return salt.hex() + \":\" + digest.hex() def check (password, stored): salt_hex, digest_hex = stored.split( \":\" ) digest = hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac( \"sha256\" , password.encode(), bytes.fromhex(salt_hex), 200_000 ) return hmac.compare_digest(digest.hex(), digest_hex) stored = make_hash( \"hunter2\" ) print (check( \"hunter2\" , stored), check( \"wrong\" , stored))"},{"id":"py.requests","s":"py-modules","q":"How do I download a web page or call a web API?","qh":"How do I download a web page or call a web API?","a":"Install <code>requests</code> and use <code>requests.get(url)</code>. <code>.json()</code> decodes JSON responses. Built-in alternative: <code>urllib.request</code>.","aka":"requests, http get, api call, download file, fetch url, urllib, json api","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> requests                       <i class=\"c\"># pip install requests</i>\n\nr = requests.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;https://api.github.com/repos/python/cpython&quot;</i>, timeout=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nr.raise_for_status()\ndata = r.json()\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(data[<i class=\"s\">&quot;stargazers_count&quot;</i>])</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import requests # pip install requests r = requests.get( \"https://api.github.com/repos/python/cpython\" , timeout= 10 ) r.raise_for_status() data = r.json() print (data[ \"stargazers_count\" ])"},{"id":"py.command-args","s":"py-modules","q":"How do I read command-line arguments?","qh":"How do I read command-line arguments?","a":"<code>sys.argv</code> is a list: <code>[script_name, arg1, arg2...]</code>. For options and help text use <code>argparse</code>.","aka":"command line arguments, sys.argv, argparse, script arguments, run with parameters","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> argparse\np = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Greet someone&quot;</i>)\np.add_argument(<i class=\"s\">&quot;name&quot;</i>, nargs=<i class=\"s\">&quot;?&quot;</i>, default=<i class=\"s\">&quot;world&quot;</i>)\np.add_argument(<i class=\"s\">&quot;--times&quot;</i>, <i class=\"b\">type</i>=<i class=\"b\">int</i>, default=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\nargs = p.parse_args()\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> _ <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(args.times):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Hello, </i>{args.name}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)</pre></figure><figure class=\"code sh\" data-tag=\"terminal\"><pre>python greet.py Ada --times 2</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import argparse p = argparse.ArgumentParser(description= \"Greet someone\" ) p.add_argument( \"name\" , nargs= \"?\" , default= \"world\" ) p.add_argument( \"--times\" , type = int , default= 1 ) args = p.parse_args() for _ in range (args.times): print ( f\" Hello, {args.name} \" ) python greet.py Ada --times 2"},{"id":"py.clear-screen","s":"py-modules","q":"How do I clear the console screen?","qh":"How do I clear the console screen?","a":"<code>os.system(&quot;cls&quot; if os.name == &quot;nt&quot; else &quot;clear&quot;)</code>. In IDLE this doesn't work (IDLE isn't a terminal); print blank lines instead.","aka":"clear screen, cls, clear terminal, clear output","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> os\nos.system(<i class=\"s\">&quot;cls&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> os.name == <i class=\"s\">&quot;nt&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;clear&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import os os.system( \"cls\" if os.name == \"nt\" else \"clear\" )"},{"id":"py.colors-terminal","s":"py-modules","q":"How do I print coloured text in the terminal?","qh":"How do I print coloured text in the terminal?","a":"ANSI codes like <code>&quot;\\033[31m&quot;</code> (red) and <code>&quot;\\033[0m&quot;</code> (reset) work in most terminals (Windows 10+ included). The <code>rich</code> or <code>colorama</code> packages make it easier. IDLE ignores them.","aka":"colored text, color terminal, ansi colors, colorama, rich, print in color","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>RED, GREEN, BOLD, RESET = <i class=\"s\">&quot;\\033[31m&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;\\033[32m&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;\\033[1m&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;\\033[0m&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{RED}<i class=\"s\">error</i>{RESET}<i class=\"s\"> </i>{GREEN}{BOLD}<i class=\"s\">ok</i>{RESET}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"RED, GREEN, BOLD, RESET = \"\\033[31m\" , \"\\033[32m\" , \"\\033[1m\" , \"\\033[0m\" print ( f\" {RED} error {RESET} {GREEN}{BOLD} ok {RESET} \" )"},{"id":"py.version","s":"py-modules","q":"Which Python version do I have?","qh":"Which Python version do I have?","a":"In a terminal: <code>python --version</code>. In code: <code>sys.version</code>.","aka":"python version, check version, python --version, which python","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code sh\" data-tag=\"terminal\"><pre>python --version          # or: py --version  /  python3 --version</pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> sys\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(sys.version_info &gt;= (<i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>), sys.version)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"python --version # or: py --version / python3 --version import sys print (sys.version_info >= ( 3 , 10 ), sys.version)"},{"id":"py.lambda","s":"py-howto","q":"What is a lambda?","qh":"What is a lambda?","a":"A one-line function without a name: <code>lambda x: x * 2</code>. Mostly used for <code>key=</code> in sorting and short callbacks.","aka":"lambda, anonymous function, inline function, lambda function, key lambda","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>double = <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> x: x * <i class=\"n\">2</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(double(<i class=\"n\">5</i>))\npairs = [(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;b&quot;</i>), (<i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>)]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">sorted</i>(pairs, key=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i> p: p[<i class=\"n\">1</i>]))</pre></figure>","see":["py.sort","tk.button-args"],"t":"double = lambda x: x * 2 print (double( 5 )) pairs = [( 1 , \"b\" ), ( 2 , \"a\" )] print ( sorted (pairs, key= lambda p: p[ 1 ]))"},{"id":"py.map-filter","s":"py-howto","q":"What do map() and filter() do?","qh":"What do map() and filter() do?","a":"<code>map(f, items)</code> applies <code>f</code> to each item; <code>filter(f, items)</code> keeps items where <code>f</code> is true. Wrap in <code>list()</code> to see them. List comprehensions often read better.","aka":"map, filter, apply function to list, map int input, list(map(int))","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>nums = <i class=\"b\">list</i>(<i class=\"b\">map</i>(<i class=\"b\">int</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;3 1 4 1 5&quot;</i>.split()))    <i class=\"c\"># handy for reading many numbers</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(nums)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">list</i>(<i class=\"b\">filter</i>(<i class=\"k\">lambda</i> n: n &gt; <i class=\"n\">2</i>, nums)))\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>([n <i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> nums <i class=\"k\">if</i> n &gt; <i class=\"n\">2</i>])              <i class=\"c\"># same thing</i></pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"nums = list ( map ( int , \"3 1 4 1 5\" .split())) # handy for reading many numbers print (nums) print ( list ( filter ( lambda n: n > 2 , nums))) print ([n for n in nums if n > 2 ]) # same thing"},{"id":"py.recursion","s":"py-howto","q":"How does recursion work?","qh":"How does recursion work?","a":"A function that calls itself on a smaller problem, with a <b>base case</b> that stops it.","aka":"recursion, recursive function, function calls itself, factorial recursive, base case","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">factorial</i>(n):\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> n &lt;= <i class=\"n\">1</i>:            <i class=\"c\"># base case</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> n * factorial(n - <i class=\"n\">1</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">total</i>(items):\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> items:\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> items[<i class=\"n\">0</i>] + total(items[<i class=\"n\">1</i>:])\n\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(factorial(<i class=\"n\">5</i>), total([<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>]))</pre></figure>","see":["py.err.recursion"],"t":"def factorial (n): if n <= 1 : # base case return 1 return n * factorial(n - 1 ) def total (items): if not items: return 0 return items[ 0 ] + total(items[ 1 :]) print (factorial( 5 ), total([ 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 ]))"},{"id":"py.fizzbuzz","s":"py-howto","q":"How do I do FizzBuzz?","qh":"How do I do FizzBuzz?","a":"Check divisibility by 15 (both) first, then 3, then 5.","aka":"fizzbuzz, fizz buzz, divisible by 3 and 5, modulo exercise","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">16</i>):\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> n % <i class=\"n\">15</i> == <i class=\"n\">0</i>:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;FizzBuzz&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">elif</i> n % <i class=\"n\">3</i> == <i class=\"n\">0</i>:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Fizz&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">elif</i> n % <i class=\"n\">5</i> == <i class=\"n\">0</i>:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Buzz&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">else</i>:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(n)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"for n in range ( 1 , 16 ): if n % 15 == 0 : print ( \"FizzBuzz\" ) elif n % 3 == 0 : print ( \"Fizz\" ) elif n % 5 == 0 : print ( \"Buzz\" ) else : print (n)"},{"id":"py.prime","s":"py-howto","q":"How do I check if a number is prime (or list primes)?","qh":"How do I check if a number is prime (or list primes)?","a":"Try dividing by every number from 2 up to the square root.","aka":"prime number, is prime, list of primes, sieve, prime checker","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">is_prime</i>(n):\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> n &lt; <i class=\"n\">2</i>:\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"k\">False</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> d <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"b\">int</i>(n ** <i class=\"n\">0.5</i>) + <i class=\"n\">1</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> n % d == <i class=\"n\">0</i>:\n            <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"k\">False</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"k\">True</i>\n\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>([n <i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">50</i>) <i class=\"k\">if</i> is_prime(n)])</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"def is_prime (n): if n < 2 : return False for d in range ( 2 , int (n ** 0.5 ) + 1 ): if n % d == 0 : return False return True print ([n for n in range ( 50 ) if is_prime(n)])"},{"id":"py.fibonacci","s":"py-howto","q":"How do I make the Fibonacci sequence?","qh":"How do I make the Fibonacci sequence?","a":"Keep the last two numbers and add them.","aka":"fibonacci, fib sequence, fibonacci numbers","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">fib</i>(n):\n    a, b = <i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n    out = []\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> _ <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(n):\n        out.append(a)\n        a, b = b, a + b\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> out\n\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(fib(<i class=\"n\">12</i>))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"def fib (n): a, b = 0 , 1 out = [] for _ in range (n): out.append(a) a, b = b, a + b return out print (fib( 12 ))"},{"id":"py.digits","s":"py-howto","q":"How do I get the digits of a number, sum them, or reverse a number?","qh":"How do I get the digits of a number, sum them, or reverse a number?","a":"Convert to a string: <code>str(n)</code>. Or use <code>% 10</code> and <code>// 10</code> to peel off digits mathematically.","aka":"sum of digits, reverse number, digits of number, count digits, split number into digits","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>n = <i class=\"n\">4721</i>\ndigits = [<i class=\"b\">int</i>(d) <i class=\"k\">for</i> d <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">str</i>(n)]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(digits, <i class=\"b\">sum</i>(digits), <i class=\"b\">int</i>(<i class=\"b\">str</i>(n)[::-<i class=\"n\">1</i>]), <i class=\"b\">len</i>(<i class=\"b\">str</i>(n)))\ntotal, x = <i class=\"n\">0</i>, n\n<i class=\"k\">while</i> x:\n    total += x % <i class=\"n\">10</i>\n    x //= <i class=\"n\">10</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(total)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"n = 4721 digits = [ int (d) for d in str (n)] print (digits, sum (digits), int ( str (n)[::- 1 ]), len ( str (n))) total, x = 0 , n while x: total += x % 10 x //= 10 print (total)"},{"id":"py.even-odd","s":"py-howto","q":"How do I check if a number is even or odd (or divisible by something)?","qh":"How do I check if a number is even or odd (or divisible by something)?","a":"<code>n % 2 == 0</code> is even. <code>n % k == 0</code> means divisible by <code>k</code>.","aka":"even odd, divisible, modulo, remainder check, multiple of","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">7</i>, <i class=\"n\">15</i>):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(n, <i class=\"s\">&quot;even&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> n % <i class=\"n\">2</i> == <i class=\"n\">0</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;odd&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;| divisible by 5&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> n % <i class=\"n\">5</i> == <i class=\"n\">0</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"for n in ( 4 , 7 , 15 ): print (n, \"even\" if n % 2 == 0 else \"odd\" , \"| divisible by 5\" if n % 5 == 0 else \"\" )"},{"id":"py.max-loop","s":"py-howto","q":"How do I find the largest number without max() (or with its position)?","qh":"How do I find the largest number without max() (or with its position)?","a":"Keep the best so far while looping. With <code>max</code>: <code>max(range(len(x)), key=x.__getitem__)</code> gives its index.","aka":"find largest, find max manually, highest score, index of max","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>scores = [<i class=\"n\">12</i>, <i class=\"n\">45</i>, <i class=\"n\">7</i>, <i class=\"n\">45</i>, <i class=\"n\">30</i>]\nbest = scores[<i class=\"n\">0</i>]\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> s <i class=\"k\">in</i> scores:\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> s &gt; best:\n        best = s\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(best, scores.index(<i class=\"b\">max</i>(scores)))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"scores = [ 12 , 45 , 7 , 45 , 30 ] best = scores[ 0 ] for s in scores: if s > best: best = s print (best, scores.index( max (scores)))"},{"id":"py.menu-loop","s":"py-howto","q":"How do I make a text menu that repeats until the user quits?","qh":"How do I make a text menu that repeats until the user quits?","a":"<code>while True:</code> show options, read a choice, act with if/elif, <code>break</code> on quit.","aka":"menu loop, console menu, choose option, text menu program, program loop","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>items = []\n<i class=\"k\">while</i> <i class=\"k\">True</i>:\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;\\n1) Add  2) Show  3) Quit&quot;</i>)\n    choice = <i class=\"b\">input</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Choose: &quot;</i>).strip()\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> choice == <i class=\"s\">&quot;1&quot;</i>:\n        items.append(<i class=\"b\">input</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Item: &quot;</i>))\n    <i class=\"k\">elif</i> choice == <i class=\"s\">&quot;2&quot;</i>:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(items)\n    <i class=\"k\">elif</i> choice <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;3&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;q&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;quit&quot;</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">break</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">else</i>:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Unknown option&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Bye&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"items = [] while True : print ( \"\\n1) Add 2) Show 3) Quit\" ) choice = input ( \"Choose: \" ).strip() if choice == \"1\" : items.append( input ( \"Item: \" )) elif choice == \"2\" : print (items) elif choice in ( \"3\" , \"q\" , \"quit\" ): break else : print ( \"Unknown option\" ) print ( \"Bye\" )"},{"id":"py.guess-game","s":"py-howto","q":"How do I make a number guessing game (console)?","qh":"How do I make a number guessing game (console)?","a":"Pick a random number, loop asking for guesses, say higher/lower, count tries.","aka":"guess the number, guessing game, higher or lower, random game","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> random\nsecret = random.randint(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntries = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n<i class=\"k\">while</i> <i class=\"k\">True</i>:\n    text = <i class=\"b\">input</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Guess 1-10: &quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> text.isdigit():\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Numbers only&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> text <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;quit&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;q&quot;</i>):\n            <i class=\"k\">break</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">continue</i>\n    tries += <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n    g = <i class=\"b\">int</i>(text)\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> g == secret:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Correct in </i>{tries}<i class=\"s\"> tries!</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">break</i>\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Higher&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> g &lt; secret <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;Lower&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":["tk.recipe-guess"],"t":"import random secret = random.randint( 1 , 10 ) tries = 0 while True : text = input ( \"Guess 1-10: \" ) if not text.isdigit(): print ( \"Numbers only\" ) if text in ( \"quit\" , \"q\" ): break continue tries += 1 g = int (text) if g == secret: print ( f\" Correct in {tries} tries! \" ) break print ( \"Higher\" if g < secret else \"Lower\" )"},{"id":"py.generators","s":"py-howto","q":"What does yield do (generators)?","qh":"What does yield do (generators)?","a":"A function with <code>yield</code> produces values one at a time when looped over, instead of building a whole list. Good for big or endless sequences.","aka":"yield, generator, lazy sequence, generator expression, iterator","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">countdown</i>(n):\n    <i class=\"k\">while</i> n &gt; <i class=\"n\">0</i>:\n        <i class=\"k\">yield</i> n\n        n -= <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> x <i class=\"k\">in</i> countdown(<i class=\"n\">3</i>):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(x)\nsquares = (n * n <i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">1_000_000</i>))     <i class=\"c\"># generator expression: no big list</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">next</i>(squares), <i class=\"b\">next</i>(squares), <i class=\"b\">sum</i>(n <i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">10</i>)))</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"def countdown (n): while n > 0 : yield n n -= 1 for x in countdown( 3 ): print (x) squares = (n * n for n in range ( 1_000_000 )) # generator expression: no big list print ( next (squares), next (squares), sum (n for n in range ( 10 )))"},{"id":"py.time-ago","s":"py-howto","q":"How do I convert seconds into minutes and seconds (or hours)?","qh":"How do I convert seconds into minutes and seconds (or hours)?","a":"<code>divmod(seconds, 60)</code> gives minutes and leftover seconds.","aka":"seconds to minutes, format duration, divmod, hh mm ss","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>total = <i class=\"n\">3725</i>\nh, rest = <i class=\"b\">divmod</i>(total, <i class=\"n\">3600</i>)\nm, s = <i class=\"b\">divmod</i>(rest, <i class=\"n\">60</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{h}<i class=\"s\">:</i>{m:<i class=\"s\">02d</i>}<i class=\"s\">:</i>{s:<i class=\"s\">02d</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"total = 3725 h, rest = divmod (total, 3600 ) m, s = divmod (rest, 60 ) print ( f\" {h} : {m: 02d } : {s: 02d } \" )"},{"id":"py.unpack-star","s":"py-howto","q":"How do I swap, unpack or \"spread\" values?","qh":"How do I swap, unpack or \"spread\" values?","a":"<code>a, b = b, a</code> swaps. <code>first, *rest = items</code> grabs the first and the rest. <code>print(*items)</code> spreads a list into separate arguments.","aka":"unpacking, star unpack, swap two variables, a b = b a, spread operator","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre>a, b = <i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>\na, b = b, a\nfirst, *middle, last = [<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">5</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(a, b, first, middle, last)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(*[<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;z&quot;</i>], sep=<i class=\"s\">&quot; | &quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"a, b = 1 , 2 a, b = b, a first, *middle, last = [ 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 ] print (a, b, first, middle, last) print (*[ \"x\" , \"y\" , \"z\" ], sep= \" | \" )"},{"id":"py.debug-print","s":"py-howto","q":"How do I debug my code (find where it goes wrong)?","qh":"How do I debug my code (find where it goes wrong)?","a":"Print variables at key points (<code>print(f&quot;{x=}&quot;)</code>), read the traceback bottom-up, test small pieces on their own, and use a debugger (IDLE: Debug &gt; Debugger; VS Code: click left of a line number to set a breakpoint, then F5; or put <code>breakpoint()</code> in your code).","aka":"debug, debugging, find bug, print debugging, breakpoint, step through code, pdb","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">average</i>(nums):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">DEBUG </i>{nums=}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)          <i class=\"c\"># what came in?</i>\n    total = <i class=\"b\">sum</i>(nums)\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">DEBUG </i>{total=}<i class=\"s\"> </i>{<i class=\"b\">len</i>(nums)=}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> total / <i class=\"b\">len</i>(nums)\n\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(average([<i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">9</i>]))</pre></figure>","see":["py.read-traceback"],"t":"def average (nums): print ( f\" DEBUG {nums=} \" ) # what came in? total = sum (nums) print ( f\" DEBUG {total=} { len (nums)=} \" ) return total / len (nums) print (average([ 2 , 4 , 9 ]))"},{"id":"py.assert","s":"py-howto","q":"What does assert do (quick tests)?","qh":"What does assert do (quick tests)?","a":"<code>assert condition, &quot;message&quot;</code> raises <code>AssertionError</code> if the condition is false. Use it to check your functions; for real test suites use <code>pytest</code>.","aka":"assert, test function, unit test, check result, pytest","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">add</i>(a, b):\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> a + b\n\n<i class=\"k\">assert</i> add(<i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>) == <i class=\"n\">5</i>\n<i class=\"k\">assert</i> add(-<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">1</i>) == <i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;should cancel out&quot;</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;all tests passed&quot;</i>)</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"def add (a, b): return a + b assert add( 2 , 3 ) == 5 assert add(- 1 , 1 ) == 0 , \"should cancel out\" print ( \"all tests passed\" )"},{"id":"py.copy-paste-run","s":"py-howto","q":"How do I run a Python file?","qh":"How do I run a Python file?","a":"In IDLE: open the file and press F5. In VS Code: the ▶ button (top right). In a terminal: go to the folder with <code>cd</code> and type <code>python file.py</code> (<code>py file.py</code> on Windows, <code>python3 file.py</code> on macOS/Linux).","aka":"run python file, run script, execute python, how to run code, python command, run in terminal, f5 idle","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code sh\" data-tag=\"terminal\"><pre>cd Documents/projects\npython my_script.py</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"cd Documents/projects python my_script.py"},{"id":"py.idle-tips","s":"py-howto","q":"How do I use IDLE well (shortcuts, running, restarting)?","qh":"How do I use IDLE well (shortcuts, running, restarting)?","a":"F5 runs the file, Ctrl+F6 restarts the shell, Alt+3 / Alt+4 comment / uncomment lines, Ctrl+] / Ctrl+[ indent / dedent, Alt+P / Alt+N cycle through past shell commands, Tab completes names.","aka":"idle, idle shortcuts, idle shell, idle editor, idle tips, comment out idle, indent idle","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code out\" data-tag=\"output\"><pre>F5          run the current file          (Run &gt; Run Module)\nCtrl+F6     restart the shell             (clears old variables and windows)\nAlt+3/Alt+4 comment / uncomment lines     (Format menu)\nCtrl+] / [  indent / dedent selection\nAlt+P/Alt+N previous / next command in the shell\nTab         autocomplete\nAlt+/       expand word from the file\nDebug &gt; Debugger, then run: step through your code line by line</pre></figure><p>On macOS use Ctrl where the list says Alt for some shortcuts; the menus always show the right key.</p>","see":["tk.err.no-mainloop-idle"],"t":"F5 run the current file (Run > Run Module) Ctrl+F6 restart the shell (clears old variables and windows) Alt+3/Alt+4 comment / uncomment lines (Format menu) Ctrl+] / [ indent / dedent selection Alt+P/Alt+N previous / next command in the shell Tab autocomplete Alt+/ expand word from the file Debug > Debugger, then run: step through your code line by line On macOS use Ctrl where the list says Alt for some shortcuts; the menus always show the right key."}]};
